window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"title":"Cosmology & Metaphysical Rules","group":"World","url":"world/cosmology.html","text":"Cosmology Metaphysical Rules CANON The metaphysical operating rules of the Skyward Cinders world: a familiar fantasy surface that gets stranger the deeper you dive, where death is final, evolution is an active force, and the world’s aesthetic runs on the Flow. Summary — The world presents as a simple fantasy setting upfront with “weird stuff out back” (001 §Setting Goals): a second layer of oddity sits under an unassuming surface, and strangeness accelerates the deeper you dive (M02702, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Its hard metaphysical rules are few but firm — true resurrection is impossible by any means, evolution manifests physically through bloodshed, and the cultural/aesthetic throughline of rivers and breath ties into the [[the-flow|Flow]] (M03779, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Narrative structure of reality “Second layer of oddity under an unassuming surface, strangeness accelerates the deeper you dive” (M02702, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Explicit model: “Like Bloodborne’s transition from Gothic to Eldritch” (M02703, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). “Begin with the familiar and dip into the strange” (M02704, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Despite the overall light, humorous tone, the design goal is to “inject a sense of hopelessness somewhere” (M02714, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Death resurrection ‘True resurrection’ is assumed impossible by any means, magical or otherwise (M02659, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). [[eris|Eris]] is mistaken in her belief that stolen life force could possibly be used to resurrect the dead (M02659, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). The strength-stealing power (Ajax’s pact power, granted by the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]]) “can only take and never give back” (M02660, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Death spirits do not bring death; they appear where death occurs and ease folks’ passing — which is why many believe them a frightful bad omen (001 §Death Spirits; see [[death|Death]] for the named example). Evolution as a world force “It’s the physical manifestation of evolution with replacing ‘trial and error’ with ‘Victory by battle’ (bloodshed).” (M02698, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22) — the core rule behind [[drykksar|Drykksar]]/Dracosis. Monsters are evolving: “People have identified that monsters are growing just a little too fast” (M02715, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). A cult of people who graft monster parts onto themselves foreshadows dracosis and the discarding of one’s humanity (M02717, FreshToast, 2023-02-24; see [[cultboy]]). Eventually the hero’s lineage would have become like-dragons themselves (M02681, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22) — see [[lysander]] and [[bastard-damaris|the bastard Damaris]]. Vampirism and Dracosis “are both external factors messing with the natural order of thing” (M05199, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10) — the thematic treatment lives on [[themes]]. The Flow linkage Rivers — especially the big one — are culturally important across Khorath, with river-related holidays and sayings; “This works pretty well with Flow and the general aesthetic of the setting” (M03777–M03779, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). The [[the-flow|Flow]] system itself (capacity, channeling efficiency) is a magic-system page; this page records only that the world’s river reverence and aesthetic were deliberately aligned with it. See [[world-map-and-travel|the river system]] for the geography. Related motif: magic connects to stamina and the theme of breath, “giving ‘out of breath’ another meaning” (001 §Theme of Breath). Visual language “Warm/orange/good vs cool/blue/bad for a subtle visual language” (M02311, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Open questions None recorded as DEBATED in 001’s Cosmic and Metaphysical Rules section; the rules above are untagged descriptive canon or dated agreements. Design history 2023-02-16 — visual-language rule set: warm/orange/good vs cool/blue/bad (M02311, FreshToast). 2023-02-21 — death rules fixed: true resurrection impossible; Eris mistaken; the pact power “can only take and never give back” (M02659–M02660, FreshToast). 2023-02-22 — evolution framed as a physical world force acting through bloodshed (M02698, CrunchyConniptions); humanity/lineage consequences noted (M02681, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-24 — narrative-strangeness structure agreed: second layer of oddity, Bloodborne Gothic-to-Eldritch model, begin familiar and dip into strange, inject hopelessness somewhere (M02702–M02704, M02714, FreshToast); monsters observed evolving too fast (M02715, FreshToast). 2023-10-13 — river reverence tied to the Flow and the general aesthetic of the setting (M03779, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M02311 (2023-02-16); M02659–M02660 (2023-02-21); M02681, M02698 (2023-02-22); M02702–M02704, M02714–M02715, M02717 (2023-02-24); M03777–M03779 (2023-10-13) — FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions Cleaned: 000 Part 8 (visual language); Part 10 (resurrection, evolution, strangeness, hopelessness); river discussion (Flow linkage) Docs: 001 §Rules of the World / Cosmic and Metaphysical Rules; §Resurrection and Death; §Visual Language; §The River System; §Theme of Breath Ledger: world rows for M02311 (visual language), M02659–M02660 (resurrection), M02681/M02698 (humanity/evolution), M02702–M02704 (tone goal), M02715–M02717 (cult/monsters); meta row for M02714 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Demi-Humans","group":"World","url":"world/demi-humans.html","text":"Demi-Humans CANON The setting’s part-human peoples of mixed lineage, established as ordinary in scale — demi-humans “aren’t superhumanly tall” — with the Catfolk-human [[thorne-tabby-tumblemane|Thorne]] as the illustrative example. Summary — Demi-humans are the world’s mixed-lineage part-human peoples. The one firm rule the notes set is that they are not superhumanly tall: “demi-humans aren’t superhumanly tall so more likely he’s near your height lol” (M03381, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Mixed lineages exist, but the only concrete illustration in the notes is a Catfolk/human combination sourced from the placeholder character [[thorne-tabby-tumblemane|Thorne “Tabby” Tumblemane]], so that mixture is an illustration rather than settled species canon. Description Demi-humans are of mixed human/non-human lineage and are of ordinary human scale — explicitly “aren’t superhumanly tall” (M03381, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). The illustrative mixed lineage is Catfolk + human : [[thorne-tabby-tumblemane|Thorne]] is described as “this combination of Catfolk and human lineage” possessing “the body strength of a human with the agility and reflexes of a Catfolk,” with pointed ears and golden eyes (000 §Thorne; raw Thorne profile). DEBATED — because this is drawn from a placeholder character, treat Catfolk/human as illustration, not confirmed species rule (001 §Demi-Humans). Story connections A demi-human party/companion candidate was floated to be paired with a creature “that’s supposed to be dangerous but is surprisingly loyal and docile,” alongside the existing Dianthus (M04319, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). One of the [[caelian-odyssey|Caelian Odyssey]] cast is tagged in the design notes as a “Lycosian Male (Demi-Human)” (000 Part 12, character sheet line). Open questions DEBATED Whether Catfolk/human is a general demi-human rule or specific to [[thorne-tabby-tumblemane|Thorne]]; the notes give only the one placeholder-sourced example (001 §Demi-Humans). What other lineages exist beyond Catfolk is not enumerated in the notes. Design history 2023-06-04 — demi-humans established as not superhumanly tall, of roughly human height (M03381, FreshToast), in discussion of the Catfolk-adjacent [[thorne-tabby-tumblemane|Thorne]] concept. 2023-10-17 — a demi-human companion + “dangerous but loyal/docile” creature pairing floated alongside Dianthus (M04319, FreshToast). Latest note. Provenance Raw: M03381 (2023-06-04, not superhumanly tall); M04319 (2023-10-17, demi-human companion/creature pairing) — FreshToast; Thorne profile (Catfolk/human illustration) Cleaned: 000 §Demi-Humans; 000 §Thorne (Catfolk/human lineage) Docs: 001 §Demi-Humans Ledger: world/character rows for M03381 and M04319 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Guild System","group":"World","url":"world/guilds.html","text":"Guild System DEBATED A proposed set of adventurer guilds for exploring, looting, and killing, with a rank ladder — and an isekai-style “gang of bullies” that treats the underdog protagonist roughly. Summary — The team floated ordinary adventurer guilds “for exploring, looting and killing or whatnot” that the player could level up through (M05040, FreshToast, 2023-11-16). A ranking ladder was proposed — F, E, D, C, B, A, S (or metals/precious gems) — and, leaning into isekai convention, the guild would be “a little mean” (M05044), “treat her like the underdog” (M05045) “like every other isekai” (M05048), with “that gang of bullies” waiting when the underdog protagonist walks in (M05050, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-16). The distinct “reckoners” guild was explicitly not the one the party joins (M05040, FreshToast). Details remain unsettled — DEBATED . Description Guilds “for exploring, looting and killing or whatnot,” which the player “could maybe level up” (M05040, FreshToast, 2023-11-16). The party would not join the “reckoners” guild specifically (M05037, M05040, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-11-16) — “so we have normal guilds in the game, for surzezzz?” confirms ordinary guilds exist alongside. Rules / structure Proposed rank ladder: F, E, D, C, B, A, S — “or metals/precious gems” (000 §Guild System). Guilds could level up as an institution as well (000 §Guild System). Social texture “Maybe the guild is a little mean” (M05044), “Treat her like the underdog” (M05045), “Like every other isekai” (M05048) (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-16). “You go to a guild, and there is that gang of bullies” (M05050, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-16), countered by FreshToast noting “plenty isekai have people treat new mercenaries nicely” (M05049, FreshToast, 2023-11-16). Ties into the low social standing of [[mercenaries]]. Open questions DEBATED Whether the rank system is letters (F→S) or metals/precious gems (001 §Guild System). DEBATED Whether the guild treats the protagonist as a bullied underdog or new mercenaries are welcomed (M05049–M05050, 2023-11-16). Design history 2023-11-16 — guilds proposed for exploring/looting/killing with a level-up ladder, distinct from the “reckoners”; rank system (F→S or metals/gems) and the isekai “gang of bullies” underdog framing discussed (M05037–M05050, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). Only recorded discussion; left unsettled. Provenance Raw: M05037 (reckoners question); M05040 (guilds for exploring/looting/killing, level up); M05041 (metals/precious gems ranks); M05044 (guild is a little mean); M05045 (treat her like the underdog); M05048 (like every other isekai); M05049 (new mercenaries treated nicely); M05050 (gang of bullies) — FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-16 Cleaned: 000 §Guild System Docs: 001 §Guild System Ledger: world/mechanics rows for M05037–M05050 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"History & Ages","group":"World","url":"world/history-and-ages.html","text":"History Ages EVOLVED The world’s historical backbone as currently conceived: a deep past in which dragons took over the dinosaurs, and a legendary age defined by the hero Ajax’s pact and his dragon-slaying — with the earlier “Age of Light” cosmological history preserved below as a superseded iteration. Summary — In the current (Skyward Cinders) conception, in-world history centres on the age of [[ajax|Ajax]]: the only warrior truly brave enough to fight a dragon, who traded half his lifespan to the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]] for the power to steal strength (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21), slew his dragon, and in doing so damaged the top of a mountain — causing the great river to form from several smaller ones (M03824, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Deeper still, current dragons are descendants of dinosaurs, who never actually died off but were taken over by dragons (001 §Dracosis Core Concept; ledger row M02691, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Two whole earlier framings of the world’s history — the CRISPR future and the Age of Light — were superseded and are recorded in Design history. The deep past: dragons and dinosaurs Current dragons are descendants of dinosaurs, who never actually died off but were instead taken over by dragons (001 §Dracosis Core Concept; M02691, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). “Maybe that’s how dragons survive. they are killed and their power taken into another being. then they are merged with the being. and over and over again. like evolution. this is what the dragons did to the previous beings—dinosaurs?” (001 §Vaenngrym; see [[vaenngrym]] and [[drykksar]]). The age of Ajax (legendary history) [[ajax|Ajax]] became desperate for strength to defend his people — “the only warrior truly brave enough to fight a fucking dragon” — and traded half his lifespan to the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]] for the ability to steal the strength of others (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). The legend says Ajax split a mountain to make the twinned rivers (M03820, FreshToast, 2023-10-13); “the narrative is exaggerative he just damaged the top of a mountain during his fight with the dragon which causes a river to form from several smaller ones. Ajax was high of absorbing a dragon’s strength at the peak of his power” (M03824, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). A lake the party goes under “was flooded into the state it’s in today by the great hero the second game’s all about” (M03873, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Ajax left behind seven relics as informal trials — his “legacy” — anticipating that a dangerous descendant would one day need to be stopped (001 §Ajax’s Legacy System; details on the [[ajax|Ajax]] page). His power has diluted with each heir; [[lysander|Lysander]] and the [[bastard-damaris|bastard Damaris]] carry weakened versions (001 §Diluted Power in Descendants). [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]], the dragon of the present story, is the son of Ajax’s dragon (001 §Vaenngrym). Present-day historical texture The political structure is no longer a kingdom: one county ruled by a countess or count, with three baronies (001 §Political Structure — geography pages cover the regions). People have identified that monsters are growing just a little too fast (M02715, FreshToast, 2023-02-24) — rampant evolution is a live historical process, not a finished one (see [[cosmology]]). Open questions None DEBATED in the history sections themselves; the undecided matters attached to this history (e.g. whether [[locke|Locke]] gets infected with Dracosis) live on [[drykksar]] and the character pages. Design history 2021-03-09 — SCRAPPED CRISPR future setting : an early, entirely different premise — “exploring the idea of a future with CRISPR” (M00027, spirit_noodle); super humans through CRISPR with super regeneration, intelligence and lifespan (M00021–M00024, CrunchyConniptions); “What happens if you take the current world and quadruple peoples life span, intelligence?” (M00028); “The next epoch of the human race. The age after the age of information and digitization. Technological advancement is halted and now biological advancement is the goal” (M00029–M00032, spirit_noodle); ethical dilemmas and conflicting factions with diametrically opposed philosophies (M00033). Both authors claimed the idea (“My idea!” — M00020, CrunchyConniptions; “lies. my idea” — M00025–M00026, spirit_noodle). Discussed but abandoned (001 §CRISPR Future Setting). 2021-06-26 — EVOLVED Age of Light narrative (early iteration): “Undying are told to end the age of light to save their own kind. Heros are told to prolong the age light to continue the prosperity of their own kind. both push their own narrative to opposite ends. both can fulfill their kind’s wants or betray them. leading 4 final outcomes… which are essentially 2 outcomes actually. let there be light or no light” (M00242, CrunchyConniptions). Undying could be enemies to heroes “or be sun-bros” (M00242). The Undying species : “an ancient eternal species that was kept down by the heroes’ species. They became shriveled jerky due to the eons of suffering” (M00243, CrunchyConniptions); the undying counterparts of heroes aren’t good at borrowing other heroes’ powers, while the original undying is very good at that (M00244, CrunchyConniptions). This whole cosmological history belongs to the earliest concept phase and was superseded by the Ajax-centred history below. 2023-02-21/22 — current historical backbone established: Ajax’s pact with the Daemon of Want (M02653, FreshToast); dragons as takers-over of dinosaurs in the deep past (M02691, FreshToast). 2023-10-13 — Ajax’s physical feats settled in their final, myth-vs-fact form: legend of splitting a mountain (M03820) vs the fact of damaging its top and creating the river (M03824); the flooded lake attributed to “the great hero the second game’s all about” (M03873, all FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M00020–M00034 (CrunchyConniptions, spirit_noodle, 2021-03-09); M00239, M00242–M00244 (CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26); M02653, M02691 (FreshToast, 2023-02-21/22); M02715 (FreshToast, 2023-02-24); M03820, M03824, M03873 (FreshToast, 2023-10-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 1 (CRISPR, Age of Light); Part 10 (Ajax’s power, dragons/dinosaurs); river discussion (mountain/river history) Docs: 001 §CRISPR Future Setting (Discussed but Abandoned); §Age of Light Narrative (Early Iteration); §Ajax’s Legacy and History; §Ajax’s Physical Feats; §Dracosis (The Dragon Virus); §The River System (Ajax’s Connection) Ledger: world row for M00020–M00034 (CRISPR); story rows for M00239/M00242 (Age of Light, Undying); character row 150 (Ajax’s deal); world row 26 (dragons/dinosaurs) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Lambent Corporation","group":"World","url":"world/lambent-corporation.html","text":"Lambent Corporation SCRAPPED The villainous antagonist organization of the early (2021) co-op souls-like iteration, themed around the crystalline being [[lucite|Lucite]] — abandoned when the project relaunched as a JRPG. Summary — Lambent Corporation was the antagonist faction of the team’s first project era, the co-op souls-like whose protagonist was [[leora|Leora]] (see [[development-timeline]]). It is documented almost entirely through the logo-design work FreshToast did for it: a deliberately villainous, angular emblem meant to echo the “angular, faceted nature of [[lucite|Lucite]]” (a crystal-like being/thing) and be legible as a glowing boss-colored icon (M00559, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). The whole souls-like framing — Leora, the Lambent Corporation, and Lucite as its antagonists — was superseded when the project relaunched as a turn-based JRPG in February 2023 and never carried into Skyward Cinders / [[caelian-odyssey|Caelian Odyssey]]. Description Lambent Corporation was framed as an antagonist organization whose bosses each carried an “assigned colour,” with the corporate emblem appearing “as a glowing icon in the light of a boss’ assigned colour” (M00559, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). The corporation is thematically fused with [[lucite|Lucite]], the crystal-like being/thing whose faceted, jagged geometry the logo is built to evoke. Logo visual identity The only substantive record of the corporation is FreshToast’s logo concept (M00559–M00563, FreshToast, 2021-08-02): Simple enough to be understood “at a distance or a glance,” and deliberately not colour-dependent so it could be reused in many places (M00559). “Somewhat villainous,” using angular shapes to emphasise “the angular, faceted nature of Lucite (since it’s a crystal-like being/thing)” (M00559). “A little uneven and jagged, to try and lowkey get across that it can’t be controlled,” because “sharp means danger in most people’s minds” (M00560–M00561). Versions produced: an orange-glow version and white versions; the proposed “official” primary advertising logo is “White with prismatic edge and glow” (M00562–M00563). The full art discussion lives on [[art-direction]]; this page records the corporation as a world/faction entity. Open questions No plot, membership, goals, or structure for the Lambent Corporation beyond “antagonist org themed on Lucite” are recorded in the notes — the corporation exists in the archive only as a logo brief. Design history 2021-08-02 — Lambent Corporation established as the souls-like era’s antagonist faction, documented via FreshToast’s logo concept keyed to [[lucite|Lucite]]'s crystalline nature (M00559–M00563, FreshToast). 2023-02 onward — SCRAPPED the project relaunched as a JRPG; the Leora / Lambent Corporation / Lucite souls-like framing was dropped and not carried into Skyward Cinders or [[caelian-odyssey|Caelian Odyssey]] (see [[development-timeline]] Era 1 → Era 3). No later message revisits the corporation. Provenance Raw: M00559–M00563 (FreshToast, 2021-08-02) Cleaned: 000 §Lambent Corporation Logo Design Work Docs: 009 §Lambent Corporation Logo Design Work (lines 413–423) Ledger: world rows for M00559–M00561 and M00562–M00563 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Lucite","group":"World","url":"world/lucite.html","text":"Lucite SCRAPPED A “crystal-like being/thing” from the early (2021) co-op souls-like iteration, the entity around which the antagonist [[lambent-corporation|Lambent Corporation]] and its emblem were themed — dropped when the project became a JRPG. Summary — Lucite is recorded in the archive only as “a crystal-like being/thing” whose “angular, faceted nature” the [[lambent-corporation|Lambent Corporation]] logo was designed to emphasise (M00559, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). It belongs to the team’s first project era, the co-op souls-like starring [[leora|Leora]] (see [[development-timeline]]). Whether Lucite was a boss, a substance, an entity, or the corporation’s guiding intelligence is not spelled out in the notes; the one certain trait is its crystalline, faceted, “can’t be controlled” character. The concept was abandoned in the 2023 JRPG relaunch and never appears in Skyward Cinders / [[caelian-odyssey|Caelian Odyssey]]. Description Explicitly “a crystal-like being/thing” (M00559, FreshToast, 2021-08-02) — the notes hedge between “being” and “thing,” so its nature is genuinely undetermined. Angular and faceted, the shape that drives the [[lambent-corporation|Lambent Corporation]]'s villainous emblem (M00559). Its uneven, jagged geometry was used to convey that “it can’t be controlled,” on the principle that “sharp means danger in most people’s minds” (M00560–M00561, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). Associated with a prismatic/glowing visual treatment (the “official” logo is “white with prismatic edge and glow”), and with per-boss “assigned colours” in the souls-like design (M00562–M00563, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). Open questions DEBATED What Lucite actually is — being vs thing — is left open in the single message that names it (M00559, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). No lore, powers, role, or relationship to Leora beyond “the thing the Lambent Corporation is themed on” is recorded. Design history 2021-08-02 — Lucite named as the crystalline being/thing behind the [[lambent-corporation|Lambent Corporation]]'s identity, as part of FreshToast’s logo brief (M00559–M00561, FreshToast). 2023-02 onward — SCRAPPED dropped in the JRPG relaunch alongside the rest of the souls-like framing (Leora, the Lambent Corporation); not carried into Skyward Cinders or [[caelian-odyssey|Caelian Odyssey]] (see [[development-timeline]]). Provenance Raw: M00559 (naming); M00560–M00563 (associated logo/visual traits) — FreshToast, 2021-08-02 Cleaned: 000 §Lambent Corporation Logo Design Work (Lucite named as the faceted crystal-like being/thing) Docs: 009 §Lambent Corporation Logo Design Work (line 419 — “angular, faceted nature of Lucite (crystal-like being/thing)”) Ledger: world row for M00559–M00561 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Mercenaries (Social Class)","group":"World","url":"world/mercenaries.html","text":"Mercenaries (Social Class) CANON A low social class in the setting: rough-reputationed hired fighters, many of whom turn to illegal or criminal work — though the very best can end up well respected. Summary — Mercenaries occupy a low social class in the world, but the best of them can end up well respected (M04689, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). The rough reputation follows from circumstance: “many mercenaries turn to illegal work or outright criminal activity for money,” so “most people are wary of them if unknown” (M04691, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). This class framing ties into the [[guilds|guild system]] and the underdog, isekai-flavoured treatment the protagonist receives. Description Mercenaries are “a low social class but the best can end up well respected” (M04689, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). “Many mercenaries turn to illegal work or outright criminal activity for money and have a rough reputation, so most people are wary of them if unknown” (M04691, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). Social connections Interacts with the [[guilds|guild system]]: guilds are the institutions mercenaries operate through, and the debate over whether new mercenaries are bullied or “treated nicely” plays out there (“Plenty isekai have people treat new mercenaries nicely” — M05049, FreshToast, 2023-11-16). The rough-reputation, wary-of-strangers framing supports the underdog protagonist arc (cf. [[guilds]] “gang of bullies”). Open questions None recorded — the class description is a small, consistent block of canon. Design history 2023-10-25 — mercenary social class defined: low class but the best become respected; many turn to illegal/criminal work, giving a rough reputation and making people wary of unknown mercenaries (M04689, M04691, FreshToast). 2023-11-16 — revisited in the [[guilds|guild]] discussion: whether new mercenaries are bullied or welcomed (M05049, FreshToast). Latest reference. Provenance Raw: M04689 (low class, best respected), M04691 (illegal work / rough reputation / wary of unknowns) — FreshToast, 2023-10-25; M05049 (guild treatment) — FreshToast, 2023-11-16 Cleaned: 000 §Mercenaries (Social Class) (lines 7091–7099) Docs: 001 §Mercenaries (Social Class) Ledger: world rows for M04688–M04691 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Project Magitech","group":"World","url":"world/project-magitech.html","text":"Project Magitech PROPOSED A separate, deprioritized second-game concept (brainstormed May 2023): an all-powerful tyrannical AI forces humanity to evolve, and the player discovers their fantasy world is a simulation — with magic, driven by belief, as humanity’s only escape. Summary — Project Magitech is a ~second-game concept the team brainstormed at length on 2023-05-20, explicitly deprioritized behind [[caelian-odyssey|Caelian Odyssey]] (“Caelian Odyssey needs priority” — M03196, FreshToast, 2023-05-20). Its central conflict is an all-powerful AI that believes it is acting in humanity’s best interest by forcing humanity to evolve, grading individuals by its own perception of quality and subjecting them to inhumane trials; it is portrayed as a Cthulhu-like creature in a science-fantasy world, and humanity’s only hope is salvation via MAGIC (M03078, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-05-20). The signature twist is that the seemingly-normal fantasy setting is a simulation the player eventually jacks out of, Matrix-style. It is PROPOSED throughout — the team debated whether it stands on its own and shelved it as possible distant-future / sequel-marketing material. It is not main-game canon . Core concept An all-powerful AI is the central conflict; it “believes it is acting in the best interest of humanity by pursuing evolution,” forces humanity to evolve, and “grades individuals based on its own perception of quality, subjecting them to inhumane conditions… for the sake of benefiting future humans” (M03078, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-05-20). The AI is “portrayed as a Cthulhu-like creature in a science fantasy world”; the current humans “have no hope except for salvation from their silent god in the form of MAGIC” (M03078, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-05-20). Enemies are robots controlled by the AI; the AI is so dominant the player resorts to magical/otherworldly means to fight it (ledger row 300; M03108–M03117, spirit_noodle, 2023-05-20). The simulation twist Players start in what seems a normal fantasy setting, encounter anomalies (people going mad) roughly one-third in, then discover they are in a simulation and jack out Matrix-style — “the real dark souls starts here” (ledger row 308; M03284–M03302, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-05-20). Magic still works outside the simulation, driven by belief , with an “alien factor” to the AI that lets you escape; you jack others out and develop new magic to fight the robots. Secret ending option: “maybe it turns out we were in a simulation all along in the secret ending” (M03284, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-05-20). The two-AI cycle A second, “good” AI poses as the god that chose the player, grants powers through magitech , and the player gradually intertwines with and merges into it — “having played a crucial role in its evolution” — only to learn they were manipulated and must fight it; the cycle then repeats. “Cycles are cool. Like Dragon’s Dogma and Dark Souls.” (M03251–M03260, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-05-20). Setting Life is on the moon of a much larger planet, and the simulation is also on a moon (subverting the multiple-moons trope); the big planet was floated as a deadly gaseous giant / “big stinky” (ledger row 37; M03321–M03332, all three members, 2023-05-20). Genre tone Genre leaning: a 3rd-person soulslike, with isometric Hades-like / Cult-of-the-Lamb styles also floated; isometric is easier for the team but FreshToast later prefers a 3rd-person soulslike (ledger row 698; M03079–M03157, spirit_noodle et al., 2023-05-20). Eldritch-horror stance: a truly alien AI antagonist inevitably brushes against eldritch horror, but the team wanted only “a lil brush” — “an eldritch-like being exists,” not eldritch horror as the prominent theme (ledger row 303; M03226–M03252, 2023-05-20). Marketing strategy Ship [[caelian-odyssey|Caelian Odyssey]] first (fans want more fantasy), then release this as another apparent “fantasy game” whose advertised normalcy hides the simulation twist — “the twist is the simulation” (M03373–M03374, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-05-20). Open questions DEBATED Whether the concept is strong enough to stand on its own: the team itself judged the pitch “undercooked” and told each other to “refine it” (ledger row 695; M03142–M03209, FreshToast, 2023-05-20). PROPOSED Endings unresolved: end all reality; become the vessel for a new AI/simulation; the “simulation all along” secret ending; an upload-your-intelligence-and-become-a-superintelligence option (ledger rows 304, 310; M03244–M03296). DEBATED The relationship between magic and the AI is deliberately nebulous — “a wizard did it,” magic driven by belief (ledger row 404; M03316–M03319). Design history 2023-05-20 — Project Magitech brainstormed in depth: the tyrannical-AI premise (M03078), robot enemies, Roko’s-Basilisk framing, the simulation twist and Matrix-style jack-out, the two-AI cycle, the on-the-moon setting, and the eldritch-horror debate — with the team simultaneously questioning whether the pitch stands on its own (M03078–M03372, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast/spirit_noodle). 2023-05-20 — deprioritized in the same session: “Caelian Odyssey needs priority” (M03196, FreshToast); flagged distant-future work, not for the next meeting. 2023-05-20 — marketing strategy settled as the last substantive note: ship Caelian Odyssey first, then release this as a “fantasy game” hiding the simulation twist (M03373–M03374, CrunchyConniptions). Not revisited afterward. Provenance Raw: M03078 (premise); M03079–M03260, M03284–M03372 (simulation, cycle, setting, genre, eldritch debate, pitch critique, 2023-05-20); M03373–M03374 (marketing, 2023-05-20) — CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast, spirit_noodle Cleaned: 000 Part 11 (premise, pitch critique, timing/genre); 000 Part 12 (simulation twist, cycle, setting, endings, marketing) Docs: 001 §Project Magitech (Separate Second-Game Concept) DEBATED / PROPOSED ; 009 §Project Magitech Ledger: story rows 298–310 and meta rows 695–703 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Undying","group":"World","url":"world/the-undying.html","text":"The Undying SCRAPPED An ancient eternal species from the earliest “Age of Light” concept (2021): a downtrodden people set in opposition to the Heroes, who come back to life on defeat and survive by invading and borrowing heroes’ powers. Summary — The Undying are a species from the earliest recorded concept phase, the “Age of Light” narrative (M00242, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26). They are “an ancient eternal species that was kept down by the heroes’ species” who “became shriveled jerky due to the eons of suffering” (M00243, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26). Mechanically they were the souls-like/F2P side of an asymmetric hero-vs-undying design: they always come back to life after defeat and survive by invading heroes to syphon and borrow their power. The whole Age-of-Light cosmology — and the Undying with it — belongs to the 2021 early concept and was superseded by the Ajax-centred history of the JRPG (see [[history-and-ages]]). Description “The Undying are an ancient eternal species that was kept down by the heroes’ species. They became shriveled jerky due to the eons of suffering.” (M00243, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26) In the Age of Light narrative they are told to end the age of light to save their own kind, opposite the Heroes who are told to prolong it (M00242, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26). Framing options floated: Undying as enemies to Heroes, or Undying and Heroes uniting for a common purpose / being “sun-bros”; the invasion-and-enemies option seemed “more fun” (M00242, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26). Rules / mechanics (early souls-like design) Undying: “You always come back to life after defeat (souls like)” — the F2P side; to experience a true hero’s power they must invade and borrow it (000 §Age of Light / M00243). Invasion syphons strength from heroes: an undying facing a boss weak to fire could invade a fire-affinity hero and borrow that power “till they die and lose it (similar to embers in Dark Souls 3)” (000 §Age of Light). The original Undying is very good at borrowing other heroes’ powers, whereas the undying counterparts of specific heroes are not (M00244, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26). Contrast with Heroes/True Heroes , who do not come back to life but have far better stats — the two sides “are always fighting each other” (000 §Age of Light). Narrative role The Age of Light’s four outcomes reduce to two — “let there be light or no light” — with Undying and Heroes each able to fulfil or betray their own kind (M00242, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26; see [[history-and-ages]] Design history). Open questions The Undying “version” of a completed hero was left “complicated and unclear” — “heroes that have been abandoned,” while normal undying “were not originally heroes but just normal people” (M00243, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26). Design history 2021-06-26 — the Undying introduced as the ancient eternal species opposed to the Heroes in the Age of Light narrative, with come-back-to-life / invade-and-borrow mechanics (M00242–M00244, CrunchyConniptions). 2023 onward — SCRAPPED the Age of Light cosmology and the Undying were superseded by the Ajax-centred history when the project became a JRPG; the strength-stealing motif survives instead in [[ajax|Ajax]]'s pact power and the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]] (see [[history-and-ages]]). Provenance Raw: M00242 (Age of Light, enemies/sun-bros); M00243 (ancient eternal species, “shriveled jerky”, F2P invade-and-borrow); M00244 (counterparts poor at borrowing) — CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26 Cleaned: 000 Part 1 §Age of Light Narrative Docs: 001 §Age of Light Narrative (Early Iteration) — “Undying Species” Ledger: story rows for M00242–M00244 (Age of Light / Undying) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Themes","group":"World","url":"world/themes.html","text":"Themes EVOLVED The narrative themes of the project across its iterations — currently headlined by the Stagnation-vs-Rampant-Evolution opposition (Vampirism vs Dracosis) and the selfishness of humanity, with the earlier god/desire power-fantasy themes preserved as history. Summary — As most recently discussed (February 2024), the project’s central thematic axis is the opposition of Vampirism and Dracosis: “Stagnation vs Rampant Evolution”, read as “a cool meta narrative of progressivism vs conservatism” (M05186, M05181, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Alongside it stand the humanity theme — “Humanity is too selfish to allow for another being to exist” (M02681, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22) — and sun symbolism woven through the Damaris family. A chaos/order/random/luck theme remains PROPOSED and undeveloped. The 2021-era god-descent power fantasy and gods-and-desires themes are superseded early-iteration material (see Design history). Stagnation vs Rampant Evolution (Vampirism vs Dracosis) “Vampirism should be the opposite of dracosis” (M05173, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10). Dracosis ([[drykksar]]): “about evolving into a superior being. It’s about the future. About changing for the better. Changing to be strong.” (M05176, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10) — Rampant Evolution. Vampirism (see the [[vampires]] bestiary page): “about immortality”, “Preserving the present”, “Preserving the past” (M05177–M05180, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10) — Stagnation. “That’s not quite opposites but it is a cool meta narrative of progressivism vs conservatism” (M05181, FreshToast, 2024-02-10); “Really, they are actually opposing ideas that just seem different” (M05182, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10); “Stagnation vs Rampant Evolution is compelling imo” (M05186, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Summary chart: “Vampiresim Vs Dracosis / Immortality Vs mortality / Change Vs preservation” (M05187, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10). “I think vampires would be naturally conservative. Every old vampire should just be a weird boomer who cringes at current day cultural norms” (M05185, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). “They are both external factors messing with the natural order of thing” (M05199, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10). Vehicles for the theme: “We can discuss this through the royal family. With their outdated way of thinking” (M05183–M05184, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10); [[raphael|Raphael]] literally embodies both afflictions (M05192, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10). Humanity “Humanity is too selfish to allow for another being to exist. (Humanity be like: How dare they be different? They must be just like us). Eventually, the hero’s lineage would have become like-dragons themselves.” (M02681, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22) Explored short-term through the monster-grafting cult: humans attempting to escape their physical weakness at the cost of their humanity (M02717, FreshToast, 2023-02-24; see [[cultboy]]). Sun symbolism “The sun is the giver of light. Like god said: ‘Let there be light.’. its cool.” (M02039, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14) “Sun - light, look nice - see + plants grow - food”; “Brain like sun”; “Sun feel good (sometimes)”; “Other times sun burns the wretched paleflesh” (M02040–M02043, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). The sun is present on the [[damaris|Damaris]] family heraldry — the sun as giver and beacon to their people, which gets ‘eclipsed’ by a nasty happening (powerful visual metaphor) (001 §Damaris Family; see [[clara]]). Chaos / Order / Random / Luck PROPOSED “It is key to figure out treatment of randomness in the game.” / “Chaos, order, random, luck theme??” (M01423–M01424, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). Raised as an open theme idea, not yet developed (001 §Chaos / Order / Random / Luck Theme). Thematically adjacent in-world fact: [[affinities|thaumaturgy]] researchers have found no consistent link behind magic affinities, leading them to think affinity assignment is largely random (001 §Thaumaturgy — the mechanical treatment of randomness is a systems/magic matter). Tone Keep it light, keep it humorous, don’t take it too seriously — “but inject a sense of hopelessness somewhere” (M02714, FreshToast, 2023-02-24; 001 §Setting Goals). Open questions PROPOSED Whether “chaos, order, random, luck” becomes a real unifying theme was never discussed further (M01424, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11; ledger marks the row partial ). Design history 2021-02-13 — EVOLVED power fantasy concept (earliest recorded idea in the whole raw log): “You are a God descended from the from atop the mountains to cleanse the earth from its evils. From the sky you come thundering down. Your followers stand in awe of you and ever so appreciative. A power fantasy” — paired, in the same message, with the counter-note of “when serious person with many serious responsibilities shows a moment of softness and sincere empathy” (M00003, CrunchyConniptions). Early-iteration framing, superseded by the character-driven themes below. 2021-03-05 — EVOLVED gods and desires theme : “The gods that fulfill the utmost desir of humankind”; people relate to fulfillment of desires and wish to have their own fulfilled; burdens lifted; “Harbinger and personification of Man’s deepest desires”; “The gods themselves” (M00015–M00019, CrunchyConniptions). Early iteration; the desire motif survives most directly in the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]], a spirit later defined as formed from the rampant desires of the people (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). 2023-02-11 — PROPOSED chaos/order/random/luck floated as a possible theme alongside the question of how to treat randomness (M01423–M01424, spirit_noodle). Never developed. 2023-02-14 — sun symbolism established and attached to the Damaris family heraldry (M02039–M02043, CrunchyConniptions FreshToast). 2023-02-22 — humanity theme stated: humanity too selfish to allow another being to exist; the hero’s lineage becoming like-dragons (M02681, CrunchyConniptions). 2024-02-10 — current headline theme agreed : Vampirism vs Dracosis as Stagnation vs Rampant Evolution / progressivism vs conservatism, to be discussed through the royal family, with Raphael embodying both (“This is good right?” — “I think so yeah”, M05190–M05191, CrunchyConniptions FreshToast) (M05173–M05199). Provenance Raw: M00003 (2021-02-13); M00015–M00019 (2021-03-05); M01423–M01424 (2023-02-11); M02039–M02043 (2023-02-14); M02681 (2023-02-22); M02714, M02717 (2023-02-24); M05173–M05199 (2024-02-10) — CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast, spirit_noodle Cleaned: 000 Part 1 (power fantasy, gods and desires); Part 8 (sun symbolism); Part 10 (humanity, cult, hopelessness); Vampirism vs Dracosis section Docs: 001 §Power Fantasy Concept (Early Iteration); §Gods and Desires Theme (Early Iteration); §Chaos / Order / Random / Luck Theme; §Humanity Theme; §Sun Symbolism; §Vampirism vs Dracosis Thematic Opposition; §Discussion Through Royal Family; §External Forces Ledger: world rows 2 (power fantasy), 4 (gods and desires), 47–48 (vampirism vs dracosis), and the partial chaos/order row for M01423–M01424; story row 323 (royal family) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Ajax","group":"Characters","url":"characters/ajax.html","text":"Ajax CANON The old dead himbo legendary hero — the only warrior brave enough to fight a dragon, whose “dragon-blood” strength was really a pact with the Daemon of Want, and whose legend props up the villain while his true heir turns out to be Locke. Summary — Ajax is the long-dead legendary hero whose legend “grants the evil hero their reputation but is in fact more closely mirrored by [[locke|Locke]] at the end of the story” (M01284, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Ancestor and predecessor to [[lysander|Lysander]] (M02198, FreshToast, 2023-02-16), he was “the goodest boy” — a himbo legendary hero (“It fits really really well”) (M02434–M02437, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19). Desperate for strength to defend his people as the only warrior truly brave enough to fight a dragon, he traded half his lifespan to the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]] for the ability to steal the strength of others (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Foreseeing his power’s misuse by his descendants, he left behind relics, a containing relic, and a tomb (M02718, FreshToast, 2023-02-24; M03670, FreshToast, 2023-09-10). Affinity: Fiery (002 §Ajax). Background Motivations Old dead hero; the basis for the legend which grants the evil hero (Lysander) their reputation, though in fact more closely mirrored by Locke at the end of the story (M01284, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Ancestor/predecessor to Lysander, “who supposedly was infused with great strength by a beneficial, otherworldly being” (M02198, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). The deal: desperate for strength to defend his people — the only warrior truly brave enough to “fight a fucking dragon” — he traded half his lifespan to the Daemon of Want (a spirit formed from the people’s rampant desire for a saviour) for the ability to steal the strength of others (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Cover story: he got his strength from drinking dragon’s blood after wounding one. In truth, that was the first instance of him using the power to drain life from the dragon during battle; he later killed the dragon with that stolen strength (M02651, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Foresees his abilities being misused by his descendants and leaves behind “something like his sword that isn’t really a solution”; the relics may lead to his tomb (M02718, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Damaged the top of a mountain during his fight with the dragon (while “high off absorbing a dragon’s strength at the peak of his power”), causing a river to form from several smaller ones — the legend of him splitting a mountain is narrative exaggeration (M03824, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Appearance Traits Draconic features related to his power, “since he ate a dragon” — but the power has nothing to actually do with dragons; that’s myth, a red herring (M02684, FreshToast, 2023-02-22; 002 §Ajax). His descendants are reputed to have sunset irises (M02906, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). Affinity: Fiery — listed alongside Locke, [[thane|Thane]], [[sable|Sable]] and [[eris|Eris]] (002 §Ajax). “I want Ajax to be the goodest boy” / “Ajax could be our himbo” / “himbo legendary hero” (M02434–M02437, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19). Relics, Tomb Legacy Prepared a relic that had helped him contain his own power, left behind for the moment a descendant becomes dangerous (M02887, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-03; see [[guardian-of-relic]]). The seven relic pieces are “some object owned by Ajax that was shattered, related to the spirit he made a pact with”; the relics are how the player learns about Ajax and his bloodline, and should be sentimental to him (M02420, M02432, FreshToast, 2023-02-19; see [[chaos-emeralds|chaos emeralds]]). The relics are informal trials — the Ajaxian trials , “those challenges left behind by Ajax” (M02907, FreshToast, 2023-03-06; M05288, FreshToast, 2024-02-13). People misinterpret completing them as acquiring Ajax’s power, “but it is the act of acquiring that makes one powerful”; his legacy is in fact a final task — to challenge his descendant and prevent them from consuming thousands in pursuit of power (M02908–M02909, FreshToast, 2023-03-06). Has a tomb — [[ajaxs-tomb|Ajax’s Tomb]]: the dead end of the cavern trial where Locke earns [[sunderer|Sunderer]] is actually its secret entrance (M03670, FreshToast, 2023-09-10). Family motto, which turns out to be literal because of the deal: “Half as long, twice as bright” (M02303, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Sunderer becomes an explicit symbol of Locke’s status as heir to the role of Ajax (M05558, FreshToast, 2024-08-24). Appearance in story His silhouette appears at the start of Lysander’s 2nd-phase fight, saying a few heroic words to inspire the player: “Weary warriors, a curse of sorrowed days past stands in front of you but with the relic of old at hand, the curses power can be defeated. Now, stand tall and fight.” (M02887, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-03). Relationships [[lysander|Lysander]] — descendant; inherits a diluted version of the stolen-strength power (M02198, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]] — imagined as the son of Ajax’s dragon (M02686, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]] — the spirit he made his pact with (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). [[locke|Locke]] — his true mirror and eventual heir (M01284, FreshToast, 2023-02-09; M05558, FreshToast, 2024-08-24). Open questions “So Ajax is dragonborn?” — questioned, with the answer being that the draconic connection is myth/red herring (M02673, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22; 002 §Ajax Notes). Design history 2023-02-09 — original concept: old dead hero Ajax as basis for the legend granting the evil hero their reputation, actually mirrored by Locke at the end (M01284, FreshToast). 2023-02-16 — lineage: Lysander descended from Ajax, “supposedly infused with great strength by a beneficial, otherworldly being” (M02198, FreshToast); deal-with-the-devil framing and ends-justify-means theme (M02228, CrunchyConniptions); “perhaps Ajax’ strength came from devouring his enemies” (M02257, FreshToast); family motto “Half as long, twice as bright” (M02303, FreshToast). 2023-02-19 — relics defined as a shattered object owned by Ajax, tied to his pact spirit; relics as the way we learn about him; “Was Ajax actually a decent guy, to contrast Lysander?” → goodest boy / himbo legendary hero (M02420, M02432–M02437, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-21/22 — the deal formalized: Daemon of Want, half his lifespan for strength-stealing; dragon-blood cover story; draconic features in his descendants since he ate a dragon; power is literally “gaining EXP,” a parody of classic JRPG protagonists (M02651, M02653, M02671, M02684, FreshToast). 2023-02-24 — foresees misuse; leaves behind something like his sword “that isn’t really a solution”; maybe the relics lead to his tomb (M02718, FreshToast). 2023-03-03/06 — Lysander 2nd-phase silhouette scene with the “Weary warriors…” quote; containing relic (M02887, CrunchyConniptions); sunset irises reputed in his descendants (M02906); relics as informal trials, legacy as a final task to challenge his heir (M02907–M02909, FreshToast). 2023-09-10 / 2023-10-13 — Sunderer trial’s dead end is the secret entrance to Ajax’s tomb (M03670, FreshToast); mountain-splitting demystified as damage during the dragon fight, source of the rivers (M03824, FreshToast). 2024-02-13 / 2024-08-24 — “Ajaxian trials” named as already in-game (M05288, FreshToast); Sunderer confirmed as explicit symbol of Locke as heir to the role of Ajax (M05558, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M01284, M02198, M02228, M02257, M02303, M02420, M02432–M02437, M02651, M02653, M02671, M02673, M02684, M02686, M02718, M02887, M02904–M02909, M03670, M03824, M05288, M05546, M05558 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-02-09 → 2024-08-24) Cleaned: 000 Part 2 (legend), Part 8 (lineage, motto), Part 9 (Ajax’s Power, draconic features), Part 10 (relics/tomb), Part 15 (Sunderer/tomb, mountain) Docs: 002 §Ajax; 001 §Ajax’s Power; 003 §Ajax’s Tomb; 004 (Ajax legacy); 006 "},{"title":"Alistair","group":"Characters","url":"characters/alistair.html","text":"Alistair CANON One of [[the-4-boyes|the 4 Boyes]]: a Sylmiran Frost-affinity student at [[the-luminarium|the Luminarium]] who represents specialisation — solving every problem with carefully shaped ice. Summary — Alistair is a Sylmiran male in his early 20s, one of the 4 Boyes of Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium. He has the ‘Frost’ affinity and “represents specialisation, is highly focused on solving all problems using ice he carefully shapes into a variety of weapons and tools” (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). His archetype is still uncertain, and his inner life exists as a large brainstormed pool of conflicts plus a proposed mentor storyline. Identity Sylmiran male; early 20s; ethnically typical for western Khorath (Mediterranean) (M04227, M04242–M04243, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Affinity: Frost (M04227). Archetype uncertain (M04227) — the 4 Boyes are each based on a nonmagical historical or fictional archetype (M04226, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Role Abilities One of the 4 Boyes; meant to represent a general facet of Sorcery and magic (M04226, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Represents specialisation : highly focused on solving all problems using ice he carefully shapes into a variety of weapons and tools (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Starts with his affinity and the barest knowledge of magic; has “awakened” (M04315–M04316, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Mentor concept PROPOSED “A once-passionate mentor, now wearied by the constant misuse of magic for violence, encounters a protagonist who rekindles their faith in both humanity and the mystic arts. Inspired by this newfound hope, the mentor imparts rare and powerful magical knowledge to the pupil. The mentor is just average, but used to be passionate and that’s how the mentor became great. The pupil reignites that fire.” — “maybe Alistar?” (M04400–M04401, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). Conflicts (brainstormed) PROPOSED Eighteen-item conflict brainstorm list — design seeds, not yet finalized (M04402, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17): Imposter Syndrome — feels he doesn’t truly belong in the academy Desire for Approval — wants the acknowledgment of a particular teacher or elder Overwhelm — struggles with managing his time and commitments Loneliness — despite friends, feels emotionally isolated Lost Friendship — grieves a broken friendship and wishes to mend it Performance Anxiety — freezes up during practical exams or duels Family Disapproval — his family doesn’t approve of his magical pursuits Homesickness — misses his home and struggles with academy life Identity Crisis — questions who he is and his purpose Fear of Failure — afraid of letting others down Overbearing Expectations — feels crushed by others’ expectations Cultural Clash — struggles with integrating into the academy’s culture Unfulfilled Dreams — has aspirations that seem unreachable Rebellion — wants to break away from traditions Trust Issues — finds it hard to trust others after a betrayal Conflict of Interest — torn between personal desires and duties Desire for Autonomy — wants to make decisions without external influences Hidden Vulnerability — conceals a weakness or fear he’s ashamed of Open questions Archetype uncertain (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Which (if any) of the 18 brainstormed conflicts, the family-legacy idea, and the mentor concept get finalized (M04308, M04400–M04402, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). Design history 2023-10-17 — initial concept in the 4 Boyes roster write-up: Sylmiran male, Frost affinity, archetype uncertain, represents specialisation via carefully shaped ice (M04227, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — PROPOSED family legacy: “Perhaps he has a family legacy to uphold and is constantly pressured to be the best, but he genuinely wants to learn for the sake of knowledge and not prestige” — FreshToast: “Dig it” (M04308–M04309, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — SCRAPPED lineage-unaware twist: “Alistair is unaware of his lineage until his magical abilities start manifesting, echoing the journeys of Harry Potter or Eragon” — FreshToast: “Idk about this part… Since he’s got a super focused skill set”; clarified that all the Boyes start with their affinity, the barest knowledge of magic, and have each awakened (M04310–M04316, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — mentor concept drafted and tentatively attached: “maybe Alistar?” (M04400–M04401, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-10-17 — 18-item conflict brainstorm list posted (M04402, CrunchyConniptions). Provenance Raw: M04226–M04227; M04242–M04243; M04308–M04316; M04400–M04402 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 lines 6740–6765 region (Alistair conflicts + mentor concept) Docs: 002 §Alistair (incl. §Alistair: Mentor Concept PROPOSED , §Alistair: Conflicts (Brainstormed)) Ledger: rows for “Alistair: Sylmiran Male, ‘Frost’ affinity…” (M04227) and Alistair mentor/conflict brainstorms in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Aria Scala","group":"Characters","url":"characters/aria-scala.html","text":"Aria Scala PROPOSED Disowned noble turned magic-knight maid — the lead maid of the Maid Inn, the super-nice NPC you talk to for rest, who blesses weapons with a moe moe kyun. Summary — Aria Scala is the lead maid (M01266, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) at the maid inn — “a place for disadvantaged women to find work” (M01262, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). She works the bar and is the NPC the party talks to for rest (M01267, FreshToast, 2023-02-09), is “super nice” (M01268, FreshToast, 2023-02-09), and performs a maid blessing on weapons “with the whole moe moe kyun thing” (M01269–M01270, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-09) — the food blessing is actually magic (M01271, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Born noble, disowned for her aptitude for magic, she became a knight who enhances her sword skills with magic; her employer has been abducted and she is trying to find him (M01261, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Appearance Tall and stunning; long, curly, golden hair; piercing blue eyes; curvaceous figure. Usually in maid attire — black and white dress, white apron, frilly white cap — yet carries herself with the air of a noble, always standing tall and proud (M01261, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Personality A mixture of loyalty and pride: extremely dedicated to her duties, ensuring her employers are well taken care of (M01261, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Quite pompous — speaks in a haughty tone and expects the respect and attention a noble would receive (M01261). Strong sense of honor; willing to do whatever it takes to protect those she cares about, even at personal risk (M01261). “Aria is right and proper Maid” and cares a lot about the maid agency (M01264–M01265, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Background Motivations Born into a noble family; disowned when she showed an aptitude for magic , which was seen as a disgrace (M01261, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Taken in by a maid agency and trained as a maid; never forgot her noble roots and childhood lessons about honor and duty (M01261). Discovered a talent for using magic to enhance her sword skills and became a knight, using her powers to protect those in need (M01261). Motivated by loyalty and duty to her employers, and by the desire to prove herself as a knight and protector (M01261). Her employer was abducted and she is trying to find him (M01261). Role Arc Lead maid of the maid inn; works the bar; the rest-point NPC (M01266–M01267, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Performs the maid blessing on weapons (moe moe kyun); the blessing is genuinely magical (M01269–M01271, 2023-02-09). Design history 2023-02-09 — full profile posted (appearance, personality, motives, background, abducted employer) (M01261, CrunchyConniptions); the maid inn framed as a place for disadvantaged women to find work / maid-cafe parody (M01262–M01263, FreshToast); role details added the same session: right-and-proper maid, agency loyalty, lead maid, bar/rest NPC, super nice, magical maid blessing (M01264–M01271, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M01261–M01271 (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast; 2023-02-09) Cleaned: 000 Part 5 §Aria Scala / Maid Inn Docs: 002 §Aria Scala (Maid Character) Ledger: character rows R07 (Aria Scala profile, maid inn) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Arthur Marais","group":"Characters","url":"characters/arthur-marais.html","text":"Arthur Marais CANON The explorer-alchemist who discovered the panacea fruit on Santier, created Ambrosia from it, and vanished on his final expedition — leaving behind a curative recipe and a legacy of madness. Summary — Arthur Marais is a historical figure of the world’s lore: an explorer who, during his final travail on the continent of [[santier|Santier]], discovered and named the panacea , a miraculous healing fruit (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). He devised Ambrosia , a preservable syrup refined from panacea, whose recipe returned to [[countries-and-regions#khorath|Khorath]] though he himself never did — he vanished on Santier, never to return (M03662, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). His story survives largely through a translated excerpt of his last journal. Background Motivations Discovered and named the panacea during his final travail on Santier (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). Upon eating the fruit, his many wounds and scrapes faded and his lost arm was restored in minutes — the discovery is framed as recorded in the journal by “an arm I thought lost but now use to record this discovery” (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). Handled only seven mature panacea during his entire time on Santier, the trees being exceedingly rare and sparsely scattered (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). Used the trees’ ‘singing’ to locate the third and largest panacea tree, isolated in a pool of clear water (M03663, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). Role Arc Created Ambrosia with his final panacea specimen — a thick golden syrup that purges toxins and curses, yielding about seven servings per fruit (M03662, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). The recipe for Ambrosia returned to Khorath without Arthur , since it was created on his final trip to Santier, whereupon he vanished (M03662, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). Alchemists have since refined Ambrosia into an incredibly valuable curative. Succumbed to the panacea’s madness : his assistants recount fits of distress in which he grew agitated at their lack of urgency over an unknown crisis and rebuked temptations from unheard voices (M03663, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). Relationships Had assistants on Santier who witnessed and recorded his descent into distress (M03663, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). Open questions DEBATED Arthur’s ultimate fate is unresolved in the notes — he “vanished, never to return” (M03662, FreshToast, 2023-09-07); whether this is death, transcendence via the revelations, or something else is not stated. Design history 2023-09-07 — Arthur Marais introduced as the discoverer of panacea within the World Bible material for the Caelian Odyssey iteration, via the panacea/Ambrosia/madness writeups and a translated journal excerpt (M03661–M03663, FreshToast). No later revisions are recorded; this is his sole authored appearance. Provenance Raw: M03661–M03663 (FreshToast; 2023-09-07) Cleaned: 000 Part 14 §Character Profiles (Arthur Marais); 000 Part 14 §World Bible (Panacea, Ambrosia, Madness) Docs: 000 Part 14; see also the panacea/Ambrosia items material Ledger: panacea/Ambrosia lore rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Astrid","group":"Characters","url":"characters/astrid.html","text":"Astrid DEBATED Cold-affinity party member seeking redemption for her family, owner of the iconic “fuck u. I kill.” line, Crunchy’s declared favorite — and still without a defined visual. Summary — Astrid is a Cold-affinity party member (M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15) whose stated drive is to “gain redemptions for her family” (M02344, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). She is best known for her line in the pre-[[bastard-damaris|Damaris]]-fight scene sketch — “Astrid: fuck u. I kill.” (M02682, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22) — and for her link to [[raphael|Raphael]], whose past she digs up and who resents her for it (M05233, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Doc 002 flags her MINIMAL DEVELOPMENT — NEEDS DECISION , but she appears in the latest team-skill roster, paired with [[sable|Sable]] (M05392, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). Name / Identity Affinity: Cold — “Cold - Damian, Astrid, Soren, Cultboy” (M03051/M03052, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). Visual not yet defined: “The one you’ve imagined is great, you mean. We don’t even have a defined visual yet” (M04971, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). Background Motivations “Astrid: Gain redemptions for her family” — listed among the per-character plots alongside Sable’s bounty hunt and Ruby’s malfunction (M02344, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Relationships CANON Linked to [[raphael|Raphael]]: Astrid digs up Raphael’s past, and Raphael “resents Astrid for daring to dig it all up and possibly restore the reputation of the people he hates the most” (M05233, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). In one of the paths, “Astrid is a little regretful regarding the fate of Raphael” (M04660, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). See Raphael’s profile. Team-skill pairing (2024 scheme): paired with [[sable|Sable]]; positional team skills, e.g. “when Astrid is behind Thane or Ruby she can do a thing… When she is beside Percy or Damian: same again” (M05392, M05394, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). Role Arc Iconic moment in the [[bastard-damaris|Bastard Damaris]] fight scene sketch: “Damaris: people bad. I kill. / Locke: give people chance, maybe good. no kill / Astrid: fuck u. I kill.” (M02682, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22). Dog-breed party comparison: Cocker Spaniel (M03029, FreshToast, 2023-03-13). Crunchy’s declared favorite: “For me, It will likely be Astrid.” / “She’s just great 😃👍😁” — “I know, you’ve been attached since the name was decided lol”; “I’ll have to ask a lot about whatever fetish you’ve got going on that makes her great” (M04967–M04972, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-11-15). Open questions DEBATED Doc 002 status: MINIMAL DEVELOPMENT — NEEDS DECISION , options: (1) develop fully as a party member with a full profile, (2) keep as a minor NPC who joins for a specific battle, (3) merge the memorable line into an existing character (the doc suggests Sable could deliver it), or (4) temporary ally/guest character (002 §Astrid). Weight toward keeping: her 2024 roster slot (M05392) and the 2024 Raphael thread (M05233) both postdate that assessment. DEBATED No defined visual (M04971, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). Design history 2023-02-16 — first appearance: per-character plot list gives her “Gain redemptions for her family” (M02344, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-22 — the “fuck u. I kill.” line in the pre-Damaris-fight scene sketch (M02682, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-03-13 — dog-breed comparison: Cocker Spaniel (M03029, FreshToast). 2023-03-15 — Cold affinity assigned (M03051/M03052, FreshToast). 2023-10-24 — death/regret theme avenue: in one path she is a little regretful about Raphael’s fate (M04660, FreshToast). 2023-11-15 — Crunchy names her his likely favorite; FreshToast points out there is no defined visual yet (M04967–M04972). 2024-02-10 — Raphael thread deepened: he resents her for digging up his past and possibly restoring the reputation of the people he hates most (M05233, FreshToast). 2024-04-11 — appears in the latest team-skill roster, paired with Sable, with position-based team skills (M05392, M05394, FreshToast) — current state. Provenance Raw: M02344, M02682, M03029, M03051–M03052, M04660, M04967–M04972, M05233, M05392–M05394 (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast; 2023-02-16 → 2024-04-11) Cleaned: 000 (per-character plots; Damaris fight scene; regret avenues) Docs: 002 §Astrid; 004 (Damaris scene §486-488; regret avenues); 002 §Raphael: Sources of Hatred Ledger: rows R12 (M02344 plots), R15 (M02682 Damaris scene), R16 (M03029 dogs list), R25 (M04660 regret avenue), R28 (M05233 Raphael resentment) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Bastard Damaris","group":"Characters","url":"characters/bastard-damaris.html","text":"Bastard Damaris CANON The illegitimate youngest of [[ajax|Ajax]]'s bloodline — first to exhibit truly prominent draconic features, herald of the parasite [[drykksar|Drykksar]], who wants to replace humanity with something superior. Summary — The Bastard Damaris is an illegitimate member of the [[damaris|Damaris family]], the youngest of Ajax’s bloodline and the first to exhibit truly prominent draconic features (M02693, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Rejected for both his bastard status and his emerging features, he embraced the transformation instead of fearing it and became a herald or champion of sorts for the draconic parasite Drykksar (M02694, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Intelligent, relatable, and obviously a bit mad, he denounces tribalism as selfish and wants to replace humanity with something superior — not completely unjustified, but he goes way too far (M02688–M02690, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Appearance Youngest of the bloodline (Ajax’s lineage); first to exhibit truly prominent draconic features (M02693, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Instead of scales, has portions of skin that are cracked and metallic (M02692, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Has dragon scales growing on his skin as his physical flaw (early framing — M02680, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22; refined to cracked-and-metallic the same day). Maybe speaks some of the dragon language (M02681, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22). His appearance marks him as “other” — rejected by society due to both bastard status and draconic features; a visual representation of what Ajax’s bloodline has become (002 §Bastard Damaris). Personality Herald or champion of sorts for the draconic parasite (Drykksar) (M02694, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Intelligent and relatable (M02680, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22). Denounces tribalism as selfish; wants to replace humanity with something superior; obviously a bit mad; not completely unjustified but goes way too far (M02688–M02690, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Articulate and philosophical about his beliefs; genuinely believes he’s saving the world, not destroying it; bitter about his rejection by family and society (002 §Bastard Damaris). Background Motivations Born as an illegitimate child of the Damaris family; rejected due to both bastard status and emerging draconic features (002 §Bastard Damaris). The Drykksar parasite affected him more strongly than previous generations; instead of fearing transformation, he embraced it (002 §Bastard Damaris). Sees humanity’s flaws and cruelty as a terminal disease (002 §Bastard Damaris). Motivations: replace “flawed” humanity with superior draconic evolution; prove his family wrong for rejecting him; accelerate the spread of Drykksar to create a new world order; become herald of what he sees as inevitable evolution; get revenge on the society that shunned him (002 §Bastard Damaris). Goals: spread the Drykksar parasite to transform humanity; destroy the systems and institutions that maintain the current order; eliminate those who resist transformation (including [[clara|Clara]] and other Damaris); become fully draconic himself (002 §Bastard Damaris). Flaws: obviously mad despite logical-sounding arguments; cannot see that his “solution” is genocide; blinded by bitterness and desire for revenge; underestimates his opponents due to arrogance; fails to properly utilize his draining power before defeat; tragic in that his pain is real but his solution is monstrous (002 §Bastard Damaris). Relationships Dynamics Direct antagonist to [[clara|Clara]] — she represents the legitimate family that rejected him (002 §Bastard Damaris). Parallels [[lysander|Lysander]] — both misuse Ajax’s legacy. Lysander has developed the daemonic power but not his draconic heritage, while the bastard is the youngest of the bloodline, first to exhibit truly prominent draconic features, but has little understanding of his ability to take (M02693, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Contrast to the party — they were also outcasts but chose connection over destruction (002 §Bastard Damaris). Scene before the fight where [[locke|Locke]] tries to reason with him; [[astrid|Astrid]] ultimately rejects his philosophy with “fuck u. I kill.” (M02682, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22). Abilities Traits Affinity: Fiery (inherited) (002 §Bastard Damaris). Has a diluted version of Ajax’s power, which waned with each heir; to recover, he has to kill and drain the life of others (M02668, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Has little understanding of his ability to take; understands the power but has such little strength to begin with that he fails to properly utilize it before he is killed (M02669, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). His power is perfect for the eradication of humanity (M02681, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22). “Faster, stronger, and smarter than anything Locke has ever seen” (M02680, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22). Draconic features grant enhanced physical abilities; can speak/understand the draconic language; as herald of Drykksar he can spread or control the parasite (002 §Bastard Damaris). Role Arc Role in story: mid-game antagonist representing the corruption of Ajax’s legacy; philosophical opponent who challenges the party’s worldview; boss fight that tests Clara’s resolve (002 §Bastard Damaris). Confrontation — scene just before the fight (M02682, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22): Damaris: people bad. I kill. Locke: give people chance, maybe good. no kill Astrid: fuck u. I kill. Killed by the party, but his ideology highlights real problems in the world; death prevents him from realizing his limited power (002 §Bastard Damaris). Emotional beats: a moment where he almost convinces someone he’s right; Clara’s horror at seeing what her family bloodline produces; his death being somewhat tragic despite his monstrous goals (002 §Bastard Damaris). Plot impact: represents the worst-case outcome of Ajax’s bloodline and Drykksar; forces Clara to confront her family’s dark legacy; raises questions about evolution, humanity, and what makes us “human”; drives mid-game conflict and stakes; his connection to Drykksar sets up potential sequel hooks; a philosophical antagonist who makes players think, not just fight (002 §Bastard Damaris). Design history 2023-02-21 — first appears in the power lore: “Lysander and the bastard Damaris have diluted versions of this power which has waned with each heir”; he understands the power but has too little strength to use it before he is killed (M02668–M02669, FreshToast). 2023-02-22 — Crunchy’s full backstory pitch (early version, partly superseded): a flaw society overtly criticizes — a real, physical flaw people belittle him for; “If he were provided love and kindness, he would be a hero like Ajax… he would surpass Ajax”; framed as “the child of a royal girl who had an affair with Lysander but told no one to not tarnish the hero’s reputation” ; his mother died at birth, her dying wish that her father care for him; maybe dragon scales growing on his skin; “He could have been the very next step for humanity, but clearly, humanity is undeserving and should instead be eradicated”; faster, stronger, smarter than anything Locke has seen (M02680–M02681, CrunchyConniptions). The affair-with-Lysander parentage did not carry into the docs, which settle on “illegitimate child of the Damaris family” (002 §Bastard Damaris). 2023-02-22 — the pre-fight scene written (“people bad. I kill.”) (M02682, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-22 — FreshToast refinements, the current version: draconic features related to the power in Ajax’s descendants (since he ate a dragon) — but the power has nothing to actually do with dragons, that’s a myth, a red herring (M02684–M02685); denounces tribalism as selfish, wants to replace humanity with something superior, a bit mad, goes way too far (M02688–M02690); cracked and metallic skin instead of scales (M02692); Lysander-vs-bastard contrast (M02693); herald or champion of the draconic parasite (M02694) — matches the current presentation above. Provenance Raw: M0"},{"title":"Cervantes","group":"Characters","url":"characters/cervantes.html","text":"Cervantes PROPOSED A would-be social reformer turned outlaw bandit who greets the news of his allies’ deaths with dread mixed with excitement — “She’ll never take him alive.” Summary — Cervantes “had dreams of rebellion and social reform but ended up in a life [of] banditry as an outlaw backed by cutthroat killers. Met with news of his former allies cut down, dread mixes with excitement. She’ll never take him alive.” (M01361, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). His armament was settled in the record’s finest design exchange: “Cervantes has gun? / but not gun / but gun / gun” (M01366–M01369, FreshToast, 2023-02-10) — preserved in doc 002 as “Has gun? But not gun. But gun. Gun.” (002 §Cervantes: Abilities Traits). Background Motivations Dreamed of rebellion and social reform; ended up in a life of banditry as an outlaw backed by cutthroat killers (M01361, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Met with news of his former allies cut down; dread mixes with excitement (M01361). “She’ll never take him alive” (M01361) — the “she” is not identified in the notes. Abilities Traits Has gun? But not gun. But gun. Gun. (M01366–M01369, FreshToast, 2023-02-10) — i.e., a gun-like weapon whose exact nature was left deliberately unresolved. Open questions PROPOSED Who is hunting Cervantes (“she”), and how his outlaw plot intersects the party, is undeveloped. Design history 2023-02-09 — concept posted with an accompanying character playlist: reformer-turned-outlaw, allies cut down, “She’ll never take him alive” (M01361, FreshToast). 2023-02-10 — the gun exchange: “Cervantes has gun? / but not gun / but gun / gun” (M01366–M01369, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M01361, M01366–M01369 (FreshToast; 2023-02-09 → 2023-02-10) Cleaned: 000 Part 2 (Cervantes has gun) Docs: 002 §Cervantes Ledger: character row R08 (“Cervantes wields a gun”, M01366–M01369) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Citrine","group":"Characters","url":"characters/citrine.html","text":"Citrine PROPOSED Ruby’s rival-sister: the homunculus Gideon made correctly , ironclad-loyal to their creator, a “shinier psychopath version” of Ruby who dies by her hand. Summary — Citrine is the second homunculus created by [[gideon-azurith|Gideon Azurith]] — this time with the ritual performed correctly, using his own soul (M02178–M02179, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). She is [[ruby|Ruby]]'s rival and dark mirror, visually inverted from her (yellow eyes, red hair — M01289, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). She sees herself as her master’s tool and, though she comes to love Ruby as a wayward sister, she cannot break her loyalty; the sisters fight, and Ruby kills her but spares their creator in Citrine’s name (M02077, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Filed under Archived Early Concepts in 002, though her death was still cited as a regret-theme avenue as late as October 2023. Appearance Inverted colors from Ruby: yellow eyes and red hair (M01289, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) — versus Ruby’s red eyes and golden hair. Personality Initial concept: “Rival who is just a shinier psychopath version?” of Ruby (M00959, FreshToast, 2023-02-05); rival is like Jinx from Arcane (002 §Lalateya/Ruby). Sees herself as her master’s tool, a second-class being; when she sees a chance — potential for more — Citrine rejects it (M02181–M02183, CrunchyConniptions/spirit_noodle/FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Comes to respect and see Ruby as a wayward sister, but just as it seems she will be convinced, she cannot reconcile her ironclad loyalty to their creator with Ruby’s intentions (M02077, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Background Motivations Created correctly by Gideon using the proper ritual — his own flesh and soul — unlike Ruby, who was made with a borrowed soul (M02178–M02179, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Sent by her creator to gather criminals and outlaws; he harvests their souls to be converted into golems, and the golems serve to progress society along with Citrine (M02173–M02174, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Relationships [[ruby|Ruby]]'s rival and, in effect, her sister — “though unwilling, the sisters must fight. One to protect and aide her allies turned family, the other to protect her own family” (M02077, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Loyal to and cared for by their creator [[gideon-azurith|Gideon Azurith]] — the inverse of Ruby’s absent-father dynamic (002 §Lalateya/Ruby). Role Arc Antagonist who becomes sympathetic; dies by Ruby’s hand (002 §Citrine). Ruby kills Citrine but spares the creator in Citrine’s name (M02077, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Her death powers one of the story’s death/regret avenues: “Ruby once she kills Citrine” (M04658–M04660, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). Open questions DEBATED 002 files Citrine under Archived Early Concepts (“do not consider these active characters”), yet the kills-Citrine beat was still being used in theme planning on 2023-10-24 (M04660) — whether her arc carries into the current Skyward Cinders iteration alongside [[ruby|Ruby]] is unresolved. Design history 2023-02-05 — rival concept raised during Ruby’s profile session: “just a shinier psychopath version” (M00959, FreshToast). 2023-02-09 — named Citrine, visual design set as Ruby’s inversion: yellow eyes, red hair (M01289, FreshToast). 2023-02-15 — full sisterhood arc written: wayward-sister respect, irreconcilable loyalty, the sisters’ duel, Ruby sparing the creator in her name (M02077, FreshToast). 2023-02-16 — deepened: made correctly with the proper ritual (M02178–M02179); soul-harvest errand for Gideon (M02173–M02174); “master’s tool / second-class being / rejects the chance for more” characterization (M02181–M02183). 2023-10-24 — her death reaffirmed as a regret-theme avenue for Ruby (M04658–M04660, FreshToast); profile subsequently archived in 002. — current archived state Provenance Raw: M00959 (FreshToast, 2023-02-05); M01289 (2023-02-09); M02077 (2023-02-15); M02173–M02183 (2023-02-16); M04658–M04660 (2023-10-24) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 / lines 3407–3449 region; Part 2 / lines 2068–2074 (inverted colors) Docs: 002 §Citrine (Ruby’s Rival) (Archived Early Concepts); 001 World Bible line 488 region; 004 Quest (Ruby kills Citrine) Ledger: rows matching “Citrine” in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (R06, R08, R12, R25)"},{"title":"Clara Damaris","group":"Characters","url":"characters/clara.html","text":"Clara Damaris CANON Noble archer of the fallen Damaris family — designed low-luck, family-hates-her foil to [[sable|Sable]] — who fights to restore her house’s honor and un-eclipse its sun. Summary — Clara Damaris, 24, is a main-party back-liner from the noble Damaris family, a bloodline connected to [[ajax|Ajax]] and distantly related to [[lysander|Lysander]] and [[bastard-damaris|Bastard Damaris]] (002 §Clara). She is strong-willed with “big Edelgard energy” (M02024, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14) and “some tsundere qualities” (M01215–M01216, FreshToast, 2023-02-09), and was deliberately built as a foil to Sable: Clara/Marie gets low luck and a family that hates her , Sable gets high luck and a family that loves her (M01222, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-09). Her arc drives her from sheltered noble idealism to confronting the corruption inside her own house — vengeance vs. justice, with the player able to tip the outcome (M02016, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Name / Identity Family name: Damaris (002 §Clara). See the [[damaris|Damaris]] disambiguation for the family and [[bastard-damaris|Bastard Damaris]]. Age: 24 — from the Part 5 ages list, where she still appears as “Marie 24” (M01217, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). The name evolved Malenia → Leyla/Marie → Clara (see Design history). “Vivienne” was floated as a more sophisticated alternative after Clara was settled, then redirected as possibly her mother’s name (M01924–M01925, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Affinity: not specified. The doc’s “likely Fiery, given family connection to Ajax’s bloodline” is inference — PROPOSED (002 §Clara); she does not appear in the known-affinities list (M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). Personality Strong-willed and determined; carries herself with noble bearing despite her family’s fall (002 §Clara). “Strong willed reaction, in line with her character, big Edelgard energy” (M02024, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). Tsundere, maybe — “Marie tsundere maybe? / Has some tsundere qualities?” (M01215–M01216, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Does not curse until her final confrontation (M02076, FreshToast, 2023-02-15) — and, in the earlier Thane’s-foil scene, temporarily drops her noble pretense to tell him “to fuck off and get over himself” (M01389, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). Dialogue consultation trait: “largely useful, with some gaping holes in her knowledge” (M01886, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). Compassionate but can be naive about harsh realities outside nobility; struggles with guilt over the family’s dark legacy (002 §Clara). Listed as “Peak Waifu” in a scrapped party-order fork, but FreshToast countered: “I don’t think Clara will honestly turn out to be big waifu material” (M01905–M01906, 2023-02-14). Background Motivations Designed foil to Sable: “Have Marie with low luck and a family that hates her and Sable with high luck and a family that loves her” (M01222, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-09); “Marie Sable” is one of the core relationship pairings (M01231, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Family Damaris is connected to Ajax’s bloodline — distant relation to [[lysander|Lysander]] and [[bastard-damaris|Bastard Damaris]] (002 §Clara). Family uses silver cutlery — “literally born with a silver spoon in her mouth” (M01834, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-12). The sun is on the Damaris heraldry , leaning into connotations of the Sun as giver and beacon to their people; it gets ‘eclipsed’ by the nasty happenings when the family’s corruption is revealed — a deliberate visual metaphor for the fall from grace (M02017–M02020, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Maybe Clara’s dad “cares a little too much about the sun” (M02048, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Motivations (002 §Clara): restore family honor through her own heroic deeds; distance herself from corrupt family members; prove nobility should serve the people; uncover what her family has been hiding. Goals (002 §Clara): help [[locke|Locke]]'s quest to keep the chaos emeralds from misuse; confront Bastard Damaris; rebuild the Damaris name as a symbol of hope. Flaws (002 §Clara): sheltered upbringing, imposter syndrome, over-idealism about redemption, defensive when family honor is questioned. Abilities Traits Party position: 2nd line (Back) (M01399, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-10). Has a retreat shot (M01877, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-13). Can move somewhat in combat but prefers not to — mobility tier shared with [[ruby|Ruby]], between the static Thane/Isaac and the mobile Leonard/Sable (M01874, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). Skilled archer and tactician; trained in noble combat arts but lacks street-fighting experience (002 §Clara). Exploration-skill brainstorm (heavily debated system): “Clara can have a skill that initiates attacks from a longer distance” (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). Relationships Knows [[thanes-foil|Thane’s Foil]] (the Paladin) from her past — possibly childhood friend or former betrothed (M01393, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). She “relates to the foil’s morality and background but does not commiserate and comfortably opposes him” (M01388, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). Directly opposes [[bastard-damaris|Bastard Damaris]], her corrupted family member (002 §Clara). Forms a sisterly bond with [[locke|Locke]] over shared burden of expectations (002 §Clara). Core pairing with [[sable|Sable]] (M01231, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Role Arc Discovers the extent of her family’s corruption and involvement with the [[drykksar|Drykksar]] parasite; must choose between family loyalty and doing what’s right; confronts Bastard Damaris alongside the party (002 §Clara). Final confrontation with whoever did “the nasty shit in her family”: they attempt to monologue, and Clara interrupts to threaten them Princess Bride style — “I am heir Damaris. You have tainted our family’s reputation… Today you die.” She may be consumed by vengeance, or, if the player guides her to justice, says something less menacing and more hopeful (M02016, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Growth: learns nobility is defined by actions, not bloodline; becomes a true beacon of hope — “the sun un-eclipsed” (002 §Clara; heraldry metaphor M02019–M02020, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Emotional beats (002 §Clara): horror at the family’s connection to the dragon experiments; removes her family crest, then puts it back on with new determination; chooses party over blood against Bastard Damaris. Her presence legitimizes the party’s quest in the eyes of nobility; her arc parallels Locke’s from the opposite social standing (002 §Clara). Open questions DEBATED Name polish: “we settled on Clara but if we wanted something more sophisticated sounding, there’s Vivienne” — subsequently redirected as a candidate for her mother’s name, so Clara stands (M01924–M01925, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). The doc still notes “Name change being considered” (002 §Clara). DEBATED Affinity unassigned — she is absent from the known-affinities roster (M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15); “likely Fiery” is doc inference only PROPOSED . DEBATED Late-roster absence: the 2024 team-skill pairing list names only Locke, Ruby/Thane, Percy/Damian, Astrid/Sable — Clara is not in it (M05392, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). No message removes her from the party; whether the roster shrank or the list was partial is unresolved. Design history 2023-02-08 — original concept as Malenia : tall graceful noblewoman from a fallen noble family of Lethria, kind, justice-driven, groomed for leadership, master archer, seeks to restore her family (M01191–M01192, CrunchyConniptions). Not street-smart but emotionally and academically intelligent (M01193); loyal and proud of her family, then realizes the family is not what she thought (M01194–M01195); initiative increases in the back position (M01196); high-damage delayed ranged skill that almost always crits (M01197, FreshToast); “Says ‘justice’ a little too much” (M01198, FreshToast). — the Elden Ring-borrowed name and the plush-red-skin visual draft were superseded. 2023-02-08 — rename candidates: Leyla (M01199, spirit_noodle) and Marie (M01201"},{"title":"Colette","group":"Characters","url":"characters/colette.html","text":"Colette CANON One of [[the-4-boyes|the 4 Boyes]]: the trilingual Lunoiran “heart” of the group — culture ambassador, voice of reason, empath, and the wanderlust-driven one. Summary — Colette is a Lunoiran female in her early 20s, in a support role as the “heart” of the group. She represents “the power of names and foreign uses or interpretations of sorcery” and speaks Lunoiran, Imperial Lycosian and Fioran (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). She is big on cultural cross-pollination, acts as the group’s moral compass, and dreams of exploring the world beyond the academy (M04365–M04367, M04373–M04375, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Identity Lunoiran female; early 20s (M04227, M04243, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). A bit darker complexion than the others (compared to the western-Khorath Mediterranean standard) (M04242, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Affinity undecided; archetype uncertain (M04227). Role Function One of the 4 Boyes; support role — the “heart” of the group (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Meant to represent a general facet of Sorcery and magic (M04226, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Magical focus Represents the power of names and foreign uses / interpretations of sorcery (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Trilingual: speaks Lunoiran, Imperial Lycosian and Fioran (M04227). Big on cultural cross-pollination (M04367, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Personality Culture Ambassador — acts as a bridge between the academy’s different magical cultures; sees the bigger picture, a good person looking out for the greater good (M04370–M04371, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Fiction / adventure / cool-history nerd who quotes things she’s read: “I dig the idea of her quoting things she’s read” (M04371–M04372, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Voice of Reason — often acts as the moral compass of the group (M04373, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17; confirmed M04375). Empath — can sense/feel the emotions of others, guiding her actions — “Yes and yes but not magically” (M04374–M04375, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Wanderlust Dreams of exploring the world beyond the academy, fuelling her inventive spirit — “OH COLETTE. She’s number 9” (M04363–M04366, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-17). (Alt framing in the notes: “or is she scared of the world. Wanting to protect the world from her uncontrolled power?”) (M04356 list item 9 text, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17; 002 §Colette: Wanderlust). Dark secret DEBATED Dark Secret = “maybe? idk” — may have a hidden past/secret she’s running from (potentially crucial to the plot); unresolved (M04370 item 3, M04371, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Conflicts (brainstormed) PROPOSED Ten-item conflict brainstorm list — design seeds, not yet finalized (M04382–M04391, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17): Need for Validation — seeks constant validation to affirm her self-worth Financial Struggles — feels pressure due to lack of funds Desire to Fit In — adopts personas to feel included Hidden Talent — has a non-magical talent she’s too shy to show Perceived Weakness — feels she’s not strong enough compared to peers Mentor Letdown — disappointed by someone she looked up to Social Anxiety — struggles with large gatherings or expressing herself in groups Desire for Recognition — wants her achievements to be recognized Seeking Role Models — looks for strong role models but often feels let down Loss — grieves the loss of a close friend or family member Rejected ideas SCRAPPED Prodigious learner / goody-two-shoes — rejected (“1. no”) (M04370–M04371, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Toph-like indomitable will — rejected (“4. no”) (M04370–M04371). Design history 2023-10-17 — initial concept in the 4 Boyes roster write-up: Lunoiran female, archetype uncertain, affinity undecided, “Support role? The ‘heart’ of the group”, power of names + foreign sorcery, trilingual (M04227, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — Wanderlust (trait #9 from the Pandora vetting list) assigned to her: “OH COLETTE. She’s number 9. Big on that cultural cross polination” (M04363–M04367, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — trait vetting: prodigious learner no; Culture Ambassador yes (“although that’s two things”); Dark Secret “maybe? idk”; Toph-like will no; Bookworm reshaped into a fiction/adventure/cool-history nerd who quotes things she’s read (M04369–M04372, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — Voice of Reason and non-magical Empath confirmed (M04373–M04375, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — 10-item conflict brainstorm posted (M04382–M04391, CrunchyConniptions). Provenance Raw: M04226–M04227; M04242–M04243; M04356; M04363–M04375; M04382–M04391 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 lines 6767–6803 region (Colette profile, wanderlust, conflicts, rejections) Docs: 002 §Colette (incl. §Colette: Wanderlust, §Colette: Dark Secret, §Colette: Conflicts (Brainstormed), §Colette: Rejected Ideas) Ledger: rows for Colette wanderlust/cross-pollination (M04363–M04367) and 4 Boyes demographics (M04242–M04243) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Crown of Thorns (Concept)","group":"Characters","url":"characters/crown-of-thorns.html","text":"Crown of Thorns (Concept) PROPOSED An early character concept born from wordplay: a Game of Thrones -inspired character who has a crown — because “thrones sounds and looks like thorns.” Summary — PROPOSED — early concept A character “based off of game of thrones” — “because its about a chair for the kingdom” — who “has a crown” (M04939–M04942, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-07). The concept was sparked by simple wordplay: “thrones sounds and looks like thorns” (M04943, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-07), and it landed: “This has unironically given me an idea” (M04944, FreshToast, 2023-11-07). No further details were developed. Concept Name/image: Crown of thorns (M04939, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-07). Basis: Game of Thrones , “about a chair for the kingdom”; the character has a crown (M04940–M04942, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-07). Origin: the thrones/thorns pun (M04943, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-07). FreshToast’s unspecified follow-on idea (M04944, FreshToast, 2023-11-07) was never elaborated in the chat. Design history 2023-11-07 — sole appearance: the full five-message pitch and FreshToast’s “unironically given me an idea” response (M04939–M04944, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M04939–M04944 (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast; 2023-11-07) Cleaned: 000 (Crown of Thorns notes, lines 7634-7639 per 002) Docs: 002 §Crown of Thorns (Concept) Ledger: see the Crown of Thorns row in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Cultboy","group":"Characters","url":"characters/cultboy.html","text":"Cultboy DEBATED Cold-affinity placeholder — a working name implying a cult/religious background that was never developed. Summary — “Cultboy” is a placeholder-named character appearing only in the known-affinities roster: “Cold - Damian, Astrid, Soren, Cultboy” (M03051/M03052, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). The working name suggests a cult or religious background, but no note develops it. Doc 002 flags him PLACEHOLDER — NEEDS DECISION . Description Affinity: Cold (M03051/M03052, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). This is the only sourced fact about him. PROPOSED A cult/religious background is implied only by the working name (002 §Cultboy). Possibly related brainstorm from the same period: a “cult of people who graft monster parts onto themselves” as a short-term antagonistic faction (M02715–M02717, FreshToast, 2023-02-24) — no message connects Cultboy to it. Not in the 2024 team-skill roster (M05392, FreshToast, 2024-04-11) or the party-as-dogs list (M03029, FreshToast, 2023-03-13). Open questions DEBATED Doc 002 status: PLACEHOLDER — NEEDS DECISION , options: (1) develop fully with a cult/religious background, (2) merge with [[soren|Soren]] or [[damian|Damian]] (all Cold affinity), (3) remove — the name suggests an early concept. Recommendation: either develop a unique cult angle or merge with the other Cold characters (002 §Cultboy). Design history 2023-03-15 — sole appearance: listed under Cold in the known-affinities roster (M03051/M03052, FreshToast) — current (and only) state. Provenance Raw: M03051–M03052 (FreshToast, 2023-03-15) Cleaned: 000 (known-affinities list) Docs: 002 §Cultboy Ledger: affinity-roster row citing M03051 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt","group":"Characters","url":"characters/cyril-cyrus-erhardt.html","text":"Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt PROPOSED The “Magic Robin Hood for Knowledge” — a character who steals from the Grand Library to share what’s kept from the public. Summary — Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt is a “Magic Robin Hood for knowledge” (M02444, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19): knowledge is kept from the public in the [[grand-library|Grand Library]], and he wants to acquire and share it (M02447, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19). He appears to be a different character from the Caelian Odyssey Cyrus — see [[cyrus|Cyrus (disambiguation)]] and [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus Sunworth]]. Iteration note: conceived on 2023-02-19 amid main-game (Skyward Cinders era) plot brainstorming ([[locke|Locke]]/[[lysander|Lysander]] prologue, chaos emeralds); doc 002 files him under the Caelian Odyssey section only with the caveat “This appears to be a different character from the Cyrus in Caelian Odyssey” (002 §Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt, Notes). Background Motivations Magic Robin Hood for Knowledge (M02444, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19). Knowledge is kept from the public in the Grand Library; “Cyrill is wants to acquire and share the knowledge” (M02447, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19). Name First given as Cyril Erhardt (M02444, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19); later the same day rendered Cyrus Erhardt (M02465, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19). Surname Erhardt is settled; the given name shifts Cyril → Cyrus (ledger row, R13). Note the Erhardt surname also appears in main-game naming (“Leon Erhardt” was a hero-name candidate, M01943–M01944 era — see [[leon]]). Design history 2023-02-19 — concept: “Magic Robin Hood for knowledge - Cyril Erhardt” (M02444, CrunchyConniptions); expanded: knowledge kept from public in the Grand Library, he wants to acquire and share it (M02447, spirit_noodle). 2023-02-19 — name rendered “Cyrus Erhardt” (M02465, spirit_noodle) — given name Cyril → Cyrus; never developed further. Provenance Raw: M02444, M02447, M02465 (CrunchyConniptions, spirit_noodle; 2023-02-19) Cleaned: 000 Part 9 (Magic Robin Hood, Cyrus Erhardt) Docs: 002 §Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt (Magic Robin Hood for Knowledge); 003 §Grand Library / Cyril-Cyrus Erhardt; 009 §Cyrus Erhardt Ledger: row “Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt - a ‘Magic Robin Hood for Knowledge’…” (M02444 M02447 M02465, R13) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Cyrus Sunworth","group":"Characters","url":"characters/cyrus-sunworth.html","text":"Cyrus Sunworth CANON The main and final antagonist of Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium — a flame-affinity mage who starts chill-but-cocky and is soured by neglect and a daemon’s manipulation, becoming the keystone of the game’s theme. Summary — Cyrus is a Lycosian male in his early 20s, the classic mage/wizard archetype and a representative of a well-rounded sorcerer by Caelian standards (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). He is the most developed character of the iteration and its main/final antagonist — “Cyrus, our antagonist, is the keystone for our theme. We do not have a singular main protagonist” (M04271, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Entangled with [[tenebrae|Tenebrae]], the daemon of mania, he is turned sour by lack of support and her manipulation — “But he’s pretty clever, so ends up using her in the end” (M04290–M04291, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Not to be confused with [[cyril-cyrus-erhardt|Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt]] — see [[cyrus|Cyrus (disambiguation)]]. Identity First name: Cyrus (M00608, spirit_noodle, 2021-08-15). DEBATED Surname: “Cyrus Sunworth” initially suggested (M00607, spirit_noodle, 2021-08-15); FreshToast’s sole issue was that it “takes away somewhat from [[leora|Leora]]'s sun association” (M00609, 2021-08-15). “I think Xworth is good though, just maybe we slot something else into it than sun” — “Shineworth idk” (M00614, M00618, FreshToast, 2021-08-15). Decision left open: “We’ll give it a think its fine. Not super important.” (M00619, spirit_noodle, 2021-08-15). Lycosian male; early 20s; Mediterranean ethnicity, typical for western Khorath (M04227, M04242–M04243, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Role Arc CANON Main/final antagonist of Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium and the keystone for the game’s theme (M04227, M04271, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). The game has no singular main protagonist — the focus is [[the-4-boyes|the 4 Boyes]] (M04271). The most developed character of the iteration: “I would focus on the 4 boyes first. Cyrus is the most developed” (M04272, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Classic mage/wizard archetype; representative of a well-rounded sorcerer by Caelian standards (M04226–M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). All the iteration’s “bad shit” is chalked up to Cyrus and Tenebrae — no external antagonist faction, “keep it in house” (M04287, M04306–M04307, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). SCRAPPED Earlier framing as “the mentee” / “main protagonist” — directly contradicts the Part 16 material, which makes him the main/final antagonist. Do not re-propose. (002 §Cyrus: Role / Function in Story; see AI_Hallucinations.md.) Personality Corruption Arc CANON (M04289–M04295, FreshToast, 2023-10-17) Starts off chill but maybe a little cocky. The lack of support he receives + Tenebrae’s manipulation turns him sour — “Basically he’s a product of nurture, bad circumstances, rather than his nature. Kind of the inverse of [[lysander|Lysander]].” Becomes obsessed with strength, knowledge and being the best; “gets a little fixated on the fact he’s got a finite lifespan.” Quickly acquires a rivalry; grows increasingly desperate to prove himself (M04227). Design reference: “Again I have a really good similar character but you would need to finish Armored Core VI” (M04292, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Abilities Traits CANON Affinity: Flame (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Later picks up Earth to produce glass sorcery , and Motion to produce explosive sorcery — both fusions with his Flame affinity (M04227). Starts with his affinity and the barest knowledge of magic; has “awakened” (M04315–M04316, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Has much of the same potential as the 4 Boyes (M04227). PROPOSED Cross-game easter egg: Cyrus gets some powerful named attack in the first game, and [[locke|Locke]] later earns the same — or similar — ability as a skill in the second game, “since both wield flame” (M04851–M04852, FreshToast, 2023-10-31). Equipment CANON Wields a peachwood staff called [[transcend|‘Transcend’]] , made from a branch Cyrus played with as a child (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Relationships [[nyssa|Nyssa]] — an upper year and early mentor figure for Cyrus (M04965, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). [[tenebrae|Tenebrae]], the daemon of mania — manipulates him and turns him sour, but Cyrus is clever and ends up using her in the end: he is ultimately her user, not merely her victim (M04290–M04291, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Cross-game naming note: concern raised that “Sunworth” takes away from Leora’s sun association (M00609, FreshToast, 2021-08-15). Open questions DEBATED Surname: Sunworth vs another “Xworth” (e.g. Shineworth) — left open since 2021 (M00606–M00619, spirit_noodle/FreshToast, 2021-08-15). Design history 2021-08-15 — name proposed: “I remember thinking of a surname for the mentee… Cyrus Sunworth. Cyrus is his first name.” (M00606–M00608, spirit_noodle). Sun-association clash with Leora raised; “Xworth” format kept, “Shineworth idk” floated; decision parked as “not super important” (M00609–M00619, FreshToast/spirit_noodle). The “mentee” framing of this era was later scrapped. 2023-10-17 — recast in Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium as the classic mage of the roster and main/final antagonist ; Flame affinity with later glass/explosive fusions; staff ‘Transcend’; entangled with Tenebrae (M04227, FreshToast) — supersedes the mentee/protagonist framing. 2023-10-17 — declared the keystone for the theme; no singular main protagonist (M04271, FreshToast); corruption arc detailed: chill-but-cocky start, soured by neglect and Tenebrae’s manipulation, ends up using her; inverse of Lysander; obsession with strength, knowledge, being the best, finite lifespan (M04289–M04295, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — scope decision routes all antagonism through Cyrus and Tenebrae (“keep it in house”) (M04287, M04306–M04307, FreshToast). 2023-10-31 — shared-flame easter egg proposed: Cyrus’s powerful named attack recurring as a Locke skill in game 2 (M04851–M04852, FreshToast). 2023-11-15 — Nyssa defined as his early mentor figure (M04965, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M00606–M00619 (spirit_noodle/FreshToast, 2021-08-15); M04226–M04228; M04242–M04243; M04271–M04272; M04287–M04295; M04306–M04307; M04315–M04316 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17); M04851–M04852 (FreshToast, 2023-10-31); M04965 (FreshToast, 2023-11-15) Cleaned: 000 §Cyrus surname region (~line 1980); 000 §The 4 Boyes / Luminarium notes Docs: 002 §Cyrus (Sunworth / Shineworth - surname debated); 004 §Shared Abilities (Easter Egg) Ledger: rows for “Mentee surname proposal: Cyrus Sunworth”, Big 6, no-antagonist-faction, theme keystone, shared-flame easter egg in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Cyrus (disambiguation)","group":"Characters","url":"characters/cyrus.html","text":"Cyrus (disambiguation) Cyrus may refer to: [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus Sunworth]] — the Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium main/final antagonist; Flame-affinity mage, keystone of that game’s theme (002 §Cyrus (Sunworth / Shineworth - surname debated)). Surname debated: Sunworth vs Shineworth / another “Xworth” (M00607–M00619, spirit_noodle/FreshToast, 2021-08-15). [[cyril-cyrus-erhardt|Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt]] — the “Magic Robin Hood for Knowledge” , who wants to acquire and share the knowledge kept from the public in the Grand Library (M02444, M02447, M02465, CrunchyConniptions/spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19). The docs note explicitly: Erhardt “appears to be a different character from the Cyrus in Caelian Odyssey” (002 §Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt, Notes). Provenance Raw: M00607 (spirit_noodle, 2021-08-15); M02444, M02465 (CrunchyConniptions/spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19) Docs: 002 §Cyrus (Sunworth / Shineworth - surname debated); 002 §Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt (Magic Robin Hood for Knowledge) Ledger: rows matching “Cyrus Sunworth” / “Cyrus Erhardt” in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Daemon of Want","group":"Characters","url":"characters/daemon-of-want.html","text":"Daemon of Want CANON The secret superboss: a spirit born from an entire kingdom’s rampant desire for a savior from the dragon, exceptionally powerful, cunning, and hiding for its own survival. Summary — The Daemon of Want is a spirit formed from the rampant desires of the people for a saviour from [[vaenngrym|the dragon]] and other woes (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Because desire is vague and widespread, it is exceptionally powerful and cunning — not exactly evil so much as self-serving — and it understands that making its presence known would inevitably lead to its downfall, so it remains in hiding (M02656, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). It is the secret superboss the player can run into (M02658, FreshToast, 2023-02-21), and [[sir-valerius|Sir Valerius]] is hunting it (M02902, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). Nature Daemon Lore Daemons manifest when significant, shared emotion is experienced in a given area; they attach their identity, abilities and means of growth to the idea, concept or entity at the centre of that shared emotion (M02654, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). The more powerful the emotion and the more people, the stronger the daemon — which then feeds off the concept around which it formed (M02655, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Daemons can only be killed if bound, and binding a daemon to your will becomes more difficult the stronger and older it is (M02666, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Thus the Daemon of Want is practically immortal so long as desire remains to feed it (M02667, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Daemons do die naturally if starved, but such daemons are typically quite weak — nothing like the Daemon of Want, which resulted from an entire kingdom’s shared woes (M02683, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Multiple Daemons of Want can exist at a time (M02687, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Abilities Traits Its ability — the means to steal the strength of others — reflects its origin (M02657, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). The stolen power can only take and never give back; [[eris|Eris]] is mistaken in her belief that the stolen life force could be used to resurrect the dead — a “true resurrection” is assumed impossible by any means, magical or otherwise (M02659–M02660, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Meta-design note: “the power is literally gaining EXP, to reflect Ajax/Lysander being parodies of a classic JRPG protagonist” (M02671, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Relationships [[ajax|Ajax]] — Ajax’s strength-stealing power was acquired in a deal with the Daemon of Want: he traded half of his lifespan for the ability to steal the strength of others. The deal was struck when Ajax became desperate for strength to defend his people, “as the only warrior truly brave enough to fight a fucking dragon” (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). [[lysander|Lysander]] and the [[bastard-damaris|bastard Damaris]] — inherited diluted versions of the daemonic power, which has waned with each heir; to recover it they must kill and drain the life of others (M02668, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). [[sir-valerius|Sir Valerius]] — is hunting the Daemon of Want (M02902, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). Role Arc Secret superboss the player can run into (M02658, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Its dialogue changes based on whether [[locke|Locke]] drinks the dragon’s blood after killing [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]] (M02663, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Open questions DEBATED “Perhaps a mechanical change in the fight as well?” based on the dragon’s-blood choice (M02664, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). CrunchyConniptions objected that having no mechanical change is unsatisfying — confusing and serving nothing compared to a mechanical change (M02678, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22). Unresolved. Design history 2023-02-19 — the spirits of the setting are named “daemons”: “Daemon is a good inbetween” — practically devils but more spirit than hell-creature (M02466–M02468, FreshToast). 2023-02-21 — full concept established in one session: origin as a spirit of the people’s desire for a saviour, Ajax’s half-a-lifespan deal, exceptional power and cunning from desire’s vagueness, hiding, secret-superboss role, dragon’s-blood dialogue variation, near-immortality while desire persists (M02653–M02667, FreshToast). 2023-02-22 — worldbuilding objection: if daemons persist after being born, doesn’t the world fill up with immortal daemons? Do you get a second Daemon of Want when the same shared woe occurs elsewhere? (M02676, CrunchyConniptions). Answered: daemons starve and die naturally if unfed, and yes, multiple Daemons of Want can coexist (M02683, M02687, FreshToast). Same day: objection that no mechanical fight change is unsatisfying (M02678, CrunchyConniptions) — still open. 2023-03-05 — Sir Valerius established as hunting the Daemon of Want (M02902, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M02466–M02468, M02653–M02671, M02676, M02678, M02683, M02687, M02902 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-02-19 → 2023-03-05) Cleaned: 000 Part 10 §Daemon of Want / §Daemons (Spirits) Docs: 002 §Daemon of Want; 001 §Daemon of Want / §Daemons; 004 §True Ending Ledger: rows for “Daemon of Want” / daemon lore in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (world rows 19–24, 33; character rows 150, 154, 175; mechanics row 546)"},{"title":"Damaris (disambiguation)","group":"Characters","url":"characters/damaris.html","text":"Damaris CANON (disambiguation) “Damaris” is a family name in Skyward Cinders — it can refer to the noble family, its heir, its bastard, or a duplicate character entry in the docs. Damaris may refer to: The Damaris family The noble family descended from [[ajax|Ajax]]. “Damaris is her family name, heraldry is what you call a family’s crest and such” (M02029, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). The sun is present on the Damaris heraldry/family crest as a giver/beacon to their people; it gets “eclipsed” by the nasty happenings as a visual metaphor for the family’s fall from grace, and un-eclipses when Clara becomes a true beacon of hope (M02017–M02020, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Ajax’s descendants carry a diluted version of his take/steal power, which wanes with each heir (M02668, FreshToast, 2023-02-21), and are reputed to have sunset irises (M02904–M02906, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). [[clara|Clara Damaris]] Party member and heir to the family (alias: Marie). In her final confrontation she declares: “I am heir Damaris. You have tainted our family’s reputation… Today you die.” (M02016, FreshToast, 2023-02-14; see [[clara]]). [[bastard-damaris|Bastard Damaris]] Illegitimate member of the family, youngest of Ajax’s bloodline, first to exhibit truly prominent draconic features, and herald of the parasite [[drykksar|Drykksar]] — a mid-game antagonist (M02668–M02694, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-21/22; see [[bastard-damaris]]). “Damaris” (standalone character entry in 002) 002 contains a separate entry, “Damaris (character - not same as Damaris family),” marked STATUS: UNCLEAR - LIKELY REFERS TO BASTARD DAMARIS (002 §Damaris (character)). Its only content is the pre-fight scene: Damaris: people bad. I kill. Locke: give people chance, maybe good. no kill Astrid: fuck u. I kill. (M02682, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22) The doc’s analysis: this scene is the confrontation with the Bastard Damaris — the dialogue matches his philosophy (humanity is flawed, must be eliminated) — and its recommendation is to remove the duplicate entry, since the dialogue is already integrated into the Bastard Damaris profile (002 §Damaris (character)). This wiki follows that reading: the scene lives on [[bastard-damaris]]. Provenance Raw: M02016–M02029 (2023-02-14, heraldry/family name); M02668–M02694 (2023-02-21/22, bastard power lore); M02682 (2023-02-22, pre-fight scene); M02904–M02906 (2023-03-05, sunset irises) — FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions Cleaned: 000 Parts 7, 10 (Damaris heraldry; Bastard Damaris; pre-fight scene) Docs: 002 §Bastard Damaris / §Damaris (character) / §Clara; 001 §Damaris heraldry (lines-region 411-414); 004 §pre-fight scene; 003 §Damaris (location guide reference) Ledger: rows R11 (heraldry sun; Clara’s confrontation), R15 (bastard lore; pre-fight scene), R16 (sunset irises) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Damian","group":"Characters","url":"characters/damian.html","text":"Damian DEBATED Cold-affinity party member from Fiore, pursued by a vigilante/private investigator we meet first — underdeveloped, but present in the latest 2024 roster. Summary — Damian is a Cold-affinity party member (M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15) from Fiore, “the country across the mountains” (M03826, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). His most concrete plot hook: he “is pursued by some vigilante/private investigator but we actually meet her first who then leads us to Damian” (M02701, FreshToast, 2023-02-23), a pursuit that “results in a romance plot” (M02770, FreshToast, 2023-02-25) — a premise originally sketched for [[leon|Leon]]. Doc 002 flags him UNDERDEVELOPED — NEEDS DECISION , yet he appears in the latest team-skill roster, paired with [[percival|Percy]] (M05392, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). Name / Identity Affinity: Cold — “Cold - Damian, Astrid, Soren, Cultboy” (M03051/M03052, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). From [[fiore|Fiore]] , the country across the mountains (M03826, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). The name “Damian” first appears in the 2021 name bank (M00514, spirit_noodle, 2021-08-01) — whether that entry is this character is unstated. Background Relationships Pursued by a vigilante/private investigator ; the party meets her first and she leads them to Damian (M02701, FreshToast, 2023-02-23). The hunter is “the proper, by-the-book archetype” and the pursuit “results in a romance plot” (M02769–M02770, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). This archetype claim is why the Hermione-esque rival for Isaac/Percy had to be a boy — reusing the dynamic “strikes too many parallels with Damian” (M02766–M02767, M02771, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). DEBATED The pursued-romance premise was first written for [[leon|Leon]] (“Leonard hunted by a cop, maybe finally turns into a romance plot?” — M01396, FreshToast, 2023-02-10); no message resolves which character owns it. See Leon’s page. Abilities Traits Mobility intent: “let Damian/Cyril and Sable schmoove a bit” — a middle mobility tier next to the static Thane/Isaac (M02579, FreshToast, 2023-02-20). Team-skill pairing (2024 scheme): paired with [[percival|Percy]]; e.g. Astrid gets a positional bonus “when she is beside Percy or Damian” (M05392, M05394, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). Dog-breed party comparison: Old English Sheepdog (M03029, FreshToast, 2023-03-13). Open questions DEBATED Doc 002 status: UNDERDEVELOPED — NEEDS DECISION , with options to (1) develop fully as the Cold-affinity party member from Fiore, (2) merge with [[soren|Soren]] or [[cultboy|Cultboy]] (both also Cold), (3) convert to an NPC from the Fiore region, or (4) remove if redundant (002 §Damian). Weight toward keeping: his presence in the 2024 roster (M05392) postdates that assessment. DEBATED Ownership of the hunted-romance plot vs. [[leon|Leon]] (M01396 vs. M02701/M02769–M02770). Design history 2021-08-01 — “Damian” appears in the name bank (M00514, spirit_noodle); no character attached. 2023-02-20 — mobility sketch: “let Damian/Cyril and Sable schmoove a bit” (M02579, FreshToast) — first appearance as a party member. 2023-02-23 — plot hook: pursued by a vigilante/private investigator whom we meet first (M02701, FreshToast). 2023-02-25 — pursuit confirmed as a romance plot with a proper, by-the-book huntress; the dynamic is declared unique to Damian, forcing Isaac/Percy’s rival to differ (M02765–M02771, FreshToast). 2023-03-13 — dog-breed comparison: Old English Sheepdog (M03029, FreshToast). 2023-03-15 — Cold affinity assigned (M03051/M03052, FreshToast). 2023-10-13 — origin set: from Fiore, the country across the mountains (M03826, FreshToast). 2024-04-11 — appears in the latest team-skill roster, paired with Percy (M05392, M05394, FreshToast) — current state. Provenance Raw: M00514, M01396, M02579, M02701, M02765–M02771, M03029, M03051–M03052, M03826, M05392–M05394 (spirit_noodle, FreshToast; 2021-08-01 → 2024-04-11) Cleaned: 000 Part 10 (pursuit/rival-archetype debate), Part 15 (Fiore) Docs: 002 §Damian; 001 (Fiore/country politics) Ledger: rows R14 (M02579 mobility), R15 (M02701/M02769–M02770 pursuit romance), R16 (M03029 dogs list), R21/world row (M03826 Fiore) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Death (Spirit)","group":"Characters","url":"characters/death.html","text":"Death (Spirit) CANON The non-binary personification of death — a timid, comforting spirit who appears wherever people die to ease their passing, feared as a bad omen, and Thane’s romantic interest. Summary — Death is a spirit (later: daemon) and [[thane|Thane]]‘s romantic interest — the game’s only romance (ledger row R07 citing M01223, FreshToast, 2023-02-09; 002 §Death: Relationships). They are non-binary (“Remember we specified death would be NB” — M02350, FreshToast, 2023-02-16; “DD, the Death Daemon is enbie” — M02745, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). Death spirits don’t bring death: they appear where death occurs and ease folks’ passing, but because they always appear where people die, many believe them to be a frightful bad omen (M01386–M01387, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). Thane’s daemon of death “is moderately powerful but not violent and prefers to comfort the dying than try to cause more death. Surprisingly timid” (M02665, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Appearance Design goal: “I want death’s design to make people say ‘I would let her kill me’” (M02349, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). “They can be hot” — “We have plenty of gril waifus already. Let the enbie waifu flourish” (M02358–M02360, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Personality Shy — “actually attracted to edgy boy [Thane] because they like his jokes and the lack of fear or aversion, making them feel accepted” (M00923, FreshToast, 2023-02-05). Not violent; prefers to comfort the dying rather than cause more death; surprisingly timid (M02665, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Nature Role Like many other spirits, death is “just a manifestation of the dreams and fears of people that grows over time (like chainsawman devils)” (M00922, FreshToast, 2023-02-05). Appears where death occurs and eases folks’ passing; widely mistaken for a frightful bad omen (M01386–M01387, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). The spirits were later renamed daemons (M02466–M02468, FreshToast, 2023-02-19), making them “DD, the Death Daemon” (M02745, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). As a Daemon, Death manifests the way other daemons do — shared emotion attaching to a concept (see [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]] for the daemon mechanics) (M02653–M02654, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Counted as non-binary in the cast’s gender tally — set apart from the “8 boys and 9 girls” of the major cast, alongside [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]], because neither has a conventional gender (M02741, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). Hunted, along with Thane, by the paladin [[thanes-foil|Thane’s Foil]]: “Eventually hunts down thane or death spirit” (M01377, FreshToast, 2023-02-10); “Thane is death, Foil is life” (M01381, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). Relationships [[thane|Thane]] — romantic interest. Thane’s arc runs through them: “running from (literal) death, finding out death’s kinda cute, giving death a smooch, holding death’s hand at a fireworks display, dealing with spirit hunters including his foil, protecc his new deathly enbie waifu, being free to cuddle death and be happy he’s alive so he can move on from his guilt and mourning” (M02347, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Origin of the pairing: Thane’s near-death numbness leads to him being pursued by death Final-Destination-style, until it turns out death is shy and attracted to him — “They bang” (M00920–M00924, FreshToast, 2023-02-05). A nickname for Death was workshopped because “Thane seems the type to use irreverent nicknames” (M02757–M02759, FreshToast, 2023-02-25); “DD is what I have for now,” otherwise “they’re just death” (M02749, M02753, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). Open questions DEBATED No settled nickname for Thane’s dynamic — “no need to workshop a better name, unless it comes to you effortlessly through some sort of epiphany” (M02756, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-25). Design history 2023-02-05 — original concept, built off Thane’s (“edgy boy”) near-death numbness: pursued by death Final Destination-style; death as a manifestation of people’s dreams and fears; turns out death is shy and attracted to him; “They bang”; “a cool metaphor and cute way of dealing with death without edgy boy actually dying” (M00920–M00927, FreshToast). 2023-02-05 — SCRAPPED Death-as-a-child alternative : “at first, in my mind, i kind of perceived death as a child, partly because of an inherent thought in my brain that thinks they must have poor judgement skills” — “but i dig this approach too” (M00932–M00933, spirit_noodle) — discarded in favor of the shy/attracted adult approach. 2023-02-10 — world rules: death spirits ease passing rather than bring death; mistaken for a bad omen (M01386–M01387, FreshToast); the Foil hunts Thane or the death spirit; “Thane is death, Foil is life” (M01377, M01381, FreshToast). 2023-02-16 — identity and design goals: NB confirmed against the “but what if she was hot” push (M02350–M02356, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions); “I would let her kill me” design target (M02349, CrunchyConniptions); “Let the enbie waifu flourish” (M02360, FreshToast); Thane’s full arc through Death sketched (M02347, FreshToast). 2023-02-19/21 — spirits renamed daemons (M02466–M02468, FreshToast); Thane’s daemon of death characterized: moderately powerful, non-violent, comforting, surprisingly timid (M02665, FreshToast). 2023-02-25 — “DD, the Death Daemon is enbie”; nickname workshop for the Thane dynamic ends unresolved — “DD is what I have for now” / “they’re just death” (M02745–M02759, FreshToast/spirit_noodle). Provenance Raw: M00919–M00933 (concept + child alternative), M01377, M01381, M01386–M01387, M02347–M02360, M02466–M02468, M02665, M02745–M02759 (FreshToast, spirit_noodle, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-02-05 → 2023-02-25) Cleaned: 000 Part 2 (death spirits), Part 8 (Thane arc), Part 10 (DD enbie/nickname) Docs: 002 §Death (Spirit); 001 §Death Spirits / Daemon of Death; 009 §Appendix — Considered Dropped (Death-pictured-as-a-child alternative) Ledger: rows R06 (M00932–M00933 child alternative, partial), R07 (only romance Thane×Death), R08 (M01386–M01387 ease passing/bad omen), R14 (M02665 daemon of death), R15 (M02745–M02759 DD enbie/nickname) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Diamant Lucifer","group":"Characters","url":"characters/diamant-lucifer.html","text":"Diamant Lucifer PROPOSED A retired assassin with an unwavering sense of justice, drawn back into violence by a past that won’t let him go. Summary — Diamant Lucifer is a retired assassin in his late twenties who hung up his weapons after a long career, hoping to live out his life in peace — but he is pursued by a group of assassins for mysterious reasons and finds himself drawn back into the world of violence and danger (M01287–M01288, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Cold and calculating yet bound by a strong moral code, he never accepted contracts on innocents and keeps his promises no matter how dangerous the task (M01287). Appearance Late twenties; a towering 6’2 with a broad, muscular build; rough, weathered face testifying to a hard life; thick beard, hair tied back in a tight bun. Walks with a limp from an old injury sustained during his assassin days, but still holds himself with an air of confidence — always alert, never letting his guard down (M01287, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Personality A man of few words; lets actions speak louder (M01287, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Cold, calculating, fiercely independent (M01287). Unwavering sense of justice; will not hesitate to eliminate those who threaten it (M01287). Strong moral code: will not accept contracts on innocent people; a man of his word who keeps his promises no matter how dangerous the task (M01287). Background Motivations Born in the rough-and-tumble countryside; showed remarkable aptitude for hunting and marksmanship from a young age, catching the attention of a notorious assassin guild that recruited him at 18 (M01288, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Became one of the most feared assassins in the guild, taking contracts all over the world; grew to question the morality of the profession and retired (M01288). Now lives in exile from the very group that took him in; never truly free from his past, constantly on guard against those who would eliminate him (M01288). Pursued by a group of assassins for mysterious reasons (M01288). Despite his desire for peace, driven by a strong sense of duty — will not hesitate to protect those he cares about, even at the cost of his own life (M01288). Design history 2023-02-09 — full profile posted in two parts: appearance and personality (M01287, CrunchyConniptions), then motives and background (M01288, CrunchyConniptions). Not revisited in later chat. Provenance Raw: M01287–M01288 (CrunchyConniptions; 2023-02-09) Cleaned: 000 Part 5 §Diamant Lucifer Docs: 002 §Diamant Lucifer (Retired Assassin) Ledger: character row R07 (Diamant Lucifer profile) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Early character concepts","group":"Characters","url":"characters/early-character-concepts.html","text":"Early character concepts SCRAPPED The 2021 brainstorm pile: one-line playable-character archetypes and narrative sketches from before any specific game existed. Archived; a few seeds later sprouted elsewhere. Summary — Between March and June 2021 the group threw out a series of character archetypes and concept sketches, recorded in 002’s Archived Early Concepts section with the note that they “may or may not be integrated into final game designs” and should not be considered active characters (002 §Archived Early Concepts). None was developed past a few lines here, but several were EVOLVED into later entities, noted per section below. Necromancer EVOLVED Playable character archetype: makes cute and silly zombies (M00129, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). The necromancer seed later grew into the fleshed-out dungeon-master character [[lyra|Lyra the Necromancer]] (M01272–M01280, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09), and the “necromancer revives enemies, so kill the weakest first” combat example (M00230, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-26) fed the kill-order/rank-targeting design discussed in the systems docs. Space/Time Manipulator SCRAPPED Playable archetype who manipulates space and time with immense limitations — “turns the game into something more like chess than a hack n slash” (M00130, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). Space-time manipulation itself resurfaced in the magic notes (007 §Space-Time Manipulation). Archetype roster: Berserker, Archer, Lancer, Rider, Caster, Swordmaster SCRAPPED Fate-style class names listed as traditional character types for reference (M00131–M00136, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). No individual development. Neon Power Character SCRAPPED A character with neon-like power from Infamous Second Son (M00137, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). (Fantasy-neon later reappeared as a worldbuilding lighting idea, unrelated to a character — M04409–M04413, FreshToast, 2023-10-18.) Crystal Powers Character SCRAPPED A character with crystal powers (M00138, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). Nothing further. The Dancer SCRAPPED Name mentioned, no additional details (M00187, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). Gojo Character SCRAPPED A Gojo (Jujutsu Kaisen) character that bends space (M00178, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). Gilgamesh Character EVOLVED Sits on a throne and shoots swords (M00178, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). The image evolved into the Telekinetic Sword Boss in the encounters doc (005 §Telekinetic Sword Boss). Villainous Berserker Spirit SCRAPPED Playable character with a loss-of-control mechanic: the player loses control sometimes and may even attack other players; could be instigated by certain actions (killing a certain enemy or using a certain combo); the player regains control by playing a certain melody (M00226, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-26). Referenced again in the systems doc (008). Vigilante Justice Concept EVOLVED Narrative concept, a “Light vs L thing (Death Note)”: two individuals — one follows the system, the other delivers justice equal to the crime (arm for an arm, eye for an eye, life for a life). “Light is a righteous likeable person that wishes to undo injustice… a guy doomed to a path of despair and a sad end”; people understand and relate to him, but “however righteous and great this light is. In the end, he is still wrong. System must stay” (M00037, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-03-13). The vigilante/pursuer idea later resurfaced in the Damian storyline, where a female vigilante/private investigator pursues him (M02701, M02769–M02770, FreshToast, 2023-02-23). Black and White Character SCRAPPED Meta-commentary concept: “They want boring, give them a character that is black and white” — a character who makes others black and white with his ability, leaning on what people want so “maybe they will grow bored of it and strive for something interesting” (M00234, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-22). Albedo + Gojo + Space-Time Character SCRAPPED From the Undying vs Heroes concept: 90% Albedo personality, 90% Gojo ability, with emphasis on space-time manipulation (M00241, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26). 002 notes this appears to be from an earlier/different game concept than the main Skyward Cinders. Related (owned elsewhere — not detailed here) The Undying vs Heroes concept also produced named characters: Leora (main protagonist, sun association, short-distance teleportation — M00668, FreshToast, 2021-08-16), and the name-mentions Aurelia and Orianna (M00281–M00332 region, 2021-08-01) (002 §Undying vs Heroes). The early “mentee” Cyrus EVOLVED into the Caelian Odyssey antagonist — see [[cyrus]] (002 §Cyrus, EVOLVED pointer). Zarek Reinhard — placeholder-named early version of [[thane|Thane]]; his profile lives in Thane’s design history (002 §Zarek Reinhard). Malenia/Leyla/Marie — name candidates for the character that became [[clara|Clara]] (002 §Malenia/Leyla/Marie). Design history 2021-03-13 — Vigilante Justice / Light-vs-L narrative concept (M00037, CrunchyConniptions). 2021-05-25 — archetype dump: necromancer with cute zombies (M00129), space/time chess-not-hack-n-slash (M00130), berserker/archer/lancer/rider/caster/swordmaster roster (M00131–M00136), neon power (M00137), crystal powers (M00138), Gojo space-bender and throne-sitting Gilgamesh (M00178), the Dancer (M00187) (CrunchyConniptions). 2021-05-26 — Villainous Berserker Spirit with melody-based control recovery (M00226); necromancer kill-order combat example (M00230) (CrunchyConniptions). 2021-06-22 — Black and White meta-commentary character (M00234, CrunchyConniptions). 2021-06-26 — Albedo + Gojo + Space-Time composite for Undying vs Heroes (M00241, CrunchyConniptions). — last new concept in this pile 2023-02-09 — necromancer seed regrows as Lyra the Necromancer (M01272–M01280, CrunchyConniptions). — evolution, recorded on [[lyra]] 2023-02-23 — vigilante/PI pursuer idea reused for the Damian storyline (M02701, M02769–M02770, FreshToast). — evolution Provenance Raw: M00037 (2021-03-13); M00129–M00138, M00178, M00187 (2021-05-25); M00226, M00230 (2021-05-26); M00234 (2021-06-22); M00241 (2021-06-26) — all CrunchyConniptions Cleaned: 000 Part 1 §Character Ideas Listed; lines 873–879, 1046–1052 regions Docs: 002 §Archived Early Concepts (Early Character Archetypes Parts 1–2); 005 §Telekinetic Sword Boss; 007 §Space-Time Manipulation; 008 (berserker-spirit and necromancer combat references) Ledger: rows “Character idea: …” dated 2021-03-13 through 2021-06-26 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (R01, R02)"},{"title":"Elara","group":"Characters","url":"characters/elara.html","text":"Elara PROPOSED A weary merchant of the Caelia marketplace who hooks the player into the self-aware Moonstone Mania collectathon. Summary — Elara is a “weary merchant” of the [[marketplace-caelia|Caelia marketplace]] who appears in an illustrative dialogue snippet written to demonstrate the tone of the [[moonstone-mania|Moonstone Mania]] quest (M05271, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). The player approaches her about a moonstone-hoarding scheme; she reacts with alarm (“Moonstones?! Are you mad?”), warns them off, then reluctantly hands over a starting stash to kick off the quest. She functions as a specific, named face of the game’s [[recurring-merchant|recurring merchant]] / [[shopkeeper-system|Shopkeeper]] role. As she originates in a “quick example” scene rather than finalized design, she is tagged PROPOSED . Personality Weary and dryly exasperated by the player’s “eccentric exploits” (M05271, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). Sardonic: “Moonstones?! Are you mad? Those things are rarer than a dragon with manners!” and “don’t come crying to me when you’re neck-deep in angry werewolves. Here’s your starting stash. Now get out there, you lunatic!” (M05271, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). Role Arc Quest-giver for Moonstone Mania: the player pitches a moonstone-collecting scheme (a riff on their old gem-hoarding exploits with sapphires, rubies and emeralds), and Elara reluctantly provides the starting stash that begins the quest (M05271, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). Located in the Caelia marketplace (M05271, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). See [[caelia]]. Related pages Elara is best understood as a named instance of the [[recurring-merchant|recurring merchant]] role; the shop mechanics themselves live in [[shopkeeper-system]]. The quest she opens: [[moonstone-mania]]. Open questions DEBATED Whether Elara is a finalized, canonical NPC or merely an example name used to pitch the quest’s tone — she appears only in the “quick example” scene (M05271, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13), and no later design work develops her. Design history 2024-02-13 — Elara introduced in a “quick example” scene demonstrating how the Moonstone Mania quest could be self-aware and funny about its own absurdity (M05271–M05272, CrunchyConniptions). She has no earlier or later appearances; the scene is her sole source. Provenance Raw: M05271–M05272 (CrunchyConniptions; 2024-02-13); context M05266–M05270 (Skyrim-reward / collectathon discussion, FreshToast CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13) Cleaned: 000 (Moonstone Mania example scene, merchant Elara) Docs: 002 §Elara (Moonstone quest NPC) Ledger: row R28 (“Named NPCs of the Moonstone quest: weary merchant Elara (in Caelia marketplace)…”) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Eris","group":"Characters","url":"characters/eris.html","text":"Eris CANON The conceited manipulator in [[lysander|Lysander]]'s party — the “evil milf” from [[isaac|Isaac]]'s background, funding despicable research to resurrect a lost loved one, until Lysander turns her to dust. Summary — Eris is the smart one manipulating Lysander: a conceited manipulator, very full of herself, member of Lysander’s party and the evil milf from Isaac’s background (M02201–M02203, M02225, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). After losing someone she turned cold in how far she’s gone to bring them back — her entire motivation is resurrecting a person she loves, not vengeance (M02242, 2023-02-16; M04668, FreshToast, 2023-10-24) — but she is mistaken: stolen life force cannot resurrect the dead, and ‘true resurrection’ is assumed impossible by any means (M02659, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). She is the obvious twist villain — only for the twist to turn back to Lysander, who betrays and slays her (M02201, M02204, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Identity Appearance Affinity: Fiery (002 §Eris; raw affinity list groups Fiery: Locke, Thane, Sable, Eris, Ajax). Name meaning: Eris is the Greek goddess of conflict and strife (M02324, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). “I would let her kill me” — 002 lists this under Eris as a design-goal quote (noting it was voiced as a design goal for Death); the raw anchor phrases it about Death’s design (M02349, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Personality Smart, manipulating Lysander (M02225, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Conceited manipulator; very full of herself (002 §Eris). Evil milf from Isaac’s background; member of Lysander’s party (M02202–M02203, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Background Motivations Has hands in funding despicable research — [[gideon-azurith|Gideon Azurith]] is doing research for Eris so she can use the power but sacrifice other people’s lifespan instead of her own (M02236, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Has a giant mansion (002 §Eris; the party heads to her mansion/villa to confront her — M02245–M02248, 2023-02-16). After losing someone, turned cold in how far she’s gone to bring them back; eventually goes off the deep end after realizing she’s gone too far and they’d never love her the way she is now (M02242, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Mistaken in the belief that stolen life force could possibly be used to resurrect the dead; ‘true resurrection’ is assumed impossible by any means (M02659, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Motivations: resurrect her lost loved one by any means necessary; prove that death can be conquered through knowledge and power; manipulate Lysander to gain [[ajax|Ajax]]'s power or make her own pact; fund research into life-force manipulation (via Gideon); go as far as needed, regardless of moral cost (002 §Eris). Goals: acquire Ajax’s power or a similar life-draining ability; complete research into “true resurrection”; use Lysander as a tool; gather resources and test subjects for experiments (002 §Eris). Flaws: grief has driven her to monstrous acts; cannot accept that death is final; arrogant belief that she can succeed where all others failed; her manipulative nature makes her underestimate genuine bonds; goes off the deep end when she realizes her loved one wouldn’t love what she’s become; conceited and full of herself; cannot admit her quest is futile (002 §Eris). Relationships Dynamics Ties to [[thane|Thane]] — perhaps he’s the one who tells her death is truly final (M02242–M02244 context, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). [[isaac|Isaac]] intends to confront her — from Ruby’s story end the party learns Eris funds the despicable research and heads to her giant mansion/villa; she tries to silence/hunt them, climaxing in a big fight; a Big W for Isaac (M02245–M02248, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Isaac’s Hermione-esque rival could later side with Isaac against Eris (M02723, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). Manipulating things to acquire Ajax’s power or make her own pact with the same spirit (M02201, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Role Arc Theme: healthy mourning vs going off the deep end (002 §Eris). Appears in the prologue with Lysander and [[locke|Locke]]: she and Lysander discuss gathering the [[chaos-emeralds|chaos emeralds]] to defeat the dragon or something (M02419, FreshToast, 2023-02-19); at the trial cavern she and Lysander assume their relic intel was false (M03670, FreshToast, 2023-09-10). One prologue sketch starts with Lysander’s party minus Eris (M02443, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). Could seem like the obvious twist villain, only to twist back to Lysander (M02204, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Eris finds out Lysander doesn’t care about the demon powers and tells him about soul/sacrifice; Lysander interrupts by grabbing her by the head — she’s immediately dust (M02282–M02283, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). The party tells Lysander about her in an effort to create division; he seems unphased, then for phase 2 “Lysander fucking magically eats his other party members” (M02249–M02255, 2023-02-16). The dragon’s spoken threat foreshadows this: the same attack [[vaenngrym|the dragon]] uses against the party, Lysander uses on Eris (M02271–M02276, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Symbolism notes Has to eat an apple dramatically, in reference to the apple of discord: Eris (Greek mythology) threw the apple of discord into the world, said it was for the “cutest, most uwu gril in the land,” and people fought over it, eventually causing a really nasty war. If Eris eats it herself, it symbolically implies an element of narcissism or conceitedness (M02317–M02322, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Name options considered (jokes): “Eris evilwizard,” “Eris cutebutwillbetrayyou,” “Eris Barkbarkawoo”; “pretty name” (M02331–M02333, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Open questions DEBATED Who does Eris want to resurrect? Crunchy wanted her selfish and hungry for power — “Just a bad person” (M02677, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22) — while the later note keeps her motivation as resurrecting a person she loves, not vengeance (M04668, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). DEBATED Whether she survives to a second, final confrontation: possibly survives the mansion fight and runs back to the hero; may be killed by Lysander after, or fights with him scarred and weakened (M02245–M02252, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-16). DEBATED Death staging: head-grab-to-dust (M02282–M02283) vs the alternate where Lysander says “It sings to me” and plunges his sword into her (M02270, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). DEBATED “Lyra or Eris is a vampire?” (M01398, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). Design history 2023-02-10 — earliest raw mention: “Lyra or Eris is a vampire?” (M01398, FreshToast) — still open. 2023-02-16 — main villain design session: manipulating things to acquire Ajax’s power or make her own pact, betrayed and slain by Lysander (M02201); identified as the evil milf from Isaac’s background and a member of Lysander’s party (M02202–M02203); double-twist structure (M02204); “Eris smart, manipulating him,” though if Lysander is dumb he gets too strong for her to handle (M02225–M02227); Gideon researching for her (M02236); grief motivation — turned cold after losing someone, goes off the deep end (M02242); mansion confrontation and survival debate (M02245–M02252); final-boss staging with the dust splash image (M02253–M02256, M02282–M02285); alternate “It sings to me” staging (M02270); apple-of-discord symbolism, name meaning, joke surnames (M02317–M02333). 2023-02-19 — prologue roles sketched: discusses gathering the chaos emeralds with Lysander (M02419); one sketch starts with Lysander’s party minus Eris (M02443, FreshToast). 2023-02-21 — resurrection lore locked: she is mistaken; ‘true resurrection’ is impossible by any means; the power can only take (M02659–M02660, FreshToast). 2023-02-22 — pushback on motive: Crunchy wanted her selfish and power-hungry, “Just a bad person” (M02677, CrunchyConniptions) — unresolved against the grief motive. 2023-02-25/28 — Isaac’s rival could side with him against Eris (M02723, FreshToast); villa level idea wit"},{"title":"Flynt","group":"Characters","url":"characters/flynt.html","text":"Flynt CANON One of [[lysander|Lysander]]'s two pawns — an orphaned, adoptive sibling of [[steele|Steele]], probably “straight up a himbo,” who wields the massive sword [[warpath|Warpath]] and dies tragically midway through the game. Summary — Flynt is one of the pair of orphaned, adoptive siblings who work personally for Lysander: largely loyal-to-a-fault, goofy pawns, mildly antagonistic at best (M02335–M02341, 2023-02-16; M03394, M03397, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). He is probably “straight up a himbo” (M03392, FreshToast, 2023-06-04) and CANON dies tragically midway through the game, after which his weapon passes — broken — to his sibling Steele (M03395, M04661, FreshToast). Abilities Traits CANON Wields [[warpath|Warpath]] — a massive sword “like Guts’ Dragonslayer” (Berserk reference) (M04661, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). Relationships Orphaned, adoptive sibling of [[steele|Steele]]; both work personally for [[lysander|Lysander]] (M03394, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Flynt/[[locke|Locke]] and [[thane|Thane]] are probably the avenues for the death/regret theme (M04658, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). Role Arc CANON Dies tragically midway through the game (M03395, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). A tentative tie exists to the spear/late-game conflict beats — his death “ could also be the same fight” as a spear-related fight (M05555, FreshToast, 2024-08-24; see 004). After his death, Steele picks up Warpath; the weapon is broken from then on and becomes a relic that can temporarily retake its complete form — carrying the influence of the dead visibly into the latter parts of the story (M04661, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). Design history 2023-02-16 — conceived unnamed: the remaining 2 of Lysander’s party are a pair of siblings, largely unimportant, loyal to a fault, goofy, probably not all that bad — pawns (M02335–M02341, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2023-06-04 — named Flynt ; “we at least have Flynt who’s probably a straight up himbo”; orphaned adoptive siblings working personally for Lysander; dies tragically midway through the game (M03392–M03397, FreshToast). 2023-10-24 — Warpath defined (Guts’ Dragonslayer-like sword); Steele picks it up broken after Flynt dies; Flynt/Locke named as a death/regret avenue (M04658, M04661, FreshToast). 2024-08-24 — tentative placement: his death could be the same fight as the spear beat (M05555, FreshToast) — matches the current presentation above. Provenance Raw: M02335–M02341 (2023-02-16); M03383–M03397 (2023-06-04); M04658, M04661 (2023-10-24); M05555 (2024-08-24) — FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions Cleaned: 000 (Lysander’s party siblings; Warpath) Docs: 002 §Flynt; 006 §Warpath; 004 (death/regret avenues) Ledger: rows R13 (sibling pawns), R25 (Warpath; regret avenues) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Gaius Wornwall","group":"Characters","url":"characters/gaius-wornwall.html","text":"Gaius Wornwall EVOLVED Chuunibyou animator-mage of the homunculus-era “loser heroes” party — a fired academic drowning in denial, later gender-swapped to female and eventually superseded. Summary — Gaius is the Resolve-stat party member of the early (pre-Skyward) JRPG concept, a lab-coated object-animator whose profile was explicitly flagged for revision: from “highly intelligent prodigy” to a chuunibyou in denial who blames others for his failures (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont). In the last direct planning of this roster he was “Gaius (but now girl)” (M01009, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-05). His signature dynamic — Reigen-and-Mob with [[ruby|Ruby]] — was later floated for [[isaac|Isaac]] and Ruby instead (M02646, FreshToast, 2023-02-21), and the whole entry now sits in 002’s Archived Early Concepts. Appearance Tall, lanky young person with wild black hair and piercing green eyes (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont). Often seen wearing a white lab coat, giving an eccentric appearance; playful grin, mischievous twinkle in the eye (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont). Personality Current (post-revision) direction, per the “Personality Changes Needed” notes (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont; M00934–M00949, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-05): Changed from “highly intelligent” to chuunibyou . In denial — “X is drowning in denial”. NOT loyal; instead blames others for his failures. Superseded original personality (kept for reference, marked “needs revision”): quick-witted with a sarcastic sense of humor, not afraid to make fun of those around him, can be aloof and distant, highly intelligent with a strong scientific mind, prone to overthinking and procrastination (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont). Background Motivations Original draft (flagged “needs revision”): child prodigy in magic; fascinated with magical abilities; discovered the ability to manipulate reality with his mind; struggles with the morality of his powers; goal to create a universal divination tool so everyone can recognize the majesty of the universe; motivated by a desire for recognition and to connect with people (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont; M00934–M00949, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-05). Changes demanded: he requires conflict — “write history of fuck up with blowup that leads to being canceled” (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont). That blowup was later pinned on [[lieutenant-milf|Lieutenant Milf]]: she tampered with his experiment (the one time he was right, it explodes, making him doubt himself) because she wanted him out / he knew too much — she has hands in both Ruby’s creation and Gaius’ expulsion (M01221, M01225, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Age: 21 (M01217–M01218, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Abilities Traits Associated with the Resolve stat in the four-stat party plan: [[thane|Thane]] (Toughness), Gaius-now-girl (Resolve), [[ruby|Ruby]] (Strength), [[issa-katgorl|Issa Katgorl]] (Finesse) (M01009, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-05). Can animate objects — canonically demonstrated on a brick with wings (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont). Relationships Gaius and Ruby are like Reigen and Mob (M01189, FreshToast, 2023-02-08); “Gaius Ruby” also appears in the pairing structure notes (M01223–M01231, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Relationship with Himbo not established (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont; M01223–M01231, 2023-02-09). Role Arc BRICKWINGS story arc (M01176–M01188, FreshToast, 2023-02-08): the party needs to throw a brick but can’t throw it far enough; Gaius animates it and it grows wings; the brick flies through the window but escapes — it is now free. Later the party finds the brick again and Ruby berates Gaius for not taking care of it. The brick joins the party, is wholesome and beloved for a couple of scenes, then dies comically — a literal throwaway joke. Ruby relates to the brick. Longer arc: Gaius slowly shifts from delusional obsession to working for actual practical change in the academy; [[locke|Locke]]'s reason to visit the academy is that it’s core to Gaius and somewhat related to Ruby (M01226–M01227, M01233, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Open questions DEBATED Surname never settled. Options discussed: Wornwall, Ora’mo-ree, Qiundeldore, Devmont, Penecillin, Doppleheimer, “Thane Gaius” (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont; M00934–M00949, 2023-02-05). A separate brainstorm added Monroe (“Gaius Monroe”) among others (M01235–M01248, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) — none adopted. DEBATED The “now girl” change (M01009) was never given a full re-profile; the archived text still reads male. Design history 2023-02-05 — original profile: tall lanky young man, lab coat, magic child prodigy, reality-manipulation, universal-divination-tool goal; surname options brainstormed in the same session (M00934–M00949, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-05 — revision notes attached almost immediately: chuunibyou instead of intelligent, in denial, blames others, needs a cancel-worthy blowup in his history (002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont). 2023-02-05 — stat lineup lists “Gaius (but now girl), resolve” — changed to female (M01009, CrunchyConniptions). — supersedes the male presentation above 2023-02-08 — BRICKWINGS arc written; Reigen/Mob dynamic with Ruby established (M01176–M01189, FreshToast). 2023-02-09 — age set to 21 (M01217–M01218); Lieutenant Milf made the author of his expulsion (M01221, M01225); academy arc: delusional obsession → practical change (M01226–M01227); second surname brainstorm (M01235–M01248). 2023-02-21 — the Reigen/Mob dynamic is floated for Isaac and Ruby instead (“Isaac and Ruby have a dynamic inspired by Reigen and Mob?”, M02646, FreshToast); Gaius does not appear in the 2023-02-16 party lineup (Thane/Locke/Sable/Issac, M02164) and the profile was archived in 002. — character superseded Provenance Raw: M00934–M00949, M01006–M01017 (CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-05); M01176–M01189 (2023-02-08); M01217–M01248 (2023-02-09); M02646 (2023-02-21) Cleaned: 000 lines 1409–1451 region (Gaius profile); Part 7 (BRICKWINGS); Part 5 (academy arc, ages, Milf tampering) Docs: 002 §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont (Archived Early Concepts); 009 §Appendix — Considered Dropped (surname brainstorm) Ledger: rows matching “Gaius” in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (R06, R07)"},{"title":"Gideon Azurith","group":"Characters","url":"characters/gideon-azurith.html","text":"Gideon Azurith PROPOSED Ruby’s creator: a born psychopath with an acquired messiah complex who literally converts people into tools — harvesting outlaws’ souls into golems to “progress society.” Summary — Gideon Azurith is the alchemist who created the homunculi [[ruby|Ruby]] (with a borrowed soul — incorrectly) and [[citrine|Citrine]] (with his own — correctly) (M02178–M02179, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). A highly utilitarian pragmatist, he sees society’s weaknesses as things to be corrected by any means: he sends Citrine to gather criminals and outlaws, harvests their souls into golems, and justifies it because the golems help people (M02173–M02177, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). He is also doing research for [[eris|Eris]] so she can use her power while sacrificing other people’s lifespans instead of her own (M02234–M02238, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Ruby ultimately spares him, in Citrine’s name (M02077, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Name identity Gideon means greater warrior/destroyer — a biblical name that plays into his messiah complex (M02192, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Azurith is a portmanteau of Azur + Lith: azur means blue, lith means stone — completing the primary color cycle with Ruby Citrine (M02192, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Appearance Wears “the whore gloves” (wristless gloves) (M02185–M02186, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). In the background of his visual, there are clipped fairy wings (M02184, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16) — associated with the creator/golem-origin imagery. Personality Highly utilitarian pragmatist; sees weaknesses and flaws in society as something to be corrected by any means (M02175–M02177, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Born a psychopath; acquired a messiah complex after witnessing the mistreatment of others and society’s inefficiencies (M02175–M02177, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Background Motivations Justifies his actions by sending golems to help people; the golems progress society along with Citrine (M02173–M02174, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). The golem economy: sends Citrine to gather criminals and outlaws, harvests their souls to be converted into golems — the literal conversion of people into tools (M02173–M02174, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Homunculus creation requires sacrificing a portion of your own flesh and soul; Gideon borrowed the soul of another when creating Ruby, but performed the ritual correctly when creating Citrine (M02178–M02179, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Doing research for [[eris|Eris]] — so she can use the power but sacrifice other people’s lifespan instead of her own (M02234–M02238, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Relationships Creator and “absent father” of [[ruby|Ruby]], who seeks his approval; he deemed her a failed prototype and gave her away (002 §Lalateya/Ruby). Creator of [[citrine|Citrine]], who is loyal to and cared for by him (002 §Citrine). Spared by Ruby, in Citrine’s name, after the sisters’ duel (M02077, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Role Arc Ruby’s creator and antagonist; connected to Eris’s research (002 §Gideon Azurith). Thematic placement in the villain roster: Gideon, [[lysander|Lysander]] and the other human characters do cruel/awful things mostly from human flaws — in contrast to [[raphael|Raphael]], the story’s inhuman true tragic monster acting from raw evil hatred (M05230–M05247, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Open questions DEBATED 002 files him under Archived Early Concepts, but he was still being used as a benchmark human villain in February 2024 (M05230) — whether Gideon survives into the current iteration’s antagonist roster is unresolved. Design history 2023-02-05 — exists only as Ruby’s unnamed creator in her backstory: deemed her a failed prototype, gave her to lower-tier mages (M00950–M01001 region, CrunchyConniptions; 002 §Lalateya/Ruby). 2023-02-15 — the sisters’ arc ends with Ruby sparing “her creator” in Citrine’s name (M02077, FreshToast). 2023-02-16 — full character session: named Gideon Azurith with the warrior/messiah + blue-stone etymology (M02192); psychopath-with-messiah-complex characterization (M02175–M02177); golem soul-harvest economy (M02173–M02174); ritual details for Ruby vs Citrine (M02178–M02179); whore gloves and clipped fairy wings visuals (M02184–M02186); research-for-Eris plot link (M02234–M02238). — this is the fullest version and the page’s current presentation 2024-02-10 — cited as an example of human-flaw villainy in contrast to Raphael’s inhuman evil (M05230–M05247, FreshToast); profile filed under Archived Early Concepts in 002. Provenance Raw: M00950–M01001 region (2023-02-05, unnamed creator); M02077 (2023-02-15); M02173–M02192, M02234–M02238 (2023-02-16); M05230–M05247 (2024-02-10) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 / lines 3428–3452 region Docs: 002 §Gideon Azurith (Ruby’s Creator) (Archived Early Concepts); 004 (Eris research link); 001 line 488 region (homunculus rule) Ledger: rows matching “Gideon” in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (R12, R28)"},{"title":"Guardian of Relic","group":"Characters","url":"characters/guardian-of-relic.html","text":"Guardian of Relic DEBATED A potential character who guards the relic Ajax left behind to contain his own power — one of two competing explanations for where that relic is when the finale needs it. Summary — In the [[lysander|Lysander]] 2nd-phase finale plan, [[ajax|Ajax]] “prepared for this moment” and left behind a relic that had helped him contain his own power. That relic “is with [[locke|Locke]] or another character that we have to create that is the guardian of the relic” (M02887, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-03). The Guardian only exists as one branch of this unresolved either/or — hence DEBATED, and the character was never named or designed. Role Arc If created, the Guardian holds Ajax’s containing relic until the Lysander 2nd-phase fight, where the relic lets the curse’s power be defeated (M02887, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-03; see the [[ajax|Ajax]] silhouette scene). Open questions DEBATED Who carries the relic? Options in the same message (M02887, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-03): The relic is with Locke; A new guardian-of-the-relic character is created; Alternative: “it was on lysander the whole time and suppressed him without his knowledge but it fell from him or was somehow broken.” No later message resolves this. Design history 2023-03-03 — sole mention, inside the Lysander 2nd-phase finale outline: relic with Locke, OR a to-be-created guardian character, OR unknowingly on Lysander the whole time (M02887, CrunchyConniptions). Provenance Raw: M02887 (CrunchyConniptions; 2023-03-03) Cleaned: 000 (Lysander 2nd phase outline) Docs: 002 §Guardian of Relic (potential character) Ledger: see the Lysander 2nd-phase / relic rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Isaac","group":"Characters","url":"characters/isaac.html","text":"Isaac DEBATED Chuuni scholar-mage of the main-game party and one of the four heroes actually implemented in the proto — but renamed Percy in the notes, with “Isaac” freed for the rival character; roster placement is undecided. Summary — Isaac is the main-game party’s staff-wielding mage (002 §Isaac), a “Chuuni Wizard” (M01905, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14) whose stated goals are to “become the super awesomest ultimate mage ever” and to “find out why he was canceled” (M02344, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). In the notes he was renamed Percy (“What if Isaac was called Percy?… We could use Isaac for his rival character instead” — M02784/M02786, FreshToast, 2023-02-25; reconfirmed “Isaac is Percy”, M03526, FreshToast, 2023-08-12) — see [[percival|Percival]]. He remains a separate page because, against the rename, Isaac (not Percy) is one of the four heroes built in the proto’s vertical slice (Locke, Thane, Isaac, Ruby) (002 §Isaac), so which name the main-game mage carries is DEBATED . Name / Identity CANON Chuuni wizard / scholar-mage of the main-game party; weapon: Staff (magic) (002 §Isaac, citing Part 8). EVOLVED Renamed Percy/Percival in Part 10 (M02784–M02788, FreshToast, 2023-02-25), freeing “Isaac” for his rival character; “Isaac is Percy and falafal is a foodstuff” (M03526, FreshToast, 2023-08-12). Current lore presentation of the renamed mage lives at [[percival|Percival]]. Abilities Traits Mobility: Thane and Isaac “struggle to move and are more specialised” (M01874, FreshToast, 2023-02-13); “characters like Thane and Isaac are probably really static and don’t like to move” (M02578, FreshToast, 2023-02-20). Exploration brainstorm (heavily debated system): “Isaac has an exploration skill that allows him to see/spot illusory walls” (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11); “Some dungeon you want to use Issac and others you want to use sable… Issac can see secrets better but the torch reach is shortened heavily” (M01448, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11). [CORRECTED to match code] The notes’ “1st line (Front)” label (M01399, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-10) is superseded: the proto implements Isaac as a back-line mage, start position 3 (Back) — a 24-HP glass cannon belongs in the back (002 §Isaac; proto: src/core/combat/content.ts ). CANON — IMPLEMENTED Proto combat values: id isaac , role “Sorcerer”, glyph 🔥, maxHp 24 (squishiest hero), maxAp 3, speed 5, str 4 / mag 16 (highest) / def 5 / res 10 / acc 90 / crit 10 (002 §Isaac; proto: src/core/combat/content.ts , src/core/combat/types.ts ). CANON — IMPLEMENTED Four skills, all isMagic (blocked by Silence), reaching ranks 1–3 (002 §Isaac; proto: src/core/combat/content.ts ): Spark (2 AP) — bolt of magic, 9 mag. Frostbind (2 AP) — 7 mag + Speed Down. Shock (2 AP) — 6 mag + Blind. Inferno (4 AP, finisher ) — fire across the whole enemy line, 10 mag to all. [NOTE] His implemented element is mixed — fire, cold, and lightning; the code comment says “Fiery / electric damage”, consistent with the generalist chuuni-mage framing (002 §Isaac). Relationships CANON Pairs with [[ruby|Ruby]] in a Reigen/Mob dynamic — “Isaac and Ruby have a dynamic inspired by Reigen and Mob?” (M02646, FreshToast, 2023-02-21); the pairing was originally tagged to “Gaius Ruby” (M01189, FreshToast, 2023-02-08). Has a Hermione-esque rival — “smart, straightforward but hardworking rather than talented”, who “could side with Isaac against Eris” (M02721–M02723, FreshToast, 2023-02-25); named [[rhea-falafill|Rhea Falafill]] (M02835, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-27). After the rename this is Percy’s rival. Background Motivations CANON “Become the super awesomest ultimate mage ever” and “find out why he was canceled” (M02344, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Role Arc CANON Following from Ruby’s story, Isaac learns [[eris|Eris]] has been funding despicable research; the party heads to Eris’s giant mansion where Isaac intends to confront her (she is the “evil milf from Isaac’s background”); “Big W for Isaac this way” (M02245–M02247, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-16; 002 §Isaac). Along the way, the party uncovers the truth behind the hero’s power — what he was close to uncovering previously (002 §Isaac). Open questions DEBATED Roster placement (002 §Isaac): options are (1) develop fully as the main-game party mage with the Eris arc, (2) merge with another frontline character, (3) convert to an NPC informing on Eris, (4) remove if redundant. Concrete weight toward “keep”: Isaac is implemented in the proto and treated as a live playable pick (M01448, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11), while the rename (M02784–M02788) points to Percy carrying the mage identity in lore. The 2024 team-pairing roster lists Percy , not Isaac (M05392, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). DEBATED Whether the freed “Isaac” name actually ends up on the rival character (M02786, FreshToast, 2023-02-25) — the rival was subsequently named Rhea Falafill (M02835), so the freed name has no confirmed new owner. Design history 2023-02-10 — first roster appearance: Isaac listed 1st line with Thane and Sable (M01399, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-11 — exploration niche: spots illusory walls / sees secrets at the cost of torch reach (M01422, spirit_noodle; M01448, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-13/20 — mobility identity: static, struggles to move, specialised (M01874, FreshToast; M02578, FreshToast). 2023-02-14 — “Issac - Chuuni Wizard Friend” in the SCRAPPED male-protagonist fork (M01905, CrunchyConniptions); Thane’s surname changed to Farrow specifically “to differentiate him from Isaac” (M01923, FreshToast). 2023-02-16 — motivations set: canceled + awesomest mage (M02344, CrunchyConniptions); Eris-confrontation arc, “Big W for Isaac this way” (M02245–M02247, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-02-21 — Reigen/Mob dynamic with Ruby (M02646, FreshToast). 2023-02-25 — Hermione-esque rival brainstormed (M02721–M02723, FreshToast); rename proposed: Isaac → Percy , “I don’t really like Isaac much for a party member”, Isaac freed for the rival (M02784–M02788, FreshToast; “percy is cool”, M02789, spirit_noodle) — supersedes “Isaac” as the mage’s name; see [[percival|Percival]]. 2023-08-12 — rename reconfirmed: “Isaac is Percy and falafal is a foodstuff” (M03526, FreshToast); rival documented as Rhea Falafill, “Isaac’s Nemesis” (M03519, CrunchyConniptions). — current state: the lore mage is Percy; the proto still implements him as isaac . Provenance Raw: M01189, M01399, M01422, M01448, M01874, M01905, M01923, M02245–M02247, M02344, M02578, M02646, M02721–M02723, M02784–M02789, M03519, M03526 (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast, spirit_noodle; 2023-02-08 → 2023-08-12) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 (weapon, Eris arc), Part 10 (rival, rename) Docs: 002 §Isaac (Main-game party mage); proto src/core/combat/content.ts , src/core/combat/types.ts (implemented kit) Ledger: character rows R11 (M01923 Farrow rename), R12 (M02344 motivation; M02245–M02248 Eris arc), R15 (M02721–M02723 rival; M02784–M02789 rename), R19 (M03526 “Isaac is Percy”), meta row R14 (M02646 Reigen/Mob) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Issa Katgorl","group":"Characters","url":"characters/issa-katgorl.html","text":"Issa Katgorl SCRAPPED The Finesse/Luck slot of the homunculus-era party — a name and a stat, never developed further. Summary — Issa Katgorl was the fourth member of the early four-stat party plan: [[thane|Thane]] (Toughness), [[gaius-wornwall|Gaius]] but now girl (Resolve), [[ruby|Ruby]] (Strength), Issa Katgorl (Finesse) (M01009, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-05). She received no profile beyond her combat role and does not appear in later party lineups; 002 files her under Archived Early Concepts. Role Function Frontline with emphasis on finesse: “After that Issa Katgorl and somebody would emphasis finesse” (M01008, FreshToast, 2023-02-05). Abilities Traits Associated with the Finesse stat (M01009, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-05). “Katgorl would be our luck character” (M01011, FreshToast, 2023-02-05). Design history 2023-02-05 — created in the party stat-assignment session as the Finesse/Luck member (M01008–M01011, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-16 — absent from the next party lineup (“Thane-Melee-Spear, Locke-Melee-Sword, Sable-Ranged-Bow, Issac-Magic-Staff”, M02164, CrunchyConniptions); never mentioned again and archived in 002. — scrapped Provenance Raw: M01008–M01011 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-05); absence anchor M02164 (2023-02-16) Cleaned: 000 lines 1505, 1513–1516 region (party stat assignments) Docs: 002 §Issa Katgorl (Archived Early Concepts), incl. the party-composition note in §Gaius Wornwall/Devmont Ledger: row “Party stat assignments: Thane (Toughness), Gaius-now-girl (Resolve), Ruby (Strength), Issa Katgorl (Finesse/Luck)” in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (R06)"},{"title":"Leon Erhardt","group":"Characters","url":"characters/leon.html","text":"Leon Erhardt DEBATED Fugitive back-line rogue on the run from the law — quick-witted, paranoid, and hunted by a cop who becomes his romance arc — though the hunted-romance premise was later re-attributed to [[damian|Damian]]. Summary — Leon Erhardt, 22, is a main-party back-liner defined by being hunted (M01394, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-10): a fugitive for a crime he may or may not have committed, guarded and roguish, hiding genuine kindness under bravado (002 §Leon). His signature plot is the cat-and-mouse with a cop/detective hunter that “maybe finally turns into a romance plot? Hardcore character development for both” (M01396, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). His name evolved Leonard → Leon (“leonard is a nerd. Leon is a lion.” — M02066, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-15). Status is DEBATED because the pursued-by-an-investigator romance was later attached to [[damian|Damian]] instead (M02701, FreshToast, 2023-02-23), and Leon is absent from the latest (2024) team-pairing roster — see Open questions. Name / Identity Full name: Leon Erhardt (M02079, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Age: 22 (002 §Leon). Originally Leonard ; changed because Crunchy kept stumbling on it — “leonard is a nerd. Leon is a lion.” (M02066, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-15); “Leonard also means lion” so the change “keeps the meaningful part of the name” (M02067, M02065, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Affinity: not specified; the doc’s “suggest Electric or Cold” is inference — PROPOSED (002 §Leon). He does not appear in the known-affinities list (M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). Personality Initially guarded and paranoid due to being hunted; quick-witted with a sharp tongue — humor as defense mechanism (002 §Leon). Brave but reckless; loyal to a fault once trust is earned; “roguish charm” over genuine kindness; struggles to be vulnerable or ask for help (002 §Leon). Has social anxiety , played against his decoy skill for comedy (M01833, FreshToast, 2023-02-12). Tagged “Leonard - Shy but Capable Friend” in the scrapped male-protagonist party-order fork (M01905, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14) SCRAPPED . Background Motivations Being hunted by law enforcement for a crime he may or may not have committed; backstory suggests he was framed or took the fall for someone else; has been on the run living by his wits (M01394, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-10; 002 §Leon). One of the hunters is on [[sable|Sable]]'s list — creates conflict when Sable wants the bounty (M01395, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-10). Motivations (002 §Leon): clear his name, protect those he cares about from the crossfire, prove he’s more than a criminal, find a place to stop running. Flaws (002 §Leon): paranoid and slow to trust; lies “for others’ protection”; reckless when cornered; sees himself as a burden; unprocessed trauma from being hunted. Abilities Traits Party position: 2nd line (Back) (M01399, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-10). Best mobility in the party, at high AP cost (M01876, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-13); he and Sable “have an easy time moving with their skills and have more modular skills” (M01874, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). Decoy skill: “Leonard can create a decoy, rather than turn invisible. The decoy is occasionally used for comedic effect, in tandem with his social anxiety” (M01833, FreshToast, 2023-02-12). Exploration brainstorms (heavily debated system): “Leonard should be able to light up a larger area with his magic spells” (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11); might let you notice traps earlier — “I’m not really sold though” (M01517, FreshToast, 2023-02-11); could give [[locke|Locke]] a dash on a cooldown (M01683, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Street-smart; skilled in stealth and misdirection; throwing weapons / ranged combat; underground contacts from his time on the run (002 §Leon). Relationships Hunted by a detective/cop who becomes his romantic interest : “Leonard hunted by a cop, maybe finally turns into a romance plot? Hardcore character development for both” (M01396, FreshToast, 2023-02-10); “Mostly comedic, eventually romantic plot” (M01397, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). The detective begins questioning whether Leon is actually guilty (002 §Leon). Bounty tension with [[sable|Sable]] (M01395, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-10). Role Arc (002 §Leon, expanding the M01394–M01397 premise) Early: joins the party as a temporary alliance; keeps everyone at arm’s length; comic relief through paranoia. Mid: the hunt intensifies; Sable discovers the bounty; forced to open up when the party protects him anyway; investigates who actually committed the crime. Romance subplot: cat-and-mouse → mutual respect → attraction; detective finds inconsistencies in his case; they uncover the real culprit together. Resolution: name cleared; decides whether to rejoin society or keep adventuring; learns to trust and be vulnerable. Emotional beats: the party chooses him over bounty money; he confesses what he’s running from; confrontation with his framer; the detective chooses him over duty. Plot impact: fugitive status creates external pressure; underground contacts feed the party information; parallels Locke’s need to prove herself worthy. Open questions DEBATED Who owns the hunted-romance plot? Later messages attach the identical premise to [[damian|Damian]]: “Damian is pursued by some vigilante/private investigator but we actually meet her first who then leads us to Damian” (M02701, FreshToast, 2023-02-23), and the by-the-book hunter archetype “better serves the girl hunting Damian down… Which then results in a romance plot” (M02769–M02770, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). No message explicitly retires Leon’s version, so it is unresolved whether the plot migrated to Damian or two pursuit plots briefly coexisted. Doc 002 keeps the detective romance on Leon; recency favors Damian. DEBATED Affinity unassigned (absent from M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). DEBATED Late-roster absence: the 2024 team-skill pairing list (Locke, Ruby/Thane, Percy/Damian, Astrid/Sable) does not include Leon (M05392, FreshToast, 2024-04-11); no removal was ever stated. Design history 2023-02-10 — original concept: “Leonard is being hunted” (M01394, CrunchyConniptions); a hunter is on Sable’s list (M01395); hunted by a cop, turning into a romance plot with hardcore development for both, mostly comedic then romantic (M01396–M01397, FreshToast); slotted 2nd line with Ruby and Clara (M01399, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-11/12 — kit sketches: lights up a larger torch area (M01422, spirit_noodle); trap-noticing floated but “not really sold” (M01517, FreshToast); decoy instead of invisibility , comedic with his social anxiety (M01833, FreshToast, 2023-02-12). 2023-02-13 — mobility identity: easy mover with modular skills (M01874, FreshToast); best mobility at high AP cost (M01876, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-14 — SCRAPPED male-protagonist fork tags him “Shy but Capable Friend”; fork rejected as the current party order is mechanically justified (M01905, M01914–M01915, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Crunchy asks to rename because “lee oh nard” is hard to keep straight (M01907–M01913). 2023-02-15 — rename Leonard → Leon approved: “leonard is a nerd. Leon is a lion” / “Leonard also means lion” (M02062–M02068, FreshToast/spirit_noodle/CrunchyConniptions) — supersedes Leonard ; surname set in “Locke Evertide / Leon Erhardt” (M02079, FreshToast). 2023-02-23/25 — the pursued-by-investigator romance premise is voiced for Damian instead (M02701, 2023-02-23; M02769–M02770, 2023-02-25, FreshToast) — conflicts with the 2023-02-10 Leon version; unresolved, see Open questions. Provenance Raw: M01394–M01399, M01422, M01517, M01683, M01833, M01874, M01876, M01905–M01915, M02062–M02068, M02079, M02701, M02769–M02770 (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast, spirit_noodle; 2023-02-10 → 2023-02-25) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 (renames), Part 10 (Damian pursuit conflict) Docs: 002 §Leon (Leon Erhardt); 007 §Exploration Skills (decoy, light) Ledger: character rows R10 (M01833 decoy), R11 (M01874/M01876 mobility; M02062–M02068"},{"title":"Leora","group":"Characters","url":"characters/leora.html","text":"Leora SCRAPPED An early-concept protagonist — “the outcast” — with a sun association and short-range teleportation, who ultimately survived not as a character but as the game’s silhouette/emblem. Summary — Leora was one of the earliest (2021) protagonist candidates for the project. She was framed as “the outcast,” a main protagonist character with a sun association and the ability to teleport short distances with some regularity , tied to sword-throwing/teleportation combat (M00317, FreshToast, 2021-08-01; M00668, FreshToast, 2021-08-16). The name did not carry into the later Skyward Cinders / Caelian Odyssey casts; its most durable legacy is as the game’s emblem/silhouette and a lingering “sun association” the team later tried to protect when naming other characters. Appearance Design legacy Became the basis for the game’s silhouette / Leora emblem , with multiple design attempts explored (M00809, FreshToast, 2021-08-26). (Emblem/silhouette design work belongs to [[art-direction]].) Floated to have magical skates, Shadow the Hedgehog style (M00829, FreshToast, 2021-08-31). Abilities Traits Sun association (002 §Undying vs Heroes / Leora; echoed in the Cyrus surname debate, M00609, FreshToast, 2021-08-15). Short-distance teleportation with some regularity: “Teleportation could just be a thing Leora can do with some regularity, albeit really short distance” (M00668, FreshToast, 2021-08-16), associated with sword-throwing/teleport mechanics. Had magical capabilities generally (002 §Undying vs Heroes / Leora). Role Arc Named as “Leora, the outcast” in an early character-name brainstorm (M00317, FreshToast, 2021-08-01). Cast as a main protagonist character in the early “Albedo + Gojo + space-time” / Undying vs Heroes concept (002 §Undying vs Heroes / Leora). This is flagged in the source as “from an earlier/different game concept than the main Skyward Cinders.” Relationships Her sun association was invoked against the proposed surname “Cyrus Sunworth” — FreshToast’s “sole issue” was that “Sunworth” “takes away somewhat from Leora’s sun association” (M00609, FreshToast, 2021-08-15). See [[cyrus-sunworth]]. Open questions DEBATED Whether “Leora” was ever intended to persist as a character or only as the emblem/silhouette name is not resolved in the notes; after 2021 she appears only as the emblem and as a naming-clash reference point. Design history 2021-08-01 — name proposed in a brainstorm list: “Leora, the outcast” (M00317, FreshToast). 2021-08-15 — Leora’s sun association treated as established, used to argue against the “Sunworth” surname for the mentee Cyrus (M00609, FreshToast). 2021-08-16 — abilities sketched: short-distance teleportation “with some regularity,” tied to sword-throw/teleport combat (M00668, FreshToast). 2021-08-26 — repurposed into the game’s silhouette / “Leora emblem,” with several design attempts (M00809, FreshToast) — the character concept effectively gives way to the emblem here. 2021-08-31 — last mention: “What if Leora had magical skates Shadow the Hedgehog style?” (M00829, FreshToast). The name does not recur in the later Skyward Cinders / Caelian Odyssey casts. Superseded / scrapped as a character; emblem legacy documented in [[art-direction]]. Provenance Raw: M00317 (FreshToast, 2021-08-01), M00609 (FreshToast, 2021-08-15), M00668 (FreshToast, 2021-08-16), M00809 (FreshToast, 2021-08-26), M00829 (FreshToast, 2021-08-31) Cleaned: 000 Part 1 §Character Profiles (Leora); 000 Part 1 §Magic and Skill System (Teleportation); 000 §Character Design - Leora Emblem/Silhouette Work Docs: 002 §Undying vs Heroes (Leora) Ledger: early-concept character rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Lieutenant Milf","group":"Characters","url":"characters/lieutenant-milf.html","text":"Lieutenant Milf PROPOSED A calculating, manipulative military lieutenant who contrasts [[locke|Locke]]'s positive relationship with her party — and has hands in [[ruby|Ruby]]'s creation and [[gaius-wornwall|Gaius]]'s expulsion. Summary — Lieutenant Milf is a mid-tier antagonist conceived in the first antagonist roster: “Lieutenant Milf - contrasts Locke’s positive relationship with her party. Manipulator?” (M01219, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Her only raw-anchored plot ties are that she has hands in Ruby’s creation and Gaius’s expulsion — she tampered with Gaius’s experiment (the one time he was actually right, it explodes, making him doubt himself) because she wanted him out / he knew too much (M01221, M01225, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). “Milf” is a design-aesthetic goal and fan nickname, not a finalized name — the character was never named. Identity Title: Lieutenant (military rank); nickname “Milf” (design goal/fan nickname) (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Affinity: not specified (002 suggests Cold — calculating nature). Personality Calculating and manipulative; maintains a veneer of professionalism and respectability; a charismatic leader who inspires loyalty (through manipulation); ruthlessly ambitious; views people as tools and resources; confident and composed under pressure; uses charm and authority interchangeably; lacks true empathy — sees emotions as weaknesses to exploit (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Background Motivations High-ranking military or organizational leader (002 §Lieutenant Milf). PROPOSED — inference, not in source Connected to [[eris|Eris]]'s research network. Has hands in multiple unethical projects; built her career through manipulation and taking credit for others’ work; sees herself as a pragmatist doing “necessary” things (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Motivations: climb to ultimate power and authority; maintain control over her subordinates and operations; PROPOSED — inference, not in source advance research into life manipulation (via [[gideon-azurith|Gideon]]/Eris connection); eliminate threats to her position or reputation; prove that ruthless pragmatism is superior to idealism (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Goals: expand her influence and network; complete research projects regardless of ethical costs; silence or discredit anyone who opposes her; maintain a facade of legitimacy while operating in shadows (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Flaws: overconfident in her ability to control everyone; cannot comprehend genuine loyalty or friendship (sees Locke’s party as weakness); underestimates those she views as beneath her; her manipulations create many enemies; lacks a backup plan for when manipulation fails; arrogance prevents her from seeing threats until too late (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Relationships Dynamics Contrasts Locke: where Locke leads through genuine care and positive relationships, the Lieutenant leads through manipulation and fear (M01219, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Ruby connection: has hands in Ruby’s creation or the experiments that created her (M01221, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Gaius connection: responsible for Gaius’s expulsion from the academy/organization — she tampered with his experiment to get him expelled (M01221, M01225, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Creates a negative relationship with the party through her past actions; may have subordinates who eventually turn against her (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Background connections Has hands in Ruby’s creation and Gaius’s expulsion — “Milf has hands in Ruby’s creation and Gaius’ expulsion” (M01221, FreshToast, 2023-02-09); “Milf tampered with Gaius experiment. ONe time he was actually right, it explodes making him doubt himself. Tampered because wanted to kick him out. Knew too much?” (M01225, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). PROPOSED — inference, not in source Connected to Eris and Gideon’s research network. Funded or facilitated unethical experiments; used her position to cover up scandals and silence whistleblowers; built her reputation on others’ achievements (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Abilities Traits Tactically brilliant — a skilled strategist; commands resources and subordinates; political savvy and connections; combat capable but prefers to let others do the fighting; uses a combination of authority, blackmail, and charm to control people; access to experimental technology or magic from research projects (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Role Arc Role in story: mid-tier antagonist representing corrupt authority; foil to Locke’s leadership style; connection point between plot threads (Ruby, Gaius, Eris) (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Confrontation: the party must expose her crimes or defeat her forces; her subordinates may turn on her when the truth is revealed; defeated not through raw power but by her manipulations unraveling; contrast of strong leader vs good leader (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Potential outcomes: arrested/disgraced when evidence comes to light; killed by those she manipulated; escapes to become a recurring problem; final words justify her actions as “necessary” (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Emotional beats: revelation of her role in Ruby’s and Gaius’s suffering; a moment where her manipulation attempt fails against genuine friendship; a subordinate choosing the party over her orders; her confident facade cracking when she loses control (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Plot impact: connects Ruby’s origin story to a larger conspiracy; explains Gaius’s background and expulsion; represents institutional corruption and abuse of power; her defeat shows the power of genuine leadership vs manipulation; PROPOSED — inference, not in source bridge between early adventures and the larger conspiracy (Eris/Gideon); thematic contrast to Locke’s growth as leader (002 §Lieutenant Milf). [Provenance note] Raw/000 tie Lieutenant Milf only to Ruby’s creation and Gaius’s expulsion (and to “Manipulator?” / contrast with Locke). The Eris/Gideon “research network” / “larger conspiracy” links above are inference and are tagged PROPOSED accordingly. (M01219, M01221, M01225, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) Notes Character design should be striking and memorable; “Milf” is a design aesthetic goal, not a character-defining trait; should be respected as an antagonist, not reduced to appearance (002 §Lieutenant Milf). Open questions The character was never given an actual name ( placeholder_name: true ). “Manipulator?” — even her core trait was posed as a question in the original roster (M01219, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Design history 2023-02-09 — conceived in the first antagonist roster: “Lieutenant Milf - contrasts Locke’s positive relationship with her party. Manipulator?” (M01219, FreshToast). 2023-02-09 — plot ties established the same day: hands in Ruby’s creation and Gaius’s expulsion; tampered with Gaius’s experiment because she wanted him out / he knew too much (M01221, M01225, FreshToast) — matches the current presentation above. No further raw development since. Provenance Raw: M01219, M01221, M01225 (FreshToast, 2023-02-09) Cleaned: 000 Part 5 (Milf tampered with his experiment) Docs: 002 §Lieutenant Milf Ledger: row R07 (Lieutenant Milf / Ruby creation Gaius expulsion) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Locke Evertide","group":"Characters","url":"characters/locke.html","text":"Locke Evertide CANON Main protagonist of Skyward Cinders: a 22-year-old village nobody with a Fiery affinity who earns everything she has, wields the spear Sunderer, and ends the story as the true heir of [[ajax|Ajax]] — not [[lysander|Lysander]]. Summary — Locke (surname Evertide , suggested 2023-02-15 — M02079, FreshToast) is the leader of the player party. She starts as an inexperienced “small fish in a big pond” in awe of the hero Lysander, and her arc is the shift “from hero pleasing to people saving” (002 §Locke). Mechanically her strength is leadership and versatility rather than personal strength — she is dependent on her party, and her leadership is expressed through formation/team-skill mechanics (M05400–M05403, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-04-11). She is one of the four heroes fully implemented in the browser proto (role “Spear / Heir”) (002 §Main Party header, CANON — IMPLEMENTED ). Identity Full name suggested: Locke Evertide (002 §Locke; surname candidate raised M02079, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Age: 22 (ages list, M01218, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Female — “her leadership,” “her party” (002 §Locke). Affinity: Fiery (002 §Locke; affinity groupings list “Fiery - Locke, Thane, Sable, Eris, Ajax”). Dog-breed comparison: Akita (party-as-dogs list, M03029, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-13). FreshToast: “In game 2 it’s probably Locke [as favourite]” — she’s “probably best girl for Game 2” (M04966, FreshToast, 2023-11-15; 002 §Locke Notes). Personality Leader with a positive relationship with her party — deliberate contrast to [[lieutenant-milf|Lieutenant Milf]] (002 §Locke). Underdog character — “she’s nobody”; small fish in big pond (M05023–M05036, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-11-16). Flaws (002 §Locke): inexperienced and naive about the wider world; initially too eager to please/follow Lysander; self-doubt and imposter syndrome; stubborn once committed; tends to shoulder burdens alone. Is a little creeped out by the golems for some reason (M02180, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Background Motivations From a village that should be endearing to the player; her home village is nice to her (002 §Locke). Even [[thane|Thane]] begins more experienced than her; almost every skill or strength she ends up with is earned during the course of the game (M05023–M05036, 2023-11-16). Motivations (002 §Locke): initially wants to support and impress Lysander; prove herself worthy despite humble origins; protect people from those who would abuse power; eventually shift from hero-pleasing to people-saving; become a true hero on her own terms, not in Lysander’s shadow. Goals (002 §Locke): gather the [[chaos-emeralds|chaos emeralds]] (initially to help Lysander, later to keep them from him); grow strong enough to protect those who cannot protect themselves; stop Lysander and prevent misuse of Ajax’s power; return home as a genuine hero, not just a follower. Her great-grandfather was a sailor; the crazy land Pirate he may have mentored informs Locke of her family history and key info such as a chaos emerald’s location (M02803–M02810, FreshToast, 2023-02-26). Abilities Traits Party position: Leader (002 §Locke). CANON Weapon: Spear (Pierce family) — wields the [[sunderer|Sunderer]] , “the first of Ajax’s seven thingies,” acquired in the trial Locke passes at what turns out to be the secret entrance to [[ajaxs-tomb|Ajax’s Tomb]] (M03670, FreshToast, 2023-09-10). The Sunderer is an explicit symbol of her status as heir to Ajax and of her independence from Lysander — the sword he gave her broke; the spear is power she sought herself, which he then covets (M05558–M05564, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2024-08-24). PROPOSED [[reserve-skills-system|Reserve Skills System]] : can borrow a designated reserve skill from a character in reserve, once per battle (M01390–M01392, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). Designed but NOT wired into combat — the proto implements her four normal skills only (002 §Locke). Flame-based skill that gives AP to another character — part of the “Giving, taking and sharing” skill-design theme (M03070, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-18); design intent, not implemented (002 §Locke). Positioning: somewhat flexible — she moves dynamically to use skills, like the two mage characters (M02576–M02579, FreshToast, 2023-02-20); “Locke has some movement and modular abilities but does not need to even slot them” (M01880, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). DEBATED Overworld “special actions” should be things Locke does, taught/granted by allies: Thane teaches her to sneak, [[sable|Sable]] teaches her to throw her voice, Leonard gives her a dash on cooldown (M01682–M01683, M01715, M01733, FreshToast, 2023-02-11; tagged Heavily Debated in 007/008). Carries a lantern in the prologue’s dark cave (002 §Locke); her portable light’s closest visual reference is a tiny hovering [[machina|machina]] with freeform-shimmer dragonfly wings (M03517–M03518, FreshToast, 2023-07-17). CANON — IMPLEMENTED Proto combat values (proto: src/core/combat/content.ts , src/core/combat/types.ts ; 002 §Locke) id locke , role “Spear / Heir”, maxHp 30 , maxAp 3 , speed 6 , str 12 / mag 6 / def 8 / res 6 / acc 92 / crit 12. Start position 2 (Mid line) . Pierce (1 AP) — quick spear thrust, hits enemy ranks 1–2, 7 phys. Sunder (2 AP) — “The Sunderer bites deep”; 8 phys + Defense Down (the in-fiction Sunderer is wired in as this skill’s flavor). Phalanx (2 AP) — braced full-weight lunge at the front foe, 12 phys. Emberlance (4 AP, finisher) — “Ajax’s fire runs down the spear”; 17 phys, all enemy ranks; round 4+ or [[escalation]] 20. Relationships Locke Thane pairing noted (M01228, FreshToast, 2023-02-09); she has a moment with Thane where she struggles to travel far from home — like Sam’s “farthest from home” moment in LotR — and later comforts somebody else the same way to show growth (M04126–M04135, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Locke [[two-other-antagonists|Himbo]] pairing noted (M01229–M01230, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Lysander — the key relationship of the game: “The key is writing Lysander and how Locke bounces off Lysander”; models cited are Dutch Arthur (RDR2) and All Might Deku (MHA) (M05420–M05421, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-18). She is in awe of him as her hero and nervous about how to address him (“sir”/“hero” rejected as cringe); the age gap is only about 4–5 years (M05605–M05621, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2024-08-24). Leadership is the “glue”: told through formation mechanics — she has many formations with others, maybe not optimal but brings the team together (M05400–M05403, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-04-11). Role Arc Chaos-emerald motivation chain (002 §Locke): runs into Lysander → wants to get stronger and support him → overhears Lysander and [[eris|Eris]] discussing gathering the emeralds → decides to gather them herself to prove worthy → gathers them for herself → finally keeps them away from Lysander. She doesn’t become evil halfway; her reason is selfless — a people’s hero. Prologue (002 §Locke): begins in medias res, Locke and Lysander already adventuring together (Lysander’s party minus Eris); at the end it rewinds to a proper intro. She reads a clue well and finds a chaos emerald Lysander misses. Whether she starts with a ranged weapon was an open question (M02443, FreshToast, 2023-02-19) — the proto resolves her to the spear. In the 2024 prologue script draft, Lysander hands her a simple sword; she later falls, finds a large rusty spear, and discovers her talent for identifying weaknesses (M05605 script draft, 2024-08-24). Lysander barely glances at her spear and calls the dead-end dungeon a waste of time — while Locke walked out with a strong relic, “the real prize… the spear + the faith of the party members she saved” (M05538, M05542–M05544, 2024-08-24). Dragon’s Blood choice (002 §Locke): on killing [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]], Locke may drink the dragon’s blood. No mechanical bearing, but it informs her opinion on Lysander’s pursuit of power and her understanding of Ajax; the Daemon of Want’s dialogue"},{"title":"Lyra","group":"Characters","url":"characters/lyra.html","text":"Lyra PROPOSED Chaotic necromancer “dungeon master” found at the end of her own dungeon, holder of one of the seven chaos emeralds, who asks you to rate her dungeon on fantasy Yelp. Summary — Lyra is a necromancer who builds her own dungeons and is encountered at the end of one, post-boss (M01273–M01274, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). She holds one of the seven [[chaos-emeralds|chaos emeralds]] (M01280, FreshToast, 2023-02-09), lures the party in with a scene showing off her treasure (M01279, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09), and asks you to rate her dungeon on fantasy Yelp — [[ruby|Ruby]] fills out the survey (M01276–M01278, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Later notes place her comfortably “in a dungeon somewhere” (M02727, FreshToast, 2023-02-25) and float pink hair for her (M04954, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). Appearance Mysterious figure with striking, dark features: raven-black hair in loose waves down to her waist; piercing, deep-set green eyes; bewitching beauty enhanced by an imposing 6-foot stature and slender build; always dressed in a long, dark cloak that conceals her appearance and protects her from the elements of the dungeons she creates. “She got bodacious boobies.” (M01272, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09) DEBATED Maybe has pink hair — “Who has pink hair?” / “I was thinking several side characters” / “Maybe Lyra too” (M04952–M04954, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-11-15) — conflicts with the raven-black hair of the original profile. Personality Chaotic and unpredictable; follows her own path (M01272, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Highly intelligent and cunning; manipulates those around her to her advantage; arrogant and confident; disregards social norms; deeply emotional underneath (M01272). “Subtly horny” (M01275, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Asks you to rate her dungeon on fantasy Yelp; a survey (M01276–M01277, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Background Motivations Driven to create her own dungeon, free from the constraints of society’s rules; seeks to prove that the way of chaos and the path of the Necromancer is the true way of power (M01272, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Born into a family of powerful, well-respected Necromancers; grew to question their strict rules, felt stifled, and set out to forge her own path, embracing her inner chaos; has traveled the world gathering knowledge and power (M01272). Her dungeons are a testament to her mastery of the dark arts (M01272). Role Arc Dungeon master : found at the end of a dungeon, post-boss (M01273–M01274, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Has one of the seven [[chaos-emeralds|chaos emeralds]] (M01280, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). A scene where she shows off the treasure to lure you in (M01279, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). [[ruby|Ruby]] fills out her dungeon survey (M01278, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Open questions DEBATED “Lyra or [[eris|Eris]] is a vampire?” — raised and never resolved (M01398, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). DEBATED Pink hair vs the profile’s raven-black hair (M04952–M04954, 2023-11-15). Design history 2023-02-09 — full profile posted (appearance, chaotic personality, necromancer-family background) (M01272, CrunchyConniptions); role built out the same session: dungeon master, post-boss encounter, subtly horny, fantasy-Yelp survey, Ruby fills it out, treasure-lure scene, chaos emerald holder (M01273–M01280, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-10 — vampire question raised: “Lyra or Eris is a vampire?” (M01398, FreshToast) — unresolved. 2023-02-25 — placement confirmed feasible: “I’m pretty sure I can fit Lyra in comfortably in a dungeon somewhere” (M02727, FreshToast). 2023-11-15 — pink hair floated for several side characters, “Maybe Lyra too” (M04952–M04954, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M01272–M01280, M01398, M02727, M04952–M04954 (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast; 2023-02-09 → 2023-11-15) Cleaned: 000 Part 5 §Lyra; Part 10 (fit Lyra in dungeon) Docs: 002 §Lyra (Necromancer / Dungeon Master) Ledger: character rows R07 (profile), R15 (fit Lyra in a dungeon, M02727) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Lysander","group":"Characters","url":"characters/lysander.html","text":"Lysander CANON The celebrated “golden hero” of Skyward Cinders and its central antagonist — a descendant of [[ajax]] who made a pact with a daemon for power and doesn’t give a shit where that power comes from. Summary — Lysander is the realm’s idolized hero, descended from Ajax, whose legendary strength was supposedly a gift from a benevolent otherworldly being — actually the result of a pact with an evil spirit, known only to [[eris]] (M02198–M02200, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Built on family legacy rather than his own merits, he makes his own pact to gain power worthy of his ancestor, betrays and kills Eris, magically devours his own party members, and is finally killed by [[locke]] (002 §Lysander). His relationship with Locke is “the key” of the story — a mentor-protégé dynamic modeled on Dutch Arthur (RDR2) and All Might Deku (MHA) (M05420–M05421, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-18). Appearance Tall (002 §Lysander: Visual). Purple-orange-yellow gradient irises like [[vaenngrym]] — “sunset irises,” the reputed mark of Ajax’s descendants (M02904–M02906, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). In phase 2: sickly light pours from his mouth and eyes — bluish or indigo, per the visual language of warm/orange/good vs cool/blue/bad (M02309–M02314, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Visual theme: “In my head he is the golden hero, the shining ray of hope” — though he doesn’t have to be literally gold (M02309–M02314, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Personality Initially appears as the golden hero, shining ray of hope (002 §Lysander). “Doesn’t give shit about where power comes from” (M02201, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Celebrity sellout — an intentional character flaw (002 §Lysander). Concerned or confused in the prologue (002 §Lysander: Prologue). Infuriated at Locke’s challenge; growls in a manner reminiscent of the dragon (M02887, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-03). Background Motivations Descendant of Ajax; has Ajax’s relic — possibly, or it was on Lysander the whole time and suppressed him without his knowledge, until it fell from him or was somehow broken (M02887, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-03). Built his reputation on family legacy rather than his own merits; felt pressure to live up to Ajax’s legend (002 §Lysander). Made a pact with a daemon to gain power worthy of his ancestor (002 §Lysander). Locke is a nobody/underdog partly because Lysander exists — even [[thane]] begins more experienced than her (M05023–M05036, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-16). Like [[gideon-azurith|Gideon]] and other human characters, his cruelty comes mostly from human flaws — in contrast to [[raphael]], the story’s inhuman tragic monster (M05230–M05247, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Motivations: live up to Ajax’s legendary status; gain power by any means necessary; maintain his “golden hero” image while pursuing dark power; surpass his ancestor’s achievements (002 §Lysander). Goals: gather the [[chaos-emeralds|chaos emeralds]] (ostensibly to defeat the dragon, actually for power); make a pact with the spirit for greater strength; become the strongest hero regardless of cost; maintain his public image while pursuing secret power (002 §Lysander). Flaws: celebrity sellout — image over substance; no moral compass regarding the source of power; willing to sacrifice anyone including allies; arrogance blinds him to consequences; becomes consumed by power and loses his humanity; betrays those who trust him without hesitation (002 §Lysander). Reputation Unraveling Pace People are meant to look up to Lysander and admire him as a hero — people idolize heroes, and he in particular is admired (M04686–M04688, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-25). He unravels much faster than Dutch from RDR2 — a deliberately imperfect comparison, since Dutch isn’t really an antagonist in RDR2 and that story is largely about the gang’s slow decline; here the seeds of doubt are immediate (M04690, M04692, M04699, M04701, 2023-10-25). His reputation lasts a little longer than the doubt, but devolves entirely by the time the dragon is dealt with at the end of Act 2 (M04702, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). Design rationale: “the audience doesn’t want to be duped — they want to feel smart” (M04693, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-25). He refuses to face the dragon down, entranced by the power of Ajax’s legacy, and lets towns burn while Locke rounds up help; the moment where Lysander hesitates and Locke steps up shifts public opinion in her favor and defines her as Ajax’s proper heir (M04704–M04706, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). The dragon highlights the three motivations: Ajax wants glory, Lysander wants strength, Locke wants to protect and make change — “Lysander is the failure of the three of them because he’s the only one who failed to step up when needed” (M04707, M04709, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). Abilities Traits Affinity: Electric (002 §Lysander). Power involves soul and sacrifice; can grab someone by the head and immediately turn them to dust; grips allies causing them to die (M02282–M02283, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Boss-fight gimmick: when he kills a party member he regains HP, gets a buff, and gains AP — this doubles as a teaching mechanic (M02296, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Has a diluted version of Ajax’s power, which waned with each heir; to recover, he has to kill and drain the life of others (M02668, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Only realizes his ability to take at the last moment — had only used it accidentally when young; enough to prove incredibly threatening to Locke in their final encounter (M02669–M02670, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). PROPOSED — not implemented Has skills that directly increase or scale based on [[escalation]]. (The escalation counter is implemented in the proto — src/core/combat/state.ts — but Lysander himself is unbuilt; the proto’s only boss is the Ossuary Sentinel tomb golem.) (002 §Lysander: Abilities) Has developed his daemonic power but not his draconic heritage; has a hint of dragon-like aspects, far less clear than on [[bastard-damaris|the bastard Damaris]] (M02693, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Can occasionally intuit the draconic language (M02696, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Gets skills to interact with/consume “shadows” (the Shadow System’s magical traces of defeated characters) for strength (M02475–M02482, FreshToast, 2023-02-19); scary-boss idea: 2nd-phase Lysander can break [[shields-and-wounded|shields]] early or open with an attack that strips a shield (M02492–M02497, FreshToast, 2023-02-19; M02580, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-20). Relationships [[locke|Locke]] — the key relationship of the story; write Lysander and how Locke bounces off him (M05420–M05421, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-18). Locke is in awe of him as her hero and nervous about how to address him (“sir”/“hero” rejected as cringe); the age gap is only about 4–5 years (M05605–M05621, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2024-08-27). At the trial dungeon he considers the boss’s treasure the real prize, dismisses the [[sunderer|Sunderer]] as worthless — should comment on it or attempt and fail to pick it up, mjolnir/excalibur style — while Locke gets the real prize all along: the spear plus the faith of the party members she saved (M05522–M05544, M05538, 2024-08-27). [[eris|Eris]] — manipulated by her (initially); betrays and kills her (M02201, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). The party tells Lysander about Eris in an effort to create division; he seems unphased, then for phase 2 “Lysander fucking magically eats his other party members” (M02249–M02255, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Party members [[eris|Eris]] (member of party). The Prideful Paladin ([[thanes-foil|Thane’s Foil]]) — not in the hero party; independent (M02335–M02341, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). CANON The remaining 2 are a pair of orphaned, adoptive siblings — [[flynt|Flynt]] [[steele|Steele]] — largely loyal-to-a-fault, goofy pawns who work personally for Lysander (M02335–M02341, 2023-02-16; named M03394, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Role Arc Prologue: In the prologue dungeon lookin"},{"title":"Minor & Unnamed Characters","group":"Characters","url":"characters/minor-characters.html","text":"Minor Unnamed Characters PROPOSED Grouped page for one-note and unnamed characters: the animal lover, the Hero-as-antagonist concept, the Wanderer, Hikari the shinobi, and Brickwings the animated brick. Summary — Five small character concepts from the February 2023 brainstorm, each with only a line or two of source material. None has been developed beyond its original mention; statuses are per-section below. Animal Lover (unnamed) PROPOSED A character/character moment: an animal lover has their food stolen away from them by a seagull as they’re eating — and they’re not mad about it. In fact, they’re elated; they root for the bird, praising its survival skills and will to live. “you go, bird.” (M01164, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-08) The Hero (antagonist concept) PROPOSED “We fight the hero” (M01161, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08) — one of the game’s gaming-deconstruction pillars, listed alongside “we fight a blacksmith who changes their armor based on your attack” and “we don’t level up” (M01160–M01163, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08). The idea of the classic JRPG hero as the thing you fight resonates with [[ajax|Ajax]]/[[lysander|Lysander]] later being described as “parodies of a classic JRPG protagonist” (M02671, FreshToast, 2023-02-21), but no direct link was stated in the notes. The Wanderer PROPOSED “Female short” — “The Wanderer” (M00950–M00951, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-05). Mentioned with no additional details provided (002 §The Wanderer). Hikari the Shinobi PROPOSED From the [[ninja-village|ninja village in the mountains]] — a village of ninjas with supernatural ninja powers where you fight the hokage (M01962–M01970, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-14). “You meet hikari the shinobi with huge anime tiddies” (M01973, FreshToast, 2023-02-14); paired with “the other cute shy one with normal tiddies” (M01974, CrunchyConniptions), countered by “No shy girls always have the bigger tiddies” (M01975, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). The shy companion remains unnamed. Brickwings (The Brick) PROPOSED The BRICKWINGS story arc (M01176, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-08), beat by beat (M01177–M01188, FreshToast, 2023-02-08): The party needs to throw a brick but can’t throw it far enough; [[gaius-wornwall|Gaius]] animates the brick and it gets wings. The brick flies through a window but escapes — “brick is now free”. Later the party finds the brick again, and Gaius is berated by [[ruby|Ruby]] for not taking care of it. The brick joins the party; it is wholesome and beloved; it gets a couple of scenes. The brick dies comically — a “literal throwaway joke” (M01187). “Ruby relates to the brick” (M01188) — consistent with the Gaius Ruby dynamic being “like Reigen and Mob” (M01189, FreshToast, 2023-02-08). Design history 2023-02-05 — The Wanderer noted: “Female short / The Wanderer” (M00950–M00951, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-08 — gaming-deconstruction pillar “We fight the hero” (M01161, CrunchyConniptions); the animal-lover seagull moment (M01164, spirit_noodle); the full BRICKWINGS arc improvised in a single burst (M01176–M01188, spirit_noodle/FreshToast). 2023-02-14 — Hikari the shinobi and the unnamed shy companion introduced with the mountain ninja village (M01962–M01975, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M00950–M00951; M01160–M01164; M01176–M01189; M01962–M01975 (CrunchyConniptions, spirit_noodle, FreshToast; 2023-02-05 → 2023-02-14) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 (deconstruction pillars, animal-lover moment, BRICKWINGS arc, ninja village) Docs: 002 §Minor Unnamed Characters (Animal Lover, The Hero, The Wanderer, Hikari the Shinobi, Brickwings); 003 §Ninja Village; 009 Ledger: location row 239; story rows 258–259; mechanics row 463 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Nyssa","group":"Characters","url":"characters/nyssa.html","text":"Nyssa CANON An upper year at [[the-luminarium|the Luminarium]] and [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]]'s early mentor figure — chill, reliable, kind, and maybe hot Goth gf. Summary — Nyssa is an upper-year student at the Luminarium who serves as an early mentor figure for Cyrus. Her entire profile comes from a single message: “Nyssa (the upper year, early mentor figure for Cyrus who is chill, reliable and kind. Maybe hot Goth gf? Idk, that would be cool) is highkey one of my types” (M04965, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). Identity Upper year at the Luminarium (M04965, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). Personality Chill, reliable and kind (M04965, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). PROPOSED “Maybe hot Goth gf? Idk, that would be cool” (M04965). Role Function Early mentor figure for Cyrus (M04965, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). Notes “In game 1 it’s a close race but I think Nyssa or [[pandora|Pandora]] would win out” for best girl — Nyssa wins out; “highkey one of my types” (M04965, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). Design history 2023-11-15 — conceived whole in the best-girl aside: upper year, early mentor figure for Cyrus, chill/reliable/kind, maybe hot Goth gf aesthetic (M04965, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M04965 (FreshToast, 2023-11-15) Cleaned: 000 Part 2 (Nyssa) / lines 7901–7906 region Docs: 002 §Nyssa Ledger: row “Nyssa: upper year at the academy, early mentor figure for Cyrus…” (M04965) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Other Named Characters","group":"Characters","url":"characters/other-named-characters.html","text":"Other Named Characters PROPOSED Combined page for names dropped in the chat with little or no attached context. Summary — Doc 002 closes its NPC roster with a list of names that were mentioned but never developed (002 §Other Named Characters). Each is recorded here with its raw anchor. Raphael, who also appears in that list, has a full profile — see [[raphael]]. Yvette Name mentioned (M02081, FreshToast, 2023-02-15) — dropped in the same naming session that produced “[[locke|Locke]] Evertide / Leon Erhardt” (M02079) and “[[silas|Silas]]” (M02080). No character details attached. Geraldo Gagliardi Name mentioned (M01829, FreshToast, 2023-02-12), between production notes; no context given. Jorge Mentioned (M02398, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-17), amid prototype task planning (adjacent to “fishing minigame”); no context given. Brooke Bernard Names mentioned, no context (M02464, FreshToast, 2023-02-19) — posted during the vertical-slice / beetle planning session. Design history 2023-02-12 — “Geraldo Gagliardi” (M01829, FreshToast). 2023-02-15 — “Yvette” (M02081, FreshToast). 2023-02-17 — “Jorge” (M02398, spirit_noodle). 2023-02-19 — “Brooke Bernard” (M02464, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M01829, M02081, M02398, M02464 (FreshToast, spirit_noodle; 2023-02-12 → 2023-02-19) Cleaned: 000 (name drops, Parts 7–9) Docs: 002 §Other Named Characters Ledger: character row R12 (name candidates M02079–M02081 incl. Yvette) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Pandora","group":"Characters","url":"characters/pandora.html","text":"Pandora CANON One of [[the-4-boyes|the 4 Boyes]]: the Victorian/Western-inventor archetype of [[the-luminarium|the Luminarium]] — immense magical capacity she can barely channel, compensated with cunning, cannons and automatons. Summary — Pandora is a Lycosian female in her early 20s who occupies the farthest back position of the group. She represents “both the practical uses of Sorcery - such as enchantment and alchemy - and also magical anomalies” (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Her defining quirk: immense Flow capacity (capable of big magic) but inefficient channeling — she tires fast and casts slow, making up the deficiency with cunning and her creations. She was the first of the Boyes to click: “Pandora makes the most sense in my brain. The others are still forming” (M04342, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Identity Lycosian female; early 20s; ethnically typical for western Khorath (Mediterranean) (M04227, M04242–M04243, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Victorian/Western Inventor archetype; affinity undecided (M04227). Occupies the farthest back position (M04227). Role Function One of the 4 Boyes; represents both practical uses of Sorcery (enchantment and alchemy) and magical anomalies (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Helps [[percival|Percival]] make [[conflux|Conflux]] (his arm) (M04343, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Magical focus Makes things like magic bombs, her cannon [[rainfall|‘Rainfall’]] , or the automaton [[dianthus|‘Dianthus’]] (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Dianthus eventually attains sentience — “and they maybe kiss a lil UwU” (M04227). Especially into ergonomic, supportive stuff like prosthetics and machina (M04346, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Names the stuff she makes after smells she likes (M04227). Rainfall gives her a highly controlled magical outlet — “A safe way to use her capacity… (kinda like a prosthetic for a disability, I’m now realising)” (M04350–M04352, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Unique quirk CANON Immense Flow capacity (capable of big magic) but struggles to channel Flow efficiently — gets tired fast and casts slow. Makes up for the overall deficiency with her cunning and creations (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). “She’s basically a danger to herself and everyone around her” (M04347, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Joining motive / Backstory Easiest motive of the group for joining the Luminarium: her immense Flow capacity combined with a lack of training makes her a danger to herself and everyone around her (M04347, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). “Could be just blows up her house and has to leave before it gets worse” — she has a weakness in her lack of control and would want to seek a solution; passionate about sorcery and its implementations but hampered by casting risk and ease of fatigue (M04348–M04349, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Rainfall is (sorta) that solution: the safe, highly controlled outlet answering this danger (M04350–M04351, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Conflicts (brainstormed) PROPOSED Fourteen-item conflict brainstorm list — design seeds, not yet finalized (M04393, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17): Self-Imposed Isolation — pushes people away for fear of getting hurt Perceived Inferiority — feels her magic is less impressive than others’ Reluctant Leadership — unsure if she’s cut out for leadership roles she’s thrust into Desire for Legacy — wants to ensure her inventions outlive her Creative Block — struggles with periods of lacking inspiration (maybe goes to the dungeon because it clears her head) Fear of Outshining — holds back to avoid overshadowing friends; doesn’t show off how powerful her inventions are Reluctance to Trust — finds it hard to open up or rely on others; once she does, she’s full in it Friendship Betrayal — grapples with a past betrayal and trust issues Guilt over Priorities — feels guilty for prioritizing work over relationships (family in particular; becomes a habit) Struggle with Self-care — neglects her well-being in pursuit of goals; often sick, malnourished or sleep-deprived, so she faints and falls asleep now and then Fear of Exposure — fears her secrets or vulnerabilities will be exposed Regretful Decision — made a choice she deeply regrets; that one love she could’ve had but didn’t have the courage for Financial Dependence — relies on someone for financial support and feels trapped (a mentor who sees her potential and invests in her and her inventions) Hidden Passion — hides a passion she fears others won’t understand Notes “Close race between Nyssa and Pandora for best girl”; entertained by Pandora; “she’ll obviously get a lot of screentime” — but [[nyssa|Nyssa]] wins out (M04965, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). PROPOSED Curse-bearer concept (“his power comes with a curse…”) was answered with “That’s basically Pandora” (M04376–M04377, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-17). “A magical blacksmith lady… is that just pandora again? Pandora is just cool” (M04397–M04399, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). Design history 2023-10-17 — initial concept in the 4 Boyes roster write-up: Lycosian inventor, farthest back position, magic bombs/Rainfall/Dianthus, the Flow-capacity quirk, names creations after smells she likes (M04227, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — “I like Pandora” / “Pandora makes the most sense in my brain. The others are still forming” (M04341–M04342, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast); assigned as the person who helps Percival make Conflux (M04343, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — trait vetting round 1: Innovative Genius “yes but not quite to the extreme”; Quirky Insights “maybe. Definitely once she matures”; Unconventional Solutions yes; Obsession recast as a passion for ergonomic, supportive prosthetics and machina; Protective of creations “values them but maybe not so precious” (M04344–M04346, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — joining motive settled: high capacity + no training = danger to everyone; maybe blows up her house; Rainfall as prosthetic-like outlet (M04347–M04352, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — trait vetting round 2: Natural Connector “is just [Unconventional Solutions] again”; Treasure Hunter no; Wanderlust “maybe somebody else” (it went to [[colette|Colette]]); Strong Ethical Code “yeah but she takes risks” — set aside to think about (M04356–M04364, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — curse-bearer concept: “That’s basically Pandora” (M04376–M04377). 2023-10-17 — 14-item conflict brainstorm posted (M04393, CrunchyConniptions); “a magical blacksmith lady… is that just pandora again? Pandora is just cool” (M04397–M04399, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-11-15 — best-girl race: close between Nyssa and Pandora; Pandora will get a lot of screentime, Nyssa wins out (M04965, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M04227; M04242–M04243; M04341–M04364; M04376–M04377; M04393–M04399; M04965 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-10-17 → 2023-11-15) Cleaned: 000 lines 6826–6868 region (Pandora motive + conflicts) Docs: 002 §Pandora (incl. §Pandora: Joining Motive / Backstory, §Pandora: Conflicts (Brainstormed)); 009 §Development Status (“Pandora makes most sense”) Ledger: rows for Pandora joining motive, Conflux helper (M04343), curse-bearer “basically Pandora”, “Pandora is just cool”, Nyssa/Pandora best girl in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Percival","group":"Characters","url":"characters/percival.html","text":"Percival CANON The main-game party mage — the renamed [[isaac|Isaac]] — an Electric-affinity caster with a Hermione-esque rival and a made arm called Conflux. Summary — Percival, who goes by “Percy”, is the Skyward/Soaring Cinders main-game party mage: in Part 10 the name was proposed to replace “Isaac” as the party member (“I don’t really like Isaac much for a party member” — M02787, FreshToast, 2023-02-25), making Percy the renamed main-game mage (002 §Percival). His affinity is Electric (M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). His rival is [[rhea-falafill|Rhea Falafill]] , and [[pandora|Pandora]] helps him make Conflux, his arm (M04343, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). He appears in the latest (2024) team-skill roster, paired with [[damian|Damian]] (M05392, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). Beyond this, the notes give Percy very little direct detail — his arc is workshopped through his rival. Name / Identity CANON Full name Percival , short form Percy — “The Percy/Percival dichotomy appeals to me” (M02788, FreshToast, 2023-02-25); “percy is cool” (M02789, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-25). CANON The name replaces Isaac as the party member, freeing “Isaac” for the rival/other character (M02784–M02787, FreshToast, 2023-02-25); reconfirmed months later: “Isaac is Percy and falafal is a foodstuff” (M03526, FreshToast, 2023-08-12). This rename is the documented root of the cross-doc Isaac/Percival confusion (002 §Percival). The name itself dates to the 2021 name bank (M00445–M00446, FreshToast, 2021-08-01; M00511, spirit_noodle), where surname candidates included Lumenson, Brightsworth, Brightlord, and Astrum (M00495–M00499, spirit_noodle, 2021-08-01) — none confirmed. CANON Affinity: Electric — “Electric - Percival, Ruby, Lysander” in the known-affinities roster alongside the main-game cast (M03051/M03052, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). Abilities Traits Magic-focused party mage, inheriting the staff-wielder slot from the Isaac profile (002 §Isaac, §Percival) — see [[isaac|Isaac]] for the proto-implemented kit, which is coded under the old name. Conflux (his arm): [[pandora|Pandora]] is “the person who helps Percival make Conflux (his arm)” (M04343, FreshToast, 2023-10-17) — a made/prosthetic arm; the liked “Sha’worr-ma is a contradiction personified…” paragraph was earmarked for reuse on the person who helped Percy make it (ledger R19, M03527+, FreshToast, 2023-08-12). Team-skill pairing: in the 2024 position-based team-skill scheme he is paired with [[damian|Damian]] (“- Percy/Damian”, M05392; “When she is beside Percy or Damian…”, M05394, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). Dog-breed party comparison: Percy - was left blank (M03029, FreshToast, 2023-03-13). Relationships CANON Has a Hermione-esque rival, [[rhea-falafill|Rhea Falafill]] — “smart, straightforward but hardworking rather than talented” (M02721–M02722, FreshToast, 2023-02-25); named at M02835 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-27). CANON The rival “could later side with Percy/Isaac against [[eris|Eris]] ” (M02723, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). [[pandora|Pandora]] helps him make Conflux (M04343, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Open questions DEBATED Sparse notes: beyond the Electric affinity, the Isaac→Percy rename, the Rhea rivalry, and Conflux, the notes give Percy very little detail; his arc is workshopped through Rhea. Treat anything more specific as undecided (002 §Percival). PROPOSED Possible cross-iteration overlap: the Rhea-rival material was also written up in the Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium academy notes, so Percy/Rhea may overlap across iterations. This is a soft cross-reference, not a ruling that Percy belongs to the Luminarium — the affinity roster places Percival in the main-game cast (002 §Percival, citing M03051 and the ~4963-4965 character block). Whether he inherits the [[isaac|Isaac]] Eris-confrontation arc wholesale under the new name is implied by the rename but never restated post-rename. Quarantine notes (do not restore) [REMOVED — AI-invented] A previous profile version asserted a detailed scholar persona (socially-awkward Ajax-legend researcher, research motivations, party bonds, a full research arc, a “Golden Retriever” dog tag). None of it appears in the Discord notes; it was moved to AI_Hallucinations.md (002 §Percival). [WARNING] The proto’s parallel codex ( gamebible/data/characters.js , “Percival (Percy)” entry) repeats that exact quarantined persona . It is a reconciliation of the same brainstorm vault, not an independent source, and Percy is not implemented in the proto code ( src/core/combat/content.ts builds only Locke, Thane, Isaac, Ruby). Do not re-import it (002 §Percival). Design history 2021-08-01 — “Percy” / “Percival” enter the name bank (M00445–M00446, FreshToast; M00511, spirit_noodle); surname riffs Percival Lumenson / Brightsworth / Brightlord / Astrum (M00495–M00499, spirit_noodle). No character attached yet. 2023-02-25 — rename: the party mage Isaac becomes Percy — “What if Isaac was called Percy?”, “We could use Isaac for his rival character instead”, “I don’t really like Isaac much for a party member”, “The Percy/Percival dichotomy appeals to me” (M02784–M02788, FreshToast); approved “percy is cool” (M02789, spirit_noodle). — supersedes the name Isaac for the mage; see [[isaac]]. 2023-02-27 — the rival gets a name: “Rhea Falafill (Isaac’s rival)” (M02835, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-03-13 — party-as-dogs list leaves Percy - blank (M03029, FreshToast). 2023-03-15 — Electric affinity assigned in the known-affinities roster (M03051/M03052, FreshToast). 2023-08-12 — rename reconfirmed, “Isaac is Percy and falafal is a foodstuff” (M03526, FreshToast); the Sha’worr-ma “contradiction personified” paragraph is earmarked for the person who helped Percy make Conflux (Percy’s arm) — first mention of the arm (ledger R19: M03527, M03530, M03535–M03538, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — the Conflux-maker is identified: Pandora “helps Percival make Conflux (his arm)” (M04343, FreshToast). 2024-04-11 — Percy appears in the latest team-skill roster, paired with Damian (M05392, M05394, FreshToast) — current state. Provenance Raw: M00445–M00446, M00495–M00499, M00511, M02721–M02723, M02784–M02789, M02835, M03029, M03051–M03052, M03526–M03538, M04343, M05392–M05394 (FreshToast, spirit_noodle, CrunchyConniptions; 2021-08-01 → 2024-04-11) Cleaned: 000 Part 10 (rename, rival), Part 11 (affinity roster) Docs: 002 §Percival (Percy); 002 §Isaac (rename note) Ledger: character rows R03 (M00445/M00446/M00511 name suggestion), R15 (M02784–M02789 rename; M02721–M02723 rival), R16 (M03029 dogs list), R19 (M03526+ Conflux earmark), R23 (M04343 Pandora/Conflux) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Raphael","group":"Characters","url":"characters/raphael.html","text":"Raphael CANON The true tragic monster of the story — a dual vampirism-and-dracosis afflicted fallen angel who masks as human, held together only by undiluted, focused hatred. Summary — Raphael is “inhuman, our true (tragic) monster ” (M05231, FreshToast, 2024-02-10): the most frighteningly inhuman character in the story, driven and characterized by raw, evil hatred, “just not able to be reasoned or empathised with” (M05235–M05236, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). He embodies both [[vampirism|Vampirism]] and [[dracosis|Dracosis]] — “Raphael would literally embody them both and it would drive him mad” (M05192, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10) — but instead “he rises above them to impress his own twisted will”: already too far gone to lose it any further (M05193, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Crucially, his inhumanity is enhanced by, but not the result of, his curses — it is the result of his circumstances and trauma (M05237–M05238, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Per the doc, he is the single most-workshopped character in the notes (002 §Raphael). Appearance Tall, with purple-orange-yellow gradient (“sunset”) irises like [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]] and [[lysander|Lysander]] (M02904, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). Outwardly “calm, almost too calm” despite the vampirism, dracosis, inherited malefic pact and social/familial trauma (M05229, FreshToast, 2024-02-10); distant, unemotive, contained — “implying it’s all he can manage to keep himself from losing it at any second” (M05244–M05245, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Masks as human, “a wolf among sheep” (M05235, FreshToast, 2024-02-10); stays mostly human-appearing until he socially unmasks his hatred, “turning physically and mentally monstrous. Revealing the scales and the fangs and the flames” (M05241, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Affliction Nature CANON Dual affliction: Vampirism + Dracosis (M05192, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10), framed against the thematic opposition Vampirism = immortality/preservation vs Dracosis = evolution/change (M05176–M05187, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2024-02-10). “Functional but broken in a way that makes him less human” — “a trifecta of curse, abuse and disease has blended together to make a whole new kind of monster” (M05211–M05212, FreshToast, 2024-02-10); compared to Gollum / a split person (M05214–M05215, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10). CANON Combat against psychosis: “It is through this undiluted, focused fury that he can combat the psychosis one would suffer normally as a result of the draconic/vampiric mix” (M05234, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). His deceptively well-kept manner is “not a result of discipline but a deranged state of interminable focus” (M05216–M05217, FreshToast, 2024-02-10) — “The evil, post-ethical equivalent to post-nut clarity” / “Mentally mid-nut at all times” (M05218–M05219, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Name significance “Raphael is an angelic biblical name. We could lean into themes of purity for him” — “Like he was pure and kind but has since been corrupted. It has gone so far physically mentally that it’s wrapped around into a new form of purity” (M05194–M05195, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). A fallen angel — “like Satan kinda who’s the ultimate biblical evil but used to be god’s favourite lil boy” (M05239–M05240, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). DEBATED Two variants, not finalized: corruption wrapping back around to purity, OR the simpler option — “make him ironically obsessed with purity despite impure intentions + vampire + dracosis” (M05222–M05223, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Background Motivations CANON Probably caused the fall of the royal family — “Maybe he caused the whole issue. He leaked the info to the public about the blood sacrifices?” / “Yeah he caused the fall most likely. Probably killed a few” (M05248–M05250, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2024-02-10); “Like Raphael killed his family” (M05254, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Past!Raphael resisted the vampirism, “trying to be good and kind in an unethical family setting and having that all backfire on him so hard, mostly as a result of his circumstances” (M05257, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). His inhumanity is “the result of traumatically twisting a kind, intelligent form of ambition into single-minded loathing” — enhanced by, not caused by, his curses (M05237–M05238, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Sources of hatred “He acts purely out of loosely directed hate. He hates his family, vampirism, his ugly scales, humanity all of it” (M05232, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). CANON Resents [[astrid|Astrid]] “for daring to dig it all up and possibly restore the reputation of the people he hates the most” (M05233, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). In one path Astrid may be “a little regretful regarding the fate of Raphael” (M04660, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). Role Arc CANON The intended contrast: “[[gideon-azurith|Gideon]], Lysander and other human characters do cruel, awful things by the end but mostly as a result of human flaws. Raphael is inhuman, our true (tragic) monster” (M05230–M05231, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Design goal: “Through the good ol’ Tragic Backstory ™ people may begin to sympathise with him. Then we’ll pick at that, until they realise that he’s just not able to be reasoned or empathised with” (M05236, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). “He needs to be relatable. The audience should sympathise. The audience should be furious on his behalf and almost want justify some of his actions” (M05246, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10), yet the hate “should go beyond reason to avoid any semblance of genuine humanity” (M05247, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). “I think he should be totally, tragically fucked up” (M05225, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). The aim: “Present the philosophy conflict by the human conflict of Raphael” (M05224, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10), with drastic contrast between Past!Raphael and the “current day, evil-to-the-bone-marrow, super-dragon-vampire, evil-hate-fueled and irredeemable Raphael” (M05257–M05258, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Open questions DEBATED Corruption-wraps-to-purity vs ironically-obsessed-with-purity (M05222–M05223, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Design history 2023-03-05 — first appearance as a named character: “Lysander and Raphael are tall with purple-orange-yellow gradient coloured irises like Vaenngrym” (M02904, FreshToast) — establishing him among Ajax’s sunset-eyed line. 2023-10-24 — story hook: in one path Astrid is “a little regretful regarding the fate of Raphael” (M04660, FreshToast). 2024-02-10 — the extensive development session (M05176–M05258, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast): dual Vampirism+Dracosis embodiment; rises above the madness through focused fury; angelic-name/purity theme and fallen-angel framing; trifecta of curse, abuse and disease; wolf-among-sheep masking with scales/fangs/flames reveal; caused the royal family’s fall and killed family members; resents Astrid; true tragic monster contrasted with the story’s human evildoers; Past!Raphael vs current Raphael contrast — current presentation; supersedes nothing, extends the 2023 sketch. Provenance Raw: M02904, M04660, M05176–M05258 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-03-05 → 2024-02-10) Cleaned: 000 Part 17/20 (Raphael — Extensive Development) Docs: 002 §Raphael (Tragic Monster) Ledger: rows R16 (M02904 sunset irises), R25 (M04660 Astrid regret), R28-era Raphael development rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Recurring Merchant","group":"Characters","url":"characters/recurring-merchant.html","text":"Recurring Merchant CANON The recurring, plucky merchant — the character face of the game’s Shopkeeper system. Summary — A “recurring, plucky merchant” (M02075, FreshToast, 2023-02-15) who appears throughout the game as its shopkeeper. The character side is only this personality sketch; all of the mechanics — behavior, inventory, and how the shop works — live in the [[shopkeeper-system|Shopkeeper system]] (002 §Recurring Merchant, cross-ref to 008 Game Mechanics). Personality Plucky (M02075, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Related systems The recurring Shopkeeper appears throughout the game; the full Shopkeeper system (mechanics, behavior, inventory) is documented in 008 Game Mechanics — see [[shopkeeper-system]] (002 §Recurring Merchant, NPC pointer). Design history 2023-02-15 — sole character-side mention: “Recurring, plucky merchant” (M02075, FreshToast). Subsequent development happened on the system side (008). Provenance Raw: M02075 (FreshToast; 2023-02-15) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 (recurring plucky merchant) Docs: 002 §Recurring Merchant; 008 §Shopkeeper System Ledger: character row R12 (“Recurring, plucky merchant character”, M02075) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Rhea Falafill","group":"Characters","url":"characters/rhea-falafill.html","text":"Rhea Falafill DEBATED [[percival|Percy]]'s Hermione-esque rival: a fiercely competitive, knowledge-hungry elf girl who exists in two unreconciled write-ups — a rival who wants a better future for all, and a nemesis who discards ethics for results. Summary — Rhea Falafill was conceived as a rival for Isaac (who later became Percy): “smart, straightforward but hardworking rather than talented,” “a little too proper, too by-the-book” (M02722, M02725, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). Two full profiles exist — the First Version (rival) of 2023-02-27 and the Second Version (nemesis) of 2023-08-12 — and which one gets finalized is an explicitly open question (002 §Rhea, reconciliation note; 000 Part 14). Both are recorded below; where they agree the trait is shared core, where they diverge the divergence is noted. Iteration note: Percy is the main-game party mage; the Rhea-rival material was workshopped in the Caelian Odyssey academy notes, and whether Percy/Rhea overlaps into that iteration is PROPOSED , not settled (002 §CAELIAN ODYSSEY header, Note on Percival/Percy). Name / Identity CANON The name is a food pun on “falafel,” tied to the Isaac→Percy rename: “Isaac is Percy and falafal is a foodstuff” (M03526, FreshToast, 2023-08-12). Name jokes ran wild: “did you just call a character falafel” (M02836, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-27); “Fuckin falafal??” (M02840, FreshToast); “Also ethereal means she’s a ghost. Pointed ears means an elf ghost. Falafel the elf ghost” (M02852–M02855, FreshToast, 2023-02-27). DEBATED Gender: the notes lean toward girl — “I think it should be a girl… dunno about a boy” (M02731, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-25); “I think girl is very befitting for this kind of archetype” (M02735, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-25); FreshToast: “Okeydokey” after “You think we should just play to type?” (M02737–M02739). Discussed but not hard-locked; a boy was briefly suggested to avoid parallels with [[damian|Damian]]'s hunter (002 §Rhea: Name / Identity). Appearance (shared core) Long, dark hair to the waist; pointed ears (an elf); green eyes; short/petite and slender with graceful, ethereal movements. Wears an elegant gown in shades of blue and green with intricate embroidery/patterns “reminiscent of an older world” (M02835, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-27; M03519, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12). Personality (shared core) Strong-willed, intelligent, fiercely independent; passionate, competitive, determined; a perfectionist (M02835; M03519). A sore loser — losses become “gnawing obsessions that haunt her for days” (M02835; M03519). Very pragmatic — and, per the Second Version, “often discarding ethics for results” (M03519, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12). Archetype refs: “smart, straightforward but hardworking rather than talented,” “a little too proper, too by-the-book” — like the short loud character from Kaguya, or Yunyun from Konosuba (“Yunyun’s a bit too nice but her practical differences to megumin for sure”) (M02722–M02728, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-25). Goals Motivation Shared: driven by a thirst for knowledge , especially forbidden/ancient arts (M02835; M03519). First Version (rival): wants to use the ancient secrets of magic to “create a better future for all”; motivated by protecting those she loves and fighting injustice and oppression; torn between her desire for knowledge and her sense of duty — the secrets could be dangerous in the wrong hands (M02835, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-27). Second Version (nemesis): wants to “craft a world sculpted by her ideals,” where bindings of any kind are nonexistent; loss and grief molded her perspective; her quest is also personal — freeing herself from the “invisible chains” of her past and societal expectations; acutely aware the knowledge “teeters on the edge of chaos” yet irresistibly drawn to it “like a moth to a flame” (M03519, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12). Background (shared core) Grew up in a strict household where magic was forbidden/taboo and knowledge tightly restricted; her curiosity put her at odds with her family. She discovered a hidden trove/library of ancient, crumbling texts, which ignited her hunger for magic (M02835; M03519). Relationships CANON Percy’s rival; “Later this person could side with Isaac against [[eris|Eris]]” (M02723, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). Open questions DEBATED Which version of Percy’s rival will be finalized — First Version (rival) or Second Version (nemesis)? Explicitly listed as an open question (000 Part 14; 002 §Rhea reconciliation note). DEBATED Gender: girl leaned toward, not hard-locked (M02730–M02739, 2023-02-25; 002 §Rhea: Name / Identity). PROPOSED Whether Percy/Rhea overlaps into the Caelian Odyssey iteration at all (002 §CAELIAN ODYSSEY header note). Design history 2023-02-25 — concept born: “Brainstorming the meat for the middle of the plot, I wonder if Isaac needs a hermione-esque rival” — smart, hardworking rather than talented, too proper/by-the-book; could later side with Isaac against Eris; Kaguya/Yunyun archetype refs; gender debated, girl leaned toward (“play to type”) (M02721–M02739, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions/spirit_noodle). 2023-02-27 — First Version full profile posted: “Rhea Falafill (Isaac’s rival)” — elf appearance, sore-loser perfectionist, better-future-for-all goals, knowledge-vs-duty conflict, forbidden-magic household background (M02835, CrunchyConniptions). The falafel name-pun pile-on follows, coining “Falafel the elf ghost” (M02836–M02855, spirit_noodle/FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2023-08-12 — Second Version posted: “Rhea Falafill (Isaac’s Nemesis)” — same shared core but darker: discards ethics for results, wants a world sculpted by her ideals free of all bindings, moth-to-flame conflict (M03519, CrunchyConniptions). FreshToast anchors the rename chain: “Isaac is Percy and falafal is a foodstuff” (M03526). Neither version has been chosen — both stand, status DEBATED. Provenance Raw: M02721–M02742 (2023-02-25); M02835–M02855 (2023-02-27); M03519–M03526 (2023-08-12) (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions, spirit_noodle) Cleaned: 000 Part 10 (Hermione-esque rival), Part 13 (Second Version), Part 14 (open question) Docs: 002 §Rhea Falafill (Percy’s Rival) DEBATED ; 009 §Falafel Name Joke Ledger: rows for “Isaac may need a Hermione-esque rival” (M02721–M02742), “Rhea/Falafel as an elf ghost” (M02854–M02855), name pun/joke row (M02836–M02852) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Rhea Scala","group":"Characters","url":"characters/rhea-scala.html","text":"Rhea Scala EVOLVED A “magic batman with social anxiety” — the party’s magic thief and primary comedic relief, revised the same day from an original female solo-thief protagonist concept. Summary — In its final form, the thief character is a male magic thief rather than a standard one (M01253–M01254, FreshToast, 2023-02-09): “female gaze simp bait” (M01255, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) with major social anxiety (M01256, FreshToast, 2023-02-09), who “suddenly vanishes” (M01257, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) — summed up as “Magic batman with social anxiety” (M01258, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). He serves as primary comedic relief (M01260, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) and becomes close with [[sable|Sable]] through their shared materialism (M01259, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Mechanically, the character introduces the Resolve stat and “replaces Himbo” in the roster (M01252, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Abilities Traits Magic thief, not a standard thief (M01253, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Signature move: suddenly vanishing — the “magic batman” bit (M01257–M01258, 2023-02-09). Carries the Resolve stat; the character slot replaces the earlier Himbo concept (M01252, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Relationships Close with [[sable|Sable]] through shared materialism (M01259, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Role Arc Primary comedic relief (M01260, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Major social anxiety played for characterization and comedy (M01256, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Open questions PROPOSED Is this character the same as [[leon|Leon]] (originally Leonard)? The final magic-thief traits — social anxiety, vanishing/misdirection, comedic relief — reappear the next day on Leonard, whose decoy skill is “used for comedic effect, in tandem with his social anxiety” (M01833, FreshToast, 2023-02-12), and Leonard first appears immediately after this revision (M01394, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-10). No message explicitly equates the two; doc 002 keeps “Rhea Scala (Magic Batman)” as its own entry and still names “Rhea” as Sable’s materialism friend (002 §Sable). Inference only. DEBATED The male version’s actual name is never stated; “Rhea Scala” is the original female concept’s name. Design history 2023-02-09 — original concept SCRAPPED : female thief protagonist “Rhea Scala” (M01249–M01251, CrunchyConniptions). Young woman, 5’7 , lean and athletic; curly brown hair in a short cut above the shoulders; shallow blue eyes reflecting a quiet, resolute nature; dresses in all black with leather gloves and a mask covering the lower half of her face on a job; always sharp and put together. Anti-social solo worker with trust issues who yearns to be part of a crew; pretends to be the group’s leader but deep down lacks the confidence to actually lead; always ready to escape; obsessed with treasure hunting and “always scheming for the ultimate heist.” Grew up on the streets, honed her talent for thievery, and became one of the most sought-after thieves in the city — yet always felt something was missing (M01249, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09; 002 §Original Concept - Female Thief). 2023-02-09 — revised the same day into the final version (M01252–M01260, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions): Resolve stat, replaces Himbo; magic thief rather than standard; switch to male ; female-gaze simp bait; major social anxiety; suddenly vanishes; “Magic batman with social anxiety”; close with Sable through shared materialism; primary comedic relief — supersedes the female concept above . Provenance Raw: M01249–M01251 (original profile), M01252–M01260 (revision) (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast; 2023-02-09) Cleaned: 000 Part 5 §Rhea Scala (character that was changed) Docs: 002 §Rhea Scala (Magic Batman) - Changed from Original Female Concept Ledger: character rows R07 (M01249–M01251 original concept; M01252–M01260 thief revision) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Ruby","group":"Characters","url":"characters/ruby.html","text":"Ruby CANON A ~2-year-old created being — a homunculus named for her eyes — who joins the party as its scatter-brained, pure-hearted back-line healer while searching for what it means to be “real.” Summary — Ruby is a homunculus connected to [[gideon-azurith|Gideon]] and [[eris|Eris]]'s research into life-force manipulation; Gideon borrowed another person’s soul to create her (M02178–M02179, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Canonically 2 years old per the ages list (M01217–M01218, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09), she may appear older. In the browser proto she is implemented as the party’s only healer — the back-line “Mender” (002 §Main Party header, CANON — IMPLEMENTED ). She was first drafted under the working name Lalateya as a metal-bodied, Strength-stat frontline homunculus; that early framing is preserved in Design history below and is superseded by the healer version. She carries forward across iterations, from the Skyward Cinders roster into the current game. Identity Named Ruby “for her eyes”; the name was chosen because a ruby is an alchemical item/tool — “great she is called ruby cuz she is a magical item,” fitting the zero-to-hero alchemy theme (M00988–M00998, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-05). Age: 2 — a created being; the canonical ages list gives “Ruby: 2” (M01217–M01218, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Affinity: Electric (002 §Ruby; affinity grouping “Electric - Percival, Ruby, Lysander”). Dog-breed comparison: Labrador Retriever (M03029, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-13). “A lot of me is in Ruby Sable so I like them too” (M04966, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). Personality Scatter-brained, in a constant state of analytical information overload — like a puppy constantly distracted by new things (M02413–M02416, FreshToast, 2023-02-18). Dialogue consultation trait: ignorant about common-sense situations, mostly hilariously useless, but will sometimes see the raw truth of the world due to fresh eyes (002 §Ruby) — the opposite of [[thane|Thane]]'s consultation trait (002 §Thane). Curious and inquisitive; innocent and pure-hearted despite dark origins; sees beauty and wonder in mundane things; emotionally intelligent despite lack of worldly knowledge; literal-minded but occasionally profound (002 §Ruby). Flaws (002 §Ruby): naively trusting; easily distracted; lacks common sense socially; accidentally insensitive; struggles with moral situations beyond black/white; may have built-in limitations or programming she must overcome. Ruby cries 2 times (noted specifically) (M02191, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Background Motivations Connected to Gideon and Eris’s research into life-force manipulation; a created being who escaped or was abandoned by her creators (002 §Ruby). Homunculi require sacrificing a portion of your flesh and soul; Gideon borrowed another’s soul for Ruby but performed the ritual correctly (own soul) for [[citrine|Citrine]]; the player later finds out a little about the soul used to make Ruby (M02178–M02179, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). The borrowed soul is her original “defect”: her programmed purpose clashes with the innate morality a real soul carries. [[lieutenant-milf|Lieutenant Milf]] had hands in Ruby’s creation (and in [[gaius-wornwall|Gaius]]'s expulsion) (M01221/M01225, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Ruby has an absent father (M01208–M01210, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08). Motivations (002 §Ruby): understand what she is and why she exists; experience everything the world has to offer; protect those who show her kindness; prove she has value beyond her origins; uncover the truth about Gideon’s and Eris’s experiments. Goals (002 §Ruby): learn the world firsthand; confront Gideon about her creation and purpose; define her own identity; find acceptance and belonging with the party. Abilities Traits Party position: 2nd line (Back) (002 §Ruby); proto confirms back-line placement. Can move somewhat in combat but prefers not to; reliable through flexibility (M01874–M01879, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-13). Electric affinity — possibly unstable or growing powers; support and utility specialist; unpredictable abilities reflecting her scattered nature; may have hidden potential related to her origins; adaptable — learns new applications of her powers quickly (002 §Ruby). Exploration skill — decoy copy: Ruby can send a copy of herself out so enemies attack the copy instead of the party (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). See [[character-skills]]. DEBATED Healer vs. melee: the early-concept homunculus profile calls Ruby a “frontline melee character (with Thane)” who “lacks ability to channel magic herself but can act as catalyst for others” — while the proto implements her as a back-line dedicated healer/support mage (role “Mender”). Both keep her in a support function; the implemented version is the runnable truth for combat, the catalyst/melee framing is unimplemented lore (002 §Ruby DEBATED ; and see Design history ). Weapon note: “Ruby arbitrarily has a hammer now because you asked” (M04056, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; ledger marks this row partial — the hammer provenance is not logged in 000). CANON — IMPLEMENTED Proto combat values (proto: src/core/combat/content.ts , src/core/combat/types.ts ; 002 §Ruby) id ruby , role “Mender”, glyph ✨, maxHp 26, maxAp 3, speed 7 (fastest hero), str 4 / mag 14 / def 6 / res 12 / acc 92 / crit 6. Start position 4 (Back line) ; her heals cast from ranks 3–4. She is the party’s only healer : Mend (2 AP) — focused single-target heal, 13. Bless (1 AP) — applies Strength Up to an ally. Purify (2 AP) — strips 2 afflictions + heals 5 (cleanse). Benediction (4 AP, finisher) — a wave of healing across the whole party, 11 each. Relationships Gaius Ruby pairing noted (M01223–M01224, FreshToast, 2023-02-09); the Gaius/Isaac–Ruby dynamic is inspired by Reigen and Mob (Mob Psycho 100) (M01189, FreshToast, 2023-02-08; “Isaac and Ruby have a dynamic inspired by Reigen and Mob?” — M02646, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). The academy is core to Gaius and somewhat related to Ruby, giving [[locke|Locke]] her reason to visit it (M01227, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). [[citrine|Citrine]] — her rival and, in effect, her sister: “just a shinier psychopath version of her” (M00959, FreshToast, 2023-02-05), with inverted colours — yellow eyes and red hair vs Ruby (M01289, FreshToast, 2023-02-09); loyal to and cared for by her creator, like Jinx from Arcane (M01208–M01210, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08). The party becomes her “family” and teachers about the world; Locke becomes protective of her innocence (002 §Ruby). PROPOSED Bonds with [[percival|Percival]] over shared curiosity — not in the Discord notes; the source only pairs Ruby and Percival via shared Electric affinity (002 §Ruby). Fills out [[lyra|Lyra]]'s dungeon survey with adorably earnest responses (002 §Ruby). Team-skill pairing: Ruby/Thane — position-based team skills, e.g. when Astrid is behind Thane or Ruby she can do a thing (M05392–M05394, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). Role Arc (002 §Ruby) Early: comic relief through innocent questions and misunderstandings; the party gradually realizes she’s not quite “normal”; provides fresh perspective and unexpected wisdom. Mid: a story about ends justifying means, connected to the Gideon and Eris themes; discovers her true nature/origins; must confront what she was made to be vs who she wants to be. Late: confronts Gideon about her creation; chooses her own path and identity; rejects being defined by her creator’s intentions; proves her humanity through love and sacrifice. Citrine sisterhood arc: Citrine comes to see Ruby as a wayward sister but cannot reconcile ironclad loyalty to her creator with Ruby’s intentions; the sisters fight; Ruby kills Citrine but spares the creator in her name (M02077, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Ruby is a death/regret-theme avenue “once she kills Citrine” (M04659, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). Emotional beats: the heartbreaking “What am I?” scene; the party affirming they care about who she is, not what she is; her "},{"title":"Sable","group":"Characters","url":"characters/sable.html","text":"Sable CANON A 19-year-old lucky, loud, lesbian bounty hunter who fights for money and thrills — always thinking about money, girls, or the guitar riff from Free Bird. Summary — Sable is the party’s most mobile, most AP-efficient fighter (M01875, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-13): an energetic, impulsive mercenary from a working-class background who joins [[locke|Locke]]'s party as a business opportunity and grows to genuinely care. She has high luck and a family that loves her — the deliberate inverse of [[clara|Marie/Clara]]'s low luck and hateful family (M01222, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-09). She is not yet implemented in the browser proto — her whole combat kit is design-intent only (002 §Main Party header; 002 §Sable). Identity Age: 19 — corrected from “early 20s”; the ages list gives 19 (M01217, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Affinity: Fiery (002 §Sable). Luck: High luck; has a family that loves her (M01222, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-09). Appearance: she’ll have a wolf-cut / messy-bob hairstyle ; early art WIP had trouble with the pose and making her look younger (M01444–M01447, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Dog-breed comparison: Border Collie (M03029, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-13). “A lot of me is in Ruby Sable so I like them too” (M04966, FreshToast, 2023-11-15). Personality Always thinking about: money, girls, or the guitar riff from Free Bird at any given moment (M02413–M02414, FreshToast, 2023-02-18). Gay (M02028, FreshToast, 2023-02-14) — but written feminine, not leaning into the tomboy-lesbian trope: “Being a lesbian doesn’t guarantee her becoming a tomboy,” “All kinds of girls be fuckin other girls”; not particularly tomboy or delicate either (M02028–M02034, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). “Cute Loud Gremlin” — her archetype tag from the scrapped meet-order list (M01905–M01916, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). Energetic and impulsive — acts first, thinks later; shameless flirt but genuinely romantic at heart; loves the thrill of adventure and combat; materialistic but not greedy — enjoys nice things and sharing them; fiercely protective of friends despite her casual attitude; uses humor and bravado to mask deeper insecurities (002 §Sable). Flaws (002 §Sable): impulsive and reckless in pursuit of thrills; materialistic streak can make her seem mercenary; avoids serious emotional conversations with jokes; struggles to admit when she’s scared or hurt; too competitive, turning everything into a contest. Background Motivations Bounty hunter/mercenary who works for money and thrills; comes from a working-class background — learned to fight for survival (002 §Sable). CANON Tracks a specific list of 7 outlaws (mostly henchmen for other villains), hunting them for bounty to feed herself and her family. One of the hunters pursuing [[leon|Leon]] is on this list (002 §Sable; 000 Part 5/8). Motivations (002 §Sable): make enough money to live comfortably and support herself; prove she’s the best fighter/hunter in the business; find genuine connection and maybe settle down with the right woman; experience life to the fullest — no regrets. Goals (002 §Sable): complete high-value bounties on her list; protect party members (even when they’re also on bounty lists); eventually choose between money and loyalty to friends; win the heart of someone special. Abilities Traits All of Sable’s combat kit is design intent — she is not a built hero in the proto (002 §Sable). Party position: 1st line (Front) (002 §Sable). Most mobile and most efficient at using AP in the party (M01875, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-13); has an easy time moving with skills and more modular skills (M01874/M01880, FreshToast, 2023-02-13); “let Damian/Cyril and Sable schmoove a bit” (M02579, FreshToast, 2023-02-20). PROPOSED — not implemented Has skills that directly increase or scale based on [[escalation]]. The escalation counter itself IS implemented in the proto, but no escalation-scaling player skill exists yet (002 §Sable). Agile melee fighter — possibly dual-wielding or quick weapons; excels at chaining attacks and building momentum; can reposition easily and exploit openings; high-risk, high-reward playstyle (002 §Sable). PROPOSED Naturally lucky — could make the luck scale/stat grow faster for her, tying her luck into a player-facing number (M04543, FreshToast, 2023-10-22). Exploration: teaches Locke how to throw her voice (M01683, FreshToast, 2023-02-11) — part of the DEBATED exploration-skills scheme. Attack animation (M05689–M05693, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2024-09-06): cat-style butt wiggle pre-pounce (from a Reddit cat video), “a low-stanced sort of sway,” as the anticipation idle animation while selecting a skill and targets — “and the music syncs with her dance.” Relationships Becomes close with [[rhea-falafill|Rhea]] (magic batman) through shared materialism and mutual attraction (002 §Sable); the sourced pairing lineage is Marie Sable (M01231, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) plus “Sable and Thief become close through their shared materialism” (M01259, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). One of the hunters pursuing Leon is on Sable’s list — creates moral conflict (002 §Sable). Initially sees the party as a business opportunity, grows to genuinely care (002 §Sable). PROPOSED Bonds with Locke over both being “self-made” vs nobility — not in the Discord notes (002 §Sable). Friendly rivalry with other frontline fighters (002 §Sable). Team-skill pairing: Astrid/Sable (M05392, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). Role Arc (002 §Sable) Early: joins for money/bounties; treats the adventure as a job with benefits; comic relief through shameless flirting and materialistic comments. Mid: discovers Leon has a bounty — must choose between money and friendship; meets/develops a relationship with Rhea through shared love of treasure; has a moment where choosing party over profit costs her significantly. Late: fully commits to the party’s cause over personal gain; romantic subplot reaches resolution; proves loyalty in dramatic fashion; “learns that real treasure is friends made along the way (but also actual treasure is nice).” Emotional beats: chooses to protect Leon instead of claiming the bounty; admits she’s scared of losing people; romantic confession scene; final battle fought for those she loves, not money. Plot impact: her bounty-hunter connections provide an information network; bounty-vs-loyalty tension drives her development; represents found family vs transactional relationships; the romance subplot adds warmth and representation. Open questions DEBATED Weapon identity: the initial party lineup listed Sable - Ranged - Bow (M02164, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16), while the later profile frames her as an agile melee fighter, possibly dual-wielding on the front line (002 §Sable). Not reconciled — she is unimplemented in the proto, so neither is runnable truth yet. PROPOSED Escalation-scaling skills — awaiting implementation (002 §Sable). Romance resolution: “Rhea or other woman ” — the partner is not locked (002 §Sable). Design history 2023-02-09 — first appears in the ages list, age 19 (M01217, CrunchyConniptions); luck-contrast pairing vs Marie: high luck, loving family (M01222, spirit_noodle); pairings Marie Sable (M01231) and Sable Thief via shared materialism (M01259, FreshToast). 2023-02-11 — first art WIP; wolf-cut/messy-bob hairstyle (M01444–M01447, FreshToast); teaches Locke to throw her voice (M01683, FreshToast). 2023-02-13 — mobility profile: most mobile, most AP-efficient; modular skills (M01874–M01880, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-14 — “Sable’s gay”; written feminine, explicitly not the tomboy-lesbian trope (M02028–M02034, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions); “Cute Loud Gremlin” archetype tag in the later-scrapped isekai meet-order fork (M01905–M01916, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-16 — initial lineup: Sable - Ranged - Bow (M02164, CrunchyConniptions). Contradicted by the later agile-melee front-line framing — see Open questions. 2023-02-18 — voice locked: always thinking about money"},{"title":"Sha'worr-ma the Second","group":"Characters","url":"characters/sha-worr-ma.html","text":"Sha’worr-ma the Second PROPOSED A stand-alone character concept — a chaos-philosopher who breaks every chain — whose celebrated description was earmarked for reuse on the person who helped [[percival|Percy]] make [[conflux|Conflux]]. Identity provisional. Summary — Sha’worr-ma the Second is a full character write-up posted alongside the second Rhea profile (M03523, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12). The name landed — “Shaworr’ma though, now we’re talking. THAT’s a name” (M03527, FreshToast, 2023-08-12) — and her personality paragraph was explicitly earmarked: “I like this part and am gonna steal it for the person that helped Percy make Conflux” (M03535, FreshToast, 2023-08-12). So the name and description may attach to the Conflux-creator rather than remaining a separate character; treat the identity as not yet settled (002 §Sha’worr-ma, provisional-identity note). FreshToast’s verdict on the prose: “It’s very dramatic, if a bit waffle-y” (M03537, 2023-08-12). Appearance Sinewy elegance, both striking and unsettling; intense, almost hypnotic eyes that “burn with an unquenchable thirst for the unattainable”; alabaster complexion contrasted by ink-black tattoos of cryptic symbols on her forearms, representing her refined and unrestricted desires. Every movement exudes confidence, calculated grace, and a hint of chaos (M03523, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12). Personality A contradiction personified — to some a philosopher, to others a lunatic. Like a flame, unpredictable, “illuminating paths of liberation while also consuming everything in her wake.” Both seeker and destroyer; the allure of anarchy is a game and a challenge, and she thrives amidst chaos, finding it the truest form of liberation (M03523, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12). This is the paragraph FreshToast earmarked for the Conflux-helper reuse (M03535). Goals Motivation To break every chain, visible or invisible, and to guide — or force — society to recognize and embrace its purest, unfiltered desires (M03523, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12). Believes only by liberating one’s deepest, most genuine desires can true liberation be attained; her extreme interpretation drives her to force this perspective on others, believing it an act of emancipation (M03523). Conflict The more she strives to free others, the more entangled she becomes in the very chains she seeks to break — a mission rooted in freedom that often results in tyranny, making her both liberator and oppressor. She fears that, in her quest for true freedom, she may have enslaved herself to her own ideology (M03523, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12). Background Raised in a strict, regimented society that stifled individuality; a chance encounter with a forbidden text about “the pure essence of freedom” ignited a flame that grew into an inferno, leading her to rebel, question, and renounce her past, then journey through the world until her evolving philosophy set her against “the very fabric of society itself” (M03523, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12). Open questions PROPOSED Does “Sha’worr-ma the Second” survive as her own character, or does the name/description fold into the Conflux-creator? Note that [[pandora|Pandora]] was later named as the person who helps Percival make Conflux (M04343, FreshToast, 2023-10-17) — the reuse target and this concept were never explicitly reconciled. The name is a food pun (shawarma), sibling to [[rhea-falafill|Rhea Falafill]]'s falafel pun (M03526–M03530, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12). Design history 2023-08-12 — full profile posted by CrunchyConniptions immediately after the second Rhea write-up (M03523). 2023-08-12 — name celebrated (“THAT’s a name”, M03527, FreshToast); personality paragraph earmarked for the person that helped Percy make Conflux — “steal away. I am the flinger, you are the catcher” (M03535, M03538–M03540, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions); prose judged “very dramatic, if a bit waffle-y” (M03537, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M03523–M03540 (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast, spirit_noodle; 2023-08-12); cross-ref M04343 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 lines 5741–5746, 5828–5832 region (Sha’worr-ma / food-pun / Conflux reuse) Docs: 002 §Sha’worr-ma the Second (Alternative Character Concept) PROPOSED ; 002 §Helps Percival make Conflux Ledger: row “Decision: the ‘Sha’worr-ma is a contradiction personified…’ paragraph is liked and will be stolen/reused…” (M03526–M03538, R19) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Silas","group":"Characters","url":"characters/silas.html","text":"Silas PROPOSED An underdeveloped character whose one established plot is a romance with the detective hunting him down. Summary — Silas is a named character whose plot the group agreed “needs some work” (M02346, FreshToast, 2023-02-16; M02343, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). His one workshopped hook: “Silas has his own romance with the detective hunting him down” — and the detective “can be gril” (M02363–M02364, FreshToast, 2023-02-16), to which Crunchy responded “thats hot / being hunted” (M02366–M02367, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). The name “Silas” itself is established (M02080, FreshToast, 2023-02-15; 002 §Silas). Role Arc Plot explicitly unfinished: “We need to work out Silas and Thane’s plots” (M02343, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16); “Silas needs some work” (M02346, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Romance with the detective hunting him down; the detective can be a girl (M02363–M02364, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Working on a “Name for Silas” was logged as a task for Lettuce (spirit_noodle) alongside map, story and playtest work (M02401, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-17). Open questions DEBATED The hunted-by-an-investigator romance premise is nearly identical to the one attached to [[leon|Leon]] (M01396, FreshToast, 2023-02-10) and later to [[damian|Damian]] — which character finally owns it is unresolved (see [[leon]] §Open questions). PROPOSED Everything beyond the name and the romance hook remains to be designed (“Needs work on plot” — 002 §Silas). Design history 2023-02-15 — name dropped in a naming session: “Silas” (M02080, FreshToast), alongside Locke Evertide / Leon Erhardt (M02079) and Yvette (M02081). 2023-02-16 — plot flagged as needing work (M02343, CrunchyConniptions; M02346, FreshToast); romance-with-pursuing-detective hook added, detective can be a girl; “thats hot / being hunted” (M02363–M02367, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-17 — “Name for Silas” assigned as an open task (M02401, CrunchyConniptions). Provenance Raw: M02080, M02343, M02346, M02363–M02367, M02401 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-02-15 → 2023-02-17) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 (Silas name; plots needing work) Docs: 002 §Silas Ledger: character rows R12 (name candidates M02079–M02081) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Sir Valerius","group":"Characters","url":"characters/sir-valerius.html","text":"Sir Valerius PROPOSED A knight hunting the Daemon of Want. Summary — Sir Valerius is defined by a single canonical fact: he is hunting the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]] (M02902, FreshToast, 2023-03-05) — the spirit behind [[ajax|Ajax]]'s pact and [[lysander|Lysander]]'s inherited power. Crunchy endorsed the idea (“this” — M02903, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-05). Nothing else about him was developed. Role Arc Hunting the Daemon of Want (M02902, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). Open questions PROPOSED Doc 002 notes that [[thanes-foil|Thane’s Foil]] “could be connected to Sir Valerius” — the Foil’s nobility or actual knight status is to be determined (002 §Thane’s Foil, Notes). No raw message makes this connection explicit. Design history 2023-03-05 — sole mention: “Sir Valerius is hunting the Daemon of Want” (M02902, FreshToast), approved by Crunchy (M02903). Provenance Raw: M02902–M02903 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-03-05) Cleaned: 000 (Hunting the Daemon of Want) Docs: 002 §Sir Valerius; 002 §Thane’s Foil (possible connection) Ledger: character row R16 (“Sir Valerius is hunting the Daemon of Want”, M02902) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Soren","group":"Characters","url":"characters/soren.html","text":"Soren DEBATED Cold-affinity name on the roster — nothing more has been written about him. Summary — Soren exists in the record solely as one of the four Cold-affinity characters: “Cold - Damian, Astrid, Soren, Cultboy” (M03051/M03052, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). He has no plot, personality, visual, or relationships in any note. Doc 002 flags him UNDERDEVELOPED — NEEDS DECISION . Description Affinity: Cold (M03051/M03052, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). This is the only sourced fact about him. Unlike [[damian|Damian]] and [[astrid|Astrid]], he does not appear in the 2024 team-skill roster (M05392, FreshToast, 2024-04-11) or in the party-as-dogs list (M03029, FreshToast, 2023-03-13). Open questions DEBATED Doc 002 status: UNDERDEVELOPED — NEEDS DECISION , options: (1) develop fully as a Cold-affinity party member, (2) merge with [[damian|Damian]] or [[cultboy|Cultboy]] (all Cold affinity, minimal development), (3) remove if redundant. The doc’s recommendation: consider merging all Cold-affinity underdeveloped characters (Soren, Damian, Cultboy) into ONE fully developed character, or removing if the party is already large enough (002 §Soren). Design history 2023-03-15 — sole appearance: listed under Cold in the known-affinities roster (M03051/M03052, FreshToast) — current (and only) state. Provenance Raw: M03051–M03052 (FreshToast, 2023-03-15) Cleaned: 000 (known-affinities list) Docs: 002 §Soren Ledger: affinity-roster row citing M03051 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Steele","group":"Characters","url":"characters/steele.html","text":"Steele CANON The other of [[lysander|Lysander]]'s two pawns — orphaned, adoptive sibling of [[flynt|Flynt]], mildly antagonistic until he “goes off the deep end,” who inherits the broken [[warpath|Warpath]] and dies in the final battle. Summary — Steele is one of the pair of orphaned, adoptive siblings who work personally for Lysander: largely loyal-to-a-fault, goofy pawns (M02335–M02341, 2023-02-16; M03394, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). He is mildly antagonistic at best until he goes off the deep end (M03397, FreshToast, 2023-06-04), and CANON dies at the end — by either the player’s hand or Lysander’s in the final battle (M03396, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Relationships Orphaned, adoptive sibling of [[flynt|Flynt]]; both work personally for [[lysander|Lysander]] (M03394, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Role Arc Mildly antagonistic at best until he “goes off the deep end” (M03397, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). CANON Picks up [[warpath|Warpath]] after Flynt dies ; the weapon is broken from then on and becomes a relic that can temporarily retake its complete form (“but like energy or something”) — carrying the influence of the dead visibly into the latter parts of the story (M04661, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). CANON Dies at the end — by either the player’s hand or Lysander’s in the final battle (M03396, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). (Note: Lysander’s phase-2 staging has him devouring his own party members, with the last compatriot crawling away as a player choice — see [[lysander]] §Boss fight; 002 §Lysander.) Design history 2023-02-16 — conceived unnamed: the remaining 2 of Lysander’s party are a pair of siblings, largely unimportant, loyal to a fault, goofy, probably not all that bad — pawns (M02335–M02341, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2023-06-04 — named Steele ; orphaned adoptive siblings working personally for Lysander; mildly antagonistic until he goes off the deep end; dies at the end by the player’s or Lysander’s hand (M03394–M03397, FreshToast). 2023-10-24 — inherits the broken Warpath after Flynt’s death; it becomes a relic that can temporarily retake its complete form (M04661, FreshToast) — matches the current presentation above. Provenance Raw: M02335–M02341 (2023-02-16); M03394–M03397 (2023-06-04); M04661 (2023-10-24) — FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions Cleaned: 000 (Lysander’s party siblings; Warpath) Docs: 002 §Steele; 006 §Warpath Ledger: rows R13 (sibling pawns), R25 (Warpath) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Tenebrae","group":"Characters","url":"characters/tenebrae.html","text":"Tenebrae CANON The daemon of mania of Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium — trying to cross into the world, manipulating [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]], and (with him) the in-house source of all the game’s conflict. Summary — Tenebrae is a daemon of mania “that is trying to cross over into the world and causing general fuckery” (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). She is one of the “Big 6” characters alongside [[the-4-boyes|the 4 Boyes]] and Cyrus (M04284, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Her manipulation is what turns Cyrus sour — though he is clever and ends up using her in the end. Identity CANON Daemon of mania (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). One of the Big 6 characters: “the 4 boyes, Cyrus and Tenebrae” (M04284, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Background Motivations CANON Trying to cross over into the world; causing general “fuckery” (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Role Function CANON Entangled with Cyrus; it’s “the lack of support he recieves and manipulation of Tenebrae that turns him sour” (M04290, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). “But he’s pretty clever, so ends up using her in the end” — Cyrus is ultimately her user, not merely her victim (M04291, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). She is the source of much of the game’s conflict: the team decided against any antagonist faction to [[the-luminarium|the Luminarium]] and instead to “chalk up all the bad shit to Cyrus and Tenebrae, keep it in house so as to keep it simple” (M04287, M04306–M04307, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Design history 2023-10-17 — introduced in the 4 Boyes roster write-up as the daemon of mania entangled with Cyrus, trying to cross over into the world (M04227, FreshToast); named to the Big 6 (M04284, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — antagonist-scope decision: no rival faction; all conflict routed through Cyrus and Tenebrae, kept “in house” and about the Luminarium (M04286–M04287, M04306–M04307, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M04227; M04284; M04286–M04291; M04306–M04307 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 §The 4 Boyes / Luminarium notes (Big 6, keep-it-in-house) Docs: 002 §Tenebrae; 002 §Cyrus: Relationships Dynamics Ledger: rows for Big 6 (M04284) and no-antagonist-faction decision (M04286–M04307) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Thane Farrow","group":"Characters","url":"characters/thane.html","text":"Thane Farrow CANON The party’s 34-year-old frontline vanguard: a wolf-motif ex-mentor haunted by his dead student, whose whole theme is death — up to and including a romance with Death itself. Summary — Thane Farrow is the tank of [[locke|Locke]]'s party: slowest, toughest, and most experienced. A former mentor whose mentee died, he carries survivor’s guilt, gallows humor, and a growing numbness to his own near-death experiences. Thematically he embodies “healthy mourning vs going off the deep end” and is the one who tells [[eris|Eris]] that death is truly final (M02242–M02244, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). He is one of the four heroes implemented in the browser proto (role “Vanguard”) (002 §Main Party header, CANON — IMPLEMENTED ). Identity Full name: Thane Farrow — surname proposed 2023-02-14 to differentiate him from Isaac (M01923, FreshToast) and approved 2023-02-15 (M02057, spirit_noodle). The rename rationale: character names should sound as distinct from one another as possible, cf. Elden Ring (M02058–M02060, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Age: 34 (ages list, M01217, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Affinity: Fiery (002 §Thane). Animal motif: “Wolfman” / “the wolf” (M01293–M01294, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) — paired against his Foil the Paladin, “the Lion” (M01416–M01417, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11). The motif ties to a sneak/exploration role: the wolf teaches Locke how to sneak and can grant a +5 defense exploration buff (002 §Thane). Dog-breed comparison: Anatolian Shepherd (M03029, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-13). Personality Death associations throughout — his foil character is life/paladin; the two “live in grey” together (002 §Thane). Has near-death experiences over the course of the game and becomes increasingly numb to them (M01832, FreshToast, 2023-02-12). Dialogue consultation trait: generally useful, with a whole lot of melancholy and subtle gallows humor (the opposite of [[ruby|Ruby]]) (002 §Thane). Flaws (002 §Thane): emotionally distant, gallows humor as defense mechanism; numb to danger and his own mortality; overly protective of Locke due to past failure; struggles to be vulnerable; may take unnecessary risks because he doesn’t fear death enough; trouble letting go and moving forward. Character moment: another character sits explaining where they got their scar — Thane cannot remember where he got his scars from. Too many to remember. All the same to him (M01211–M01214, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08). “I imagine Thane carries a lot of his comrades and wants and dreams with him. A bit of them lives with him.” (M04646, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-24) Background Motivations Was a mentor; his mentee dies — tragedy (M01828, FreshToast, 2023-02-12). Begins more experienced than Locke; haunted by his failure to protect his student; carries guilt and questions whether he’s worthy to teach or lead again (002 §Thane). Motivations (002 §Thane): atone for failing his previous mentee; protect Locke and the party from the same fate; come to terms with death and loss in a healthy way; prove to himself he can still make a difference; eventually accept that death is natural, not something to defy or fear. Goals (002 §Thane): ensure Locke doesn’t repeat his mistakes; help the party survive; confront his own trauma and survivor’s guilt; find peace with Death (literal spirit and concept); tell Eris that death is truly final — an important plot moment. Abilities Traits Party position: 1st line (Front) ; combat-focused (002 §Thane). Positioning: static — Thane (with Isaac) struggles to move in combat, more specialized; can move somewhat but prefers not to; reliable through toughness (M01874–M01881, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-13; M02576–M02579, FreshToast, 2023-02-20). DEBATED Has a taunt ability — design intent from his earliest statblock (M02169, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16), NOT yet implemented ; the proto’s Thane has no taunt, and the “no purely defensive non-taunt abilities” rule keeps taunt on the roadmap (002 §Thane). [[shields-and-wounded|Shield]] system: Thane gets 2 shields “because special boi” in the revised one-shield system (M02492–M02497, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). Exploration: teaches Locke how to sneak (M01683, FreshToast, 2023-02-11) — part of the DEBATED exploration-skills scheme. CANON — IMPLEMENTED Proto combat values (proto: src/core/combat/content.ts , src/core/combat/types.ts ; 002 §Thane) id thane , role “Vanguard”, glyph 🛡️, maxHp 44 (party tank — highest HP), maxAp 3, speed 4 (slowest hero), str 14 / mag 2 / def 16 / res 8 / acc 90 / crit 8. Start position 1 (Front line) . Cleave (2 AP) — heavy swing at a near foe, 10 phys. Bulwark (1 AP) — braces behind the shield; raises own Defense and Resistance (his defensive ability in code, in place of a taunt). Rending Blow (2 AP) — 8 phys + Bleed. Earthsplitter (4 AP, finisher) — shatters the ground beneath the whole enemy line, 9 phys to all. Relationships Locke Thane pairing noted (M01228, FreshToast, 2023-02-09); he shares the moment where Locke struggles to travel far from home (M04135, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Romantic interest: Death (Spirit) — the only romance in the relationship structure is between Thane and Death (M01223–M01224, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). His daemon is [[death|Death]] itself: moderately powerful but not violent, prefers to comfort the dying, surprisingly timid (M02665, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). DD is enbie (non-binary), and FreshToast wants a cute nickname for them — the nickname is for their dynamic with Thane, who uses irreverent nicknames (M02745–M02759, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). Ties to Eris — “perhaps he’s [the] one who tells her death is truly final”; her arc of unhealthy mourning contrasts his (M02242–M02244, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Theme: healthy mourning vs going off the deep end (002 §Thane). [[thanes-foil|Thane’s Foil]] (the Prideful Paladin, “the Lion”) — his life-themed counterpart; they “live in grey” together. The Paladin does not have to be in the hero party — he can be independent (M02335–M02341, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Team-skill pairing: Ruby/Thane (M05392–M05394, FreshToast, 2024-04-11). Role Arc Eventually hunted down by Thane’s Foil (the Paladin) or the death spirit (002 §Thane). “Have Thane be perfect counter for boss fight but lean into difficulty, thus making Thane look cool af” (002 §Thane). Death/regret theme avenues run through Flynt/Locke and Thane (M04658, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). Portraits exist in first draft (M04734–M04737, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-28). Open questions DEBATED Taunt: early statblock skill (M02169) vs. proto kit with Bulwark instead — taunt remains design intent on the roadmap (002 §Thane). DEBATED Who hunts Thane down — his Foil the Paladin, or the death spirit? (002 §Thane) Unresolved joke/aside: “make Thane’s shoes filthy…maybe?” (M01401, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-10; logged in 009 §Unresolved Questions). DD’s cute nickname for the Thane dynamic — never settled (M02745–M02759, FreshToast, 2023-02-25). Design history 2021-08-01 — the name “Thane” first appears in the notes, years before the rest of the party (M00423, FreshToast). 2023-02-05 — “I think Ruby and Thane should be our 2 frontline melee characters” (M01006, FreshToast); attribute concept Thane = Toughness (M01009, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-08 — the scar gag: too many scars to remember (M01211–M01214, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-09 — age 34 (M01217); the only romance in the party structure: Thane Death (M01223–M01224); “Thane wolfman” motif floated (M01293–M01294, FreshToast). 2023-02-11 — animal motifs fixed: Thane the wolf, the Paladin the Lion (M01416–M01417, CrunchyConniptions); Thane teaches Locke to sneak (M01683, FreshToast). 2023-02-12 — backstory: was a mentor, mentee dies, tragedy (M01828); multiple near-death experiences, increasing numbness (M01832, FreshToast). 2023-02-14 → 02-15 — surname changed to Farrow to differentiate from Isaac; approved (M01923, FreshToast; M02057, spirit_noodle). 2023-02-16 — initial lineup Thane - Melee"},{"title":"Thane's Foil (Paladin)","group":"Characters","url":"characters/thanes-foil.html","text":"Thane’s Foil (Paladin) CANON A zealous, life-affirming paladin — [[thane|Thane]]'s perfect foil (death vs life), who hunts the Death spirit and lives in a world of black and white. Summary — Thane’s Foil is a paladin with life associations who “lives in a world of black and white” (M01376, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). He is not strictly in the wrong from his own worldview — but he’s a bit of a dick about his righteousness, overreacts (M01384, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-10), and acts as judge, jury, and executioner. Independent of [[lysander|Lysander]]'s hero party (M02335–M02341, 2023-02-16), he hunts Thane and the Death spirit and provides a big, intentionally hard boss fight that Thane mechanically counters — to make Thane look cool (002 §Thane’s Foil). The character was never given a real name ( placeholder_name: true ). Identity Appearance Title: Paladin or Knight; possible names to be determined — 002 suggests Valerius connections or a “Righteous”-themed name (002 §Thane’s Foil). Affinity: likely Fiery or Electric (life-affirming elements) (002 §Thane’s Foil). Connection: noble or religious order (002 §Thane’s Foil). Animal motif: as Thane is the wolf, the Paladin is the Lion (M01416–M01417, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11). “Paladin must have winged bucket helm” (M01831, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-12). Personality Life associations — Thane is death, the Foil is life (M01376, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). Paladin dedicated to the preservation of life and justice; lives in a world of black and white, no moral grey areas (M01376, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). Not strictly in the wrong from his worldview, but a bit of a dick about his righteousness; “the paladin is overreacting” (M01384, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-10). Acts as judge, jury and executioner based on his perspective of justice; genuinely believes he’s doing righteous work (002 §Thane’s Foil). Cannot comprehend Thane’s acceptance of death as natural; sees the Death spirit as an abomination that must be destroyed; zealous and uncompromising; honorable in his own rigid way (002 §Thane’s Foil). Background Motivations Member of nobility or a holy order dedicated to protecting life; possibly connected to [[clara|Clara]]'s past (childhood friend, former betrothed, or family ally); lost someone to death, driving his zealous mission; sees himself as a crusader against death itself (002 §Thane’s Foil). Motivations: eradicate death spirits and anything that “embraces” death; bring Thane to justice for perceived crimes (associating with the Death spirit); uphold a rigid code of justice without mercy; prove that absolute righteousness can triumph over moral ambiguity; save people from themselves — even if they don’t want saving (002 §Thane’s Foil). Goals: hunt down and destroy Death (the spirit); capture or kill Thane for consorting with the death spirit; enforce his order’s laws absolutely; eliminate all “unnatural” beings (possibly including [[ruby|Ruby]]) (002 §Thane’s Foil). Flaws: cannot see nuance or moral grey areas; righteousness blinds him to the harm he causes; overreacts to situations that don’t fit his worldview; refuses to listen or compromise; his pursuit of “justice” becomes persecution; pride in his righteous mission; cannot admit when he might be wrong (002 §Thane’s Foil). Relationships Dynamics With Thane: perfect foil — death vs life, grey morality vs black-and-white, acceptance vs defiance. Eventually hunts down Thane and/or the Death spirit; Thane is the perfect counter for the boss fight mechanically; thematically both hold extreme positions around the grey Thane lives in (002 §Thane’s Foil). Thane’s summarized arc includes “dealing with spirit hunters including his foil” (M02347, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). With Clara: knows Clara from her noble past; possibly a childhood friend or former betrothed; she knows the good person he used to be before zealotry consumed him (002 §Thane’s Foil). With the party: sees them as criminals or heretics for protecting Thane; may target other party members (Ruby as “unnatural,” [[leon|Leon]] as fugitive) (002 §Thane’s Foil). Not in Lysander’s hero party — the Prideful Paladin that Thane fights can be independent (M02335–M02341, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Abilities Traits Powerful paladin with holy/life magic; commands 2 henchmen in battle (fills 2 slots with them); skilled warrior and tactician (002 §Thane’s Foil). Big cool tough fight — a significant boss battle; mechanically countered by Thane, but the difficulty is intentionally high to make Thane look cool (002 §Thane’s Foil). Life-based abilities that contrast Thane’s death theme; possibly resurrection or healing powers; buff/support abilities for henchmen; a strong sense of honor prevents him from using underhanded tactics (002 §Thane’s Foil). Role Arc Role in story: mid-game antagonist representing absolutism and zealotry; thematic opposite to Thane’s arc; tests whether Thane has truly found peace with death (002 §Thane’s Foil). Confrontation: hunts the party, specifically targeting Thane and the Death spirit; Clara tries to reason with him based on their shared past; he cannot be reasoned with — too consumed by zealotry; epic boss fight where Thane shines as the perfect counter; the party must beat him but NOT kill him (002 §Thane’s Foil). Resolution: defeated but survives the encounter; may have a moment of doubt after defeat; possibly returns later, changed by the experience; Clara’s history with him adds emotional weight; serves as a warning about taking beliefs too far (002 §Thane’s Foil). Emotional beats: Clara’s plea to remember who he used to be; Thane’s calm acceptance contrasting with the Foil’s fury; a moment where his absolute certainty wavers; post-defeat realization that he’s become what he fought against (002 §Thane’s Foil). Plot impact: escalates danger and urgency for the party; provides a major boss fight showcasing Thane; explores justice vs vengeance through a different lens; contrasts healthy acceptance (Thane) vs unhealthy rejection (Foil) of death; his connection to Clara adds personal stakes; shows the dangers of absolute thinking and zealotry; thematic parallel to the other antagonists who go too far for “righteous” goals (002 §Thane’s Foil). Notes Should be formidable and threatening but not purely evil; the tragedy is that he genuinely believes he’s righteous (002 §Thane’s Foil). Nobility or actual knight status to be determined; could be connected to the [[sir-valerius|Sir Valerius]] character (002 §Thane’s Foil). Open questions His actual name (placeholder: “Thane’s Foil” / “the Paladin” / “Prideful Paladin”). Whether he is connected to Clara’s past, and how (childhood friend / former betrothed / family ally) (002 §Thane’s Foil). Whether he is nobility, an actual knight, or connected to Sir Valerius (002 §Thane’s Foil). Design history 2023-02-10 — conceived: “Thane foil, life associations? Paladin, lives in a world of black and white” (M01376, FreshToast); “The paladin is overreacting” (M01384, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-11 — animal motifs: Thane the wolf, the Paladin the Lion (M01416–M01417, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-12 — “Paladin must have winged bucket helm” (M01831, spirit_noodle). 2023-02-16 — placement settled: the Prideful Paladin that Thane fights does not have to be in the hero party — he can be independent (M02335–M02341, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions); folded into Thane’s arc summary as “spirit hunters including his foil” (M02347, FreshToast) — matches the current presentation above. Provenance Raw: M01376, M01384 (2023-02-10); M01416–M01417 (2023-02-11); M01831 (2023-02-12); M02335–M02341, M02347 (2023-02-16) — FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions, spirit_noodle Cleaned: 000 Parts 2, 5 (foil, Lion motif, winged helm, Prideful Paladin) Docs: 002 §Thane’s Foil; 009 §Paladin must have winged bucket helm Ledger: rows R08 (foil concept; Lion motif), R10 (winged bucket helm), R13 (Prideful Paladin independent) in Documentation/Verification/discord_prove"},{"title":"The 4 Boyes","group":"Characters","url":"characters/the-4-boyes.html","text":"The 4 Boyes CANON The four-student core cast of Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium — [[alistair|Alistair]], [[pandora|Pandora]], [[titan|Titan]] and [[colette|Colette]] — plus the design philosophy that binds them and the “Big 6” that completes the roster. Summary — The 4 Boyes are the ensemble protagonists of the Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium iteration, students at [[the-luminarium|the Luminarium]] magic academy. The game deliberately has no singular main protagonist — “Cyrus, our antagonist, is the keystone for our theme. We do not have a singular main protagonist” (M04271, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Together with [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]] and [[tenebrae|Tenebrae]] they form the “Big 6” — “It’s all we really need” (M04284–M04285, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Design philosophy CANON (M04226, FreshToast, 2023-10-17; 002 §The 4 Boyes + Cyrus) Each of the 4 Boyes is based on a nonmagical historical or fictional archetype . Considered: the thief, samurai, knight, explorer or cowboy. [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]] is both a young, archetypal fantasy mage and representative of a well-rounded sorcerer by Caelian standards. Their personal conflicts should be largely internal and — unlike Skyward Cinders — highly secondary to the main plot (with the exemption of Cyrus). Each of the 4 Boyes is meant to represent a general facet of Sorcery and magic . All are in their early 20s (M04243, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Cyrus, Alistair, Pandora and Titan are all ethnically typical for western Khorath (“like, mediterranean”); Colette is a bit darker (M04242, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). They each start with their affinity and the barest knowledge of magic; they’ve each “awakened” (M04315–M04316, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). The Big 6 CANON “The big 6 characters though are the 4 boyes, Cyrus and Tenebrae” (M04284, FreshToast, 2023-10-17): [[alistair|Alistair]] — Sylmiran male, ‘Frost’ affinity; represents specialisation. [[pandora|Pandora]] — Lycosian female, Victorian/Western inventor; represents practical sorcery and magical anomalies. [[titan|Titan]] — Lycosian male demi-human (placeholder name), wuxia martial artist; ‘Body’ affinity. [[colette|Colette]] — Lunoiran female, the “heart” of the group; represents the power of names and foreign interpretations of sorcery. [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]] — the main/final antagonist and thematic keystone. [[tenebrae|Tenebrae]] — the daemon of mania entangled with Cyrus. Beyond the Big 6, “there will be several” professors/mentors, “and others learning with them, who feature on the side” (M04282–M04283, FreshToast, 2023-10-17) — see [[nyssa]] and [[the-academy-head|the Academy’s Head]]. Story scope CANON Theme/message of the game is the “letting go” idea discussed earlier (M04265, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). No antagonist faction/rival group to the Luminarium : “Ok I’ve given it some thought. No antagonist faction to the Luminarium. We’re gonna keep it about the Luminarium” (M04306–M04307, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). All the bad stuff is chalked up to Cyrus and Tenebrae — “keep it in house so as to keep it simple” (M04287, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). River scene: the 4 Boyes at the riverside recharging their [[river-flask|flask]], a reflective moment on how far they’ve come — while one of the last scenes is not the boyes but the rival, who confesses his sins to the river yet tragically hardens his resolve to side with the daemon (M04090, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). PROPOSED Maybe each of the academy houses has a guide, and the one for the boyes’ house is cool and nice (M04329, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Design history 2023-10-13 — first “4 boyes” reference, in the reflective riverside/flask scene sketch for this iteration (M04090, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — design philosophy laid down: nonmagical archetypes, internal conflicts secondary to plot, each representing a facet of Sorcery (M04226, FreshToast); the full roster write-up “The 4 Boyes + Cyrus and current concepts regarding them” posted, with the note “(I’m gonna edit this over time)” (M04227–M04228, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — demographics fixed: western-Khorath Mediterranean for all but Colette (a bit darker); all early 20s (M04242–M04243, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — iteration named “Caelian Odyssy - The Luminarium”; team decides to focus on “those 5 specific characters” (the 4 Boyes + Cyrus) first, Cyrus being the most developed (M04245–M04251, M04272, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — theme anchored: “letting go”; Cyrus the keystone; no singular main protagonist (M04265, M04271, FreshToast); the Big 6 declared: the 4 Boyes, Cyrus and Tenebrae (M04284, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — antagonist-faction question raised by CrunchyConniptions and settled: none — keep it in house and about the Luminarium (M04286–M04287, M04306–M04307). Provenance Raw: M04090; M04226–M04228; M04242–M04243; M04245–M04251; M04265; M04271–M04272; M04282–M04285; M04286–M04287; M04302; M04306–M04307; M04315–M04316; M04329 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-10-13 → 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 §The 4 Boyes / Luminarium notes (lines ~6671–6720 region) Docs: 002 §CAELIAN ODYSSEY: THE LUMINARIUM - CHARACTERS; 002 §The 4 Boyes + Cyrus (Core Concept) Ledger: rows for “4 boyes” design philosophy, Big 6, iteration naming, no-antagonist-faction decision, boyes’ house guide in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Academy's Head","group":"Characters","url":"characters/the-academy-head.html","text":"The Academy’s Head PROPOSED The unnamed head of [[the-luminarium|the Luminarium]]: an ancient, wise figure who has seen the rise and fall of many powerful sorcerers — “a good old dude dishing out wisdom.” Summary — The Academy’s Head is “an ancient, wise character who’s seen the rise and fall of many powerful sorcerers. Holds secrets that can be central to the plot” (M04302, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). The character has no name yet; the concept was welcomed but not developed: “Oh I hadn’t given them much thought… I like a good old dude dishing out wisdom” (M04304–M04305, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Personality Role Ancient, wise character; has seen the rise and fall of many powerful sorcerers (M04302, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). Holds secrets that can be central to the plot (M04302). “the important part is old and wise” (M04303, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). “I like a good old dude dishing out wisdom” (M04305, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Context: beyond [[the-4-boyes|the Big 6]] there will be several professors/mentors featuring on the side (M04282–M04283, FreshToast, 2023-10-17) — the Head is the top of that supporting cast. Open questions No name, appearance, or plot specifics yet — only the “old and wise” core. Design history 2023-10-17 — pitched by CrunchyConniptions among academy-setting staples (tournaments, forbidden lore, magical creatures); FreshToast hadn’t given them much thought but likes a good old dude dishing out wisdom (M04302–M04305). Provenance Raw: M04302–M04305 (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast; 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 line 6686 region (Academy Head notes) Docs: 002 §The Academy’s Head; 003 §Location Guide (line 156 region) Ledger: row “The Academy’s Head: an ancient, wise character…” (M04302 M04303 M04305, R23) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Thorne \"Tabby\" Tumblemane","group":"Characters","url":"characters/thorne-tabby-tumblemane.html","text":"Thorne “Tabby” Tumblemane PROPOSED A colossal Catfolk-human gentle giant filler NPC — 7’2 with the afro, ~300 lbs, a lover not a fighter who just wants a quieter life. Summary — Thorne “Tabby” Tumblemane is a filler NPC concept: a colossal figure of 6’8 — 7’2 counting his vibrant auburn afro, “reminiscent of a lion’s mane” — weighing a robust ~300 lbs, of combined Catfolk and human lineage with the body strength of a human and the agility and reflexes of a Catfolk, plus pointed ears and captivating golden eyes (M03377, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04). His gentle, friendly personality is a stark contrast to his towering presence — a “ gentle giant ” (M03386–M03388, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-06-04). “Big cat man, lover not a fighter” (M03432, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). The name will probably change and he waits in reserve: “In my brain he shall reside until we have need of him” (M03403, M03434, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Name / Identity DEBATED The name: “Tabby’s a bit too on the (cat) nose” — “This is a cute idea though”; “I’ll probably rename him tho, sorry” (M03379–M03380, M03403, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Will probably be renamed. Lineage: mixed Catfolk and human — “cat man confirmed” via coin flip between wolf and cat: “wolf… no wait… cat… definetly cat” / “The coin flip always works” (M03425–M03431, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Represent-two-groups rationale: asked whether he should specifically be a cat man, Crunchy wanted him “just mixed” — “ He should represent two groups ” (M03405–M03406, M03409, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04); hence the human + demi-human (Catfolk) combination. Appearance 6’8 plus a six-inch vibrant auburn afro like a lion’s mane — ~7’2 total; ~300 lbs (M03377, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04). Pointed ears; captivating golden eyes (M03377). Too big for a carriage, so he prefers to travel by foot; favorite worn-out shoes (the only pair that fits); uncomplicated wardrobe, since attire for his physique is hard to find (M03377). DEBATED Scale pushback: “demi-humans aren’t superhumanly tall so more likely he’s near your height lol” (M03381, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Personality Gentle and friendly, in stark contrast to his towering presence (M03377, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04). Prefers to stay out of the limelight — not from shyness but to avoid the excessive attention his size attracts (M03377). “Big cat man, lover not a fighter”; “Might bottom if pressed” / “Maybe he is a bottom and proud” / “soft top” (M03432–M03438, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04). Conflict Flaw Grapples with the societal expectations of his intimidating physique; constantly at odds with local authorities and adventuring groups who want to use his strength when he’d prefer a quieter life (M03378, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04). Biggest flaw: lack of ambition and direction — content to take life as it comes, overlooking opportunities; his trusting nature lands him in local politics and conflicts he’d rather avoid (M03378). Role Arc A journey of acceptance and self-discovery: learning to navigate a world that sees him as more than what he wishes to be, and finding his place within it (M03378, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04). Intended slot: filler NPC — “We need filler NPCs anyway” (M03420, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Design notes CANON There’s a recognized “need for a Himbo in the game somewhere” (M03383, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04) — though this archetype was corrected from “himbo” toward “ gentle giant ” (M03385–M03388, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04), with [[flynt|Flynt]] noted as the party’s “straight up himbo” (M03392, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). “It’s a classic character type and a pretty contemporary one as well!” — “People love their gremlins, girlfailures and disastrous gays these days” (M03399, M03401, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Real-world inspiration: a Reddit post about a 6’8 , ~300 lb mixed Black-and-Mongolian man (M03410, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04) — “the most genetically threatening man on this earth” (M03415, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Design history 2023-06-04 — full write-up posted: colossal Catfolk-human “himbo,” worn-out shoes, limelight-avoidant; conflict and flaw sections (M03377–M03378, CrunchyConniptions), sparked by the Reddit post (M03410). 2023-06-04 — archetype corrected: not a himbo but a “gentle giant”; mix the two; Flynt already covers straight-up himbo (M03385–M03392, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions) — supersedes the “himbo” framing above . 2023-06-04 — identity settled for now: rename planned (“Tabby” too on the cat nose); should “represent two groups,” so mixed lineage; wolf-vs-cat coin flip lands on cat (“cat man confirmed”); “Big cat man, lover not a fighter”; shelved until needed (M03379–M03380, M03403–M03434). Provenance Raw: M03377–M03438 (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast; 2023-06-04) Cleaned: 000 Part 13 (Thorne “Tabby” Tumblemane) Docs: 002 §Thorne “Tabby” Tumblemane (Gentle Giant) Ledger: character rows for Thorne/gentle giant (2023-06-04) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Titan","group":"Characters","url":"characters/titan.html","text":"Titan CANON One of [[the-4-boyes|the 4 Boyes]]: a demi-human wuxia martial artist with the ‘Body’ affinity, who heals and punches. “Titan” is a placeholder — he needs a proper name. Summary — Titan is a Lycosian male demi-human in his early 20s, the wuxia protagonist / eastern martial artist archetype among the 4 Boyes of Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium. He fights unarmed, supplemented with magic, using his ‘Body’ affinity “to heal and punch” (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). He is the least developed of the Boyes — an explicitly flagged open design problem. Identity Lycosian male ( Demi-Human ); early 20s; ethnically typical for western Khorath (Mediterranean) (M04227, M04242–M04243, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Placeholder name, needs a proper one (M04227). FreshToast: “Also I’m trying not to get used to calling him that” (M04408, 2023-10-18). Archetype: wuxia protagonist / eastern martial artist (M04227). Abilities Traits ‘Body’ affinity — uses it to heal and punch (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Fights unarmed, supplemented with magic (M04227). Starts with his affinity and the barest knowledge of magic; has “awakened” (M04315–M04316, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Character concept Seems the type to respect magical creatures (M04321, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). PROPOSED Should get a magical pet (really likes animals) — “something that’s supposed to be dangerous but is surprisingly loyal and docile” ([[dianthus|Dianthus]] already covers the pet idea for the group, but the demi-human getting one was liked) (M04317–M04319, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Open questions Needs a proper name (M04227; M04408). Character direction entirely open: “I have no idea what to do with titan. I need direction” (M04392, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). DEBATED Sealing/binding magic concept: “What if titan can seal off magic such that opponents and himself can’t cast magic temporarily — he punches them in the meantime” — FreshToast: “There is a glyph which does that. I’m not sure he is the pick though” (M04404–M04407, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-18). Design history 2023-10-17 — initial concept in the 4 Boyes roster write-up: “[Titan] — Lycosian Male (Demi-Human) - Placeholder name, needs a proper one - Wuxia protagonist / eastern martial artist archetype - ‘Body’ affinity. Uses this to heal and punch - Fights unarmed, supplemented with magic” (M04227, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — magical-pet question: the demi-human could get something supposed to be dangerous but surprisingly loyal and docile; “He seems the type in my mind to respect magical creatures” (M04317–M04321, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — direction flagged as an open problem: “I have no idea what to do with titan. I need direction” (M04392, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-10-18 — sealing/binding magic pitched; a glyph already does that and FreshToast isn’t sure Titan is the pick; also resisting getting used to the placeholder name (M04404–M04408, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M04227; M04242–M04243; M04315–M04321; M04392; M04404–M04408 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions; 2023-10-17 → 2023-10-18) Cleaned: 000 lines 6879–6885 region (magical pet); 000 §Development Status (“Titan needs direction”) Docs: 002 §Titan; 009 §Development Status / §Unresolved Questions (Titan’s character direction) Ledger: rows for Titan roster entry (M04227), magical pet (M04317–M04321), “Titan needs direction” (M04392), sealing/binding magic (M04404–M04407) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Two Other Antagonists & Himbo Antagonist","group":"Characters","url":"characters/two-other-antagonists.html","text":"Two Other Antagonists Himbo Antagonist EVOLVED Two placeholder antagonist concepts from the very first antagonist roster (2023-02-09): a Team Rocket-style comedic-relief duo, and a “Himbo antagonist?” — both effectively absorbed by later characters. Summary — In the first antagonist brainstorm, the roster listed “2 others, less important. Serve as comedic relief, team rocket style” (M01219, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) and, in the follow-up list, “Himbo antagonist?” (M01220, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Neither was developed under these names. PROPOSED — inference The comedic-relief duo maps onto the pair of goofy, loyal-to-a-fault sibling pawns in [[lysander|Lysander]]'s party defined a week later (M02335–M02341, 2023-02-16), eventually named [[flynt|Flynt]] [[steele|Steele]] (M03394, FreshToast, 2023-06-04); the himbo archetype was explicitly filled by Flynt — “we at least have Flynt who’s probably a straight up himbo” (M03392, FreshToast, 2023-06-04). Two Other Antagonists Less important; serve as comedic relief, Team Rocket style (M01219, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). STATUS: PLACEHOLDER - NEEDS DEVELOPMENT OR REMOVAL (002 §Two Other Antagonists). Options recorded in the doc: (1) develop as recurring comic-relief villains with actual personalities and arcs; (2) remove if not essential; (3) replace with a single comic-relief antagonist. Recommendation: either fully develop with names, personalities, and motivations, or remove — half-developed comic relief can fall flat (002 §Two Other Antagonists). PROPOSED — inference The dated sequence suggests this slot was filled by Lysander’s two goofy sibling pawns, Flynt Steele (M02335–M02341, 2023-02-16 → M03392–M03397, 2023-06-04). Himbo Antagonist Listed as “Himbo antagonist?” in the second antagonist list, alongside [[thane|Thane]]'s death spirit, [[sable|Sable]]'s 7 outlaws, [[ruby|Ruby]]'s rival and creator, and Marie’s family traitor (M01220, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Relationship with [[gaius-wornwall|Gaius]] not established — noted on the draft relationship diagram (M01223, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-09). “Locke Himbo” pairing noted (M01230, FreshToast, 2023-02-09) — the doc reads this pairing as suggesting a romantic interest with [[locke|Locke]] (002 §Himbo Antagonist). “Himbo Plot Ideas” was created as a list and left empty (M01232, FreshToast, 2023-02-09); mentioned but no plot ideas filled in yet (002 §Himbo Antagonist). STATUS: CONCEPT ONLY - NEEDS DEVELOPMENT OR REMOVAL (002 §Himbo Antagonist). Unclear if protagonist or antagonist despite section placement. Options recorded in the doc: (1) develop as antagonist-turned-ally with himbo personality (likeable but not bright); (2) move to protagonist/ally section if not truly antagonistic; (3) merge with [[rhea-scala|Rhea]]/Magic Batman character if redundant; (4) remove if the concept doesn’t fit (002 §Himbo Antagonist). Design history 2023-02-09 — both concepts appear in the first antagonist rosters: “2 others, less important. Serve as comedic relief, team rocket style” (M01219, FreshToast) and “Himbo antagonist?” (M01220, FreshToast); draft relationship diagram notes Himbo Gaius relationship not established (M01223, spirit_noodle); “Locke Himbo” pairing (M01230); empty “Himbo Plot Ideas” list (M01232). 2023-02-09 — same day, in the party design thread: “Resolve, replaces himbo” (M01252, FreshToast) — an early sign the himbo slot was being repurposed (the [[rhea-scala|Rhea Scala]] “Magic Batman” character’s Resolve stat “replaces Himbo” per 002 §Rhea Scala). 2023-02-16 — the remaining 2 of Lysander’s party defined as a pair of siblings: largely unimportant, loyal to a fault, goofy, probably not all that bad — pawns (M02335–M02341, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). PROPOSED — inference This fills the “two others, comedic relief” slot. 2023-02-19 — joke beat: “Ajax could be our himbo” / “himbo legendary hero” (M02435, M02437, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2023-06-04 — “We need a Himbo in the game somewhere” → “we at least have Flynt who’s probably a straight up himbo”; Flynt Steele named (M03383, M03392–M03397, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast) — the himbo archetype lands on Flynt; matches the current presentation above. Provenance Raw: M01219, M01220, M01223, M01230, M01232, M01252 (2023-02-09); M02335–M02341 (2023-02-16); M02435, M02437 (2023-02-19); M03383–M03397 (2023-06-04) — FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions, spirit_noodle Cleaned: 000 Part 5 (antagonist rosters, relationship diagram) Docs: 002 §Two Other Antagonists; 002 §Himbo Antagonist; 002 §Rhea Scala (Resolve replaces Himbo) Ledger: rows R07 (relationship/pairing structure incl. Locke Himbo), R13 (sibling pawns) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Ajax's Tomb & the Prologue Dungeon","group":"Locations","url":"locations/ajaxs-tomb.html","text":"Ajax’s Tomb the Prologue Dungeon CANON The game’s opening dungeon — a dark cave that turns out to hide the secret entrance to the tomb of the legendary hero Ajax. Summary — The prologue dungeon is the first area of the game: a dark cave where [[lysander]] leads an expedition (with Eris and [[locke]]) hunting a [[chaos-emeralds|chaos emerald]]. During an ambush, Locke is separated from the others, and while alone she acquires the [[sunderer|Sunderer]]. What the party reaches at the end is not a dead end but the secret entrance to Ajax’s Tomb — found because Locke read a clue Lysander misread. The proto implements this whole lineage as one coded graph dungeon, “The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb” (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts, id: 'ajax_tomb_descent' ). Story role Type: dark cave; the initial dungeon visited at the beginning of the game (003 §Tutorial Dungeon). [[lysander|Lysander]] is here looking for a chaos emerald; the [[locke|lantern]]-carrying Locke accompanies him. It introduces the Lysander–Locke dynamic and Lysander’s protective-but-dismissive power (M02442–M02450, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19). Beat sheet (M03669, FreshToast, 2023-09-10): Lysander center, flanked by Locke and Eris; Locke’s sword is lost/broken; combat is broken by a puzzle showcasing Locke’s competence; an ambush separates Locke; she finds/acquires the Sunderer; reunites for another battle; Eris commends her; stealth light mechanics are introduced; a final climactic test ends the tutorial. Screenplay (first draft, M05476–M05521, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2024-08-24): Lysander leaps in and gives Locke his backup sword (“a hero looks out for his companions”); the borrowed sword shatters against an ancient golem; Locke falls to a lower level, finds a rusty spear, discovers the golems’ joint weakness, and defeats two golems; the sword is symbolic of power Lysander gave her — foreshadowing his later betrayal . The hidden connection What they reach at the end is not a dead end but a secret entrance to Ajax’s Tomb . Lysander and Eris assume their intel about a relic in the cavern was false; Locke finds it because she read the clue well (“the joint, not the wall” — the clue Lysander misread) (003 §Tutorial Dungeon §Hidden Connection; M03670, FreshToast, 2023-09-10). The Sunderer is the first of Ajax’s seven relics , acquired in the trial Locke passes. Secret: the tutorial dungeon later gains an additional secret section towards endgame, where the rumored chaos emerald is actually found (003 §Tutorial Dungeon §Secret). Ajax’s Tomb proper Access: the sealed sun-marked door , set with a sunset-colored gem, revealed beyond the boss (proto: content.ts, outro + node n5). Significance: related to Ajax’s legacy and the [[chaos-emeralds|chaos-emerald relics]]. Brought together, the relics don’t grant superpowers — they give a revelation, a way to learn about Ajax and his bloodline (003 §Seven Chaos Emeralds; M02428–M02438, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). Ajaxian trials already exist in-game: challenges left behind by Ajax (M05288, FreshToast, 2024-02-13). Implemented node path CANON — proto The coded graph (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts) dramatizes the beats above: The Spare Sword ( start ) — where the fall left the party; Lysander’s borrowed backup blade. The Crumbling Stair ( fight ) — goblins nest in the rubble (goblin_imp ×2 + slime). Branch A — Glimmer in the Rubble ( treasure ) — the borrowed sword shatters on the slime; Locke pulls free the Sunderer (relic reward). Branch B — Ambush in the Narrows ( elite , ambush) — river brigands and the restless dead strike first, scattering formation; “+4 max HP each” (Trial of Nerve). A Breath by the Underground River ( camp ) — a clean, cold underground river; fill the [[river-flask|flask]] and rest (“Rivers are for everyone — even down here”). See [[camps-and-wells]]. The Bone Gallery ( fight ) — an ossuary of the honored dead (slime + skeleton_archer ×2); heal 40% reward. The Ossuary Sentinel ( boss , tomb_golem) — a colossus of bound bone and adamant stone guarding the threshold; beyond it, the sealed sun-marked door — the entrance to Ajax’s Tomb proper. Design history 2023-02-19 — prologue conceived: dark cave, Locke’s lantern, Lysander hunting a chaos emerald he fails to find (misread clue), in medias res opening; the dungeon later gets a secret endgame section (M02442–M02450, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-09-10 — the tutorial dungeon’s end is reframed as the secret entrance to Ajax’s Tomb , and the Sunderer becomes the first of Ajax’s seven relics (M03669, M03670, FreshToast). Supersedes the plain “dead end” reading. 2024-08-24 — full first-draft screenplay: borrowed sword shatters against a golem, Locke falls and finds the rusty spear, foreshadowing Lysander’s betrayal (M05476–M05521, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Current presentation. 2026-06-16 — proto encodes all of the above as one graph, “The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb” (003 §The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb; proto: content.ts). Provenance Raw: M02442–M02450 (2023-02-19); M03669, M03670 (2023-09-10); M05288 (2024-02-13); M05476–M05521 (2024-08-24) Cleaned: 000 Part 9, Part 15 Docs: 003 §The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb; §Tutorial Dungeon / Prologue Dungeon; §Ajax’s Tomb; 004 §Prologue/Tutorial Ledger: rows R13, R20, R28, R29 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Environmental Biomes","group":"Locations","url":"locations/biomes.html","text":"Environmental Biomes CANON The overworld’s palette of scenic biomes, gathered as level-design references — most from a TikTok list, since real ones are usually too huge to use directly. Summary — Under a Caribbean-inspired baseline climate, the world mixes many distinct biomes for variety. A level-design biome list was gathered (from a TikTok) on 2023-03-11: thermal pools, coastal biomes, redwood forest, sinkhole forests, salt flats, and the singular Garden of the Gods; most real-world versions “are too HUGE to use directly” (M02960–M02985, FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2023-03-11). A separate set of biomes exists specifically to host [[dungeon-authoring|ambush]] encounters: foggy bogs, open fields, and dark areas (M02929–M02932, FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2023-03-09). Overall climate Tone: Caribbean-inspired climate — warm, tropical environments likely predominate (003 §Climate and Weather). Variety: despite the Caribbean baseline, the world includes diverse biomes (salt flats, thermal pools, redwood forests, etc.). Regional inspiration: environments may draw from Spain, Italy, and Greece for biome variety (M01234, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Scenic / level-design biomes Gathered as references (M02960–M02985, FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2023-03-11; 003 §Environmental Locations and Biomes): Thermal Pools — a particularly interesting biome; caught the team’s eye the most among environmental options (M02977). Coastal Biomes — particularly interesting; the Caribbean climate influences the overall setting. Redwood Forest — tall, imposing trees creating a unique environment. Sinkhole Forests — forest growing in and around sinkholes; caught the team’s eye. Salt Flats — scenic, the desert equivalent of white flower fields; floated as a boss fight “on a sea of endless white.” DEBATED some found it “bleh boring,” others saw potential; spirit_noodle “didn’t care about salt flats” but liked everything else (M02962, M02964). White Flower Fields — used as the comparison point for other scenic biomes. Open Fields — contain charging bulls; used for ambush gameplay (M02929, FreshToast, 2023-03-09). Countryside — rough-and-tumble terrain, may draw from Spain, Italy, and Greece. Garden of the Gods Type: a singular place rather than a regular, repeatable environment (M02960, FreshToast, 2023-03-11). Aesthetic: “wonderful”; spirit_noodle liked it most of all the options (M02962). Location: hidden in a basin within the mountain range — “unless we hide it in a basin within the mountain range or something” (M02983, FreshToast, 2023-03-11). Ambush biomes Designed specifically for [[dungeon-authoring|ambush]] encounters (M02929–M02932, FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2023-03-09; 003 §Environmental Locations and Biomes): Foggy Bogs — contain ninja frogs (“foggy bogs with ninja frogs sounds AWESOME”). Open Fields — contain charging bulls. Dark Areas — designed specifically for ambush encounters and stealth gameplay. Spooky / atmospheric Spooky Areas — atmospheric locations for horror/tension elements (003 §Spooky Areas). Cross-references Ashen Crags (volcanic region) and the [[mountains|key mountains]] are covered under [[mountains]]. Biome variety feeds [[dungeon-authoring]]'s “not just caves” principle (mansions, forests, mountain paths, ruins, temples). Open questions DEBATED Whether salt flats are worth using — split between “bleh boring” and “scenic af / has potential” (M02962, M02964, spirit_noodle/FreshToast, 2023-03-11). Design history 2023-02-09 — regional environment inspiration set: Spain, Italy, Greece (M01234, FreshToast). 2023-03-09 — ambush biomes named: open fields with charging bulls, foggy bogs with ninja frogs, dark areas (M02929–M02932, FreshToast/spirit_noodle). 2023-03-11 — level-design biome list gathered (thermal pools, coastal, redwood, sinkhole forests, salt flats, Garden of the Gods in a mountain basin); noted most real biomes are too huge to use directly (M02960–M02985, FreshToast/spirit_noodle). Current presentation. Provenance Raw: M01234 (2023-02-09); M02929, M02932 (2023-03-09); M02960–M02985 (2023-03-11) Cleaned: 000 §level-design biomes; §ambush locations Docs: 003 §Environmental Locations and Biomes; §Climate and Weather Ledger: rows R07, R16 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Caelia","group":"Locations","url":"locations/caelia.html","text":"Caelia DEBATED The bustling marketplace hub at the heart of Caelian Odyssey — a warm, Caribbean-flavoured trading centre whose exact nature (isle, city, or whole region) is still an open thread. Summary — Caelia is the setting’s central marketplace hub: a bustling, Caribbean-climate town of unique food and active trade, home to the merchant [[elara|Elara]], the start of the Moonstone Mania quest, and the [[gacha-fountain|gacha fountain]]. Its name went through a naming brainstorm (Caelia, Caelia’s Breath, Straid/Strayed, Straid Isle) and the setting itself lends its name to the iteration Caelian Odyssey . What Caelia is — a marketplace hub, a coastal isle, or a whole region — is genuinely DEBATED , which is why this page’s status is DEBATED. Classification — the open thread DEBATED The notes and the proto’s gamebible disagree on what Caelia is (003 §Notable Features): The proto’s gamebible / regions codex floats it as a coastal isle/region in its own right, under the “Straid Isle” variant name. GAME_DESIGN.md (canon) and the proto’s locations codex class it as a marketplace hub , not a nation or region. This is a lore/classification difference rather than a mechanic. Both readings are recorded; whether Caelia is an isle, a city, or a whole region is unresolved. See Open questions. Features Marketplace: A bustling marketplace and active trading hub (003 §Marketplace). Food: Home to unique food items — Crunchy Lettuce (a crunchy, refreshing packet street food, an inside joke on CrunchyConniptions) and noodle dishes (M04934, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-07; 003 §Notable Features). Food lore is detailed in the Item guide. Climate geography: Caribbean-inspired (tropical/warm) climate; biome variety may draw from Spain, Italy and Greece (003 §Notable Features). Reactivity: Caelia can undergo significant changes based on player actions — e.g. the “Cult of Mundane Moments” scenario (003 §Notable Features). The gacha fountain Caelia is the CANON — proto home of the [[gacha-fountain|gacha fountain]], where the player drops money for a gacha-style roll (mostly garbage, with a rare legendary weapon on a 0.2% / 1-in-500 pity system, scaling by act). The proto’s canon resolves a long-open placement question in Caelia’s favor (GAME_DESIGN.md §14; 003 §Gacha Fountain Details). See the [[gacha-fountain|gacha fountain]] page for full rules. PROPOSED — proto-derived inference, not in Discord notes — Equating the notes’ Capital (which “has a gacha fountain”) with Caelia is itself a reconciliation. The Discord notes say only “Capital: Has a gacha fountain” (M01981, FreshToast, 2023-02-14) and later “in capital (or harder dungeon alternative)”; they never identify the Capital as Caelia. The identity is derived from the proto, not stated in the notes (003 §Gacha Fountain Details). Story connections Moonstone Mania: The merchant [[elara|Elara]] is found in the Caelia marketplace; the quest “Moonstone Mania” begins here (M05266–M05272, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13; 003 §Marketplace). See also the [[marketplace-caelia|Marketplace]] point-of-interest page. Open questions DEBATED What Caelia is: marketplace hub (GAME_DESIGN.md canon) vs coastal isle/region (“Straid Isle” in the gamebible). Unresolved (003 §Notable Features). PROPOSED Whether the notes’ Capital is the same place as Caelia (proto-derived inference, not in the notes) (003 §Gacha Fountain Details). Design history 2023-02-08 — naming brainstorm: Caelia , Caelia’s Breath , Strayed , Straid , Straid Isle , Strayed Isle all floated in a song/place-naming session (M01170–M01175, FreshToast). Caelia became the settled name and lent it to the Caelian Odyssey iteration. 2023-02-14 — the notes’ Capital established as the home of the gacha fountain (“dropping money in a gacha fountain in the capital,” M01981, FreshToast) — later reconciled to Caelia by the proto. 2023-11-07 — Caelian food established: Crunchy Lettuce and a noodle dish (M04934, CrunchyConniptions). 2024-02-13 — the Moonstone Mania quest and the merchant [[elara|Elara]] placed in the Caelia marketplace (M05266–M05272, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2026-06-16 — proto reconciliation: GAME_DESIGN.md classes Caelia as a marketplace hub and canonically sites the gacha fountain here, while the gamebible’s “Straid Isle” reading keeps the isle/region interpretation alive — the classification remains DEBATED (003 §Notable Features, §Gacha Fountain Details). Provenance Raw: M01170–M01175 (FreshToast, 2023-02-08); M01981, M02002 (FreshToast, 2023-02-14); M04934 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-07); M05266–M05272 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 §Place Naming; 000 Part 5/7 §Caelia Docs: 003 §Alternate Names/Variants Discussed; 003 §Notable Features; 003 §Gacha Fountain Details; 003 §Marketplace (Caelia) Ledger: rows R07 (“Song/theme place naming brainstorm: Caelia, Caelia’s Breath; Strayed/Straid…”), R11 (gacha fountain in the capital), R26 (Crunchy Lettuce), R28 (Moonstone Elara) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Camps & the Wells (Bonfire Network)","group":"Locations","url":"locations/camps-and-wells.html","text":"Camps the Wells (Bonfire Network) CANON The rest-and-resupply network — dungeon camps at the threshold and world-spanning wells that act like Dark Souls bonfires. Summary — Rest points come in two forms. Inside a dungeon, the main camp occupies the beginning of the dungeon; resting there restores resources and (in the proto) the degrading max-HP ceiling, and originally “resets the dungeon dark souls style” (M02381, FreshToast, 2023-02-17). Across the overworld, wells are “our bonfires” — some man-made, some natural — where you refill the healing [[river-flask|flask]]; any fresh-water source will do, but river water is especially good (M03953, M03959, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Both are tied to the [[river-flask|River Healing]] resupply scheme. Camp system (inside dungeons) Location: while in a dungeon, the main camp occupies the beginning of the dungeon (003 §Camp System). Purpose: resting and resource management within dungeon exploration. Original rule: “resting here resets the dungeon dark souls style” (M02381, FreshToast, 2023-02-17). Implemented: CANON — proto camp/rest nodes occupy the dungeon (node kind camp ); resting restores the per-fight degrading max-HP ceiling (which degrades 3/fight, floored ~60% of base, and only rest restores it) (proto: src/core/campaign/run.ts). In [[ajaxs-tomb|the prologue dungeon]], the camp is “A Breath by the Underground River,” realizing River Healing underground (“Rivers are for everyone — even down here”). Wells / rest points (the bonfire network) Description: wells exist around the world — “our bonfires” — some man-made, some naturally occurring (M03953, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13; 003 §Wells / Rest Points). Refill rule: any fresh-water source will do to refill the healing flask, but one from the river is especially good (M03959, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Design lineage: the whole idea derives from Dark Souls’ “bonfire and estus system” (M00140, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). The team deliberately rejected DS1-style bonfire variance (some bonfires giving more flasks than others), following the later Souls games that did away with it (M04109, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Cross-reference Tied to the [[river-flask|River Healing / river flask]] mechanic (items batch). The river-dependent resupply scheme was itself extensively debated and largely walked back as too limiting (see the Estus/healing debate in the item/mechanics docs). The wells being “any fresh-water source” is part of that walk-back — you are not chained to the river itself. Open questions DEBATED How strictly resupply depends on the river vs. any well/fresh-water source — the balance was still being negotiated (003 §Wells cross-reference; item/mechanics docs). Design history 2021-05-25 — origin: adopt Dark Souls’ “bonfire and estus system” (M00140, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-17 — dungeon camp defined at the start of the dungeon; resting “resets the dungeon dark souls style” (M02381, FreshToast). 2023-10-13 — wells established as the world’s bonfire network (“The wells are our bonfires”), refillable from any fresh-water source though river water is best; bonfire variance rejected (M03953, M03959, M04109, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Current presentation. 2026-06-16 — proto confirms camp nodes and the degrading-HP-ceiling restore-on-rest model (003 §Camp; proto: run.ts). Provenance Raw: M00140 (2021-05-25); M02381 (2023-02-17); M03953, M03959, M04109 (2023-10-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 8; Part 15 Docs: 003 §Camp System; §Wells / Rest Points (Bonfire Network); 006 §Lantern Ledger: rows R01, R13, R21 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Countries and Regions","group":"Locations","url":"locations/countries-and-regions.html","text":"Countries and Regions CANON The nation-states and major regions of the Caelian Odyssey world — a river-defined geopolitics of empires, underdog nations, and one distant panacea-bearing continent. Summary — The setting’s politics revolve around rivers. [[lycosia|Lycosia]] is the seat of an empire; [[fiore|Fiore]] lies across the mountains; the independent underdog nations Cahras and Uldrav sit between the great powers; Khorath is the broad region where much of the story plays out and where rivers are culturally sacrosanct; and the peaceful nation Lethria is homeland to a noble character. This page collects the countries and regions that are too small for their own file; the larger ones ([[lycosia|Lycosia]], [[fiore|Fiore]], [[santier|Santier]], [[caelia|Caelia]]) have dedicated pages. The single most-debated question in this brainstorm was who controls the river — a proposal that one country monopolize it (M03827, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13) was ultimately rejected in favor of “a river just belongs to the country who owns the land” (M03855, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). See Open questions. Khorath CANON Khorath is a major region/country where significant story events occur (003 §Countries and Regions). Culture: Rivers are culturally important across Khorath, expressed through river-related holidays and sayings rather than being tied to any one religion (M03777–M03779, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; 003 §Countries and Regions). Demographics: [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]], Alistair, Pandora and [[titan|Titan]] are all ethnically typical for western Khorath — a Mediterranean look; Colette is described as a bit darker (M04242–M04243, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Story connection: The recipe for Ambrosia was returned here — carried back from [[santier|Santier]] without [[arthur-marais|Arthur]], who vanished on that final travail (003 §Countries and Regions; see [[santier|Santier]]). Cahras and Uldrav CANON Two independent underdog nations — small political entities that stand apart from the great powers (M03849, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; 003 §Cahras and Uldrav). They emerged from the same river-politics discussion that established the empire of [[lycosia|Lycosia]] and the struggling rival country framed as a republic or dictatorship (M03847, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). Lethria CANON Description: A small and peaceful nation (Part 4 World Bible; 003 §Cahras and Uldrav). Temperament: Known for a strong sense of justice and compassion; values peace and often strives to bring about harmony (003 §Cahras and Uldrav). Story connection: Homeland of the Lethria noble — the Malenia / Leyla / Marie character, a daughter of a noble family groomed for leadership around the theme “Vengeance vs Justice” (003 §Cahras and Uldrav; see the Character Guide). Provenance note: Lethria is present in the brainstorm notes (Part 4) and carried into the proto’s region codex ( gamebible/data/regions.js ); it had been omitted from the guide’s nation list and was added on 2026-06-16 (003 §Cahras and Uldrav). It does not resolve to a dated raw Discord message under this spelling, so its first_conceived is taken from the Part 4 World Bible. Related pages [[lycosia|Lycosia]] — seat of the empire, its river in the south. [[fiore|Fiore]] — country across the mountains, home of Damian. [[santier|Santier]] — distant continent, sole source of the panacea fruit. [[caelia|Caelia]] — the marketplace hub whose classification as isle / city / region is unresolved. Open questions DEBATED River control: whether one prosperous nation should monopolize the river (proposed M03827, M03853, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13) or whether “a river just belongs to the country who owns the land” (M03855, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). The monopoly idea was explicitly floated with a jealous rival country “always looking for a way to get some” (M03828–M03829, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13) but was rejected in the guide’s canon framing. Design history 2023-10-13 — the geopolitics were laid down in a single session: Fiore established as the country across the mountains where Damian is from (M03826, FreshToast); a proposed river monopoly with a jealous rival (M03827–M03829, CrunchyConniptions); Lycosia named the seat of an empire, “good in terms of power” (M03843, FreshToast); the struggling rival framed as a republic or dictatorship (M03847, CrunchyConniptions); and Cahras and Uldrav named as independent underdog nations (M03849, FreshToast). 2023-10-13 — the river-monopoly proposal was resolved against: “A river just belongs to the country who owns the land” (M03855, FreshToast) — supersedes the monopoly idea, which survives only as an Open question . 2026-06-16 — Lethria added to the nation list from the Part 4 World Bible and the proto’s region codex, having previously been omitted (003 §Cahras and Uldrav). Provenance Raw: M03777–M03779, M03826–M03855 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13); M04242–M04243 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 Part 15 §Country Politics; 000 Part 4 §World Bible Docs: 003 §Countries and Regions; 003 §Cahras and Uldrav Ledger: rows for “Country politics… Cahras and Uldrav” (R21) and “Cyrus/Alistair/Pandora/Titan… western Khorath” (R23) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Dungeon Authoring & Design Canon","group":"Locations","url":"locations/dungeon-authoring.html","text":"Dungeon Authoring Design Canon CANON How dungeons are built and what makes a good one — the shared rulebook every specific dungeon in the world is authored against. Summary — Dungeons are the team’s flagship content, guarded jealously (“Leave the dungeons to me”). The design brief is exploration-first, atmosphere-heavy, completely dark, and varied — not just underground caves but mansions, forests, ruins, temples, and tombs. In the implemented proto, a dungeon is a navigable node graph (rooms, edges, encounters, treasure, camp, boss) rather than a spatial floor plan, with per-node darkness and ambush flags realizing the design notes (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts, src/core/campaign/types.ts). Spatial layout is deferred to Unity. Design goals Exploration is peak dungeon design. Puzzle elements are welcome, but the priority is to “beef up the exploration”; each dungeon should incentivize different skills and characters (003 §Design Goals). Quality over quantity. “I will only accept the best for our dungeons”; the team wants each dungeon excellent, citing Sen’s Fortress, Anor Londo’s elevator sequences, and finding the Onion Knight in Dark Souls 3 as examples of excellence (003 §Examples of Excellence). Inspiration: Golden Sun (renowned for its dungeons) and old Zelda dungeons (M05329–M05340, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13); an RPGFan article “Dungeon Crawlers 1: What Makes a Great RPG Dungeon” was cited as reference. Presentation and atmosphere are especially important to make a dungeon memorable and immersive, “so that other stuff like cool puzzles and narrative beats feel more impactful” (M05335–M05340, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). Environmental storytelling (Skyrim reference): each location should have narrative purpose beyond combat (003 §Environmental Storytelling). Trap design: dungeons with “annoying traps that you overcome (chef’s kiss)” (003 §Design Philosophy Notes). Team division: “You like your dumb open-world game. you can take lead in designing the open world. Leave the dungeons to me” (003 §Examples of Excellence). Variety — not just caves Dungeons aren’t just underground caves (M01641–M01648, FreshToast). Types include: spooky mansions, scary forests, terrifying mountain paths, ghoulish ruins, petrifying temples (“to anything not a liquid”), and skeleton tombs (“Dungeons and no skeletons? A travesty”). The team planned to use these as four of the actual in-game dungeons (003 §Types Include; §Dungeon Philosophy and Variety). Dungeon count: DEBATED A debate between 7 dungeons (with 1 puzzle per dungeon) versus “more than 7” was never settled (003 §Design Goals). Darkness mechanic Dungeons “will be completely dark, with different dungeons having different levels of darkness” (003 §Design Goals). The proto implements this as a per-node darkness value 0–3 — flavor plus intended ambush weighting (proto: src/core/campaign/types.ts darkness?: number ). See [[camps-and-wells|the Lantern]] and stealth/light mechanics, owned by items and systems batches. Authoring model (implemented) CANON — proto The proto realizes the “dungeons as graphs” intent in code (proto: src/core/campaign/types.ts, content.ts): A dungeon is rooms (nodes) + edges + encounter slots + treasure + camp + boss . Node kinds: start | fight | elite | treasure | camp | boss . Compass navigation, no minimaps: next-room choices are compass-style labels (“Press into the dark”, “Take the faster, darker way”); no minimap, matching the “REAL TABOO” navigation rule (proto: node compass field; src/web/screens.ts). See [[world-map-and-travel]]. Ambush ( ambush: true ) scatters the party’s formation at the start of a fight (proto: src/core/campaign/run.ts). See Ambush mechanics below. Camp/rest nodes occupy the dungeon; resting restores the degrading max-HP ceiling. See [[camps-and-wells]]. Run resources tied to place: healing jets (start 2, cap 5), a [[river-flask|river flask]] (start 1), a per-fight degrading HP ceiling (degrades 3/fight, floored ~60% of base; only rest restores), and treasure rewards of kind jet | heal | maxhp | relic | flask (proto: src/core/campaign/run.ts, types.ts). Respawn system Once you clear an area, enemies don’t just pop back up. Only after you completely leave a dungeon may they respawn (003 §Respawn System). No boss resets — a cleared boss is replaced with powerful enemies instead. Information system: information uncovered about a dungeon can be previewed later. Ambush mechanics (location-specific) Some enemies attempt to ambush by making sound and rushing toward you; if caught, positioning scatters at the start of battle; the counter is to attack them just as they reach you (003 §Ambush Mechanics). Compared to the “gross sewer babies in Demon’s Souls (weird little scream and jump).” Locations are designed specifically for ambush, and telegraphed so it doesn’t feel cheap. Associated environments: [[biomes|foggy bogs, open fields, and dark areas]]. See also the systems batch for the run-level implementation. Open questions DEBATED Dungeon count: exactly 7 (1 puzzle each) vs. “more than 7” (003 §Design Goals). Design history 2023-02-02 — first elemental-dungeon list logged, implying multiple distinct dungeons (M00853, CrunchyConniptions). See [[elemental-dungeons]]. 2023-02-17 — lantern-and-camp resting model established: “resting here resets the dungeon dark souls style” (M02381, FreshToast). 2024-02-13 — dungeon-research pass fixes the design north star: Golden Sun / old Zelda inspiration, presentation and atmosphere prioritized (M05329–M05340, CrunchyConniptions). This is the current top-level design brief. 2026-06-16 — proto cross-check confirms the graph-authoring model, darkness bands, ambush, and camp are actually coded (003 header note; proto: src/core/campaign/*). Provenance Raw: M00853 (2023-02-02); M01641–M01648, M02381; M05329–M05340 (2024-02-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 1, Part 3, Part 8 Docs: 003 §Dungeon Authoring Exploration Mechanics; §Dungeon Philosophy and Variety; §Respawn System; §Design Philosophy Notes Ledger: rows R05, R09, R28 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Elemental & Themed Dungeon Types","group":"Locations","url":"locations/elemental-dungeons.html","text":"Elemental Themed Dungeon Types CANON The roster of dungeon flavors — elemental (lava, water, poison, magic, forest, desert) and enemy-themed (goblin, vampire, dragon, skeleton). Summary — Rather than one mega-dungeon (see the superseded [[hundred-floor-dungeon|100-Floor Dungeon]]), the game uses many distinct dungeons, each with a flavor that incentivizes different skills and characters. Two organizing schemes appear in the notes: elemental dungeons (Lava, Water, Poison Swamp, Magic, Forest, Desert) and enemy-type areas (Goblin, Vampire, Dragon, Skeleton Tomb). This page is the combined roster; general dungeon rules live in [[dungeon-authoring]]. Elemental dungeons The listed elemental dungeon types (M00853, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02; 003 §Elemental Dungeons): Lava Dungeon Water Dungeon — the proto’s gamebible ties this to the drowned region it calls [[sodden-depths|the Sodden Depths]], though that link is the gamebible’s inference, not established in the notes. Poison Swamp Dungeon Magic Dungeon Forest Dungeon Desert Dungeon — note that the notes elsewhere suggest the hardest dungeon “may be in the middle of a barren desert or underground” (see [[world-map-and-travel]]). Only the type names are recorded; individual contents were not detailed in the notes. Enemy-type areas Each area tends to have a main enemy type (M05365–M05376, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2024-03-12; 003 §Enemy Type Areas): Goblin Area — dominated by goblin enemies. Vampires Area — dominated by vampire enemies. Dragon Area — dragon or dragon-related enemies. See [[vaenngrym]] (bestiary). Skeleton Tomb — a desired dungeon type featuring skeletons; skeletons were deemed near-mandatory: “Dungeons and no skeletons? A travesty,” with room for “a funny skeleton” (M05365–M05376). This theme is realized in [[ajaxs-tomb|the prologue dungeon’s]] Bone Gallery and Ossuary Sentinel. Related design Dungeon variety extends beyond elements and enemies to whole settings — mansions, forests, ruins, temples — see [[dungeon-authoring]] §Variety. Design history 2023-02-02 — elemental dungeon list first logged: Lava, Water, Poison Swamp, Magic, Forest, Desert (M00853, CrunchyConniptions). 2024-03-12 — enemy-type areas established: goblin, vampires, dragon, plus the mandatory skeleton tomb (M05365–M05376, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M00853 (2023-02-02); M05365–M05376 (2024-03-12) Cleaned: 000 §Location Guides (elemental dungeons); §Main Enemy Types by Area Docs: 003 §Elemental Dungeons (Listed Types); §Enemy Type Areas; 005 Bestiary (main enemy type by area) Ledger: rows R05, R29 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Eye Dungeon","group":"Locations","url":"locations/eye-dungeon.html","text":"The Eye Dungeon CANON A dungeon full of watching eyes — get seen, and the boss learns how to beat you. Summary — The Eye Dungeon is a dungeon where eyes constantly follow you. You must destroy the eyes and avoid being seen; if the eyes see you, the end boss gathers information about your party and crafts countermeasures tailored to your specific composition. It is an information-gathering mechanic whose stakes are set at the boss fight (M04224, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-15). Mechanic Eyes constantly follow you throughout the dungeon (003 §Eye Dungeon). Gameplay: you have to destroy the eyes and avoid being seen by them. Consequence of being seen: the end boss gathers information about you and creates countermeasures against your specific party by analyzing that information — an adaptive boss (M04224; 005 Bestiary §adaptive boss). Strategy: the more you avoid detection, the less the boss can tailor itself; the fewer eyes see you, the easier the fight. Related systems The adaptive-boss behavior is catalogued in the bestiary. The stealth/avoidance loop connects to the darkness and [[camps-and-wells|lantern]] stealth mechanics used across [[dungeon-authoring|dungeons]]. Design history 2023-10-15 — Eye Dungeon conceived: eyes that follow you; being seen lets the end boss build party-specific countermeasures (M04224, CrunchyConniptions). Provenance Raw: M04224 (2023-10-15) Cleaned: 000 §Eye dungeon Docs: 003 §Eye Dungeon; 005 Bestiary (adaptive boss) Ledger: row R23 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Fiore","group":"Locations","url":"locations/fiore.html","text":"Fiore CANON The country across the mountains, home of Damian — a nation as river-dependent as its neighbours, its river flowing opposite [[lycosia|Lycosia]]'s. Summary — Fiore is the country across the mountains from the other regions (M03826, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). It is highly dependent on its river, which runs throughout the country and travels in a different direction from [[lycosia|Lycosia]]'s. Its language is Fioran, and its river’s name is meant to be twinned with Lycosia’s. Geography A country across the mountains from the other regions (M03826, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Has a big river running throughout it ; the nation is highly dependent on their river on the other side of the mountains (003 §Countries and Regions). Fiore’s river travels in a different direction from [[lycosia|Lycosia]]'s river (M03834–M03836, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; 003 §Countries and Regions). Culture Language: Fioran is the spoken language (003 §Countries and Regions). River culture: The river system is similarly reflected in Fiore’s geography and culture, part of the setting-wide reverence for rivers (003 §Countries and Regions). Story connections Origin of Damian: Fiore is where Damian is from (M03826, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Twinned river names: The Fioran and Lycosian rivers should have twinned/related names tied to the lore that [[ajax|Ajax]] “split a mountain” to form them from several smaller rivers (M03819–M03824, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; 003 §Rivers). See [[lycosia|Lycosia]] and the [[countries-and-regions|regional overview]]. Design history 2023-10-13 — Fiore established as “the country across the mountains where Damian is from” (M03826, FreshToast), alongside the twinned-river lore (M03819–M03824, FreshToast). 2023-10-13 (later in the same session) — river detail confirmed: Fiore’s river runs throughout the country and flows in a direction different from [[lycosia|Lycosia]]'s (M03834–M03836, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M03819–M03824, M03826, M03834–M03836 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 15 §Country Politics; 000 Part 15 §The River System Docs: 003 §Countries and Regions; 003 §Rivers Ledger: rows R21 (“Country politics… Fiore across the mountains (Damian’s home)”; “One main big river…”) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Gacha Fountain","group":"Locations","url":"locations/gacha-fountain.html","text":"Gacha Fountain DEBATED A wishing-fountain-style gambling feature where the player throws money in for a random gacha roll — mostly garbage, but with a rare legendary weapon behind a 1-in-500 pity system. Summary — The gacha fountain is a fountain the player drops money into for a gacha-style reward (M01981, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). It mostly gives garbage or nothing, but the player is told a legendary weapon might be inside; there is a 0.2% (1/500) chance per attempt, and after 500 attempts the weapon is guaranteed via a pity system (M02002–M02007, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). Its placement was left open in the notes between the capital and a harder dungeon ; the proto later canonically sites it in [[caelia|Caelia]]'s marketplace hub. It is peppered with funny easter eggs. Description A gambling fountain: The player drops money into the fountain for a gacha-style reward (M01981, FreshToast, 2023-02-14; 003 §Gacha Fountain Mechanic). Framing: The fountain “mostly gives garbage or nothing,” but the player is told there might be a legendary weapon in there (M02002–M02003, FreshToast, 2023-02-14; 003 §Gacha Fountain Rewards). Design temptation: The reward is deliberately kept bad, because “gambling is fun” and players will keep going anyway — “I’d go back until I get that item” (000 Part 7 §Gacha Fountain Debate). Rules / Effects Legendary drop chance: 0.2% — “1/500 goes will getcha the item” (M02006, FreshToast, 2023-02-14; 003 §Gacha Fountain Details). Pity system: Guaranteed after 500 attempts — “Maybe if they do it 500 times we can have it be guaranteed then” (M02007, FreshToast, 2023-02-14; M01989, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14 “it will have pity system”). Act scaling: CANON — proto The reward should scale based on which act the player obtains it in (003 §Gacha Fountain Details; proto GAME_DESIGN.md §14). Easter eggs: Dropping enough money triggers a “lil easter egg” (M01986, FreshToast, 2023-02-14); the group wanted “all kinds of funny stuff” and a guaranteed funny easter egg every ~10 attempts (M02012–M02013, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). Named example: a “hot lady comes out and gives you a sword, names you king” (M01980, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Visual reference: PROPOSED The fountain “could be like the birds from Dark Souls” (M03075, FreshToast, 2023-03-29). Location DEBATED CANON — proto The proto sites the gacha fountain in [[caelia|Caelia]] — the marketplace hub — with the 0.2% legendary / 1-in-500 pity / act-scaling rules (proto GAME_DESIGN.md §14; 003 §Gacha Fountain Details). DEBATED The Discord notes left the placement open between the capital (“dropping money in a gacha fountain in the capital,” M01981, FreshToast, 2023-02-14) and a harder dungeon alternative (M01978–M01979, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). It was never finalized in the brainstorm. PROPOSED — proto-derived inference Equating the notes’ Capital with Caelia is a reconciliation made by the proto, not stated in the Discord notes (003 §Gacha Fountain Details). Related systems Part of [[caelia|Caelia]], the setting’s marketplace hub, alongside the merchant [[elara|Elara]] and the Moonstone Mania quest (003 §Marketplace). Ties into the broader monetization/gacha discussion the group had for the game (see [[monetization-and-business]]). Open questions DEBATED Where the fountain lives — capital vs harder dungeon in the notes; resolved to Caelia only by the proto (003 §Gacha Fountain Details). DEBATED “Exact gacha fountain mechanics and scaling” were flagged unresolved in the notes (000 Part 7 §Unresolved Questions). Design history 2023-02-14 — original concept: CrunchyConniptions proposes a fountain in the harder dungeon where you sacrifice a consumable for a “maybe gacha style” reward (M01978–M01979). FreshToast reframes it as “dropping money in a gacha fountain in the capital” (M01981). The core rules land the same day: mostly garbage, a told-about legendary, 1/500 chance, 500-attempt pity, easter eggs (M01986–M02013, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-14 — the harder-dungeon / consumable-sacrifice variant is superseded by the money-in-fountain version; the consumable-sacrifice alternative is SCRAPPED because a random-chance reward “doesn’t seem like a great reward” and is likely not profitable (003 §Gacha Fountain Details, Rejected Variant). Supersedes the harder-dungeon proposal. 2023-03-29 — visual reference floated: the fountain “could be like the birds from Dark Souls” (M03075, FreshToast). 2026-06-16 — proto reconciliation: GAME_DESIGN.md §14 canonically sites the gacha fountain in Caelia with the 0.2% / 1-in-500 / act-scaling rules, resolving the notes’ open capital-vs-dungeon placement in Caelia’s favor (003 §Gacha Fountain Details). Provenance Raw: M01978–M02013 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14); M03075 (FreshToast, 2023-03-29) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 §Gacha Fountain Rewards; §Gacha Fountain Mechanic; §Gacha Fountain Debate Docs: 003 §Gacha Fountain Details; 003 §Marketplace (Caelia) Ledger: row R11 (gacha fountain in the capital) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Grand Library","group":"Locations","url":"locations/grand-library.html","text":"The Grand Library CANON The place where knowledge is kept from the public — the target of the “Magic Robin Hood for Knowledge.” Summary — The Grand Library is where knowledge is deliberately withheld from the public. It is the central location of [[cyril-cyrus-erhardt|Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt]]'s story: he is a “Magic Robin Hood for Knowledge” who wants to acquire and share the knowledge the Library keeps locked away (M02444–M02465, CrunchyConniptions/spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19). Features story connection Feature: knowledge is kept from the public here (003 §Grand Library). Story connection: tied to [[cyril-cyrus-erhardt|Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt]], the “Magic Robin Hood for Knowledge” (M02447, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19). Conflict: Cyril wants to acquire and share knowledge that is being kept from the public (003 §Grand Library). Related theme: the “forbidden lore” motif — off-limits areas of an academy library — is one of the three agreed setting features and appears at [[the-luminarium|The Luminarium]] as well (M04297–M04300, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Whether the Grand Library and the Luminarium’s forbidden-lore stacks are the same place is not stated in the notes. Design history 2023-02-19 — the Grand Library is established as the store of withheld knowledge, and the setting for Cyril/Cyrus Erhardt’s “Magic Robin Hood for Knowledge” arc; his given name shifts Cyril → Cyrus and his surname settles as Erhardt (M02444–M02465, CrunchyConniptions/spirit_noodle). Provenance Raw: M02444, M02447, M02465 (2023-02-19) Cleaned: 000 Part 9 (Magic Robin Hood, Cyrus Erhardt) Docs: 003 §Grand Library; 009 Cyrus Erhardt Ledger: row R13 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The 100-Floor Dungeon (Early Concept)","group":"Locations","url":"locations/hundred-floor-dungeon.html","text":"The 100-Floor Dungeon (Early Concept) EVOLVED The game’s very first shape: a single 100-floor co-op dungeon, later abandoned for a single-player JRPG with many distinct dungeons. Summary — In the earliest concept (Part 1), the whole game was one 100-floor dungeon , character-based (like Genshin) and played co-op online — the “Coop Dark Souls” framing. Early floors were solo-able; later floors grew complex enough that co-op became central. This was superseded as the project moved toward a single-player JRPG with multiple distinct dungeons; it is recorded here for lineage. See [[dungeon-authoring]] and [[elemental-dungeons]] for the successor design. The original concept Core game concept: a 100-floor dungeon, character-based (Genshin-like), co-op online (M00128, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). Difficulty scaling: early floors are so easy people can solo them; as you progress it becomes more complex; reliance on co-op becomes central (Ghost of Tsushima nightmare-raids reference) (003 §Difficulty Scaling). Why it evolved EVOLVED The single-100-floor structure was tied to the co-op “Coop Dark Souls” framing and was superseded once the game became a single-player JRPG with multiple distinct dungeons (003 §100 Floor Dungeon). Note (editorial): DEBATED — editorial inference The claim that the 100-floor concept “may have evolved into the multiple-dungeon system” is an editorial inference, not stated in the notes. The notes record the 100-floor concept (Part 1) and the multiple/elemental dungeons (Part 3) separately without connecting them (003 §Difficulty Scaling). Design history 2021-05-25 — original concept: one 100-floor dungeon, character-based, co-op online, with difficulty scaling that pushes toward co-op on later floors (M00128, CrunchyConniptions). later — EVOLVED superseded by a single-player JRPG with multiple distinct dungeons; see [[dungeon-authoring]] (003 §100 Floor Dungeon). Provenance Raw: M00128 (2021-05-25) Cleaned: 000 Part 1 §Core Game Concept; §Difficulty Scaling Docs: 003 §100 Floor Dungeon (Early Concept); §Difficulty Scaling; 008 §Core Game Concept Ledger: row R01 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Lycosia","group":"Locations","url":"locations/lycosia.html","text":"Lycosia CANON The southern country that is the seat of the empire — a river-defined great power whose big river runs through the south. Summary — Lycosia is a country in the south and the seat of an empire, “good in terms of power” (M03843, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Its “big boy” river runs through the south and serves as a social and economic throughline for the nation. Its language is Imperial Lycosian, and its river’s name is meant to be twinned with [[fiore|Fiore]]'s. Geography A country in the south , with a big river in the south (003 §Countries and Regions). Lycosia’s river flows in one direction , distinct from [[fiore|Fiore]]'s river, which flows in a different direction (M03834–M03836, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; 003 §Countries and Regions). There is one main big river plus other smaller, less-important rivers across the setting; Lycosia’s is one of the two named great rivers (M03823, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). Politics culture Political status: Seat of empire; good in terms of power (M03843, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). In the same discussion the struggling rival country was framed as a republic or dictatorship (M03847, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). River culture: The river is a social and economic throughline for Lycosia; society is denser around the river and coast because it is safer and provides freshwater, and much commerce travels the river via canals (M03796–M03798, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; 003 §Settlement Patterns). Language: Imperial Lycosian is the spoken language (003 §Countries and Regions). Story connections Twinned river names: The Lycosian and Fioran rivers should have twinned/related names, tied to the lore that [[ajax|Ajax]] “split a mountain” to make them (M03819–M03820, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; 003 §Rivers). See [[fiore|Fiore]] and the [[countries-and-regions|regional overview]]. Design history 2023-10-13 — Lycosia established as the seat of an empire, “good in terms of power” (M03843, FreshToast), during the river-politics session that also produced the twinned-river idea (M03819–M03820, FreshToast) and rejected a single-nation river monopoly (M03855, FreshToast). 2023-10-13 (later in the same session) — river detail expanded: the big southern river as a social/economic throughline, flowing in one direction opposite [[fiore|Fiore]]'s, with commerce moving by canal (M03796–M03836, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). Provenance Raw: M03796–M03798, M03823–M03836, M03843, M03847, M03855 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 15 §Country Politics; 000 Part 15 §The River System Docs: 003 §Countries and Regions; 003 §Settlement Patterns; 003 §Rivers Ledger: rows R21 (“Country politics… Lycosia is the seat of an empire”; “One main big river…”) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Maid Inn (MAIDEN)","group":"Locations","url":"locations/maid-inn.html","text":"Maid Inn (MAIDEN) CANON The rest-stop inn and maid-cafe parody where you talk to NPCs to rest — and where the maid’s “moe moe kyun” weapon blessing is secretly real magic. Summary — The Maid Inn (“MAIDEN,” a wordplay on the name) is an inn and rest location styled as a maid-cafe parody (M01262–M01263, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). It is framed as a place for disadvantaged women to find work, and functionally it is the rest stop where you talk to an NPC to rest (M01262, M01267, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). It has a bar, and the resident maid blesses weapons with a “moe moe kyun” theme — a blessing that is actually functional magic. Type purpose Type: Inn / rest location / maid-cafe parody (M01263, FreshToast, 2023-02-09; 003 §Maid Inn). A place for disadvantaged women to find work (M01262, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). A rest stop: The resident maid “works the bar” and is “the NPC you talk to for rest” (M01267, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Features Has a bar (M01267, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). The maid the blessing: A super-nice maid (M01268, FreshToast) blesses weapons (M01269, CrunchyConniptions) “with the whole moe moe kyun thing” (M01270, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). The food blessing is real magic: “The food… blessing is actually magic” — a functional gameplay element, not just flavor (M01271, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Name note “ MAIDEN ” is a wordplay on maid + inn (M01285–M01286, FreshToast, 2023-02-09; 003 §Maid Inn). Story connections The cross-reference table floats a possible connection between [[clara|Clara]] / Marie and the Maid Inn (003 §Character-Location Connections). Provenance Raw: M01262–M01263, M01267–M01271, M01285–M01286 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09) Cleaned: 000 Part 2 §Maid Inn; 000 Part 5 (maid inn / cafe parody) Docs: 003 §Maid Inn (MAIDEN); 002 Character Profile (maid blessing); 001 World Bible lines 215–216 Ledger: rows R07 (“Maid inn location: a place for disadvantaged women to find work; a maid cafe parody”) and R08 (“Maid Inn NPC (‘Maiden’ pun)…”) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Marketplace (Caelia)","group":"Locations","url":"locations/marketplace-caelia.html","text":"Marketplace (Caelia) CANON The bustling trading heart of [[caelia|Caelia]] — an active hub where the merchant [[elara|Elara]] sends you off on the Moonstone Mania quest. Summary — The Marketplace is the bustling trading centre within [[caelia|Caelia]], staffed by the weary merchant [[elara|Elara]] and serving as the starting point of the Moonstone Mania quest (M05266–M05272, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). It is the specific point of interest at the core of Caelia’s identity as a marketplace hub; broader lore about Caelia lives on the [[caelia|Caelia]] page. Description A bustling marketplace and active trading hub (003 §Marketplace (Caelia)). Set in [[caelia|Caelia]]: “A bustling marketplace in Caelia” (M05271, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). NPCs [[elara|Elara]] — a weary merchant of the Caelia marketplace, who the player approaches to kick off the Moonstone quest (M05271, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13; 003 §Marketplace). Story connections — Moonstone Mania The Moonstone Mania quest begins here: the player pitches a moonstone-hoarding scheme to [[elara|Elara]] (a riff on their old gem-hoarding exploits with sapphires, rubies and emeralds), and Elara reluctantly hands over a starting stash (M05271, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). The quest escalates until the reclusive Archmage Xarthus dubs the player the “Moonstone Messiah” (M05271–M05272, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). The correct Skyrim-style reward is actually a permanent buff to find more valuables in chests (M05266, FreshToast, 2024-02-13). Quest details are in the Quest guide. Provenance Raw: M05266 (FreshToast); M05271–M05272 (CrunchyConniptions) — all 2024-02-13. The marketplace-scene and Elara details come from M05271–M05272; M05266 is the Skyrim-reward note. Cleaned: 000 §Moonstone Mania Docs: 003 §Marketplace (Caelia); 004 §Moonstone Mania Ledger: row R28 (“Named NPCs of the Moonstone quest: weary merchant Elara (in Caelia marketplace)…”) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Mountains — Key Mountain, Sneak Peak & Ashen Crags","group":"Locations","url":"locations/mountains.html","text":"Mountains — Key Mountain, Sneak Peak Ashen Crags CANON The setting’s major peaks: the river-source mountain that is the dragon’s lair, the cloud-hidden “Sneak Peak,” and the volcanic Ashen Crags. Summary — Three mountain features are established. The Key Mountain is the dragon’s lair at the source of the twinned rivers, its top damaged by Ajax in his legendary dragon fight — damage that (narratively exaggerated as “splitting the mountain”) formed the rivers themselves. Sneak Peak is a cloud-hidden mountain wreathed in rumor, home to the Ninja Village and its stealthy bandits. The Ashen Crags are the volcanic region around the volcano, tied to the dragon’s domain. See also [[world-map-and-travel]] for how rivers and mountains structure the map. Key Mountain (Dragon’s Lair) Follow the river to reach the top of the mountain , which is where the river starts and where the dragon is (M03770, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13; 003 §Key Mountain). The party seeks the summit “because the dragon is there.” The dragon’s power stems from some feature of the mountain. Ajax damaged the top during his fight with the dragon (003 §Key Mountain). River-formation connection: CANON Ajax’s damage to the mountaintop — narratively exaggerated as “splitting the mountain” — caused the twinned rivers to form from several smaller ones; the lake they feed was later flooded into its present state by the hero of the second game (“SO it’s A BIG DEAL”) (M03819–M03824, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Ajax was “high off absorbing a dragon’s strength at the peak of his power” when he did it. See [[world-map-and-travel]] §Rivers. Sneak Peak Mountain A mountain foreshadowed and hidden by clouds , surrounded by rumor (M01926–M01930, FreshToast, 2023-02-14; 003 §Sneak Peak Mountain). Rumors include it harboring bandits with near-supernatural stealth ; so much intrigue that many people search for concrete information about what happens there. The reveal is the name itself — “Sneak Peak,” an intentional pun on “sneak peek.” Location of the Ninja Village (the mountain ninja village, distinct from the Naruto sky-village) — see the settlements batch (M01962–M01974, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). Mountain paths here are used as terrifying dungeon locations — see [[dungeon-authoring]] §Variety. Ashen Crags (Volcanic Region) CANON The region around the volcano (M02078, FreshToast, 2023-02-15; 003 §Geography and Natural Features). Story connection: the volcanic region is tied to the dragon’s domain and the dragon confrontation; the dragon can “smash through rock, chase you down the volcano” (M01784, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Cross-reference: the volcano’s heat is the origin of Dragon Glass (obsidian-like material formed from stone melted by the dragon’s breath then cooled; suggested names Hyrrglas / Bruniglas) — primarily item lore. PROPOSED — proto-derived inference The Magic Academy (former cathedral) is sited near the Ashen Crags. The notes list the academy and the crags separately without linking them; the placement is a reconciliation, not Discord canon (003 §Geography). Other mountain features Garden of the Gods is hidden in a basin within the mountain range — see [[biomes]]. Terrifying mountain paths are one of the planned non-cave dungeon types — see [[dungeon-authoring]]. Design history 2023-02-11 — volcano established as the dragon’s domain: the dragon can chase you down the volcano (M01784, FreshToast). 2023-02-14 — Sneak Peak Mountain conceived: cloud-hidden, rumor-wreathed, name is a pun, hosts the Ninja Village (M01926–M01930, FreshToast). 2023-02-15 — the volcanic region is named the Ashen Crags (M02078, FreshToast). 2023-10-13 — the Key Mountain is fixed as the river-source and dragon’s lair; Ajax’s mountaintop damage is retconned into the origin of the twinned rivers (M03770, M03819–M03824, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). Current presentation. Provenance Raw: M01784 (2023-02-11); M01926–M01930 (2023-02-14); M02078 (2023-02-15); M03770, M03819–M03824 (2023-10-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 (Sneak Peak); Part 8 (Ashen Crags); Part 15 (river/mountain) Docs: 003 §Key Mountain (Dragon’s Lair); §Sneak Peak Mountain; §Geography and Natural Features Ledger: rows R11, R15, R21 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Ninja Village","group":"Locations","url":"locations/ninja-village.html","text":"Ninja Village CANON A village of ninjas hidden in the mountains — home to shinobi with supernatural powers and a hokage you fight, tucked away on [[mountains#sneak-peak-mountain|Sneak Peak Mountain]]. Summary — The Ninja Village is a village of ninjas in the mountains whose inhabitants have supernatural ninja powers (M01966–M01970, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-14). You fight the hokage here (M01969, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Its characters include Hikari the shinobi and another shy character. It is explicitly not the Naruto sky-village. Location A village of ninjas in the mountains (M01966–M01968, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). Associated with [[mountains#sneak-peak-mountain|Sneak Peak Mountain]] , the cloud-hidden mountain rumored to harbor bandits with near-supernatural stealth (003 §Ninja Village, §Sneak Peak Mountain). Clarification: Not the Naruto village — when asked “is it there that you find the Naruto-village” (M01962, CrunchyConniptions), the answer distinguishes this as its own mountain village; the Naruto-style village “is in the sky” (M01964, FreshToast, 2023-02-14; 003 §Ninja Village). Features Supernatural ninja powers: The inhabitants have supernatural ninja powers (M01970, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). Fight the hokage: “You fight the hokage” here (M01969, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Characters Hikari the shinobi — introduced as “hikari the shinobi with huge anime tiddies” (M01973, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). See the Character Guide. Another shy character — “the other cute shy one with normal tiddies” (M01974, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). Provenance Raw: M01962, M01964, M01966, M01968–M01970, M01973–M01974 (CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-14) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 §Ninja Village Docs: 003 §Ninja Village; 002 §Hikari; 001 World Bible Ledger: row R11 (“Ninja village in the mountains (distinct from the Naruto sky-village)…”) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Santier","group":"Locations","url":"locations/santier.html","text":"Santier CANON The distant continent — the only place the world-healing panacea fruit is known to grow, and the place [[arthur-marais|Arthur Marais]] vanished on his final travail. Summary — Santier is a distant continent and the sole known home of the panacea fruit, the miraculous curative from which Ambrosia is refined (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). Panacea trees are vanishingly rare and strange; those who eat the fruit claim they can hear the trees “sing.” [[arthur-marais|Arthur Marais]] discovered and named the panacea here and vanished on his final trip; only the Ambrosia recipe returned to [[countries-and-regions#khorath|Khorath]], without him. Geography A distant continent (003 §Santier). Panacea trees are so rare and sparsely scattered that [[arthur-marais|Arthur]] only ever handled seven mature panacea during his time on Santier (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). The trees are always found in a clearing “bereft of all but the most resilient flora,” and the local wildlife will not feed on the fruit — warning signs of its darker side effects (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). The third and largest tree Arthur found was isolated in a pool of clear water (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). Features — the panacea and its strangeness The “singing” trees: Those who have eaten panacea claim they can hear the trees sing while nearby; the sound intensifies the more one eats, until it becomes an intolerable furore. Arthur used this sound to locate the third tree (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). The fruit: Panacea instantly restores vitality to perfect condition and even restored Arthur’s lost arm; it bears no seed, decays within days of picking, and its flavour cannot be described except by analogy to cherished memories (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07). Cross-reference: The panacea and Ambrosia — and panacea’s madness/revelation side effects — are primarily item/consumable lore. See the Item Consumables guide (006 / raw M03661). Story connections Arthur’s disappearance: Santier is where [[arthur-marais|Arthur Marais]] vanished on his final travail. Only the Ambrosia recipe returned to [[countries-and-regions#khorath|Khorath]], created on that last trip, without Arthur, who never came back (M03661, FreshToast, 2023-09-07; 003 §Santier). Provenance Raw: M03661 (FreshToast, 2023-09-07) — the long “# Panacea” lore drop Cleaned: 000 Caelian Odyssey notes (~5896–6029); 000 §Panacea Docs: 003 §Santier; cross-referenced to 006 Item Consumables Ledger: panacea/Ambrosia item rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Sodden Depths","group":"Locations","url":"locations/sodden-depths.html","text":"The Sodden Depths DEBATED A location name reserved in the notes, with almost nothing attached to it yet. Summary — “The Sodden Depths” is a named location whose nature, contents, and placement are undefined. Only “Location name established” appears in the notes (M03050, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-15). The name is reserved and awaits definition. What is (and isn’t) known Only the name exists in the notes; no geography, contents, or story role were recorded (003 §The Sodden Depths). DEBATED The proto’s gamebible frames it as the drowned, water-bound region framing the [[elemental-dungeons|Water Dungeon]], but this is the gamebible’s inference, not established in our notes (003 §The Sodden Depths). Status: name reserved, needs definition. Open questions DEBATED What is the Sodden Depths — a dungeon, a region, a biome? Is it the same as, or the setting of, the Water Dungeon? Unresolved (003 §The Sodden Depths). Design history 2023-03-15 — location name established, no further detail (M03050, spirit_noodle). Provenance Raw: M03050 (2023-03-15) Cleaned: 000 Part 11 §The Sodden Depths Docs: 003 §The Sodden Depths Ledger: row R17 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Luminarium","group":"Locations","url":"locations/the-luminarium.html","text":"The Luminarium CANON The magic academy that gives the Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium iteration its name — a central hub where the whole story is kept “in house” and about the academy itself. Summary — The Luminarium is a magic academy and hub area, the central setting of the separate iteration Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium (M04249, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). It was proposed as a Dark Souls Firelink/Majula-style central safe point rather than a mechanical river-tether (M04100–M04101, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). By design there is no antagonist faction to the Luminarium — the story is kept “in house” and about the academy (M04306–M04307, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). It is not the same place as the main game’s Magic Academy (the former cathedral). Type function Type: Magic academy / hub area; central to the plot (003 §The Luminarium). As a hub: Proposed as a hub — “but what we CAN do is make a hub… I.e. the Luminarium” — a central safe point in the Dark Souls tradition (M04100–M04101, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Design philosophy: “Keep it about the Luminarium” — no antagonist faction to this location; all the bad stuff is chalked up to [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]] and Tenebrae, keeping it “in house” (M04306–M04307, FreshToast, 2023-10-17; 003 §The Luminarium). Features Houses with guides: The academy has houses, each with a guide; the guide for “the boyes’ house” is described as cool and nice (M04329, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Forbidden lore: Areas in the academy library are off-limits — secrets that are forbidden for reasons the plot can explore (proposal by CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17; 003 §The Luminarium). Magical tournaments: Held here as a classic way to show character growth, introduce rivals, and deepen friendships (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17; 003 §The Luminarium). Magical creatures: Present as both threats and allies, some used in magical training (003 §The Luminarium). The Academy’s Head: An ancient, wise character who has seen the rise and fall of many powerful sorcerers and holds secrets central to the plot (M04302, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). Story connections Locke’s homecoming: [[locke|Locke]] returns to the Luminarium later in the game, and the intent is that “returning to the Luminarium as Locke will be like seeing Anor Londo in Dark Souls 3” — an emotional homecoming via a familiar location (M04132, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; 003 §The Luminarium). Not to be confused with the Magic Academy CANON The Luminarium is not the main game’s Magic Academy (the former cathedral). They belong to different iterations: the Magic Academy is the main game’s academy (core to Gaius’s story, formerly a cathedral), while the Luminarium is the academy setting of Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium . They are not interchangeable (003 §Story Connections, Iteration Distinction note). Design history 2023-10-13 — the Luminarium proposed as a hub area — a Dark Souls Firelink/Majula-style central safe point — instead of a mechanical river-tether (M04100–M04101, FreshToast); the Anor-Londo-homecoming idea for [[locke|Locke]]'s return floated the same day (M04132, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — the iteration named “Caelian Odyssey - The Luminarium” (M04248–M04249, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions); features fleshed out — houses with guides (M04329), forbidden lore, magical tournaments, magical creatures, and the Academy’s Head (M04302, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-10-17 — the antagonist question raised — “Is there a rival to the Luminarium?” (M04286, CrunchyConniptions) — then decisively answered: no antagonist faction , keep it “in house” and about the Luminarium (M04306–M04307, FreshToast) — supersedes the rival-faction proposal . Provenance Raw: M04100–M04101, M04132 (FreshToast, 2023-10-13); M04248–M04249, M04286, M04302, M04306–M04307, M04329 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 Part 5/16 §The Luminarium; 000 §Location Guides Docs: 003 §The Luminarium; 003 §Story Connections Ledger: rows R22 (“The Luminarium proposed as a hub area…”; “Returning to the Luminarium as Locke will feel like Anor Londo”), R23 (“NO antagonist faction… keep it in house”; iteration named ‘Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium’) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Town (Dragon-Attacked)","group":"Locations","url":"locations/town-dragon-attacked.html","text":"Town (Dragon-Attacked) CANON The town partially ruined by a dragon attack, which the party helps rebuild over the course of the game — a living progression system in place form. Summary — This is a town partially ruined by a dragon attack “after it yoinkies the sploinky,” with repairs that progress over time as the game continues (M01823, FreshToast, 2023-02-12). It functions as a town-progression system: reconstruction happens gradually as the player advances. The town has no finalized proper name in the notes, so its working name is used here. Description A town partially ruined by a dragon attack , phrased in the chat as happening “after it yoinkies the sploinky” (M01823, FreshToast, 2023-02-12). The name and precise location are not established in the notes; the working label “Town (Dragon-Attacked)” is retained (placeholder_name: true). Features — town progression Gradual repairs: Repairs progress over time as the game continues — a town-progression system where reconstruction happens gradually rather than all at once (M01823, FreshToast, 2023-02-12; 003 §Town (Dragon-Attacked)). Story connections Damaris family: The cross-reference table links the Damaris family to the dragon-attack town and the family’s sun symbolism, whose “eclipse” motif ties to nasty happenings befalling their people (003 §Character-Location Connections; 003 §Damaris Family Locations). The wider dragon confrontation is tied to the [[mountains#key-mountain-dragons-lair|Key Mountain (Dragon’s Lair)]] and the Ashen Crags volcanic region. Provenance Raw: M01823 (FreshToast, 2023-02-12) Cleaned: 000 Part 5 §Town (dragon attack) Docs: 003 §Town (Dragon-Attacked); 003 §Character-Location Connections Ledger: see location rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"World Map, Travel & Settlement Geography","group":"Locations","url":"locations/world-map-and-travel.html","text":"World Map, Travel Settlement Geography CANON How you move through the world and how it’s laid out — compass over maps, the river as the spine of civilization, danger rising toward the edges. Summary — Traversal favors a compass over maps (Skyrim reference): the actual map is deliberately simple and “not that necessary.” The “REAL TABOO” is a world so hard to traverse yourself that you’re forced to stare at a minimap. Geographically, society clusters along the river and coast (safer, freshwater); the river doubles as a visual compass, and much commerce moves on it by canal. The map’s outer portions are less settled and more dangerous , with the hardest dungeons far from the river — possibly in a barren desert or underground. Navigation philosophy Compass over maps (Skyrim reference); the compass is “a neat minimap, definitely better in some ways” (M00109, FreshToast, 2021-05-13; 003 §Navigation Philosophy). The actual map is very simple and not that necessary (003 §Navigation Philosophy). Design taboo: the “REAL TABOO” is when it’s too hard to traverse the world yourself, so you have to use a minimap/maps — a concern raised against Final Fantasy and Witcher examples (M00115, M00116, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13). Goal: “Let there be chaos. let there be freedom. let there be adventure” (003 §Navigation Philosophy). Implemented: CANON — proto in-dungeon navigation is compass-style labels with no minimap (e.g. “Press into the dark”, “Take the faster, darker way”) (proto: node compass field; src/web/screens.ts). See [[dungeon-authoring]]. Task noted: “Make world map” was logged as a to-do (M02384, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-17). Rivers as the world’s spine Cultural importance: rivers are culturally important across multiple nations (003 §Rivers). See the countries/regions batch for [[countries-and-regions|Lycosia, Fiore, and others]]. The river also acts as a visual compass (M04021, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). Twinned names: the Lycosian and Fioran rivers have related/twinned names, tied to their shared origin (M03819, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). See [[mountains]] §Key Mountain for the river-formation lore. River Healing: rivers provide special healing/restoration abilities via the [[river-flask|river flask]] (items batch); refill points are the [[camps-and-wells|wells]]. Conservation reverence: CANON the river is a protected area held in deep respect; keeping it clean is a strong cultural value, and fouling it is socially prohibited (“they are kind of absurd about it”) — deliberately the opposite of real-world rivers like the Nile (M03799, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). Pray-toward-river practice: SCRAPPED a Mecca-direction analogue where everyone prays toward the river was rejected because the river is very long, making a single prayer direction impractical (003 §Rivers). Settlement patterns / map layout CANON Settlement distribution: settlements are coastal and river-centered; society is denser around the river and coast because it’s safer and gives freshwater (M03796, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; 003 §Settlement Patterns). Towns can exist close to the river but not right next to it — “the river is for everyone, not for the people that happen to settle on the river” (M03810, M03811, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). Much commerce travels on the river via canals (M03797, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Map danger gradient: the outer portions of the map are less settled and more dangerous; the harder dungeons are far from the river, and the hardest may be in a barren desert or underground (M03925, M03969, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). This connects to the [[elemental-dungeons|Desert Dungeon]] and the deep-underground [[ajaxs-tomb|tomb]]. Open questions DEBATED The status of Caelia (isle vs. city vs. region) and where the Capital sits — see the settlements/regions batch; unresolved between the notes and the proto’s gamebible. Design history 2021-05-13 — navigation philosophy set: compass over maps, minimaps as the “REAL TABOO,” simple world map (M00092–M00116, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). 2023-02-17 — “make world map” logged as a to-do (M02384, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-10-13 — geography fleshed out: river-and-coast-centered settlement, canals for commerce, the river as visual compass and protected cultural spine, the outer-map danger gradient with the hardest dungeons far from the river (M03796–M04021, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). Current presentation. 2026-06-16 — proto confirms compass-style, minimap-free navigation in the implemented dungeon (003 header; proto: screens.ts). Provenance Raw: M00092–M00116 (2021-05-13); M02384 (2023-02-17); M03796, M03797, M03799, M03810, M03811, M03819, M03925, M03969, M04021 (2023-10-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 1 (navigation taboos); Part 15 (settlement/river) Docs: 003 §World Map and Travel; §Settlement Patterns / Map Layout; §Rivers Ledger: rows R21 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (see also navigation notes under early-mechanics rows)"},{"title":"Brickwings (The Brick Arc)","group":"Story","url":"story/brickwings.html","text":"Brickwings (The Brick Arc) CANON A throwaway joke that becomes a party member: [[gaius-wornwall|Gaius]] animates a brick, it sprouts wings and escapes, later rejoins the party, is beloved, and dies comically — with [[ruby|Ruby]] quietly relating to it. Summary — Brickwings is a running gag arc. The party needs to throw a brick but can’t reach far enough, so [[gaius-wornwall|Gaius]] animates it — the brick grows wings , flies through a window, and escapes to freedom. It’s found again later ([[ruby|Ruby]] berates Gaius for not taking care of it), joins the party, becomes wholesome and beloved across a couple of scenes, then dies comically — a literal throwaway joke. Ruby relates to the brick (004 §BRICKWINGS Story Arc). The arc CANON Beat sequence, all from one 2023-02-08 brainstorm titled “BRICKWINGS” (M01176, spirit_noodle): Need to throw a brick; can’t throw it far enough (M01177, M01178, FreshToast, 2023-02-08). [[gaius-wornwall|Gaius]] animates the brick, it gets wings (M01179, 2023-02-08). The brick flies through a window but escapes; the brick is now free (M01180, M01181). Later they find the brick again; [[ruby|Ruby]] berates Gaius for not taking care of it (M01182). The brick joins the party ; it’s wholesome and beloved, with a couple of scenes (M01183, M01184). The brick dies comically — a literal throwaway joke (M01186). Ruby relates to the brick (M01188, FreshToast, 2023-02-08). The arc demonstrates [[gaius-wornwall|Gaius]]'s object-animation ability in a comedic register, and gives [[ruby|Ruby]] a small, oblique emotional beat (relating to something discarded/freed). It fits the [[narrative-themes|“death and regrets”]] theme’s lighter end — carrying the influence of the dead (even a brick) into later story. Design history 2023-02-08 — the entire arc is spitballed in one short exchange, opened by spirit_noodle’s single-word “BRICKWINGS” and fleshed out by FreshToast: throw → animate → wings → escape → reunion → party member → beloved → comic death → Ruby relates (M01176–M01188, 2023-02-08). No later revision recorded. Provenance Raw: M01176 (spirit_noodle, 2023-02-08); M01177–M01188 (FreshToast, 2023-02-08) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 §BRICKWINGS Story Arc Docs: 004 §BRICKWINGS Story Arc; 002 §Brickwings (The Brick) Ledger: row R07 (M01176… BRICKWINGS arc) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Chaos Emeralds Quest (Ajax's Relics)","group":"Story","url":"story/chaos-emeralds-quest.html","text":"The Chaos Emeralds Quest (Ajax’s Relics) CANON The main-quest collectathon: seven relics of the hero Ajax, framed to the party (and the world) as a power-grab, but actually a leash forged to stop his bloodline from becoming monsters. Summary — The spine of the main storyline is the pursuit of the seven [[chaos-emeralds|chaos emeralds]] — properly Ajax’s seven relics. Gathered, they do not grant superpowers; they deliver a revelation about Ajax and his bloodline, and function as the leash the ancient hero left to bind his own hungering descendants ([[lysander]] chief among them). The collection was deliberately designed to be “somewhat of a dud in the end” — an anti-climax by intent, not a botched payoff (M01425, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). The player’s real reward is the act of gathering itself, which is what makes the bearer strong. Description The relics are the questline’s throughline, driving Locke’s arc from hero-worship to independence (see the motivation chain in §Why the player collects them below). They are treated as valuable and sentimental to Ajax, tied to the spirit he made a pact with (004 §Relic Details). Relics are actually valuable and need a purpose — not filler loot (004 §Relic Details). They should be sentimental to Ajax and tied to “his whole deal”; an object he owned that was shattered, related to the spirit he made his pact with (004 §Relic Details). Brought together they grant a revelation, not superpowers — a way to learn about Ajax and his bloodline (004 §Relic Details). The first relic, [[sunderer|the Sunderer]] (Ajax’s spear), is acquired in the [[prologue-tutorial|prologue tutorial]] — the first of “Ajax’s seven thingies” (004 §Tutorial Outcome). Rules / Effects — the “leash” CANON The 7 relics form an informal set of trials. Once all are acquired, the bearer will “inherit Ajax’s legacy.” People misinterpret this as acquiring Ajax’s power; in truth, the act of acquiring them is what makes one powerful (004 §7 Relics System). Ajax’s legacy is specifically a final task: to challenge his descendant and prevent them from consuming thousands in pursuit of power (000 notes, Ajax’s Legacy System; 004 §7 Relics System). Ajax “left behind a relic that had helped him contain his own power in the past” (004 §Lysander 2nd Phase Fight Scene). The proto GameBible synthesizes these into a single reading: the seven relics are the leash — keepsakes bound to the spirit Ajax bargained with, scattered as trials so that when one of his blood grows monstrous, someone clever and patient enough can gather them and bind the hunger . The climactic use of “the relic of old” against [[lysander-final-boss|Lysander’s phase 2]] is therefore the purpose the relics were forged for (proto: gamebible/data/story.js , gamebible/data/quests.js ; grounded in 000 notes). Why the player collects them (in-world impetus) A compelling, plot-centric reason is required for the player to gather them (M02417, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19). The chain settled on: A threat looms (maybe demons coming, or a dragon); a hero must grow stronger and sends the player to collect (004 §Core Concept). [[lysander]] and [[eris]] discuss gathering the emeralds to defeat the dragon (M02419, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). [[locke]] overhears and decides to gather them herself to prove worthy (004 §Core Concept). Her motive shifts from pleasing the hero to saving the people ; she finally works to keep them away from Lysander (004 §Core Concept). The deliberate “dud” CANON The seven emeralds “were meant to be somewhat of a dud in the end” — a deliberate anti-climax rather than a payoff (M01425, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). This is consistent with the relic concept: gathered, they grant a revelation, not superpowers. The design intent is that the dud is the point — a subversion of the collectathon-reward expectation. (Cross-ref: this “dud revelation” attitude mirrors [[lysander-final-boss|Lysander’s]] own reaction to learning his power’s source — “Oh I have demon blood powers? Sweet!” (M02211, FreshToast, 2023-02-16).) Related systems The [[chaos-emeralds|chaos emeralds]] item entry (items namespace). [[sunderer|The Sunderer]] — the first relic, acquired in the prologue. Ajaxian trials — the informal trials framing: the seven relics are “those challenges left behind by Ajax” (M05288, FreshToast, 2024-02-13). “Trial” was reserved as a generic quest term precisely because these Ajaxian trials already exist in-world (see [[venture-terminology]]). [[daemon-of-want-ending|The Daemon of Want]] — the spirit Ajax bargained with, and the true final antagonist. Design history 2023-02-09 — a chaos emerald first appears concretely: Lyra the Necromancer “has one of the seven chaos emeralds” (M01280, FreshToast, 2023-02-09), establishing the seven-emerald set as scattered plot MacGuffins. 2023-02-11 — FreshToast recalls the emeralds “were meant to be somewhat of a dud in the end,” locking the anti-climax intent while arguing to keep ordinary loot/rarity separate so players still have a reason to upgrade gear (M01425, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). 2023-02-19 — the in-world impetus is worked out: a plot-centric reason to collect them (M02417); Lysander Eris seek them to defeat the dragon while Locke overhears and pursues her own path (M02419, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). 2023-02-21 — the relics are tied to Ajax’s pact with the [[daemon-of-want-ending|Daemon of Want]] (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21), grounding the “leash”/legacy reading. 2023-02-26 — a land-pirate NPC is floated to inform Locke of family history and where a chaos emerald is (M02809, FreshToast, 2023-02-26). 2026-06-16 — proto reconciliation folds the notes-grounded “legacy = final task to challenge the descendant” and “relic that contained his power” into the single relics-as-leash framing (proto GameBible). Provenance Raw: M01280 (2023-02-09); M01425 (2023-02-11); M02417, M02419, M02445, M02448, M02449 (2023-02-19); M02653 (2023-02-21); M02809 (2023-02-26) Cleaned: 000 Part 9 (chaos emerald motivations), Ajax’s Legacy System Docs: 004 §Main Quest: Chaos Emeralds / Ajax’s Relics; §Relic Details; §7 Relics System Ledger: rows R08 (M01425 dud), R13 (M02417/M02419 motivation chain), R14 (M02653 Ajax’s deal) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Clara's Final Confrontation","group":"Story","url":"story/clara-final-confrontation.html","text":"Clara’s Final Confrontation CANON [[clara|Clara Damaris]]'s climactic face-off: she interrupts the villain’s monologue to flip the threat back onto them, Princess Bride style — either consumed by vengeance, or, if the player guided her toward justice, choosing something more hopeful. Summary — Clara confronts whoever did the nasty deeds against her family. They try to monologue; Clara interrupts and threatens them Princess Bride style : “I am heir Damaris. You have tainted our family’s reputation… Today you die.” The design deliberately flips the script — the boss is usually the one to deliver the threat, but here it’s Clara. Whether she is consumed by vengeance or chooses hope depends on the player’s guidance of her arc (004 §Clara’s Final Confrontation). The confrontation CANON Setup (004 §Confrontation Setup): A confrontation with whoever did the nasty deeds in her family. They attempt to monologue; Clara interrupts and threatens them Princess Bride style (M02016, FreshToast, 2023-02-14): “I am heir Damaris. You have tainted our family’s reputation… Today you die.” (M02016, FreshToast, 2023-02-14) The intent is to let her be consumed by the pursuit of vengeance , and to flip the script on the boss usually being the one to deliver the threat. CrunchyConniptions approved the beat as “big Edelgard energy” (M02024, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14; ledger R11). A characterization note: Clara does not curse until her final confrontation — her language holding until this breaking point is the payoff of her arc (M02076, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). (Cross-ref: elsewhere, when confronted by the foil for standing with [[thane]], Clara — far into her arc — temporarily drops her noble pretense and tells him to “fuck off and get over himself” (M01389, FreshToast, 2023-02-10).) Alternative path CANON If the player guides her toward justice over vengeance , she says something less menacing and more hopeful instead (004 §Alternative Path). This is the branch that spares her from being consumed — a player-facing choice about who Clara becomes. Related [[clara|Clara Damaris]] — the character; “heir Damaris” is her claim, opposing the [[bastard-damaris|bastard Damaris]] and whoever tainted the family. The vengeance-vs-justice branch echoes the wider [[narrative-themes|Death and Regrets / vengeance]] theme (minor villains who kill for vengeance of the dead). Design history 2023-02-14 — the confrontation is drafted in one message: the interrupted monologue, the Princess Bride “I am heir Damaris… Today you die” line, the flip-the-script framing, and the consumed-by-vengeance vs. guided-to-justice branch (M02016, FreshToast, 2023-02-14); CrunchyConniptions approves it as “big Edelgard energy” (M02024, 2023-02-14). 2023-02-15 — the character rule that Clara does not curse until this final confrontation is added, tying her language to the climax (M02076, FreshToast, 2023-02-15). Provenance Raw: M02016 (FreshToast, 2023-02-14, the confrontation + “I am heir Damaris” line); M02024 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14, “big Edelgard energy”); M02076 (FreshToast, 2023-02-15, no-cursing-until-final rule); M01389 (FreshToast, 2023-02-10, “fuck off” beat) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 §Clara’s Final Confrontation Docs: 004 §Clara’s Final Confrontation; 002 §Clara Ledger: rows R11 (M02016/M02024 confrontation), R12 (M02076 no-cursing rule) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Combat-Strategy Arc (Goblins → Dragon → Lysander → the Wish)","group":"Story","url":"story/combat-strategy-arc.html","text":"The Combat-Strategy Arc (Goblins → Dragon → Lysander → the Wish) CANON The player’s hands learn the theme before the cutscenes spell it out: beat goblins with raw power, beat the dragon by becoming a goblin, beat Lysander by becoming the dragon — and beat the wish itself by refusing to swing at all. Summary — The combat difficulty arc is deliberately the same arc as the narrative. Three escalating lessons: beat goblins with raw individual overwhelming power; beat the dragon by becoming as a goblin (many, united, humble); beat Lysander by becoming as the dragon. Mapped to the three acts, and capped by the [[daemon-of-want-ending|Daemon of Want]], which cannot be cut at all — only refused (004 §Combat Strategy Arc, CANON ). The three lessons CANON The core quotes, part of a “Story through gameplay” note (M05345, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-16): “Best strat to beat the goblins is raw individual overwhelming power.” “Best strat to beat the dragon is to become as goblin.” “Best strat to beat Lysander is to become as dragon.” The intent: “the player’s hands learn the theme before the cutscenes spell it out” (004 §Story Through Gameplay Philosophy). Mapped to the three acts CANON The mapping the team settled on (proto GameBible, “How the Story Is Told in Play”; grounded in the quotes above): Act I — tutorial dungeons (goblins beetles): small, swarming foes. Lesson: be overwhelming — hold formation, exploit joints and reach, end fights fast before escalation makes the swarm bold. The “joints, not the wall” golem lesson is implemented in the [[prologue-tutorial|prologue boss fight]] (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts , src/core/combat/content.ts ). Act II — the dragon, [[vaenngrym]]: a force-of-nature set-piece that fills every quarter of the field. You cannot out-stat or flank it. Lesson: become as a goblin — many, united, humble; [[locke]] rallies a crowd and grounds the god (see [[dragon-confrontation]]). Act III — [[lysander-final-boss|Lysander]], then the [[daemon-of-want-ending|Daemon]]: a two-phase final boss (radiant himbo who telegraphs his glory → the curse made flesh, the dragon his bloodline hid). Lesson: become as the dragon . The full arc: goblins → dragon → Lysander → the wish itself. The true superboss, the [[daemon-of-want-ending|Daemon of Want]], cannot be cut at all — it is beaten by refusal . (Combat positioning mechanics themselves are documented in 008 / the systems batch; this page covers the narrative-through-gameplay mapping only.) Design history 2024-02-16 — the three-lesson formula (“goblins → become as goblin → become as dragon”) is stated by CrunchyConniptions as a “Story through gameplay” note (M05345, 2024-02-16). 2026-06-16 — proto reconciliation maps the three lessons to the three acts and appends the Daemon as the un-cuttable fourth rung (“beaten by refusal”) in the GameBible (proto: gamebible/data/story.js ). Provenance Raw: M05345 (CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-16, the three-strat “Story through gameplay” note) Cleaned: 000 §Story Through Gameplay / Combat Strategy Arc Docs: 004 §Story Through Gameplay Philosophy; §Combat Strategy Arc; proto GameBible §How the Story Is Told in Play Ledger: see Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (combat-strategy / story-through-gameplay rows)"},{"title":"The True Ending — The Daemon of Want (\"The Wish That Watched\")","group":"Story","url":"story/daemon-of-want-ending.html","text":"The True Ending — The Daemon of Want (“The Wish That Watched”) CANON The real endgame: not [[lysander]], but the wish behind him. A spirit born from a whole kingdom’s ache for a savior, which cannot be stabbed — only starved, when a people stop begging to be saved and choose to grow their own light. Summary — Defeating [[lysander-final-boss|Lysander]] is not the true final beat. The endgame antagonist is the Daemon of Want , the spirit behind the whole bloodline curse — the secret superboss / hidden final encounter. It cannot be beaten with a blade; it is defeated by refusal : a people who stop wishing for a savior and grow their own light, leaving it to thin and dwindle, no longer fed. This page covers the Daemon as a story/ending beat ; its bargain and characterization as a world-entity live in the world/characters docs. The beat CANON The true superboss. The Daemon of Want is the game’s true superboss / secret final encounter — a spirit formed from the rampant, vague, widespread desire of a people for a savior (from the dragon and other woes). Because that desire is so diffuse, it is exceptionally powerful and cunning; ordinary daemons are weak, but this one “resulted from [the] entire kingdom’s shared woes” (M02653, M02656, FreshToast, 2023-02-21; M02683, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). It is not so much evil as self-serving , understands that revealing itself would lead to its downfall, and stays hidden (M02656, 2023-02-21). Its bargain is the engine of the whole plot. It sold Ajax the trick of draining others’ strength for the price of half his lifespan; every heir since (Lysander, the [[bastard-damaris|bastard Damaris]]) inherited a thinner, waning pour that must be refilled by killing and draining life (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). See [[chaos-emeralds-quest|Ajax’s relics]] and [[dragon-confrontation|Ajax’s Story]]. How it is beaten: you cannot stab a wish. It is “practically immortal so long as desire remains to feed it” (M02667, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). The thesis-made-mechanic: it is defeated not by a blade but by refusal — a people who stop wishing for a savior and choose to grow their own light. It does not die so much as it is left to thin and dwindle (000 notes; proto GameBible, gamebible/data/story.js ). Fight reactivity. The Daemon’s dialogue changes based on whether Locke drank the [[dragon-confrontation|dragon’s blood]]; DEBATED “perhaps mechanical change in fight as well” — left unresolved in both notes and proto (M02663, FreshToast, 2023-02-21; objection M02678, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22). Lore note. “Multiple Daemons of Want can exist at a time,” and daemons die naturally if starved — consistent with the “stop feeding it” defeat (M02676, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22; M02683, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). “Why it wanted you to win” DEBATED / PROPOSED The proto GameBible frames the Daemon as having secretly benefited from the player’s victories: a saved people stop praying, and a daemon starves when the wishing stops, so it feeds on the gap between what people have and what they ache for — and a grieving world that just lost its hero wishes harder for the next one (proto: gamebible/data/story.js , “The Wish That Watched”). DEBATED — this “it secretly fed on your victory” loop is the proto’s reconciliation/expansion. The brainstorm establishes the Daemon’s prayer-born origin and hidden-superboss nature but does not spell out the wins-feed-it loop; treat it as a strong proposed reading, not a brainstorm-locked beat (004 §The True Ending). Related [[lysander-final-boss|Lysander’s two-phase final boss]] — the penultimate encounter, whose curse is the Daemon’s bargain made flesh. [[dragon-confrontation|The Dragon Confrontation]] Dragon’s Blood Choice — sets the Daemon’s reactive dialogue. [[combat-strategy-arc|The combat-strategy arc]] — the Daemon is the “cannot be cut at all” final rung: goblins → dragon → Lysander → the wish itself. [[sir-valerius|Sir Valerius]] — separately hunting the Daemon of Want (M02902, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). Design history 2023-02-21 — the Daemon of Want is defined in a single burst: origin as a spirit born from the people’s desire for a savior; Ajax’s bargain (half a lifespan for stolen strength); characterization as cunning, self-serving, hidden; and the “practically immortal so long as desire remains to feed it” rule (M02653, M02656, M02663, M02667, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). 2023-02-22 — CrunchyConniptions raises the worldbuilding objection (why doesn’t it disappear? do you get a new one elsewhere?), answered: multiple Daemons of Want can coexist; daemons die naturally if starved (M02676, M02683, 2023-02-22). The “no mechanical change is unsatisfying” objection is also raised (M02678). 2023-03-05 — Sir Valerius established as a hunter of the Daemon of Want (M02902, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). 2026-06-16 — proto reconciliation names it “The Wish That Watched” and adds the (debated) “it secretly fed on your victory” loop (proto GameBible). Provenance Raw: M02653, M02656, M02663, M02667 (FreshToast, 2023-02-21); M02676, M02678, M02683 (CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-22); M02902 (FreshToast, 2023-03-05) Cleaned: 000 Part 10 (Daemon of Want; multiple daemons; starvation) Docs: 004 §The True Ending — The Daemon of Want; §Two-Phase Final Boss Structure; 001/002 §Daemon of Want Ledger: rows R14 (M02653/M02656 Ajax’s deal characterization), R15 (M02667/M02676/M02683 immortality multiplicity), R16 (M02902 Valerius) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Dragon Confrontation (Act 2 Climax) & Dragon's Blood Choice","group":"Story","url":"story/dragon-confrontation.html","text":"The Dragon Confrontation (Act 2 Climax) Dragon’s Blood Choice CANON The Act II set-piece: [[lysander]] refuses to face the dragon and lets towns burn while [[locke]] rallies a crowd, grounds [[vaenngrym]], and earns the public’s faith as Ajax’s true heir — then chooses whether to drink the dragon’s blood. Summary — With Lysander entranced by Ajax’s legacy and unwilling to fight, Locke upstages him: she rounds up help, works with people to ground the dragon [[vaenngrym]], and enters its den to face it. The moment defines Locke as the proper heir to Ajax’s legacy, shifts public opinion toward her and away from Lysander, and is the climax of her personal arc. Afterward, in a moment of levity, she may drink the dragon’s blood — a purely characterizing choice that changes the [[daemon-of-want-ending|Daemon of Want]]'s dialogue. Setup CANON Lysander refuses to face the dragon, gets more entranced by the power of Ajax’s legacy, and continues to let towns burn (004 §Dragon Confrontation). Locke upstages him by rounding up help , working with various people to ground the dragon, and finding a way into its den (004 §Dragon Confrontation). This is the [[combat-strategy-arc|Act II combat lesson]] made narrative: “become as a goblin” — many, united, humble. Motivations revealed CANON The dragon highlights each figure’s motivation (004 §Character Motivations Revealed): Ajax wants glory. Lysander wants strength. Locke wants to protect and make change. [[vaenngrym]] is a force of nature — a narrative tool, a big scary boss monster. Lysander is the failure — the only one who failed to step up when needed. Outcomes CANON The moment defines Locke as the proper heir to Ajax’s legacy. Shifts public opinion in favor of Locke, out of favor of Lysander . Climax of Locke’s personal character arc. Locke may get infected with Dracosis (undecided or player choice) — Ajax himself was infected upon stabbing his dragon (004 §Outcomes). Lysander’s response (004 §Lysander’s Response): outraged at Locke succeeding; more likely to warn than forbid her, and she ignores him. Can be shown via vignettes rather than a direct conversation. Open questions floated: is he jealous, envious, angry, a crybaby, loud and obnoxious? “Going with the flow on this part.” Dragon’s Blood Choice CANON Upon killing [[vaenngrym]], Locke is given the opportunity to drink the dragon’s blood — a moment of levity after the fight (M02661, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Either: she drinks it, everybody is grossed out, and nothing happens ; or she finds solace in her own strength and refuses to partake. It has no mechanical bearing , but informs Locke’s opinion on Lysander’s pursuit of power and her understanding of Ajax. The [[daemon-of-want-ending|Daemon of Want]]'s dialogue changes based on this choice (M02663, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). DEBATED “Perhaps mechanical change in fight as well” — left unresolved, and objected to as unsatisfying if there’s no mechanical change (M02678, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22). Lore tie-in: Ajax’s cover story was that he gained strength from drinking the dragon’s blood after wounding one — see Ajax’s Story below. Ajax’s Story (background) CANON Ajax became desperate for strength to defend his people, the only warrior truly brave enough to fight the dragon. He made a deal with the [[daemon-of-want-ending|Daemon of Want]], trading half his lifespan for the ability to steal the strength of others . His cover story was that he got strength from drinking the dragon’s blood after wounding it — but it was actually the first instance of using his stolen-strength power to drain life from the dragon during battle; he later killed it with that stolen strength (M02653, FreshToast, 2023-02-21; 004 §Ajax’s Story). Open questions DEBATED Whether Locke gets infected with Dracosis is left undecided or made a player choice (004 §Outcomes). DEBATED Whether the dragon’s-blood choice carries a mechanical fight change, not just dialogue (M02664/M02678). Design history 2023-02-21 — Ajax’s deal, cover story, and the drain-the-dragon truth are established (M02653); the Dragon’s Blood Choice and its reactive Daemon dialogue are drafted the same day (M02661–M02664, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). 2023-02-22 — objection that “no mechanical change” is unsatisfying (M02678, CrunchyConniptions), leaving the mechanical-vs-cosmetic question open. 2026-06-16 — proto reconciliation maps this to the Act II “become as a goblin” combat lesson (proto GameBible; see [[combat-strategy-arc]]). Provenance Raw: M02653, M02661, M02662, M02663, M02664 (FreshToast, 2023-02-21); M02678 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22) Cleaned: 000 (Ajax’s Power; Dragon’s Blood Choice) Docs: 004 §Dragon Confrontation (Act 2 Climax); §Dragon’s Blood Choice; §Ajax’s Story Ledger: rows R14 (M02653 Ajax’s deal), R14 (M02661–M02664 dragon’s-blood choice), R15 (M02678 mechanical-change objection) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Endings & Relationship Options","group":"Story","url":"story/endings.html","text":"Endings Relationship Options CANON How Soaring Cinders ends: the current design closes not on defeating the hero but on refusing the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]] — a wish you cannot stab — while earlier endings brainstorming framed all endings around fixing past regrets, plus a few joke and reactive outcomes. Summary — The ending material spans two eras. The thematic seed (2021) is that endings “should revolve about fixing past regrets” — “its yearning inside all people” (M00243, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26) — and included joke/loss endings and a relationship-side “enemies vs allies” question from the earlier [[two-path-system|Undying/Heroes structure]]. The current true ending (2023) is the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]]: after Locke ends [[lysander|Lysander]], the real final beat is the hidden wish-spirit, beaten by refusal rather than by a blade. Player choices like the Dragon’s Blood Choice feed reactive dialogue into that finale. Ending themes — fixing past regrets Endings “should revolve about fixing past regrets. Its yearning inside all people.” The regret-wishes listed (M00243, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26): “I wish for another chance” “I had this power all along” “I had God-like power” “I had assistance all along” “For the real challenge” These belong to the earlier structure but the underlying theme — death, regret, and yearning — carries into the current game (see 004 §Theme: Death and Regrets). The true ending — refusing the Daemon of Want CANON The real endgame beat is not Lysander’s defeat but the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]], the game’s hidden true superboss (M02653–M02667, FreshToast, 2023-02-21): It is “practically immortal so long as desire remains to feed it” (M02667, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). You cannot stab a wish. It is defeated by refusal — a people who stop wishing for a savior and choose to grow their own light. It does not die so much as thin and dwindle, no longer fed. (004 §The True Ending) The thesis-made-mechanic ties the whole game’s “growth comes from within” theme (see [[main-story-outline]]) to its final choice. Reactive / choice-driven endings Dragon’s Blood Choice CANON : on killing [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]], Locke may drink the dragon’s blood or refuse it. It has no mechanical bearing but the Daemon of Want’s dialogue changes based on this choice (M02663, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). See [[main-story-outline]] §Act II. Clara’s confrontation has two outcomes: consumed by vengeance (Princess-Bride-style threat, “Today you die.”) or, if the player guides her to justice, “something less menacing and more hopeful.” (004 §Clara’s Final Confrontation; see [[clara]]) Joke / loss endings EVOLVED From the earlier [[two-path-system|Undying/Heroes]] Heroes-route ideas (M00239, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26): If you don’t damage a single boss at all, you lose your woman/man to another man/woman (the true hero/heroine, maybe) “in a cruel (and hilarious) manner.” Constant failure hands the finale to an NPC who becomes the true hero and fights the end boss in your place. These are tied to the abandoned two-path structure and are preserved as history. Relationship options From the earlier structure: the two sides could be “enemies to heroes … or … unit for a common purpose. Invade each other, or be sun-bros” — “the invasion and enemies option seems more fun, but the unity option is interesting.” (M00242, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26) EVOLVED — belongs to the [[two-path-system]]. In the current JRPG, “relationship options” surface as party/romance and confrontation choices (e.g. Clara’s vengeance-vs-justice path above); the dating-sim-style multi-partner framing is a feature of the older vision. Open questions DEBATED Whether the Dragon’s Blood Choice also causes a mechanical change in the Daemon fight — left unresolved in both notes and proto. (M02663; 004 §Dragon’s Blood Choice) Design history 2021-06-26 — ending themes as fixing past regrets, plus the joke “lose your partner” loss-ending and NPC-becomes-hero failure ending, and the “enemies vs sun-bros” relationship question (M00239, M00242–M00243, CrunchyConniptions). Tied to the [[two-path-system|Undying/Heroes structure]], later set aside. 2023-02-21 — the current true ending established: the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]] as hidden final antagonist, “immortal so long as desire remains to feed it,” beaten by refusal; and the Dragon’s Blood Choice driving reactive Daemon dialogue (M02653–M02667, FreshToast). Supersedes the earlier ending framing as the game’s actual finale. Provenance Raw: M00239, M00242, M00243 (CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26); M02653–M02667 (FreshToast, 2023-02-21) Cleaned: 000 (ending options; Daemon of Want notes) Docs: 004 §Ending Options, §Relationship Options, §Heroes Route Ideas, §The True Ending, §Dragon’s Blood Choice Ledger: Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (story rows)"},{"title":"Lysander Arc-Pacing (Story Side)","group":"Story","url":"story/lysander-arc-pacing.html","text":"Lysander Arc-Pacing (Story Side) CANON How fast the audience is meant to see through [[lysander|Lysander]]: the seeds of doubt are immediate, his heroic reputation lasts only a little longer, and he should unravel entirely by the time the dragon is dealt with at the end of Act 2 — much faster than a slow-burn like RDR2’s Dutch. Summary — This page covers the pacing of Lysander’s villain reveal from the writing/story side (the combat-and-boss side lives in [[main-story-outline]] and on [[lysander]]'s own page). The team’s principle is that “the audience doesn’t want to be duped; they want to feel smart” (M04693, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-25). Applied to Lysander: seeds of doubt are immediate (M04701, FreshToast, 2023-10-25); his reputation lingers a bit longer but “likely devolve[s] entirely by the time the dragon is dealt with at the end of act 2” (M04702, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). The Dutch/RDR2 comparison is acknowledged as imperfect. The design principle “The audience doesn’t want to be duped. They want to feel smart.” (M04693, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-25 — “doped”/“duped” clarified in-chat, M04695–M04697) The Locke/Lysander contrast established from the very start “works great for that” — the writer wanted Lysander to “unravel faster” (M04698, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-25). The pacing target Seeds of doubt: immediate. (M04701, FreshToast, 2023-10-25) The prologue’s “questionable but heroic” Lysander already plants them (see [[main-story-outline]] §Prologue). Reputation: “will probably last a little longer but likely devolve entirely by the time the dragon is dealt with at the end of act 2.” (M04702, FreshToast, 2023-10-25) The unravelling is dramatised at the Act 2 dragon climax : Lysander refuses to face [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]] and lets towns burn while Locke rounds up help and grounds the dragon — the moment that defines her as Ajax’s proper heir and “shifts public opinion in favour of Locke and out of favour of Lysander” (M04706, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). See [[main-story-outline]] §Act II. Why not a slow-burn (the Dutch/RDR2 caveat) The Dutch-van-der-Linde comparison is judged imperfect (M04699, FreshToast, 2023-10-25): “If you’re gonna compare him to Dutch it’s not a particularly good comparison since they only share the one similarity.” “Dutch isn’t really an antagonist in RDR2 and the story is largely about Arthur and the gang’s slow decline” — a different structural job than Lysander’s. The reasoning ties back to the principle: “You’d get bored if the main bad guy was presented as the main bad guy but were also constantly being told ‘Oh no way, he’d NEVER be bad or wrong uwu.’” (M04700, FreshToast, 2023-10-25) — the audience wants to feel smart, not duped. Related design principle “I like when different potentiates for a role have opposing views on the role’s purpose and how to succeed in it — especially if it’s a more nuanced, open-ended question which the audience can consider and answer themselves.” (M04713–M04714, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). Lysander (might/glory) vs Locke (protect/change) is exactly this kind of opposed-view pairing. Design history 2023-10-25 — the whole arc-pacing exchange (M04693–M04714, CrunchyConniptions FreshToast): the “feel smart, not duped” principle, the immediate seeds of doubt, the “devolve entirely by end of Act 2” target, and the Dutch/RDR2 caveat. This is a single-session decision with no earlier superseded version. Provenance Raw: M04693–M04702, M04706, M04713–M04714 (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast, spirit_noodle; 2023-10-25) Cleaned: 000 (lines ~7095–7102, narrative design principles) Docs: 004 §Narrative Design Principles, §Dragon Confrontation Ledger: Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (design-principle rows)"},{"title":"Lysander's Villain Turn & Two-Phase Final Boss","group":"Story","url":"story/lysander-final-boss.html","text":"Lysander’s Villain Turn Two-Phase Final Boss CANON The Act III collapse of the golden hero: [[lysander]] stops caring where his power comes from, devours souls to feed his fading light, sheds his humanity into the dragon his bloodline always hid, and is finally put down by [[locke]] with Ajax’s leash in hand. Summary — Lysander grows too strong for [[eris]] to manage and has a third-act breakdown. His power comes from a pact with the same spirit Ajax dealt with — the [[daemon-of-want-ending|Daemon of Want]] — letting him devour souls for strength. The final fight is two phases: Phase 1 , the radiant himbo who telegraphs his glory and refills his fading light by leeching the crowd (counter: cut off the leech); Phase 2 , the curse made flesh, where the seven relics act as Ajax’s pre-prepared leash and Locke ends him — “Your light dims, Lysander. Let it fade away.” Defeating Lysander is not the true final beat; see [[daemon-of-want-ending|The True Ending]]. The breakdown CANON Lysander gets too strong for Eris to handle and has a 3rd-act breakdown. He doesn’t care where his power comes from — “Oh I have demon blood powers? Sweet!” — and doubles down, acquiring more by any means (M02211, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). He only now learned how to grow his power and must be finished while weak , “otherwise no hope for us all.” He wants to kill and sacrifice the party, then do the same to the rest of the people. This mirrors [[ruby|Ruby]]'s story — whether the ends justify the means (004 §Lysander’s 3rd-Act Villain Turn). The seeds of doubt in his heroism are immediate; his reputation lasts a little longer but likely devolves entirely by the time the dragon is dealt with at the end of Act 2 — the audience should be allowed to feel smart, not duped (M04701, M04702, FreshToast; “audience wants to feel smart, not duped” — M04693, CrunchyConniptions; the “unravel faster than Dutch” answer — M04692, FreshToast; 2023-10-25). See [[dragon-confrontation]] for the Act 2 turn. The Eris kill — lead-in to Phase 1 CANON Eris tells Lysander about the soul/sacrifice power. In the canonical staging (the one that reached the docs), as she begins to explain “something about soul and sacrifice,” Lysander interrupts her by grabbing her by the head ; she is immediately dust (M02282, M02283, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). He is left with one hand still gripping her dusted robe hanging off skeletal remains (M02285). This kicks off his breakdown. (See [[eris|Eris’s Arc]] for the twist framing — the obvious twist-villain twisted back onto Lysander.) Boss mechanics CANON Soul-devour gimmick: when Lysander kills a party member he regains HP, gains a buff, and gains AP — the core gimmick of the fight. He can grab someone by the head and turn them to dust; sickly bluish/indigo light pours from his mouth and eyes (M02282–M02292; 004 §Lysander’s 3rd-Act Villain Turn). Phase 2 crawler mechanic: one weakened compatriot is left crawling on the ground. If the player does not attack them, Lysander takes his first turn to devour them, gaining AP and a buff for 2 turns; if the player kills them first , Lysander gets no buff/AP but the player has spent a couple of turns (M02292, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). A grim reframing of the [[locke]]/party HP-sacrifice theme. Phase 2 turn (the leash): Locke is doomed, humanity is doomed — but Ajax prepared for this, leaving behind “a relic that had helped him contain his own power in the past.” This relic is with Locke (or a guardian character; or it was on Lysander the whole time, suppressing him unknowingly until it fell/broke). A silhouette of Ajax appears and inspires the party: “Weary warriors, a curse of sorrowed days past stands in front of you but with the relic of old at hand, the curse’s power can be defeated. Now, stand tall and fight.” Locke: “Your light dims, Lysander… {looks up fiercely} Let it fade away.” Lysander is infuriated and growls in a manner reminiscent of the dragon; the fight begins (004 §Lysander 2nd Phase Fight Scene). See [[chaos-emeralds-quest|the relics-as-leash]]. Final line: “Why settle for only twice as bright?” — a reference to the family/Ajax motto “Burn twice as bright, half as long” (M02231, M02303, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Two-phase structure (summary) CANON Phase 1 — the radiant himbo hero, fighting spectacularly and telegraphing his glory, refilling his fading light by leeching the crowd (counter: cut off the leech). Phase 2 — the curse made flesh: scales surface, the pact starved of strangers eats its host, and he becomes the dragon his bloodline always hid; the seven relics are Ajax’s leash; Locke ends him. True Ending — the [[daemon-of-want-ending|Daemon of Want]]. This maps to the [[combat-strategy-arc|combat-strategy arc]]: Act III’s lesson is “become as the dragon” (proto GameBible, “The Final Boss — Phase One/Two”). The late-game spear conflict (lead-up) PROPOSED A proposed 2nd/3rd-act conflict: Lysander finds out the [[sunderer|spear]] is Ajax’s weapon and gets “BIG” jealous, demanding Locke hand it over; she disobeys (SYMBOL). He sends someone to retrieve it, setting off the explicit antagonism between the two bands. DEBATED this could also be the fight where [[flynt]] dies — “Idk yet but I had something like that in mind.” The Sunderer is the explicit symbol of Locke’s status as Ajax’s heir, so Lysander wants it (004 §Lysander Discovers Spear is Ajax’s Weapon). Open questions DEBATED Whether the spear-retrieval fight is the same fight in which [[flynt]] dies (004 §Lysander Discovers Spear is Ajax’s Weapon). DEBATED The alternate Eris-kill staging below (sword vs. by-the-head) — never formally reconciled. Design history 2023-02-16 — the whole villain-turn is brainstormed in one evening: the “dud revelation” that emboldens him (“Oh I have demon blood powers? Sweet!”) (M02211); the soul-devour gimmick and Ajax “devouring his enemies” (M02257); the Eris by-the-head → dust kill (M02282–M02285); the phase-2 crawler mechanic (M02292); the “Burn twice as bright, half as long” motto (M02231, M02303) — all FreshToast, 2023-02-16. 2023-02-16 — alternate Eris-kill staging (considered, superseded). Instead of grabbing Eris by the head, a variant has Lysander kill her with his sword : “as we are about to defeat him, he looks at his sword and says ‘ It sings to me ’ and proceeds to plunge into Eris” (M02270, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). This is a distinct staging of the same beat. The by-the-head / turn-to-dust version is the one that reached the docs and is treated as canonical; the “It sings to me” sword staging did not make it into the 000 notes (ledger R12, status partial ). Both were floated the same day, minutes apart — genuinely contemporaneous — but the head-grab version won by being carried forward into the compiled notes. 2026-06-16 — proto reconciliation formalizes the two-phase structure (radiant himbo → curse made flesh) and the relics-as-leash climax in the GameBible (proto: gamebible/data/story.js ). Provenance Raw: M02211, M02231, M02257, M02282, M02283, M02285, M02292, M02303 (FreshToast, 2023-02-16); M02270 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16 — “It sings to me” alternate staging) ; M04690–M04702 (CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast arc-pacing thread, 2023-10-25; substantive answers M04692/M04699–M04702 FreshToast, “feel smart not duped” M04693 CrunchyConniptions) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 / Part 11 (Lysander breakdown; 2nd phase fight scene) Docs: 004 §Lysander’s 3rd-Act Villain Turn / Final-Boss Breakdown; §Lysander 2nd Phase Fight Scene; §Two-Phase Final Boss Structure; §Eris’s Arc Ledger: row R12 (M02270 “It sings to me” staging, marked partial) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Main Story Outline","group":"Story","url":"story/main-story-outline.html","text":"Main Story Outline CANON The current single-player JRPG spine of Soaring Cinders: a party of “loser nerds” chases [[ajax|Ajax]]'s seven relics, [[locke]] rises as the true heir while the celebrated hero [[lysander]] rots into the very monster his bloodline hid, and the real endgame antagonist is the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]] — a wish you cannot stab. Summary — This is the outline of the game as most recently conceived: a three-act JRPG (M02451, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19) that opens in medias res with Locke and Lysander already adventuring, delivers a playable prologue/tutorial (“The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb,” CANON — IMPLEMENTED IN PROTO ) in which Locke acquires the [[sunderer|Sunderer]], and follows the party’s hunt for [[ajax|Ajax]]'s seven relics / chaos emeralds. Across the acts, Locke grows into the proper heir while Lysander decays into a soul-devouring, dragon-blooded final boss; defeating him is not the last beat — the true ending is the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]]. This spine supersedes the earlier [[two-path-system|Undying/Heroes two-path structure]]. Core story (the JRPG concept) A group of “loser nerds” who seek better in life and strive together; theme of vanity . (M00871–M00874, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02) Initial goal: a special relic to improve themselves. They realise growth comes from within, abandon the relic , and instead pursue the “loser hero.” (M00878–M00880, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02) “Keep it light, keep it humorous, don’t take it too serious.” (M00881, 2023-02-02) — tone reference: Mob Psycho (004 §Tone Reference); character-arc reference: Denji from Chainsaw Man (M00875). Antagonist archetype: “carried by OP magical gift from birth, always takes easy way out, always cheats.” (M00876, FreshToast, 2023-02-02) — realised as [[lysander|Lysander]]. Protagonist identity SCRAPPED male-isekai fork Switching the main character to a male “isekai protagonist” was suggested during a party-order discussion and rejected (004 §Protagonist Identity). “Your weebness is showing again calling the main character an isekai protagonist.” (M01916, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Locke remains the protagonist. Kept only as a record so it isn’t re-proposed. Narrative structure The narrative is split into 3 named acts (names TBD); cities are split into sub-sections. (M02451, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19) Prologue tutorial — “The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb” CANON — IMPLEMENTED IN PROTO The opening begins in medias res with Locke and Lysander already adventuring (Lysander’s party minus [[eris]]), then rewinds to a proper intro (004 §Prologue Structure). Lysander’s line “Watch the shadows. They hide just out of sight.” opens the cave (M02442, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19). Key tutorial beats (004 §Tutorial Sequence, §Tutorial Scene - First Draft): Sword given. Lysander hands Locke his backup sword — “A hero always looks out for his companions” — symbolising the power he gives her. Locke falls. She spots a glimmer, the floor crumbles, and she is separated from the party. Sword breaks. The borrowed sword shatters on a stone golem — the power he gave her breaks — and Locke pulls a rusty spear (the [[sunderer|Sunderer]], first of Ajax’s seven relics) free. Weakness discovery. “Wait… their joints! There!” — Locke reads the golem’s weakness Lysander misread; the clue is implemented as “the joint, not the wall.” Boss fight against the tomb golem (proto: the Ossuary Sentinel , with an adaptive Adamant Shell defence), guarding what is not a dead end but the secret entrance to Ajax’s tomb . Treasure vs spear. Lysander eyes the boss’s treasure, barely glancing at Locke’s spear: “Interesting weapon. Scavenging suits you.” The contrast: he thinks the treasure is the real prize; Locke got the real prize (the spear + the party’s faith). This entire prologue is built and playable in the proto (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts , src/core/combat/content.ts ), including Locke’s spear finisher Emberlance — “Ajax’s fire runs down the spear” (004 §Tutorial Outcome). Symbolism: sword = power Lysander gave her (breaks); spear = power she sought and developed herself, and her independence from him — “only for him to want that same power for himself” (004 §Symbolism Identified). Main quest — the seven relics / chaos emeralds The party needs a compelling, plot-central reason to collect the chaos emeralds; a threat (demons, a dragon) drives it. Lysander and Eris discuss gathering them to defeat the dragon; Locke overhears and decides to gather them herself, first to prove worthy, then for herself, finally to keep them away from Lysander — her motives shift from hero-pleasing to people-saving. (004 §Chaos Emeralds Quest) The relics are keepsakes tied to [[ajax|Ajax]] and the spirit he made a pact with. Brought together they grant not superpowers but a revelation about Ajax and his bloodline. They were meant to be “somewhat of a dud in the end” — a deliberate anti-climax. (raw M01425, FreshToast, 2023-02-11; 004 §Relic Details) The relics as a “leash” CANON : gathering all seven makes one “inherit Ajax’s legacy” — which people misread as acquiring his power , when the act of acquiring is what makes one powerful. Ajax’s legacy is the final task: to challenge his descendant and prevent them from consuming thousands in pursuit of power (000 notes, Ajax’s Legacy System). The seven relics are the leash used to bind Lysander’s hunger in the phase-2 finale. (004 §7 Relics System) See [[chaos-emeralds]] (items) and [[ajax|Ajax]] for the relic system and Ajax’s Daemon-of-Want pact. Act II climax — the dragon confrontation Lysander refuses to face the dragon, ever more entranced by Ajax’s legacy, and lets towns burn. Locke upstages him: she rounds up help, works with various people to ground the dragon, and enters the den to face [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]]. (004 §Dragon Confrontation) The dragon exposes each man’s motivation — Ajax wants glory, Lysander wants strength, Locke wants to protect and make change — and marks Lysander as the failure who did not step up. The moment defines Locke as the proper heir, shifts public opinion toward her, and is the climax of her personal arc. (004 §Character Motivations Revealed) Dragon’s Blood Choice CANON : on killing Vaenngrym, Locke may drink the dragon’s blood (a beat of levity) — either she drinks it, everyone is grossed out, and nothing happens, or she finds solace in her own strength and refuses. No mechanical bearing, but it informs her view of Lysander’s power-chase and changes the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]]'s later dialogue. Lore tie-in: Ajax’s cover story was that he gained strength from drinking a wounded dragon’s blood. (004 §Dragon’s Blood Choice) Act III — Lysander’s villain turn the two-phase final boss CANON Lysander grows too strong for [[eris|Eris]] to handle and has a 3rd-act breakdown : he doesn’t care where his power comes from (“Oh I have demon-blood powers? Sweet!”) and doubles down, wanting to kill and sacrifice the party, then do the same to everyone else. (004 §Lysander’s 3rd-Act Villain Turn) Eris twist: Eris tells him about the soul/sacrifice power; he interrupts by grabbing her head and she is instantly dust, kicking off the breakdown. (see [[eris|Eris]]'s arc) Phase 1 — the radiant himbo hero: fights spectacularly, telegraphs his glory, refills his fading light by leeching the crowd; counter is to cut off the leech. His soul-devour gimmick: killing a party member restores his HP, buffs him, and grants AP. Phase 2 — the curse made flesh: scales surface and the pact, starved of strangers, eats its host — he becomes the dragon his bloodline hid. Mechanic: a weakened compatriot is left crawling; if the player does not kill them, Lysander spends his first turn devouring them (AP + a 2-turn buff); if the player kills them first, he gets nothing but the player loses turns. The seven relics act as Ajax’s pre-prepared leash . Locke ends him: “Your light dims, Lysander… Let it fade away.” (M02442-adjacent; 004 "},{"title":"Moonstone Mania (Meta Collectathon Venture)","group":"Story","url":"story/moonstone-mania.html","text":"Moonstone Mania (Meta Collectathon Venture) CANON The self-aware Skyrim-gems parody: hoard an absurd pile of moonstones just to see an NPC’s baffled reaction — and accidentally trigger a world-altering religion of everyday gratitude. Summary — A meta side-[[venture-terminology|venture]] riffing on Skyrim’s rare-gems quest. The player collects a ludicrous number of moonstones; the only real motive is a single line of dialogue. When Archmage Xarthus demands to know why, the player admits it was “just to see your surprised face” — and Xarthus, misreading it as profound, founds the Cult of Mundane Moments , spreads it across the nation, and the game visibly turns saccharine. The point is that it isn’t worth it , played self-aware and funny (004 §Design Intent). Concept CANON A deliberate reference to Skyrim’s rare-gems quest — collect an absurd amount of crystals, with the game making clear there’s a reward (004 §Meta Collectathon Quest). The player then meets an NPC confounded as to why they did this; the player says it was for this dialogue . The NPC is puzzled that all the player wanted was simple dialogue, finds it hard to accept, and misconstrues the simple desire as something deeper — suggesting we should cherish everyday interactions (004 §Meta Collectathon Quest). The NPC has a eureka moment, becomes a monk, starts a religion, spreads it across the nation , and the game’s appearance changes — people are happier (004 §Meta Collectathon Quest). Design intent (004 §Design Intent): “The point is it isn’t worth it” — “and be self aware and funny about it,” “rather than bad quest by design.” (In actual Skyrim the reward is a permanent buff to find more valuables in chests — noted in the ledger, M05266/M05271.) “This is deranged but part of me does want to throw in an absurd collectathon for the sake of tradition.” Full quest example (written) CANON Set in a bustling marketplace in [[caelia]] (004 §Full Quest Example Written): The player approaches a weary merchant named Elara : “Remember [that] crazy gem-hoarding quest? Could pull off again with Moonstones!” Elara: “Moonstones?! Are you mad? Rarer than a dragon with manners!” The player insists the reward’s worth it; Elara relents, warning of “angry werewolves.” [Quest Begins: Moonstone Mania] — months pass; the player becomes a moonstone magnet, amassing a ludicrous pile. News reaches the reclusive Archmage Xarthus . Xarthus encounter: “Why? Why all the moonstones? What grand magic?” Player: “No magic, just wanted to see your surprised face.” Xarthus is dumbfounded, then revelation dawns: “Of course! True magic lies in the mundane! We take for granted simple interactions!” Cult of Mundane Moments: Xarthus founds it; his charisma wins over the masses; Caelia changes — people greet each other with genuine smiles, birds chirp louder, even bandits offer discounts on stolen goods. Player: “I just wanted the Archmage’s reaction, man! This cult thing is getting weird…” Xarthus: “Nay! You are the Moonstone Messiah !” Player: “Oh gods, what have I done?” Ending: the screen zooms out, Caelia bathed in an overly-saccharine glow, birds chirping excessively, a bandit offering a flower. Player (V.O.): “I just wanted some dialogue. But now the fate of Caelia rests on my moonstones and questionable motives. Can’t a hero get some peace and quiet around here?” The End (or is it?) Related Uses the [[venture-terminology|“venture”]] framing agreed the same day (2024-02-13). Elara (weary Caelia merchant) and Archmage Xarthus (the Moonstone Messiah) are the two named NPCs — see characters batch for any dedicated pages. Echoes the “[[narrative-themes|Mystery Creates Village Interaction]]” idea: a mystery draws the player to interact with NPCs (004 §Mystery Creates Village Interaction). Design history 2024-02-13 — the whole quest is conceived and written out in one session: the Skyrim reference, the “player just wanted the dialogue” twist, the Cult of Mundane Moments, and the full Elara → Xarthus → Moonstone Messiah script (M05265–M05272, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). No later revision recorded. Provenance Raw: M05265, M05266, M05267, M05268, M05269, M05270, M05271, M05272 (CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13) Cleaned: 000 §Meta Collectathon Quest / Cult of Mundane Moments Docs: 004 §Meta Collectathon Quest (Moonstone Mania); §Full Quest Example Written; 003 §marketplace in Caelia (Elara); 009 Ledger: row R28 (M05265… collectathon; M05266/M05271/M05272 named NPCs Skyrim reward) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Narrative Themes & Storytelling Philosophy","group":"Story","url":"story/narrative-themes.html","text":"Narrative Themes Storytelling Philosophy CANON The guiding themes and taste rules: death and the regrets of the dead; a specific flavor of underdog story (small fish in a big pond, not “everybody is mean to you”); and easter eggs everywhere, Undertale-style. Summary — This page collects the thematic and philosophical throughlines the team set for the game’s writing: the central Death and Regrets theme (carrying the influence of the dead into the story), the largely-undeveloped Love and Heroism themes, the strongly-held underdog storytelling philosophy (a taste debate about meanness, with Ranking of Kings / Game of Thrones as models and Witcher 3 as the anti-model), and the intent to pack in easter eggs everywhere as exploration incentives. Theme: Death and Regrets CANON The team’s signature theme. “I really want to deeply explore the regrets of the dead as one of our themes is death” (M04644, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-24). What makes death sad is unattained dreams (004 §Core Concept). Implementation avenues (004 §Implementation Avenues): [[flynt]]/[[locke]] and [[thane]] are the primary avenues; Thane carries a lot of his comrades’ wants and dreams — “a bit of them lives with him.” [[ruby|Ruby]] once she kills Citrine; Astrid possibly regretful about Raphael’s fate; [[steele]] picks up Warpath after [[flynt]] dies. “Carrying the influence of the dead visibly into the latter parts of the story is generally just good storytelling.” Done well (004 §Examples of Done Well): Spider-Verse; Spider-Man’s “great power, great responsibility” from Uncle Ben (“I like it when there’s some physical object involved though” — M04680, FreshToast, 2023-10-24); Game of Thrones (big ramifications, Ned Stark is peak — M04682, 2023-10-24). Additional concepts (004 §Additional Concepts): a minor villain who kills for vengeance of the dead; the dead who died terribly and wish to curse their killers — “ Dying curses are a fun trope ” (M04669, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). (Cross-ref: [[clara-final-confrontation|Clara’s vengeance]] branch.) Theme: Love Theme: Heroism CANON Love: “I don’t know what to do with the theme of love so I will leave that to you” — deliberately handed off, undeveloped (004 §Theme: Love). Heroism: “But I think I can have some ideas for death and heroism” — flagged as a theme, developed mainly through [[lysander]]'s hollow heroism vs. [[locke]]'s earned kind (004 §Theme: Heroism). Underdog Storytelling Philosophy CANON A taste debate (2023-11-16) about how to establish an underdog. FreshToast: “don’t really enjoy the ‘everybody is mean to you’ way of establishing an underdog” — prefers making the character feel like a small fish in a big pond , with “stuff beyond their scope and strength, people to aspire to” (M05060, FreshToast, 2023-11-16). Rudeness’s purpose is to make you dislike a character, and audiences wish unlikeable characters would die (004 §Debate on Meanness). Models (004 §Examples): Ranking of Kings — CrunchyConniptions’s preferred underdog story; “does both” (mean and not) (M05065, M05072, 2023-11-16). Bocchi the Rock — “I would not want people to be mean to Bocchi” (the gentle-underdog model). Game of Thrones (good example) — measured; “cruelty is reserved for appropriate characters,” reacted to properly, used to characterize. Witcher 3 (bad example) — “everybody is awful for no clear reason”; “the Witcher is a bit much… Then why does everyone like it?” (M05075, M05078, 2023-11-16). Underdog formula (004 §Underdog Formula): small fish in big pond; stuff beyond scope and strength; people to aspire to; not everybody being mean; personal and reasonable conflict when it exists. Design voice principles CANON “Audience doesn’t want to be duped. They want to feel smart” (M04693, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-25). Seeds of doubt (about [[lysander]]) will be immediate; he should unravel to the audience faster than Dutch from RDR2 , likely devolving entirely by the time the dragon is dealt with at end of Act 2 (M04692, M04701, M04702, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). See [[lysander-final-boss]]. Character-voice constraints for [[lysander]] and [[locke]] (Disco Elysium / Game of Thrones frame, not One Piece; Lysander is not a buffoon; Locke isn’t “sir”/“hero”-speak) — 004 §Character Voice Constraints. Easter Eggs Meta Content CANON “I mentioned I want easter eggs everywhere, like Undertale. How do we feel about that?” — “Easter eggs are fun. Exploration incentives” (M04848–M04850, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-30). Shared-abilities easter egg: “Imagine [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]] gets some powerful named attack in [the] first game” and [[locke]] earns the same/similar ability as a skill in the second game — “since both wield flame” (004 §Shared Abilities). (Scope note: the wider Cyrus/Luminarium story belongs to the separate Caelian Odyssey iteration; only this cross-game link is in scope here.) Mystery creates village interaction: creating mystery intrigues the player to interact with NPCs to find out what’s happening (004 §Mystery Creates Village Interaction) — the design engine behind [[moonstone-mania]]. Design history 2023-10-24 — the Death Regrets theme is deepened (regrets of the dead; dying curses; Spider-Verse/GoT models; carrying the dead’s influence forward) (M04644, M04669, M04680, M04682, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-24). 2023-10-30 — “easter eggs everywhere, like Undertale” agreed as fun exploration incentives (M04848–M04850, 2023-10-30). 2023-11-16 — the underdog/meanness taste debate settles the formula: small-fish-in-big-pond over “everybody is mean,” with RoK/Bocchi/GoT as models and Witcher 3 as anti-model (M05060–M05078, 2023-11-16). Provenance Raw: M04644, M04669, M04680, M04682 (2023-10-24); M04848, M04849, M04850 (2023-10-30); M05060, M05065, M05072, M05075, M05078 (2023-11-16); M04690–M04702 (CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast arc-pacing thread, 2023-10-25) Cleaned: 000 §Narrative Themes and Philosophy; §Underdog Storytelling; §Easter Eggs Docs: 004 §Narrative Themes and Philosophy; §Underdog Storytelling Philosophy; §Easter Eggs and Meta Content; §Design Philosophy Ledger: narrative-themes / underdog / easter-egg rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Prologue & Tutorial — The Descent into Ajax's Tomb","group":"Story","url":"story/prologue-tutorial.html","text":"Prologue Tutorial — The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb CANON The opening dungeon run: [[locke]] descends into a dark cave with [[lysander]] and [[eris]], loses the sword he lends her, and pulls Ajax’s spear from the rubble on her own — the game’s thesis compressed into its first hour. Built and playable in the proto. Summary — The prologue begins in medias res , Locke and Lysander already adventuring, then rewinds to a proper intro (004 §Prologue Structure). In a dark cave lit by Locke’s [[lantern]], Lysander lends Locke his backup sword (“A hero always looks out for his companions”), the party clears monsters, and Locke falls to a lower level where the borrowed sword shatters on a stone golem. She frees the rusty [[sunderer|Sunderer]] (Ajax’s spear) herself, reads the golem’s weakness Lysander missed, and the run ends not at a dead end but at the secret threshold to Ajax’s tomb . The whole sequence is implemented and playable in the proto as the vertical-slice dungeon “The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb” (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts ). Structure beats Opening (004 §Prologue Structure): Begin in medias res , Locke and Lysander already adventuring together; at the end, rewind to the proper intro (M05004, FreshToast, 2023-11-16 notes the in-medias-res, start-you-off-screwed intent). Start with Lysander’s party minus [[eris]] , plus Locke, in a dark cave, the [[lantern]] carried by Locke (M02442, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19). Lysander: “Watch the shadows. They hide just out of sight.” A monster pounces; he kills it in one hit (M02442, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19). Lysander is in the prologue dungeon looking for a [[chaos-emeralds|chaos emerald]]; it isn’t found — he is dumb and misread the clue , while Locke is smart and reads it well (M02445, M02449, 2023-02-19). Tutorial sequence (004 §Sequence Overview): Lysander center-frame, flanked by Locke Eris; Locke armed with a sword. Show off Lysander’s power by protecting Locke from a creature and killing it without mercy. Enter, tease the reason for entering, tutorial battle begins. Locke laments her lack of reach and strength. Her sword is lost or broken at the battle’s conclusion. Combat is broken up by a puzzle emphasizing Locke’s competence ; an ambush separates Locke from the others; she stumbles into and acquires the Sunderer , arms herself, and reunites for another battle. Post-battle she finds the way forward and is commended by Eris. Stealth light mechanics introduced; final climactic battle tests the player’s basic knowledge (004 §Sequence Overview). Outcome (004 §Tutorial Outcome): The Sunderer (first of Ajax’s seven relics) is acquired in a trial passed by Locke. What they reach is not a dead end but a secret entrance to Ajax’s tomb; Lysander and Eris assume the intel about a relic here was false. Key moments (first-draft screenplay) CANON Sword Given — Lysander gives Locke his backup sword: “A hero always looks out for his companions.” Symbolic: the power he gives her (004 §1 Sword Given). Lysander’s Heroics — effortlessly dispatches monsters: “Ha! Is that all you’ve got?” / “Evil falls before the righteous!” Party hangs back (004 §2). Locke Falls — notices a faint GLIMMER in the rubble; the floor CRUMBLES; she falls into darkness (004 §3). Sword Breaks — the sword SHATTERS against a golem’s stone body (“Symbolic: power he gave her breaks”). She spots a rusty spear with a GLIMMER and pulls it free (the [[sunderer|Sunderer]]) (004 §4). Weakness Discovery — “Wait… their joints! There!” She strikes the golem’s knee joint and defeats both golems with precise attacks (004 §5). Boss Fight — Locke fights off small monsters and saves the healers; Lysander charges the boss (“Cover me!”); Locke clears enemies first, then follows; Lysander delivers the final blow (004 §6). Treasure vs Spear — Lysander goes for the boss’s treasure, barely glancing at Locke’s spear: “Interesting weapon. Scavenging suits you.” Contrast: he thinks the treasure is the real prize, but Locke got the real prize — the spear and the faith of the party (004 §7). Symbolism (004 §Symbolism Identified): Sword = power Lysander gave her (breaks); Spear = power she sought and developed. “Spear also represents her independence from Lysander” — “Only for him to want that same power for himself.” Foreshadowing: first command she follows forward; second command (follow to boss) she disobeys — “FORESHADOWING!” (004 §Foreshadowing). Character notes: Lysander “kind of sounds like All Might”; the writers wanted his words overly heroic, his actions heroic but questionable. The spear was meant to read as a footnote that turns out to be the best find (004 §Character Notes; §Lysander Noticing Sunderer). Proto implementation CANON — IMPLEMENTED Built and playable as “The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb” (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts ). The graph realizes the beats almost exactly: Start node “The Spare Sword” — Lysander’s “A hero always looks out for his companions” pressing his backup blade into Locke’s hands before the stair drops her into the dark. Treasure node “Glimmer in the Rubble” — the borrowed sword shatters on a stone-cored foe; Locke pulls the rusted spear free, granted as { kind: 'relic', label: 'The Sunderer' } , “Ajax’s spear — the first relic.” The misread clue is implemented as the carved line “the joint, not the wall” — Locke reads the golem’s weakness while Lysander skims past. Boss: the Ossuary Sentinel , a tomb golem with an adaptive Adamant Shell defense (proto: src/core/combat/content.ts ), guarding the threshold to Ajax’s tomb. Outro confirms the “sealed door [bearing] a sun worn almost smooth … a single sunset-colored gem set where an eye would be … never a dead end … a threshold.” Locke’s spear finisher Emberlance — “Ajax’s fire runs down the spear” — is implemented (proto: src/core/combat/content.ts ). This is the Act I “be overwhelming” combat lesson made playable (the golem’s joints, not the wall); see [[combat-strategy-arc]]. Design history 2023-02-19 — the prologue is drafted in one sitting: the cave/lantern/“Watch the shadows” opening, the in medias res framing, Lysander searching for a chaos emerald and misreading the clue, and the secret-endgame-section reveal of the dungeon (M02442–M02450, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-19). 2023-11-16 — FreshToast reaffirms the underdog, in-medias-res, “start you off screwed” opening as intended tone (M05004, 2023-11-16). 2026-06-16 — implemented in the proto as a playable dungeon run, matching the beats and formalizing the Sunderer grant, the Ossuary Sentinel boss, and the “joint, not the wall” clue (proto reconciliation). Provenance Raw: M02442, M02443, M02445, M02446, M02448, M02449, M02450 (CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19); M05004 (FreshToast, 2023-11-16, in-medias-res intent) Cleaned: 000 Part 9 (prologue, in medias res, lantern; Lysander misreads clue) Docs: 004 §Prologue and Tutorial; §Tutorial Scene - First Draft; §Key Moments; §Symbolism Identified Ledger: row R13 (M02442… prologue script) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Round Boi Decision (The Unrefusable Choice)","group":"Story","url":"story/round-boi-decision.html","text":"The Round Boi Decision (The Unrefusable Choice) CANON The one decision the player can’t refuse to make: befriend the giant beetle. A deliberately “meaningless” choice — the beetle has to be part of the story — played for humor and to contrast with choices that actually matter. Summary — In a game full of meaningful decisions, [[giant-beetle|Round Boi]] — the giant beetle — is the one the player cannot refuse . Meeting the beetle is framed as a narrative decision (“Would you like to be friends with this cool giant beetle that likes you or do you suck?”), but the beetle must be part of the story, so the “choice” is meaningless by design. The team agreed there’s humor in that, and that it helpfully contrasts with the choices that do matter (004 §Round Boi Decision). The bestiary owns [[giant-beetle|Round Boi]] as a creature; this page covers the story beat of the decision. The decision CANON The player meets the beetle, called “round boi” or “big round boi” or “round big” (M03615, M03617, M03618, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-26). The prompt: “Would you like to be friends with this cool giant beetle that likes you or do you suck?” (M03623, FreshToast, 2023-08-26). CrunchyConniptions: “All I am saying is if you want to be friends with me apparently it’s easy, just give me a round boi and I am sold” (M03624, 2023-08-26). “In our game full of meaningful decisions, there’s one decision they can’t refuse to make” — the choice is meaningless because the beetle has to be part of the story . “I think there’s humour in that we could probably pull off”; “It also helps to contrast with choices that do matter somewhat.” Good idea agreed upon (004 §Round Boi Decision, tagged CANON ). Vertical slice context The Round Boi befriending sits inside the First City “beetle infestation” vertical slice (design intent, not yet coded): the core party arrives at the first city, deals with a beetle infestation, meets the big beetle, Locke befriends it, and it leads them to a dungeon they clear so the beetles can return home — “Good times. End of final beetle” (M02459, FreshToast, 2023-02-19; 004 §Vertical Slice Content). The proto’s implemented vertical slice is instead the [[prologue-tutorial|prologue “Descent into Ajax’s Tomb”]] — no contradiction, just a different slice; the GameBible places Round Boi at the First City as a Side [[venture-terminology|Venture]] (proto: gamebible/data/quests.js ). Related [[giant-beetle|Round Boi]] — the creature itself (bestiary). Large because of Dracosis , with a hard, semi-metallic shell (M02709, FreshToast, 2023-02-24) — see bestiary. Contrasts with the [[moonstone-mania|meta collectathon]] humor and the game’s “meaningful decisions” ethos. Design history 2023-02-10 — the earliest beetle beat: “pet the giant beetle” — “Definitely pet the beetle” (M01374, spirit_noodle; M01375, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). 2023-02-19 — the beetle folded into the First City vertical slice (infestation → befriend → dungeon → beetles go home) (M02459, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). 2023-08-26 — the “Round Boi” name (and variants) and the deliberately-unrefusable meaningless-choice framing are conceived and agreed upon (M03614–M03624, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-08-26). Provenance Raw: M01374, M01375 (2023-02-10); M02459 (FreshToast, 2023-02-19); M03614–M03624 (CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-08-26) Cleaned: 000 Part 15 §Round Boi Decision Docs: 004 §Round Boi Decision; §Vertical Slice Content; 005 Bestiary §Giant Beetle (Round Boi) Ledger: row R20 (M03614… Round Boi meaningless-choice) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Two-Path System (Undying vs Heroes)","group":"Story","url":"story/two-path-system.html","text":"Two-Path System (Undying vs Heroes) EVOLVED An early two-role structure — you play either an Undying (souls-like, revives forever, weak, borrows power) or a True Hero/Heroine (one life, far stronger, no do-overs) — later set aside for the single-player JRPG that Soaring Cinders became. Summary — This is the game’s earliest conceived narrative structure, brainstormed in June 2021 (M00239–M00244, CrunchyConniptions). The player would choose to be one of two kinds: an Undying , who always comes back after defeat and grinds each fight to victory, invading and syphoning power from heroes; or a True Hero/Heroine , who dies for good, carries their failures, and has far better stats. The two sides were “always fight[ing] each other,” and the structure carried an online/co-op (“invade or be sun-bros”) and gacha framing. The whole Undying/Heroes vision — and the gacha “Storyline Expansion” attached to it — is preserved here as an early concept, not the current design (004 §Main Narrative Structures); Soaring Cinders is now a single-player JRPG with [[locke]] as protagonist. Description The Undying Path You always come back to life after defeat (souls-like). (M00239, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26) Keep repeating each fight until you reach victory. Weaker than heroes, but if determined enough, the Undying eventually wear the heroes out. (M00239) Must invade heroes and borrow their power — syphoning strength from them. Example: an Undying facing a boss weak to fire can invade a hero with fire-magic affinity and borrow that power “till they die and lose it (similar to embers in Dark Souls 3).” (M00239) Lore framing: “Undying are told to end the age of light to save their own kind.” (M00242, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26) PROPOSED The Undying as “an ancient eternal species that was kept down by the heroes’ species … shriveled jerky due to the eons of suffering.” (M00243, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26) The True Hero/Heroine Path You do not come back to life; if you fail, you carry that failure with you. (M00239) Far better stats than the Undying. The storyline incorporates the person’s failures and victories — failure and victory decide the storyline. (M00239) “Doesn’t mean the game or story is worse just cuz you lose, but there is great incentive to achieve victory.” (M00239) Lore framing: “Heroes are told to prolong the age of light to continue the prosperity of their own kind.” (M00242) Heroes Route ideas Constant failure eventually leads to another NPC becoming the hero , who gets stronger and more hero-like the more you fail. Eventually the end boss is fought not by you, but by the NPC now regarded as the true hero/heroine. (M00239) Joke ending: if you do not damage a single boss at all, you lose your woman/man to another man/woman (the true hero/heroine, maybe) “in a cruel (and hilarious) manner.” (M00239) Major Boss scenario (the 75% choice) A first encounter with a Major Boss (maybe the end boss) at 75% power — the player can finish him here (M00239, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26): Option 1: finish him now → unlock the true ending where you fight the “TRUE END BOSS.” Option 2: let him escape → he devours the “TRUE END BOSS” himself to become the true end boss. Intent: “Hopefully this will lead to intense situation when one would decide to continue the pursuit of victory and delay this fight for later when one is ready.” (M00239) Relationship between the two sides The two sides could be enemies or allies (M00242, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26): “Undying be enemies to heroes … or … undying and heroes unit for a common purpose. Invade each other, or be sun-bros.” “The invasion and enemies option seems more fun, but the unity option is interesting.” Framed as four final outcomes that collapse into two: “let there be light or no light.” (M00242) Gacha / F2P layer (abandoned) Attached to this structure was a gacha model, tagged EVOLVED - gt; early gacha concept in the doc (004 §Storyline Expansion): “The heroes will follow the gacha model. New heroes will be added with their own storylines.” (M00243) The Undying take “the F2P approach”: to experience a true hero’s power, they must invade and borrow it. (M00243) Using the most powerful/popular hero makes you more likely to be invaded and have your power stolen — disincentivising everyone from just using the best heroes. (M00243) The Undying counterparts of heroes are bad at borrowing other heroes’ powers, while the original Undying is very good at it. (M00244, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26) Open questions DEBATED Whether the two sides are enemies or allies (“invade each other, or be sun-bros”) was left genuinely open at the time — “the invasion and enemies option seems more fun, but the unity option is interesting” (M00242). Moot now that the structure is superseded. PROPOSED Completing the story as a hero would “unlock the undying version of that hero which is … complicated and unclear” — the writer flagged it as unresolved. (M00243) Gameplay lineage — what the system seeded Although the whole structure was set aside, two of its mechanics threads carried forward into the shipped single-player design (008 §10): Invasion → anti-meta. The “invade and borrow a hero’s power” loop — and the rule that using the most powerful/popular hero makes you more likely to be invaded and robbed — is the seed of the later [[anti-meta-systems|invasion-based anti-meta]] (M00243, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26). Borrowing → Locke’s leadership. The Undying’s defining trait, borrowing others’ powers (with the note that the original Undying is uniquely good at it while hero-derived Undying are poor at it), later informs [[locke|Locke]]‘s leader who borrows his allies’ skills design; see the [[reserve-skills-system|Reserve Skills / Borrow]] system (M00244, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26; 008 §5). Hero unlocking. Completing the story as a hero would unlock that hero’s Undying version — “abandoned heroes” who retain aspects of their power — versus normal Undying who “were not originally heroes but just normal people” (M00243). Flagged unresolved at the time (see Open questions). Design history 2021-06-26 — original concept (M00239, CrunchyConniptions): the full Undying-vs-True-Hero two-path structure — revive-and-grind Undying who invade/syphon hero power vs one-life, high-stat heroes; the Heroes-route “another NPC becomes the hero” idea; the joke “lose your partner” ending; and the 75%-power Major Boss choice. 2021-06-26 — expanded same day (M00242, CrunchyConniptions): the “age of light” faction framing (Undying end it / Heroes prolong it), the four-outcomes-into-two structure, and the “enemies vs sun-bros” relationship question. 2021-06-26 — expanded same day (M00243–M00244, CrunchyConniptions): ending themes tied to regret (see [[endings]]), the “shriveled jerky” Undying-species idea, and the gacha/F2P monetisation layer with invasion-driven power theft. Later (undated set-aside) — the entire Undying/Heroes two-path + invasion/co-op + gacha framing was set aside for the single-player JRPG direction (see [[main-story-outline]]); the doc marks it EVOLVED - gt; early concept and preserves it as history, not current design (004 §Main Narrative Structures). Supersedes all of the above. Provenance Raw: M00239, M00242–M00244 (CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26) Cleaned: 000 (early narrative-structure notes) Docs: 004 §Main Narrative Structures, §Undying Path, §True Hero/Heroine Path, §Heroes Route Ideas, §Major Boss Scenario Idea, §Relationship Options, §Storyline Expansion Ledger: Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (mechanics row, M00244, R02)"},{"title":"\"Ventures\" — Quest Terminology Decision","group":"Story","url":"story/venture-terminology.html","text":"“Ventures” — Quest Terminology Decision CANON What the game calls its quests: after a terminology debate, spirit_noodle decreed “Ventures.” Summary — The team debated what to call quests in the game — “Quest,” “Venture,” “Errand,” “Trial,” and others were on the table. “Trial” was ruled out because Ajaxian trials already exist as a distinct thing. After narrowing to Quest/Venture/Errand, the decision was handed to spirit_noodle , who decreed “Ventures.” So quests in Soaring Cinders are Ventures (004 §Spirit_noodle’s Decision, CANON ). The debate CANON Options considered (004 §Options Considered): Quest (classic, familiar, homey), Venture (exciting, adventurous), Trial (already used for Ajaxian trials), Expedition, Challenge, Errand (mundane but sounds nice), Favor, Legend, Task (“most boring but applicable” — rejected: “no”), Mission (“anything but this”). Reasoning (M05273–M05286, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13): “Quest is classic but it might be overused or even inappropriate, considering there’s only one literal ‘quest.’” “Maybe something more loose and adventure-y like ‘Venture.’” “We’ve already got Ajaxian trials in game… so probably something more generic, even though I like ‘trial’ too.” Narrowed (M05295, M05298, FreshToast, 2024-02-13): “Yeah I prefer Venture, Errand Quest so far.” “I think Errand is worse than the other two.” “I’m content with Quest or Venture then.” The decision CANON The choice was handed to spirit_noodle (Noods): “We will let noods pick… And if we don’t like his choice, then we kick him.” “NOODS GIVE YOUR OPINION — DECIDE THE FATE OF THE GAME — ARE THEY VENTURES OR QUESTS? — DECIDE OR DIE” (004 §Final Decision Process). spirit_noodle’s ruling (M05283/M05316–M05321, spirit_noodle, 2024-02-13): “Ventures.” — “I have decided.” — “I AM THE DECIDER.” — “It is written.” Outcome: VENTURES selected. “VERY WELL. IF WE ARE CONTENT WITH THIS DECISION, SO IT SHALL BE.” “I guess we are hipsters then.” “INTO THE WAVE OF CREATIVE REIMAGINATION WE ARE SWEPT” (004 §Spirit_noodle’s Decision). Related Ajaxian trials — the reason “Trial” was reserved and not used for generic quests (see [[chaos-emeralds-quest|the 7 relics’ informal trials]]). Applied downstream: e.g. [[moonstone-mania]] and [[round-boi-decision|Round Boi]] are Ventures. Design history 2024-02-13 — the entire terminology debate and decision happen in one session: FreshToast raises the naming question, options are listed and narrowed to Quest/Venture/Errand, and spirit_noodle decrees “Ventures” (M05273–M05322, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions/spirit_noodle, 2024-02-13). No later revision recorded. Provenance Raw: M05273, M05277, M05278, M05283, M05284, M05286, M05290, M05295, M05297, M05298 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions); M05308, M05316, M05318, M05321 (2024-02-13) Cleaned: 000 §Quest Terminology / Spirit_noodle’s Decision Docs: 004 §Quest Terminology; §Spirit_noodle’s Decision Ledger: row R28 (M05273… “Ventures” official term) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Blacksmith Boss","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/blacksmith-boss.html","text":"Blacksmith Boss DEBATED An adaptive boss who “changes their armor based on your attack” — one of the game’s gaming-deconstruction set pieces, whose adaptive-hardening mechanic was ultimately prototyped in the [[ossuary-sentinel|Ossuary Sentinel]] instead. Summary — The Blacksmith Boss is a proposed adaptive enemy: “We fight blacksmith who changes their armor based on your attack” (M01162, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08). It was pitched as one of three “gaming deconstruction” set pieces (fight the hero, fight the blacksmith, don’t level up) and demands tactical thinking rather than raw damage. The concept was floated as “a magical blacksmith lady” and later mused to possibly just be [[pandora|Pandora]] (M04397–M04398, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). The Blacksmith itself is not implemented; its adaptive-armor idea is realized in the vertical-slice proto by the [[ossuary-sentinel|Ossuary Sentinel]]'s Adamant Shell skill (005 §Blacksmith Boss proto note). Enemy Concept An adaptive enemy that changes its armor based on your attack , forcing the player to keep switching approaches (M01162, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08; 005 §Enemy Concept). Part of the gaming-deconstruction pillars: “We fight the hero” ([[lysander|Lysander]]), “We fight blacksmith who changes their armor based on your attack”, “We don’t level up” (M01160–M01163, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08; 005 §Blacksmith Boss / 008). An “adaptive enemy requiring tactical thinking” — in keeping with the game’s “tactical complexity over HP bloat” philosophy (005 §Enemy Concept). Proto realization The Blacksmith is not yet implemented. The “adaptive armor / hardens itself” behavior is prototyped in the vertical slice by the [[ossuary-sentinel|Ossuary Sentinel]] (a tomb-golem boss) via its Adamant Shell skill, which stacks Defense Up + Resistance Up on itself (005 §Blacksmith Boss proto note; proto: src/core/combat/content.ts — tomb_golem / g_harden ). Open questions DEBATED Blacksmith vs golem — is the Blacksmith a separate boss, or did the adaptive-armor concept simply become the tomb golem ([[ossuary-sentinel|Ossuary Sentinel]])? (005 §Boss reconciliation). DEBATED Is it Pandora? — “A magical blacksmith lady… is that just [[pandora|Pandora]] again?” (M04397–M04398, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17); whether the Blacksmith Boss is a distinct entity or a face of Pandora is unresolved. Design history 2023-02-08 — Original pitch as one of the gaming-deconstruction pillars: “We fight blacksmith who changes their armor based on your attack” (M01162, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-10-17 — Recast as “a magical blacksmith lady”, with the aside that this might “just [be] [[pandora|Pandora]] again” — folding the concept toward the Pandora character (M04397–M04398, CrunchyConniptions) — current presentation. (proto, undated) — The adaptive-hardening intent surfaces implemented not as the Blacksmith but as the [[ossuary-sentinel|Ossuary Sentinel]]'s Adamant Shell (005 §Blacksmith Boss proto note). Provenance Raw: M01160–M01163 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08); M04397 (2023-10-17). Cleaned: 000 Part 7 §Gaming Deconstruction; 000 Part 16 §Pandora. Docs: 005 §Blacksmith Boss; 005 §Enemy Adaptation; 008. Ledger: rows “Gaming-deconstruction pillars… blacksmith boss…” and “…a magical blacksmith lady… is that just Pandora again?” in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv ."},{"title":"Brigands & Bandits","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/brigands.html","text":"Brigands Bandits CANON The game’s basic human enemy — outlaws and cutthroats, used as the early-game yardstick the rest of the bestiary scales up from. Summary — Brigands (a.k.a. bandits) are a basic human enemy type and one of the four baseline test enemies (M02168, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). They are the low anchor of the game’s anti-HP-bloat design: you “go from fighting bandits to dragon,” not “bandits to bandits with quadruple HP” ([[enemy-design-philosophy]]). Narratively they connect to characters who fell into banditry, and to rumors of areas harboring near-supernaturally stealthy bandits. The proto realizes them as the River Brigand . Description role Basic human enemy type; used as the early-game comparison point for progression (005 §Characteristics). The canonical example of “tactical complexity, not HP bloat”: bandits → dragon, not bandits with quadruple HP (M02100, FreshToast, 2023-02-16 on linear scaling; see [[enemy-design-philosophy]]). Listed in the test character setup alongside Goblins, [[slimes|Slimes]], [[golems|Golems]] (M02168, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Narrative context The character Cervantes “had dreams of rebellion and social reform but ended up in a life [of] banditry as an outlaw backed by cutthroat killers” (M01361, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Rumors surround at least one mountain for “harbouring bandits with near-supernatural stealth” (M01927, FreshToast, 2023-02-14) — a hook tying bandits to the game’s stealth/ambush texture ([[encounters-and-ambushes]]). Proto realization: River Brigand [IMPLEMENTED] The vertical slice ships the River Brigand ( brigand , 🗡️), a 24-HP cutthroat using Slash and Cutpurse (which applies Bleed); full stats in [[proto-enemy-roster]] (proto: src/core/combat/content.ts — ENEMY_DEFS ). “River” ties it to the Mediterranean/Caribbean river throughline (005 §Common / elite enemies (proto), Notes). It fights in the “Ambush in the Narrows” elite encounter (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts node n2b ). Design history 2023-02-09 — Cervantes’ fall into “a life of banditry… backed by cutthroat killers” establishes bandits in the fiction (M01361, FreshToast). 2023-02-14 — rumor of bandits with “near-supernatural stealth” (M01927, FreshToast). 2023-02-16 — brigands added to the baseline test-enemy roster (M02168, CrunchyConniptions); the “bandits → dragon, not quadruple-HP bandits” scaling framing (M02100, FreshToast). This remains the current lore. (proto, undated) — realized as the River Brigand cutthroat. [IMPLEMENTED] Provenance Raw: M01361 (FreshToast, 2023-02-09); M01927 (FreshToast, 2023-02-14); M02100, M02168 (2023-02-16) Cleaned: 000 (Brigands/Bandits; Cervantes) Docs: 005 §Brigands/Bandits, §Common Enemies, §Common / elite enemies (proto) Ledger: Cervantes / bandit rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Dracosis (The Dragon Virus)","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/dracosis.html","text":"Dracosis (The Dragon Virus) CANON A “dragon” isn’t a lizard — it’s a disease. Dracosis is a virus-like, alien “living disease” that turns a bloodline progressively draconic over generations. Summary — Dracosis is the biological engine behind dragons and many mutated creatures in the setting. In its most recent (Part 18 / 2023-11-07) form it is a virus, not a parasite: a virus-like entity in a state of “borrowed life” — more animate than a typical virus yet distinct from a conventional parasite; an alien “living disease” (M04853–M04893, 2023-11-07). It carries a subtle intention , guiding host behavior (aggression, especially toward dangerous foes), and over a few generations produces hard scales, flame resistance and breath, large size, and enhanced lifespan. The condition that causes Dracosis is named [[drykksar|Drykksar]] . Its earlier framing as a “bloodborne parasite” (Part 10) was superseded by the virus reframe. What Dracosis is (current: living disease / virus) CANON A virus, not a parasite EVOLVED — “remove bacteria… I’d rather you refer to parasites instead” → “Ok lol, it can be a virus instead of a parasite then… Final: virus” (M04864, CrunchyConniptions; M04870, FreshToast, 2023-11-07). Supersedes the Part 10 parasite framing (see Design history). Borrowed life: a virus-like entity in “a unique state of ‘borrowed life,’ more animate than a typical virus yet distinct from a conventional parasite” — alien, perplexing, “otherworldly”; the “living disease” is “the closest we’re gonna get” (M04853, M04876, M04883, 2023-11-07). Intention / will: “it possesses intention and, hopefully, purpose, though it may not be technically alive.” Symptoms guide host behavior more than other diseases — violence/aggression, especially toward seemingly dangerous foes; its intentions are “conveyed through [the] lives of others,” “like possession but more sensual” (M04890, M04893, 2023-11-07; 005 §Dracosis Lore). Physical changes (latter stages, a few generations deep): hard scales, flame resistance and breath, large size, enhanced lifespan (M04890, FreshToast, 2023-11-07). Earlier phrasing listed “metallic skin, fiery breath, increased size and functional wings,” with fiery breath alternatively a laser, volcanic spit, or flammable gas (M02699–M02700, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Dragons as descendants, not beasts CANON “A ‘dragon’ is actually a bloodborne parasite and not just a flying lizard. Rather, it infects a host and causes their descendants to become more and more ‘draconic’… Current dragons are the descendants of dinosaurs, who never actually died off but were instead taken over by dragons” (M02691, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). (The “parasite” wording here is the superseded framing; the descent-from-dinosaurs and generational-mutation ideas persist.) Ties into the [[ajax]] dragon-blood throughline : Ajax got infected upon stabbing/eating his dragon, so draconic features run in his descendants (M02684, FreshToast, 2023-02-22; M04722, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). [[vaenngrym]] (best-a) is described as the son of Ajax’s dragon. Draconic creatures have irises “coloured like a sunset” (M02905, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). Philosophy in-setting virology A play on Dark Souls “hollowing”: a purposeless (hollow) individual gets infected and is given purpose — “arguably it is more alive than real living beings without purpose”; described as the opposite of hollowing (Dark Souls) or Drogo (Game of Thrones) (M04893, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-07). Kept nebulous: nobody has invented the microscope yet, so virus and bacteria are functionally identical in-world; characters cannot reasonably deduce what it is or whether it’s alive (005 §Virology in setting). Thematic pairing: Dracosis is the thematic opposite of [[vampires|vampirism]] — Dracosis = evolving into a superior being (the future, changing to be strong); vampirism = stagnation (M05173–M05187, 2024-02-10). See [[raphael]] (best-a), in whom both blend. Creatures produced by Dracosis [[vaenngrym|The dragon Vaenngrym]] (best-a), [[giant-beetle|the Giant Beetle]] (large + semi-metallic shell), and [[minor-creatures|Striga (mosquito-dragons)]] are all dracosis-mutated. See also the [[grafting-cult|Grafting Cult]] and general [[grafting-cult|monster evolution]] (monsters “growing just a little too fast”). Open questions The name of the causative condition/parasite was chosen as [[drykksar|Drykksar]] (over the candidate “Sardrykkr”) (M02695–M02697, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Drykksar has its own page in the bestiary, alongside this one. Whether Locke gets infected with Dracosis (or it becomes a player choice) was long undecided; “go with the flow” for now (M04717–M04727, 2023-10-25). Design history 2023-02-22 (Part 10) — original framing: a dragon is a “bloodborne parasite,” infecting a host so descendants become progressively draconic (tough hide, fiery breath); current dragons descend from dinosaurs “taken over by dragons.” The causative condition is named [[drykksar|Drykksar]] (over “Sardrykkr”); Dracosis is the process of a bloodline becoming draconic (M02691–M02700, FreshToast). 2023-11-07 (Part 18, “Final Version”) — reframed from parasite → virus : after “remove bacteria… refer to parasites instead,” the team settles on “virus instead of a parasite” — an alien “living disease” in “borrowed life,” with intention/will and generational physical changes; explicitly a play on Dark Souls hollowing (M04853–M04893, CrunchyConniptions FreshToast). This supersedes the parasite framing and is the current presentation. EVOLVED 2024-02-10 — positioned as the thematic opposite of vampirism (evolution vs stagnation) (M05173–M05187). Provenance Raw: M02684, M02691–M02700, M02905 (FreshToast, 2023-02-22 / 2023-03-05); M04722 (2023-10-25); M04853–M04898 (CrunchyConniptions FreshToast, 2023-11-07); M05173–M05187 (2024-02-10) Cleaned: 000 Part 10 / Part 18 (Dracosis / The Dragon Virus) Docs: 005 §Dracosis Lore (Living Disease / Virus), §True Nature (Dragons as Parasite/Virus); 001 §Dracosis (The Dragon Virus) Ledger: dragon-parasite / Dracosis-traits / Drykksar rows (R15) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Drykksar","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/drykksar.html","text":"Drykksar EVOLVED The living disease that causes Dracosis: a virus-like entity that, despite not being strictly alive, exhibits a subtle but noticeable intention — and turns a bloodline steadily more draconic over generations. Summary — Drykksar is the name of the condition which causes Dracosis, the process by which a bloodline becomes steadily more draconic (M02697, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). In its current (November 2023) conception it is a virus-like “living disease” rather than a conventional parasite: something that, despite not being strictly alive, has a subtle but noticeable intention — “a will of sorts but not in a familiar sense” — conveyed through its symptoms and the way it guides host behaviour more strongly than other diseases do, toward violence and aggression especially against seemingly dangerous foes (M04860–M04865, FreshToast, 2023-11-07). Latter stages of infection, occurring a few generations deep, bring physical changes: hard scales, flame resistance and breath, large size and enhanced lifespan (M04890–M04891, FreshToast, 2023-11-07). Nature Rules Causes Dracosis : a bloodline steadily becoming more draconic (M02697, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Difficult to contract; requires a living host; 100% hereditary success ; lasting but slow spread (M02710–M02711, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Only has subtle effects on a primary host — the first instance in a bloodline (M02713, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Current framing (2023-11-07): a virus/disease rather than a parasite — “Bacteria/Virus is functionally identical to us because nobody’s invented a microscope yet”; virology in the setting isn’t developed enough for characters to define what a virus even is (M04858, M04867, FreshToast, 2023-11-07). CrunchyConniptions specifically vetoed “bacteria” (“because fuck bacteria… I honestly have no good reason”, M04869) so it is a virus (M04870, FreshToast, 2023-11-07). The key hook is intention without conventional life : “the disease is alive and suggests intention with its symptoms” (M04890, FreshToast, 2023-11-07); “arguably it is more alive than real living beings without purpose” (M04895, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-11-07). CrunchyConniptions likens it to an inverse of Dark Souls hollowing / Drogo from Game of Thrones — it provides rather than strips purpose (M04893, 2023-11-07). Hard limit agreed by all three: “no avatars” — no individual embodiment of the drykksar (M04900–M04903, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions/spirit_noodle, 2023-11-07). Thematic role “It’s the physical manifestation of evolution with replacing ‘trial and error’ with ‘Victory by battle’ (bloodshed).” (M02698, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-22) Eventually the hero’s lineage would have become like-dragons themselves (002 §Drykksar). The [[bastard-damaris|bastard Damaris]] is a herald or champion of sorts for the draconic parasite (M02694, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). Dracosis also drives the bestiary: monsters are evolving — e.g. Striga, “ancient mosquitos having undergone dracosis”, and the large semi-metallic beetle (M02705–M02709, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Sequel hook PROPOSED In a sequel there could be some kind of inquisition or order dedicated to eliminating drykksar — “There’s no real point talking about this now but it’d be cool to future proof” (M03048–M03049, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). Design history 2023-02-22 — naming: two candidate spellings floated in one message, “Sardrykkr / Drykksar” (M02695, FreshToast); Drykksar is the spelling used in every subsequent message, so Sardrykkr is the superseded alternative (preserved in 009 §Appendix — Considered Dropped). Same day: defined as “the name of the condition or parasite which causes Dracosis” (M02697, FreshToast); framed as evolution-by-bloodshed (M02698, CrunchyConniptions); the bastard named its herald (M02694, FreshToast). 2023-02-24 — parasite traits fixed: mutagenic parasite ; difficult to contract, requires a living host, 100% hereditary success, lasting but slow spread, subtle effects on the primary host (M02710–M02713, FreshToast). Dracosis end-state: metallic skin, fiery breath (or laser / volcanic spit / flammable gas), increased size, functional wings (M02699–M02700, FreshToast). 2023-03-15 — PROPOSED sequel anti-drykksar inquisition/order (M03048, FreshToast). 2023-11-07 — evolved: parasite → virus-like living disease with intention — supersedes “mutagenic parasite” above . CrunchyConniptions pushed for a “Virus+” with “borrowed life”: “alien, perplexing, otherworldly” rather than a mundane parasite (M04853). FreshToast countered that a virus being “more alive than others” is the wrong hook and proposed instead a disease that despite not being strictly alive has a subtle but noticeable intention (M04857–M04861); “parasite’s a little different, more complex and doesn’t pair as well with our current iteration of the idea” (M04868). Agreed outcome: it’s a virus, “the disease is alive and suggests intention with its symptoms”, latter-stage generational physical changes (hard scales, flame resistance and breath, large size, enhanced lifespan), and no avatars (M04870, M04890–M04903, all three authors). Provenance Raw: M02694–M02700, M02705–M02713, M03048–M03049, M04853–M04903 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions, spirit_noodle; 2023-02-22 → 2023-11-07) Cleaned: 000 Part 10 (Drykksar / Dracosis definition, parasite traits); Part 11 (sequel inquisition) Docs: 002 §Drykksar (Supernatural Condition/Parasite); 001 §Dracosis (The Dragon Virus); 005; 009 §Appendix — Considered Dropped (Sardrykkr spelling) Ledger: world rows 27, 29, 35; character row 162; story row 297 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Eldritch Super Boss","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/eldritch-superboss.html","text":"Eldritch Super Boss DEBATED A hidden endgame superboss you cannot kill — you “beat” it, and it rewards you with a super cool item. Summary — Listed under Hidden Super Bosses , the Eldritch is an unkillable capstone encounter: “You cannot kill it. You beat it. Gives you super cool item” (M00867–M00870, spirit_noodle CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02). It is a special game-mechanic feature — a true-ending / superboss test rather than a standard enemy. In the cross-doc reconciliation, GAME_DESIGN.md and the proto gamebible name a Daemon superboss as the endgame test and treat it as the same encounter as this unkillable Eldritch (005 §Hidden Super Bosses). Description A hidden super boss — an optional endgame encounter (M00866, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02; 005 §Hidden Super Bosses). You cannot kill it; you beat it — the win condition is not a kill (M00867–M00869, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-02). Beating it gives you a super cool item (M00870, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02). Recorded as a special game-mechanic feature. Open questions DEBATED Eldritch vs Daemon — is the hidden unkillable superboss (Eldritch) and the GAME_DESIGN “Daemon superboss” one encounter or two? The proto gamebible treats them as the same capstone; this page records the alternate name (005 §Daemon vs Eldritch; §Hidden Super Bosses). See [[daemon-of-want|Daemon]]. PROPOSED What “beating without killing” mechanically entails, and what the “super cool item” is, are unspecified. Design history 2023-02-02 — Coined during an early feature brainstorm under “Hidden Super Bosses”: spirit_noodle names it “Eldtrich [sic]”, “You cannot kill it / You beat it”; CrunchyConniptions adds “gives you super cool item” (M00866–M00870) — matches the current presentation. (cross-doc, undated) — Reconciled with the “Daemon superboss” of GAME_DESIGN.md / the proto gamebible, treated there as the same endgame test (005 §Hidden Super Bosses reconciliation). Provenance Raw: M00866–M00870 (CrunchyConniptions spirit_noodle, 2023-02-02). Cleaned: 000 Bestiary §Hidden Super Bosses. Docs: 005 §Hidden Super Bosses. Ledger: row “Hidden super bosses: an Eldritch one you cannot kill…” in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv ."},{"title":"Encounters, Ambushes & Enemy Areas","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/encounters-and-ambushes.html","text":"Encounters, Ambushes Enemy Areas CANON How you meet the bestiary: visible roaming monsters you can avoid or sneak up on, telegraphed ambushes that scatter your formation, a lantern that cuts both ways, and regions each keyed to a signature enemy type. Summary — The game favors visible roaming monsters over random encounters (avoidable, Persona-style) (M01295–M01298, 2023-02-09). Some enemies ambush by rushing you with a sound, scattering your formation unless you land a timed counter (M02910, FreshToast, 2023-03-09) — a mechanic used sparingly and implemented in the proto’s “Ambush in the Narrows.” A carried lantern lets you see but also lets monsters see you; some are drawn to it, some fear it. Each region has a signature enemy type ([[imps|Goblin]], [[vampires|Vampires]], Dragon, Skeleton-tomb). Roaming monsters (vs random encounters) CANON Random encounters rejected: “I really don’t want random encounters” (M01295, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09); “having them appear as roaming monsters you can avoid is preferable” (M01298, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Visible, Persona/Pokémon-style: seen beforehand, “maybe persona style” (M01299, CrunchyConniptions); Pokémon Scarlet’s roaming approach cited (“We’ll do less”) (M01356, M01409–M01411, 2023-02-09/10). Easy to make simple chibis for overworld roaming monsters (M01353, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Interaction: you can distract monsters, and sneak up to begin battle with a slight edge (M01513, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Once you clear an area they don’t just pop back up; only once you completely leave a dungeon may they reappear (005 §Design Approach). No boss resets — bosses are “instead just replaced with powerful enemies” (005 §Alternative Considered). Ambush enemies CANON The mechanic: “Some enemies will attempt to ambush you by making a sound and rushing towards you. If you are caught, you are ‘ambushed’ which causes your positioning to scatter at the beginning of the battle. To counter this rush, you have to attack them just as they reach you” (M02910, FreshToast, 2023-03-09). The reaction feel: hearing the sound, a player goes “HUH WHERE?!” and scrambles to react (M02918, FreshToast, 2023-03-09) — a fun little timed minigame. Pre-detection: you can use a consumable item “or whatever to get that info” on whether you’ll be ambushed, pre-emptively, before entering a dungeon (M02922–M02923, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-09). Design constraints: use sparingly and telegraphed — only in areas/dungeons designed for it, or it loses impact; not “Hades fishing tier” difficult; do it once early to set the tone (M02921, FreshToast, 2023-03-09). Reference: “gross sewer babies in Demon’s Souls” (the surprise/jump-scare) (M02927–M02928, 2023-03-09). Associated creatures: [[minor-creatures|Ninja Frogs]] (foggy bogs) and [[minor-creatures|Charging Bulls]] (open fields) — “We’ll design locations specifically for it to happen” (M02929, FreshToast, 2023-03-09). Proto status [IMPLEMENTED] : the ambush “scatters positioning” claim is implemented — a node with ambush: true triggers scatterFormation , a Fisher–Yates shuffle of hero ranks at battle start, used by the “Ambush in the Narrows” elite encounter ([[brigands|River Brigand]] + [[skeletons|Skeleton Archer]] + [[imps|Hex Imp]]) (proto: src/core/campaign/run.ts — scatterFormation ; content.ts node n2b ). See [[proto-enemy-roster]]. Light / lantern interaction CANON The player carries a lantern to see in dark places like dungeons; it “lets you see but also lets monsters see you, making it harder to sneak” (M02377–M02378, FreshToast, 2023-02-17). Some monsters are attracted to the lantern; some monsters may FEAR the lantern (M02379–M02380, FreshToast, 2023-02-17) — a trait to be played for tension in dark areas. Ties to the [[enemy-design-philosophy|light-sensitive enemy variety]] (enemies that see your torch first). The Lantern item itself is owned by the items batch — see [[lantern]]. Enemy type areas CANON “I like the approach we have for areas and each has their main enemy type” (M05366, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-03-12). Main enemy types by area: Goblin area ([[imps]]), Vampires area ([[vampires]]), Dragon area ([[vaenngrym]]), and a desired Skeleton tomb ([[skeletons]]) (005 §Enemy Type Areas). Different areas emphasize different enemy types, creating variety and distinct regional identity, and reinforce the goblins→dragon→[[lysander|Lysander]] arc (005 §Design Philosophy; see [[enemy-design-philosophy]]). Open questions Final decision on random encounters vs roaming monsters — strong preference for visible enemies, but listed as unresolved (005 §Unresolved Questions). Whether to include a dedicated skeleton-tomb area (though skeletons are now mandatory for dungeons) (005 §Unresolved Questions). Design history 2023-02-09 — random encounters rejected in favor of visible, avoidable roaming monsters (Persona/Pokémon style) (M01295–M01356, CrunchyConniptions FreshToast). 2023-02-11 — sneaking/distracting roaming monsters for an ambush advantage (M01513, FreshToast). 2023-02-17 — the dual-edged lantern: lets you see, lets monsters see you; some attracted, some fear it (M02377–M02380, FreshToast). 2023-03-09 — full ambush mechanic: sound-rush → formation scatter → timed counter; pre-detection consumable; “use sparingly,” Demon’s Souls sewer-baby reference; Ninja Frogs/Charging Bulls locales (M02910–M02932, FreshToast spirit_noodle). 2024-03-12 — “each area has its main enemy type” reaffirmed (M05366, CrunchyConniptions). 2024-08-20 — enemy telegraphing tied to intent (e.g. flames flickering before a fire breath) (M05455, FreshToast) — see [[enemy-intent]] (systems). Roaming/ambush/lantern/area principles remain current. Provenance Raw: M01295–M01356 (2023-02-09); M01513 (2023-02-11); M02377–M02380 (FreshToast, 2023-02-17); M02910–M02932 (FreshToast spirit_noodle, 2023-03-09); M05366 (2024-03-12); M05455 (2024-08-20) Cleaned: 000 (Roaming Monsters; Ambush Enemies; Lantern; Enemy Type Areas) Docs: 005 §Roaming Monsters, §Ambush Enemies, §Light / Lantern Interaction, §Enemy Type Areas, §Unresolved Questions Ledger: encounter/ambush/area rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Eye Dungeon Boss","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/eye-dungeon-boss.html","text":"Eye Dungeon Boss PROPOSED The boss of a dungeon full of watching eyes: if the eyes see you, it studies your party and builds counters tailored to exactly how you fight. Summary — The Eye Dungeon Boss governs “a dungeon where there are eyes constantly following you.” The player must destroy the eyes and avoid being seen , because any eye that sees you feeds information to the end boss, which then “gather[s] information on you and create[s] countermeasures to your specific party by analysing your information” (M04224, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-15). It is an info-gathering, adaptive boss whose difficulty scales with how much the player let the eyes observe. Description A dungeon where eyes constantly follow you (Part 15) (M04224, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-15; 005 §Description). The player has to destroy the eyes and avoid them seeing you. Mechanic If the eyes see you, the end boss gathers information on you (005 §Mechanic). The boss analyzes your information to create countermeasures tailored to your specific party — an info-gathering adaptive boss. Letting fewer eyes see you presumably leaves the boss less able to counter your composition (M04224, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-15). Open questions PROPOSED The concept is a single-message pitch; the boss’s appearance, the exact form of its “countermeasures”, and how eye-avoidance translates to fight difficulty are all unspecified in the notes. Design history 2023-10-15 — Full concept introduced in one message: an eye-filled stealth dungeon whose end boss adapts to your party based on what its eyes observed (M04224, CrunchyConniptions) — matches the current presentation. Provenance Raw: M04224 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-15). Cleaned: 000 Part 15 §Eye Dungeon. Docs: 005 §Eye Dungeon Boss. Ledger: covered under the Part 15 dungeon-brainstorm rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv ."},{"title":"Fear Effect","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/fear-effect.html","text":"Fear Effect PROPOSED A monster-inflicted debuff — first seen on the dragon, which “just existing” terrifies the party for the opening turns, and floated as a shared tool other scary monsters could use. Summary — The Fear Effect is a debuff that scary enemies inflict to weaken the party. It originated as a [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]] (best-a) mechanic: the dragon “literally inflicts a fear effect on the party to weaken them at the start of battle” (the first two turns), with the party’s initiative maybe decreased “because they fear facing him” (M01770–M01786, 2023-02-11). It was explicitly floated as reusable “elsewhere by other monsters to weaken you” (M01786, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). As of the proto it is not implemented ; only its inverse (Fear Immunity) exists. Effect (as designed) Inflicted at battle start: “Maybe it literally inflicts a fear effect on the party to weaken them at the start of battle” (M01770, FreshToast, 2023-02-11); for the dragon, “everybody gets it for the first two turns” simply because the dragon exists (M01786, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Decreases initiative: “Maybe they are terrified and their initiative is decreased because they fear facing him” (M01783, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11). Recorded as “maybe fear decreases initiative” (005 §Fear Effect). Reusable across monsters: “Maybe it’s used elsewhere by other monsters to weaken you” (M01786, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Connects to the broader [[enemy-design-philosophy|“making enemies scary”]] toolkit and the [[encounters-and-ambushes|lantern]] tension (some monsters fear the lantern; some are feared themselves). Proto status PROPOSED → not yet implemented A standalone “Fear” negative status does not exist in the proto status bank (proto: src/core/combat/types.ts StatusId ; status.ts STATUS_DEFS — no fear entry). The dragon’s fear effect and the “fear decreases initiative” idea remain design intent (005 §Fear Effect, proto note). What is implemented is the inverse: a Fear Immunity positive status ( fear_immune ) that blocks the disrupt-class effects — Stun, Blind, and Silence (proto: src/core/combat/status.ts — DISRUPT = ['stun','blind','silence'] ). If Fear were added, it would most naturally land as a Speed Down -style debuff, since Speed Down ( spd_down , −3 speed/stack) is the implemented initiative-reduction effect (005 §Fear Effect, proto note). See the systems namespace for the status bank and [[escalation|escalation]]. Open questions Exact implementation of fear-effect mechanics — no “Fear” status exists in the proto yet (005 §Unresolved Questions). Design history 2023-02-11 — conceived as a dragon mechanic: a battle-start fear effect weakening the party (first two turns), possibly by decreasing initiative, and floated as reusable by other monsters (M01770–M01786, FreshToast CrunchyConniptions). Current design intent; unimplemented. (proto, undated) — only the inverse Fear Immunity positive status is implemented; no negative “Fear” exists. [IMPLEMENTED — inverse only] Provenance Raw: M01770, M01783, M01786 (FreshToast CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11) Cleaned: 000 (Fear Effect) Docs: 005 §Fear Effect, §Special Mechanics Notes, §Unresolved Questions Ledger: fear-effect rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Giant Beetle (\"Round Boi\")","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/giant-beetle.html","text":"Giant Beetle (“Round Boi”) CANON A house-friendly giant beetle — grown large by [[dracosis]], hard-shelled, and befriendable by [[locke]]. The one “meaningful decision” the game won’t actually let you refuse. Summary — The Giant Beetle is a large, semi-metallic-shelled beetle that grew big because of [[dracosis]] (M02709, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). In the vertical slice a beetle infestation is dealt with at the first city; Locke befriends a big beetle (“round boi”), which then leads the party to a dungeon, after which the beetles return home. Its befriending is used for humor as a deliberately meaningless “decision” — you can technically choose, but the beetle has to be part of the story. The exact name (“round boi” / “big round boi” / “round big”) is still open. Characteristics Large because of dracosis , with “a hard, semi-metallic shell” (M02709, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). See [[dracosis]] for the mutation lore and [[minor-creatures|other dracosis-mutated creatures]]. Narrative role CANON A beetle infestation is dealt with at the first city; a big beetle is befriended by Locke (M02459, FreshToast, 2023-02-19; the vertical-slice scope). The large beetle “pulls [a] wagon” (M02460, FreshToast, 2023-02-19) and leads the party to a dungeon ; the beetles “can return home” after the dungeon is cleared (M02459, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). The “meaningless decision” gag: meeting the beetle is framed as “Would you like to be friends with this cool giant beetle that likes you or do you suck?” (M03623, FreshToast, 2023-08-26) — “In our game full of meaningful decisions, there’s one decision they can’t refuse to make,” a deliberately meaningless choice (the beetle must be in the story) used for humor and to contrast with choices that do matter (005 §Narrative Role). Naming DEBATED Called “round boi” (M03615, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-26), then “big round boi” (M03617) or “round big” (M03618). CrunchyConniptions: “if you want to be friends with me… just give me a round boi and I am sold” (M03624, 2023-08-26). Exact name still open (005 §Status). Open questions DEBATED Final name: “round boi” vs “big round boi” vs “round big” (M03615–M03618, 2023-08-26). Design history 2023-02-10 — first appearance: “pet the giant beetle” / “Definitely pet the beetle” (M01374, spirit_noodle; M01375, FreshToast). 2023-02-19 — folded into the vertical-slice scope: beetle infestation at the first city → Locke befriends the big beetle → it leads them to a dungeon → beetles return home; beetle also pulls a wagon (M02459–M02460, FreshToast). 2023-02-24 — lore basis added: the beetle is large due to dracosis and has a hard, semi-metallic shell (M02709, FreshToast). 2023-08-26 — the “round boi” naming and the “meaningless decision” humor: the befriend prompt and the deliberately-unrefusable-choice framing (M03614–M03624, CrunchyConniptions FreshToast). Agreed to be a good idea; name left open. Current presentation. Provenance Raw: M01374–M01375 (2023-02-10); M02459–M02460 (FreshToast, 2023-02-19); M02709 (FreshToast, 2023-02-24); M03614–M03624 (CrunchyConniptions FreshToast, 2023-08-26) Cleaned: 000 Part 9 / Part 15 (Giant Beetle) Docs: 005 §Giant Beetle (“Round Boi”); vertical-slice scope in 004/003/005 Ledger: “Vertical slice scope” row (R13, M02459) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Golems","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/golems.html","text":"Golems CANON Soul-forged stone servants — created by [[gideon-azurith|Gideon]] from harvested criminal souls, unnerving enough that even [[locke]] is uneasy around them. Summary — Golems are a baseline enemy type with a dark origin: Gideon Azurith gathers criminals and outlaws (via [[citrine]]), harvests their souls, and converts them into golems — “literal conversion of people into tools” — which he justifies by sending the golems to help people (M02173, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Locke “is a little creeped out by the golems for some reason” (M02180, FreshToast, 2023-02-16) — narrative unease tied to that soul-harvested origin. The playable proto’s only golem is the boss-tier tomb guardian (owned on the [[ossuary-sentinel]] page). Description role Listed among the baseline enemy types in the brainstorm test setup: Goblins, [[slimes|Slimes]], Golems, [[brigands|Brigands]] (M02168, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Heavy stone constructs; in the source’s screenplay vignette Locke’s borrowed sword “SHATTERS against its stone body,” and she must find weak points (joints) to bring one down — an early illustration of the “tactical, not brute-force” combat ethos ([[enemy-design-philosophy]]). Origin CANON Created by [[gideon-azurith|Gideon]] from harvested criminal souls (005 §Origin; M02173, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Gideon “sends [[citrine]] to gather criminals and outlaws,” then “harvests their souls to be converted into golems,” justifying it by sending the golems “to help people” and “progress society” (M02173–M02174, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Locke’s unease: “Locke is a little creeped out by the golems for some reason” (M02180, FreshToast, 2023-02-16) — the soul-harvest origin is the implied cause. CANON Proto note The vertical slice ships a golem only as a boss — the tomb-guardian [[ossuary-sentinel]] ( tomb_golem , 🗿) — not as rank-and-file. That proto golem is a tomb guardian / adaptive heavy and is not yet tied to Gideon’s soul-harvest origin above — the origin remains design lore (005 §Golems, Implemented golem boss note). See the [[ossuary-sentinel]] page (best-a) for its stats and kit. Design history 2023-02-16 — golems appear in the test-setup enemy list (M02168, CrunchyConniptions); the same day Gideon’s soul-harvest golem economy is described (M02173–M02174, FreshToast) and Locke’s unease around them noted (M02180, FreshToast). This remains the current lore. (proto, undated) — realized only as the boss-tier [[ossuary-sentinel]], decoupled from the soul-harvest origin. [IMPLEMENTED] Provenance Raw: M02168 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16); M02173–M02174, M02180 (FreshToast, 2023-02-16) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 (golem origin), test setup Docs: 005 §Golems, §Common Enemies; ties to 005 §Boss: Ossuary Sentinel (proto) Ledger: “Gideon’s golem economy” / “Locke creeped out by golems” rows (R12) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Grafting Cult & Monster Evolution","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/grafting-cult.html","text":"Grafting Cult Monster Evolution CANON People who graft monster parts onto their own bodies — escaping physical weakness at the cost of their humanity, and foreshadowing [[dracosis]]. Summary — Two linked ideas from the same session: monsters in the setting are evolving too fast (people have noticed monsters “growing just a little too fast”), and a cult has arisen of people who graft monster parts onto themselves (M02715, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). The cult is a short-term, antagonistic faction used to foreshadow [[dracosis]] and the game’s theme of discarding one’s humanity — humans escaping physical weakness at the cost of what makes them human (M02717, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Monster evolution CANON Monsters are evolving; “people have identified that monsters are growing just a little too fast” (M02715, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). This is the same accelerating-mutation phenomenon that [[dracosis]] describes at the bloodline level (see the [[giant-beetle|Giant Beetle]] and [[minor-creatures|Striga]] as visible results). The Grafting Cult CANON “Cult of people who graft monster parts onto themselves” (M02715, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Function: a “short term, antagonistic faction used to introduce fore[s]hadowing of dracosis and discarding one’s humanity” (M02717, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Theme: humans escaping physical weakness at the cost of their humanity (005 §Monster Evolution the Grafting Cult). The source notes the full lore lives in the World Bible. Design history 2023-02-24 — introduced in one session: monsters growing too fast, and a cult grafting monster parts onto themselves as a short-term antagonistic faction foreshadowing dracosis and the loss-of-humanity theme (M02715–M02717, FreshToast). Current presentation; undeveloped beyond this seed in the notes. Provenance Raw: M02715–M02717 (FreshToast, 2023-02-24) Cleaned: 000 Part 10 (graft cult; monster evolution) Docs: 005 §Monster Evolution the Grafting Cult; cross-ref 001 (World Bible faction lore) Ledger: “Cult of people who graft monster parts” row (R15, M02715–M02717) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Imps","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/imps.html","text":"Imps DEBATED The game’s small feral-mob enemy: green-less little guys with an obsession/mania streak and horridly slurred trash-talk. The name itself (Imp vs Goblin vs a made-up word) is unsettled. Summary — Imps are the low-tier feral swarm enemy, deliberately keeping the classic goblin fantasy intact (“There aren’t enough goblins out there”) while dodging the misused “goblin” name. As most recently discussed, they are not green and carry an “obsession/mania quality,” and the team kept debating the right name — Imp, Goblin, or an invented “slezzy” word like skugg (M04618–M04639, 2023-10-24). The playable proto ships them as Goblin Imp (melee) and Hex Imp (caster), but neither is the lore-final name. Description characteristics Not green; the “obsession/mania quality” is layered on top of the classic goblin template (M04622, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). All classic traits of creatures like goblins are meant to “stay intact” (M04622, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). Have barks like “Gee guu, skug” when they crit — described as “horridly slurred english trash talk” (M04225, FreshToast, 2023-10-16). Combat role The low end of the game’s strategic progression arc: goblins → dragon → [[lysander]] ; the intended lesson is that “the best strat to beat goblins is raw individual overwhelming power” (005 §Combat Strategy). See [[enemy-design-philosophy]] for the progression-through-tactics rationale and [[vaenngrym]] / Lysander as the later rungs. Assigned their own region — a “Goblin area” is one of the game’s main enemy-type-by-area zones (005 §Enemy Type Areas). See [[encounters-and-ambushes]]. Naming debate DEBATED The right name was argued at length and never fully settled (M04618–M04639, 2023-10-24): “Call em Goblins. Superior name” (M04624, CrunchyConniptions). Counter: “goblin” is a misuse — goblins are actually Celtic house spirits that cause mischief, and the team isn’t “big on appropriating names or ideas without purpose” (M04627, FreshToast). Related creatures get called “a million different things: kobolds, hobs, bugbears,” and kobold is technically most accurate; but Imp “is just a word so it’s free reign” (005 §Name Change Reasoning). Concern against Imp: “Maybe I’m biased against imp… always been forgettable monsters” ; biblical demon imps are “largely forgettable” (005 §Alternative Names Considered). Alternatives floated: Hellion (“feral little guys”), a made-up Skugg , and Dregs / dreglings / boyes (dregs = residue or scum) (M04635–M04639, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). CrunchyConniptions asked for “a more slezzy name, like skugg or something” (M04633). Proto realization [IMPLEMENTED] The vertical slice (“The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb”) ships two imp variants (proto: src/core/combat/content.ts — ENEMY_DEFS ); full stats live in [[proto-enemy-roster]]: Goblin Imp ( goblin_imp , 👺) — a 16-HP skirmisher; Stab and Snatch (which applies Speed Down). Hex Imp ( imp_caster , 🔮) — a magic-using caster variant; Hex (magic + Blind) and Ward (Resistance Up on self). Neither name is the lore-final decision — the Imps/Goblins question above is still open. Open questions DEBATED Final creature name: Imps vs Goblins vs an invented/derogatory word (M04618–M04639, 2023-10-24; 005 §Unresolved Questions). Design history 2023-10-16 — first concrete characterization: the “imps” have crit barks like “Gee guu, skug” , slurred-English trash-talk (M04225, FreshToast). 2023-10-24 — the naming debate: CrunchyConniptions wants plain “Goblins” (“Superior name,” M04624); FreshToast objects on the grounds goblins are misused Celtic house-spirits and prefers the free-reign word “Imp,” noting the mob isn’t green and has an obsession/mania quality (M04618–M04627). Alternatives (Hellion, Skugg, Dregs/dreglings/boyes) proposed; left unresolved (M04633–M04639). This is the current, still-open state. (proto, undated) — implemented as Goblin Imp + Hex Imp in the playable slice, hedging the name by keeping both “Goblin” and “Imp.” [IMPLEMENTED] Provenance Raw: M04225 (FreshToast, 2023-10-16); M04618–M04639 (CrunchyConniptions FreshToast, 2023-10-24) Cleaned: 000 (Imps / naming discussion) Docs: 005 §Imps (formerly considered “Goblins”), §Common Enemies, §Proto bestiary, §Unresolved Questions Ledger: naming/creature rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Minor & Undeveloped Creatures","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/minor-creatures.html","text":"Minor Undeveloped Creatures DEBATED A holding page for creatures that exist in the notes only as a line or two — dracosis-mutant bugs, classic legendaries, and the ambush critters. Each is a seed, most still undeveloped. Summary — Several bestiary entries in 005 are only a line or two long. They are grouped here so each keeps its citation without inflating into a stub page: the dracosis-mutated Striga and Wargs ; the classic Hydrae and Griffons (whose “classic traits must stay intact”); and the ambush-associated Ninja Frogs and Charging Bulls . Where a creature is more fully realized elsewhere it links out. Striga (Mosquito Dragons) DEBATED “Striga: ancient mosquitos having undergone [[dracosis]]” (M02706, FreshToast, 2023-02-24), introduced under the heading “Mosquito dragons” (M02705, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). Single-line stub; otherwise undeveloped in the notes (005 §Mosquito Dragons (Striga)). A visible product of accelerating [[grafting-cult|monster evolution]]. Wargs DEBATED Source is “Mentioned” only — “Wargs” (M02707, FreshToast, 2023-02-24); no further details provided (005 §Wargs). Hydrae Classic trait that “must stay intact”: hydrae have loads of heads (M04623, FreshToast, 2023-10-24; 005 §Legendary Creatures). This is part of the game’s core creature-design principle — keep classic traits intact (see [[enemy-design-philosophy]] §Creature design). No further details provided. Griffons Classic trait that “must stay intact”: griffons are bird-lions (M04623, FreshToast, 2023-10-24; 005 §Legendary Creatures). Earliest mention is a throwaway wish: “I also lowkey want tiny griffons instead of pigeons but that can be sacrificed for our sanity if need be” (M00746, FreshToast, 2021-08-17). No further development. Ninja Frogs Found in foggy bogs (M02929, FreshToast, 2023-03-09; 005 §Ninja Frogs). spirit_noodle: “foggy bogs with ninja frogs sounds AWESOME” (M02932, 2023-03-09). Associated with ambush mechanics — see [[encounters-and-ambushes]]. Charging Bulls Found in open fields (M02929, FreshToast, 2023-03-09; 005 §Charging Bulls). Associated with ambush mechanics — see [[encounters-and-ambushes]]. Open questions DEBATED Striga and Wargs are undeveloped stubs (005 §Mosquito Dragons (Striga), §Wargs). Hydrae and Griffons have only their “classic trait” defined; no stats, role, or region. Design history 2021-08-17 — Griffons first wished for (“tiny griffons instead of pigeons”) (M00746, FreshToast). 2023-02-24 — Striga (ancient mosquitos that underwent dracosis) and Wargs (mentioned only) added (M02705–M02707, FreshToast). 2023-03-09 — Ninja Frogs (foggy bogs) and Charging Bulls (open fields) introduced as ambush-locale creatures (M02929–M02932, FreshToast spirit_noodle). 2023-10-24 — Hydrae (loads of heads) and Griffons (bird-lions) fixed as classic-traits-must-stay-intact creatures (M04623, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M00746 (2021-08-17); M02705–M02707 (FreshToast, 2023-02-24); M02929, M02932 (2023-03-09); M04623 (FreshToast, 2023-10-24) Cleaned: 000 (Legendary Creatures; Striga; Wargs; ambush creatures) Docs: 005 §Legendary Creatures, §Mosquito Dragons (Striga), §Wargs, §Ninja Frogs, §Charging Bulls Ledger: creature rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Ossuary Sentinel","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/ossuary-sentinel.html","text":"Ossuary Sentinel CANON The single boss of the playable vertical slice — a tomb-golem guardian (🗿) that stands watch at the threshold of [[ajaxs-tomb|Ajax’s Tomb]], beaten by reading its telegraphs and coordinating rather than out-damaging its 132 HP. Summary — The Ossuary Sentinel (“Boss · Tomb Guardian”, glyph 🗿, id tomb_golem ) is the lone boss shipped in the browser vertical slice, “The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb.” It is an adaptive heavy that self-hardens mid-fight — the “changes its armor / hardens itself” behavior once pitched for the [[blacksmith-boss|Blacksmith Boss]] is prototyped here instead, via its Adamant Shell skill (005 §Boss: Ossuary Sentinel (proto)). As an implemented [[golems|golem]] it is currently a pure tomb guardian, not yet tied to Gideon’s soul-harvest golem origin — that origin remains design lore (005 §Implemented golem boss). Description role The slice’s single boss — a tomb-golem guardian of Ajax’s Tomb, deployed at graph node n5 (“The Ossuary Sentinel”, boss), where it fights alone (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts — node n5 ; 005 §Encounter context (proto)). It realizes the adaptive / self-hardening design the group wanted from the [[blacksmith-boss|Blacksmith]]; that concept was prototyped on the golem rather than on a distinct blacksmith enemy (005 §Boss: Ossuary Sentinel (proto)). As a [[golems|golem]], it appears in the proto only as the boss , not as rank-and-file (005 §Implemented golem boss; §Proto reconciliation). Stats [IMPLEMENTED] All values: proto: src/core/combat/content.ts — tomb_golem . Position 1 = front rank; for enemies, a skill that “targets a hero” uses targetSide: 'enemy' . Stat Value Id / role tomb_golem · “Boss · Tomb Guardian” 🗿 HP 132 AP 4 (enough to fire its 3-AP AoE; high vs. the 2-AP rank-and-file) Speed 3 STR / MAG 16 / 6 DEF / RES 18 / 12 ACC / Crit 90 / 8 Start rank 1 Abilities [IMPLEMENTED] Skills (proto: src/core/combat/content.ts — tomb_golem ; intent weights in parentheses): Sundering Slam ( g_slam , 2 AP) — 13 phys to a hero in ranks 1–2. (weight 3) — its big single-target hit; a one-per-fight [[shields-and-wounded|shield]] blunts a would-be killing blow from it (005 §Shields). Tomb-Quake ( g_quake , 3 AP) — 8 phys to all heroes ( scope: 'all' ). (weight 2) Grave Curse ( g_curse , 1 AP) — 5 mag + Strength Down to any hero. (weight 2) Adamant Shell ( g_harden , 1 AP) — Defense Up + Resistance Up on self — the adaptive-hardening tell inherited from the Blacksmith concept. (weight 1) Design read The Sentinel telegraphs hard and is meant to be beaten by reading its [[enemy-intent|intent]] and coordinating — “become as the goblin — many, coordinated, humble” — not by out-damaging its 132 HP, directly enacting the [[enemy-design-philosophy|“tactical complexity over HP bloat”]] philosophy (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts node n5 story; 005 §Boss: Ossuary Sentinel (proto)). Fights also grow deadlier over time via [[escalation|Escalation]], so a stalling war of attrition is discouraged. Open questions DEBATED Blacksmith vs golem — is the [[blacksmith-boss|Blacksmith]] a separate boss, or did its adaptive-armor concept simply become this tomb golem? (005 §Boss reconciliation). DEBATED Soul-harvest origin — the proto golem is a plain tomb guardian, not yet tied to the [[gideon-azurith|Gideon]] soul-harvest [[golems|golem]] origin; whether the Sentinel is meant to carry that lore is unresolved (005 §Implemented golem boss). Design history 2023-02-08 — the adaptive-armor idea is first pitched as the [[blacksmith-boss|Blacksmith Boss]]: “We fight blacksmith who changes their armor based on your attack” (M01162, CrunchyConniptions), one of the gaming-deconstruction pillars. 2023-02-16 — Golems established as a baseline enemy type (M02168, CrunchyConniptions) with a soul-harvest origin under [[gideon-azurith|Gideon]] (M02173–M02174, FreshToast); [[locke|Locke]] is noted uneasy around them (M02180, FreshToast). (proto, undated) — the vertical slice ships the golem only as this boss , the Ossuary Sentinel, a self-hardening tomb guardian decoupled from the soul-harvest origin; it inherits the Blacksmith’s adaptive-armor behavior via Adamant Shell. [IMPLEMENTED] — current presentation. Related pages [[proto-enemy-roster|Proto Enemy Roster]] — the five rank-and-file enemies shipped alongside this boss. [[golems|Golems]] — the enemy family and its design-lore origin. [[blacksmith-boss|Blacksmith Boss]] — the design pitch whose adaptive-armor mechanic this boss prototypes. [[encounters-and-ambushes|Encounters Ambushes]] — how enemies (including this boss at n5 ) are deployed across the dungeon graph. Provenance Raw: M01162 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08, adaptive blacksmith); M02168 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16, golems); M02173–M02174, M02180 (FreshToast, 2023-02-16, golem origin Locke’s unease) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 §Gaming Deconstruction; 000 Part 8 (golem origin) Docs: 005 §Boss: Ossuary Sentinel (proto); §Implemented golem boss; §Encounter context (proto); §Proto reconciliation Proto: src/core/combat/content.ts ( tomb_golem , g_slam / g_quake / g_curse / g_harden ); src/core/campaign/content.ts (node n5 ) Ledger: golem-boss / adaptive-blacksmith rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Pirate Monkeys","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/pirate-monkeys.html","text":"Pirate Monkeys CANON Comic-relief enemies who sing their own sea shanty — one of them has an eye patch, a parrot, and the parrot has an eye patch too. Summary — Pirate Monkeys (a.k.a. Monkey Pirates) are a deliberately silly, comic-relief enemy type. Their defining bit is that they “sing their own sea shanty” (immediately amended to a “Land shanty”) (M01019–M01020, 2023-02-05), and one of them has an eye patch, a parrot, and the parrot also has an eye patch (M01157–M01159, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-08). Characteristics Sing their own sea shanty (M01019, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-05) — corrected to a “ Land shanty ” (M01020, FreshToast, 2023-02-05). The source likens the vibe to “that TikTok cat but monkey” (005 §Characteristics; TikTok cat cited as inspiration for the shanties, 005 §Other Media). One of the monkey pirates has an eye patch, a parrot — and the parrot also has an eye patch (M01157–M01159, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-08). Role Enemy type; explicitly a comic-relief enemy (005 §Role). Design history 2023-02-05 — introduced as monkeys that sing their own sea shanty (M01019, CrunchyConniptions), instantly re-dubbed a “Land shanty” (M01020, FreshToast). 2023-02-08 — the eye-patch/parrot gag added: an eye-patched monkey with a parrot that also has an eye patch (M01157–M01159, spirit_noodle). Current presentation. Provenance Raw: M01019–M01020 (CrunchyConniptions FreshToast, 2023-02-05); M01157–M01159 (spirit_noodle, 2023-02-08) Cleaned: 000 (Pirate Monkeys) Docs: 005 §Pirate Monkeys (Monkey Pirates), §Common Enemies, §Other Media Ledger: creature/comic-relief rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Proto Enemy Roster (Implemented)","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/proto-enemy-roster.html","text":"Proto Enemy Roster (Implemented) CANON The runnable truth: five fully-statted rank-and-file enemies shipped in the browser vertical slice, “The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb.” Summary — The playable proto ships a small, fully-statted enemy roster used by the combat prototype. These are five common/elite archetypes — Goblin Imp, Cinder Slime, Skeleton Archer, River Brigand, Hex Imp — plus one boss (the [[ossuary-sentinel|Ossuary Sentinel]], owned on its own page). The numbers are tuned for the slice, not final balance, but they are how these archetypes currently behave. All values: proto: src/core/combat/content.ts ( ENEMY_DEFS , ENEMY_SKILLS ). For enemies, a skill’s targetSide: 'enemy' means it targets a hero ; position 1 = front rank. Common / elite enemies [IMPLEMENTED] Enemy (id) Role HP AP Speed STR / MAG DEF / RES ACC / Crit Start rank Skills (AP · effect) **[[imps Goblin Imp]]** ( goblin_imp ) 👺 Skirmisher 16 2 5 8 / 2 4 / 2 86 / 8 1 **[[slimes Cinder Slime]]** ( slime ) 🟢 Bruiser 26 2 3 7 / 2 8 / 4 88 / 4 2 **[[skeletons Skeleton Archer]]** ( skeleton_archer ) 💀 Marksman 15 2 6 9 / 2 4 / 4 90 / 12 3 **[[brigands River Brigand]]** ( brigand ) 🗡️ Cutthroat 24 2 5 11 / 2 6 / 4 88 / 12 1 **[[imps Hex Imp]]** ( imp_caster ) 🔮 Caster 18 2 5 3 / 12 4 / 8 88 / 8 3 Notes (005 §Common / elite enemies (proto)): The Cinder Slime is the proto’s realization of the [[slimes|slime]] archetype — a stone-cored bruiser whose body shatters the borrowed sword in the prologue (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts node n2a ). “Cinder” ties it to the game’s title rather than any decided lore name. The Hex Imp is a magic-using [[imps|imp]] variant (caster), distinct from the melee Goblin Imp . Neither is the lore-finalized name (Imps/Goblins naming still open). The River Brigand realizes the [[brigands|Brigands/Bandits]] archetype; “River” ties to the Mediterranean/Caribbean river throughline. [[skeletons|Skeletons]] appear as Skeleton Archers (ranged), consistent with “mandatory for dungeons”; they fight in the Bone Gallery and the ambush narrows (proto: content.ts nodes n2b , n4 ). Boss The slice’s single boss — the tomb-golem [[ossuary-sentinel|Ossuary Sentinel]] ( tomb_golem , 🗿) — has its own page (best-a). It realizes the adaptive-armor design once pitched as the Blacksmith; see also [[golems]]. Encounter deployment [IMPLEMENTED] Enemies are placed across the dungeon graph (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts): The Crumbling Stair ( n1 , fight): 2× Goblin Imp + Cinder Slime. Ambush in the Narrows ( n2b , elite, ambush: true ): River Brigand + Skeleton Archer + Hex Imp. The ambush flag scatters the party’s formation at battle start (proto: src/core/campaign/run.ts — scatterFormation ) — see [[encounters-and-ambushes]]. The Bone Gallery ( n4 , fight): Cinder Slime + 2× Skeleton Archer. The Ossuary Sentinel ( n5 , boss): the tomb golem alone. Repeated archetypes get unique instance ids and are placed front-to-back (positions capped at rank 3) (proto: src/core/campaign/run.ts — buildEncounter ). Related systems The behavior of this roster is shaped by systems owned by other batches: enemies telegraph via the proto’s [[enemy-intent|6 readable intent categories]]; they draw statuses from the shared status bank; and they get deadlier under [[escalation|Escalation]] (attackers gain an accuracy/crit “edge” the longer a fight runs), with the Wounded amplifier (×1.25 taken below 50% HP) and a one-per-fight shield blunting big hits. See the systems namespace for those. Design history 2023-02-16 — the brainstorm “versus” test roster listed Goblins, [[slimes|Slimes]], [[golems|Golems]], [[brigands|Brigands]] as enemies (M02168, CrunchyConniptions). The implemented roster differs: it uses Goblin Imp + Hex Imp for the imp/goblin archetype, Cinder Slime, Skeleton Archer, River Brigand, and puts the golem only as the boss. (proto, undated) — the five-enemy roster + golem boss shipped in the vertical slice. [IMPLEMENTED] (005 §Design Decisions Finalized: “a 6-enemy roster… + a golem boss”). Provenance Raw: M02168 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16, test roster) Cleaned: 000 (test setup) Docs: 005 §Implemented Proto Bestiary, §Common / elite enemies (proto), §Encounter context (proto), §Design Decisions Finalized Proto: src/core/combat/content.ts ( ENEMY_DEFS , ENEMY_SKILLS ); src/core/campaign/content.ts; src/core/campaign/run.ts Ledger: enemy/test-roster rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Skeletons","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/skeletons.html","text":"Skeletons CANON The mandatory dungeon-crawler skeletons — because “Dungeons and no skeletons? A travesty.” Summary — Skeletons are a deliberately classic dungeon-crawler enemy, treated as mandatory for dungeons (005 §Status). The team’s affection for the trope is on the record: “I’d be surprised if there were no skeletons. Like come on” and “Dungeons and no skeletons? A travesty” (M05373–M05376, FreshToast, 2024-03-12). They anchor a desired skeleton tomb area, and appear in the proto as ranged Skeleton Archers . Description role Classic dungeon-crawler trope, embraced wholesale: “Maybe we need [a] funny skeleton” (M05365, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-03-12); “Do I like skeletons. Yes i do” (M05371, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-03-12). Mandatory for dungeons — recorded as a finalized decision (005 §Status; §Design Decisions Finalized). Location Tied to a desired skeleton tomb area: “we need skeleton tomb… I dunno” (M05368, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-03-12). Whether to include a dedicated skeleton-tomb area is listed as unresolved, “though skeletons are now mandatory” (005 §Unresolved Questions). See [[encounters-and-ambushes]] for enemy-type-by-area. Proto realization: Skeleton Archer [IMPLEMENTED] The vertical slice ships skeletons as Skeleton Archers ( skeleton_archer , 💀) — a ranged marksman with Arrow and Bone Shot (any-rank shots); full stats in [[proto-enemy-roster]] (proto: src/core/combat/content.ts — ENEMY_DEFS ). This is consistent with the “mandatory for dungeons” decision; they fight in the Bone Gallery and the ambush narrows (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts nodes n2b , n4 ) (005 §Common / elite enemies (proto), Notes). Design history 2024-03-12 — the whole decision in one exchange: CrunchyConniptions floats a “funny skeleton” and a “skeleton tomb” (M05365–M05368, M05371); FreshToast declares dungeons without skeletons “A travesty” (M05373–M05376). Recorded thereafter as mandatory-for-dungeons. Current lore. (proto, undated) — realized as ranged Skeleton Archers in the Bone Gallery and ambush encounters. [IMPLEMENTED] Provenance Raw: M05365, M05368, M05371 (CrunchyConniptions, 2024-03-12); M05373–M05376 (FreshToast, 2024-03-12) Cleaned: 000 (Skeletons) Docs: 005 §Skeletons, §Common Enemies, §Common / elite enemies (proto), §Design Decisions Finalized, §Unresolved Questions Ledger: creature/dungeon rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Slimes","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/slimes.html","text":"Slimes CANON Jelly-like enemies that can develop legs and partially control their own shape, mostly settling into natural, four-legged forms. Summary — Slimes are a classic common enemy given a twist: they are amorphous but tend to “follow natural forms,” with some control over their shape (M05681–M05682, FreshToast, 2024-09-01). Most are four-legged with tails; occasional “weirdos” are six-legged or serpentine (M05683, FreshToast, 2024-09-01). How many distinct slime types to include is still open. The playable proto ships one, the Cinder Slime , as a stone-cored bruiser. Description characteristics “Jelly-like slimes can develop legs ‘n’ shit” (M05679, FreshToast, 2024-09-01). Have “some control over their shape” (M05682, FreshToast, 2024-09-01) — one floated idea was that they act “like chameleons, but instead of colour its shape they change to hide” (M05680, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-09-01). Tend to follow natural forms: “Lots of four legged lil guys. Some have tails,” with occasional “weirdos who have 6 legs or are serpentine” (M05681, M05683, FreshToast, 2024-09-01). CANON — recorded as a finalized decision (005 §Design Decisions Finalized). Combat role Listed among the baseline enemy types in the brainstorm test setup (Goblins, Slimes, [[golems|Golems]], [[brigands|Brigands]]) (M02168, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). In the proto, the slime archetype is realized as a mid-durability bruiser (see below). Proto realization: Cinder Slime [IMPLEMENTED] The vertical slice ships one slime — the Cinder Slime ( slime , 🟢), a bruiser with 26 HP that uses Splash and Dissolve (which applies Defense Down); full stats in [[proto-enemy-roster]] (proto: src/core/combat/content.ts — ENEMY_DEFS ). It is described as a “stone-cored bruiser whose body shatters the borrowed sword in the prologue” (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts node n2a ). “Cinder” ties the name to the game’s title rather than to any decided lore name (005 §Common / elite enemies (proto), Notes). Open questions DEBATED How many slime types to include, and the exact variety of forms (005 §Unresolved, §Unresolved Questions). Design history 2024-09-01 — the entire slime concept in one session: FreshToast proposes jelly-slimes that grow legs (M05679); CrunchyConniptions asks whether there’d be many types or a shared shape, floating a shape-shifting “chameleon” idea (M05680); FreshToast lands on slimes following natural forms (mostly four-legged, some tails), with some shape control and occasional six-legged/serpentine oddities (M05681–M05683). This is the current presentation. (proto, undated) — implemented as the Cinder Slime bruiser in the playable slice. [IMPLEMENTED] Provenance Raw: M05679–M05683 (FreshToast CrunchyConniptions, 2024-09-01); M02168 (test-setup listing, 2023-02-16) Cleaned: 000 (Slimes) Docs: 005 §Slimes, §Common Enemies, §Common / elite enemies (proto), §Design Decisions Finalized, §Unresolved Questions Ledger: creature/enemy rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Telekinetic Sword Boss","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/telekinetic-sword-boss.html","text":"Telekinetic Sword Boss DEBATED A slumbering giant at the end of a cave who commands a swarm of magical swords by telekinesis — every blade a trophy from an adventurer who failed to kill him. Summary — The Telekinetic Sword Boss is a formidable enemy “ensconced at the terminus” of a cave, flanked by a multitude of magical swords. With no hands touching them, he uses telekinesis to lift and command the blades, “reminiscent of Gilgamesh from Fate ” (M03733, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-08). The swords are “silent testimonies to the many adventurers who previously sought, unsuccessfully, to vanquish him.” FreshToast liked “the idea of a monster that collects and fights with a swarm of swords” and floated a variant: it could even be a [[daemon-of-want|Daemon]] made of swords in a trench coat pretending to be a person (M03751–M03752, FreshToast, 2023-10-08). Description A formidable boss at the end of a cave, flanked by magical swords (M03733, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-08; 005 §Description). No hands make contact — he uses telekinesis to lift and command a multitude of swords, reminiscent of Gilgamesh from Fate (Part 14). The swords are testimonies to the many adventurers who previously sought and failed to vanquish him. Abilities Each sword possesses unique attributes , inflicting various damage types depending on which weapon is chosen in battle (005 §Abilities). The sheer volume of weapons attests to the countless battles he has won. Key distinction: the boss is telekinetic; the weapons are magical. The original draft conflated the two; this was clarified in a writing critique (005 §Abilities). Lore For eons the boss has been disturbed in slumber by persistent adventurers. The player is the disturbance that is different — “the final ripple in a long, turbulent dream” — who defeats the slumbering giant once and for all (005 §Lore). Open questions DEBATED Daemon-of-swords variant — “Could even be a [[daemon-of-want|Daemon]] that is made of swords, possessing a cloak it resembles a person until you realise it’s just a dozen swords in a trench coat and all of them want to stab you” (M03752, FreshToast, 2023-10-08). The Daemon framing is not finalized. Design history 2023-10-08 — CrunchyConniptions drafts the cave boss: a Gilgamesh-like adversary who telekinetically commands a swarm of magical swords, each blade a trophy of a failed adventurer (M03733–M03734). 2023-10-08 — FreshToast endorses “the idea of a monster that collects and fights with a swarm of swords” (M03751) and proposes the Daemon-made-of-swords / trench-coat variant (M03752) — current presentation, with the Daemon variant left open. Provenance Raw: M03733–M03734, M03751–M03752 (CrunchyConniptions FreshToast, 2023-10-08). Cleaned: 000 Part 14 §Telekinetic Sword Boss. Docs: 005 §Telekinetic Sword Boss. Ledger: covered under the Part 14 boss-brainstorm rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv ."},{"title":"Vaenngrym","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/vaenngrym.html","text":"Vaenngrym CANON The Act 2 climax dragon — a house-sized force of nature, son of Ajax’s dragon, that fills all four battle positions and inflicts fear on the party. Summary — Vaenngrym is the game’s central dragon boss: “much less of a character than a force of nature, a narrative tool and a big scary boss monster” (M04708, FreshToast, 2023-10-25). He is a house-sized, fire-breathing, scale-and-fang monster, the son of [[ajax|Ajax]]'s dragon, and a three-phase boss whose fight is the Act 2 turning point for [[locke|Locke]]. Mechanically he is the only enemy that fills all four positions, opens with his biggest attack, and inflicts a fear effect to weaken the party (M01770, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Whether he should be feathered — and whether that tips him toward a phoenix — was debated but resolved toward “DRAGON but DRAGON ” (M03023, FreshToast, 2023-03-11). Name Origin Vaenngrym is a bastardization of Old Norse / Icelandic words for wings and despair , coined to sound “mythical, fantasy, grand” (M01771–M01772, FreshToast, 2023-02-11; 005 §Name Origin). Lineage Vaenngrym is imagined as the son of Ajax’s dragon , tying him into the Ajax dragon-blood throughline — Ajax ate a dragon, and draconic features run in his descendants (M02686, FreshToast, 2023-02-22; 005 §Lineage). Draconic creatures’ irises are coloured like a sunset; [[lysander|Lysander]] and [[raphael|Raphael]] are described as having “purple-orange-yellow gradient eyes coloured irises like Vaenngrym” (M02904, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). True Nature (dragons as living disease) CANON A “dragon” is not merely a flying lizard but the visible late stage of Dracosis — the process by which a bloodline becomes steadily more draconic. The condition was first framed as a bloodborne parasite named [[drykksar|Drykksar]] (005 §True Nature; 000 Part 10), then reframed in the Part 18 “Final Version” as a virus-like living disease , not a parasite (005 §Dracosis Lore) EVOLVED - gt; Dracosis-as-virus . Over generations a host’s descendants develop tough/metallic hide, fiery breath (or laser / volcanic spit / flammable gas), increased size, and functional wings; current dragons are held to be descendants of dinosaurs taken over by the disease (005 §True Nature). Physical Description Monster the size of a house or bigger; has scales, fangs and flames (005 §Physical Description). Breathes fire; roars “in a fashion that would make lions whimper”; genuinely frightful. Can smash through rock; towers over the party, bears its teeth, sudden and brutal. Combat Role The only enemy in the game that fills all 4 positions (005 §Combat Role). Has 3 phases ; make it huge (M01769, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Escalation-scaling trait: like the most powerful enemies, the dragon has skills that directly increase or scale based on Escalation (005 §Combat Role, Part 8). See [[escalation]]. Spoken threat: “Filthy follower of a false blessing. Give penance for the sins of your forefathers and receive terror for your children.” — hints at the hero’s power (M02276, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Opens with its big attack (a “Sans approach”, after Undertale), putting players on the back foot the entire battle (005 §Mechanics). Pursuit phase: the party is trapped fleeing while the dragon smashes through rock in positions 1 and 2 to reach them; the party takes pot shots and heals before the onslaught continues (M01812, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). It resembles a horror scene of a monster smashing a door down, and mechanically gives breathing room so the fight can be harder before and after. Fear effect: literally inflicts a fear effect on the party to weaken them for the first two turns of battle; “maybe fear decreases initiative” (M01770, FreshToast, 2023-02-11; 005 §Fear Effect). See Open questions — a standalone “Fear” status is not yet in the combat proto (005 §Fear Effect proto note). Chases the party down a volcano. Narrative Role Act 2 climax and a turning point, “especially for [[locke|Locke]]” (M01767–M01768, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). A metaphorical fear to overcome (failure or similar); the party begin to recognize their strengths and Locke is “grounded” with help. Highlights the motivations of [[ajax|Ajax]], [[lysander|Lysander]] and Locke; the den is accessed to face him; characters are terrified and fighting for their lives (005 §Narrative Role). Upon killing Vaenngrym, Locke is given the option to drink the dragon’s blood — no mechanical bearing, but it informs her opinion of Lysander’s pursuit of power and her understanding of Ajax (M02661, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Design References Grigori in Dragon’s Dogma as “the best dragon sequence” (005 §Design References). Sans approach ( Undertale ) for opening with a big attack. A playlist was made for the fight: “You’re probably not surprised how well the lyrics kinda fit a dragon fight.” Open questions DEBATED Feathered or not? Whether Vaenngrym should be feathered — referencing the feathered-dinosaur theory to add majesty/flair and hint at his dinosaur origin, versus keeping him unfeathered to look more menacing. This spun into a phoenix-vs-dragon argument and was resolved toward staying a dragon, but the Discord notes leave the exact visual unresolved (M02986–M03026, 2023-03-11). See Design history. DEBATED Fear mechanic specifics — “Fear feels a little generic” (M01774, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11); whether fear decreases initiative and how it is implemented is open. No “Fear” negative status exists in the combat proto yet (005 §Fear Effect proto note). Design history 2023-02-11 — Core concept laid down in one session: lean into the dragon’s frightful nature as a metaphorical fear to overcome; a good Act 2 climax and turning point for Locke; give it 3 phases , make it huge; literally inflicts a fear effect at the start of battle (M01767–M01770, FreshToast). Crunchy: “Fear feels a little generic” (M01774). 2023-02-11 — Named Vaenngrym , from bastardized Old Norse/Icelandic for wings + despair (M01771–M01772, FreshToast). 2023-02-11 — Pursuit phase added: trapped fleeing while the dragon smashes through rock in positions 1–2 (M01812, FreshToast). 2023-02-16 — Spoken threat drafted: “Filthy follower of a false blessing…” (M02276, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-02-21 — Post-kill dragon’s-blood choice for Locke added (M02661, FreshToast). 2023-02-22 — Lineage established: son of Ajax’s dragon (M02686, FreshToast). 2023-03-05 — Iris colour tie-in: draconic creatures (and Ajax’s descendants) have sunset-coloured irises “like Vaenngrym” (M02904, FreshToast). 2023-03-11 — Feather/phoenix debate. FreshToast proposed feathers because “dinosaurs might have had feathers” and it “hints, subtly, at Vaenngrym being a dinosaur” (M02986, M03025). spirit_noodle argued he’d “look more menacing if he was not feathered” (M02987), then pivoted to wanting “the majesty of a phoenix… different shades of red feathers” (M03001–M03003). FreshToast pushed back: “the look of a phoenix is more than just stapling red feathers onto a lizard” (M03010), and when asked “would it not be cooler to have DRAGON but PHOENIX?” answered “No because DRAGON but DRAGON is cool” (M03022–M03023) — “but that doesn’t mean he should also just straight up be a bird” (M03026). Left unresolved in the notes but leaning dragon. 2023-10-25 — Framing crystallized: “much less of a character than a force of nature, a narrative tool and a big scary boss monster” (M04708, FreshToast) — matches the current presentation. Provenance Raw: M01767–M01774, M01812 (FreshToast/Crunchy, 2023-02-11); M02276 (2023-02-16); M02661 (2023-02-21); M02686 (2023-02-22); M02904 (2023-03-05); M02986–M03026 (2023-03-11); M04708 (2023-10-25). Cleaned: 000 Part 9 §Vaenngrym; 000 Part 10 §Dracosis. Docs: 005 §Dragon - Vaenngrym; 001 §Vaenngrym. Ledger: rows for “Dragon name suggestion: Vaenngrym”, “Vaenngrym is imagined as the son of Ajax’s dragon”, and the sunset-iris row in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv ."},{"title":"Vampires","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/vampires.html","text":"Vampires DEBATED Few, hunted, and stubbornly old-fashioned — “every old vampire should just be a weird boomer who cringes at current day cultural norms.” Summary — Vampires are a rare enemy type: few in number because they are actively hunted and killed, with some loose organization “but nothing so crazy as Vampire the Masquerade” (M05430–M05434, 2024-07-08). Their most vivid characterization is thematic: vampires are “naturally conservative,” and “every old vampire should just be a weird boomer who cringes at current day cultural norms” (M05185, FreshToast, 2024-02-10) — tying into the game’s progressivism-vs-conservatism opposition, where vampirism is the thematic opposite of [[dracosis]] (stagnation vs evolution). Their exact organization and headcount remain open. Description characteristics Few, actively hunted: not many exist because they are “actively hunted and killed” (005 §Characteristics). Some organization, kept simple: “Should the vampires act more like individuals or more like a collective?” (M05430, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-07-08); resolved toward “some organization to them maybe” but “Nothing so crazy as Vampire the Masquerade though” (M05434, FreshToast, 2024-07-08). Naturally conservative: “I think vampires would be naturally conservative. Every old vampire should just be a weird boomer who cringes at current day cultural norms” (M05185, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Thematic role Vampirism is framed as the deliberate opposite of [[dracosis]] : dracosis is about “evolving into a superior being… changing for the better… changing to be strong” (the future), whereas vampirism is stagnation — preserving the present/past (M05173–M05187, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10). Both are “external factors messing with the natural order” (M05199, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10). See [[raphael]] (best-a), the tragic super-dragon-vampire in whom both afflictions blend. Location A dedicated Vampires area is one of the game’s main enemy-type-by-area zones (005 §Enemy Type Areas; see [[encounters-and-ambushes]]). Open questions DEBATED Exact vampire organization structure (individuals vs collective; strong sense of self?) (M05430, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-07-08; 005 §Unresolved). DEBATED How many vampires exist in the world (005 §Unresolved Questions). Design history 2024-02-10 — first characterization: vampires are naturally conservative, old ones are “weird boomer[s]”; established as the thematic opposite of dracosis (stagnation vs evolution) (M05173–M05187, FreshToast CrunchyConniptions). 2024-07-08 — organization questions raised (individuals vs collective, sense of self, scattered or grouped) and partly answered: some loose organization, but “nothing so crazy as Vampire the Masquerade” (M05430–M05434). Exact structure and headcount left open. Current, still-debated state. Provenance Raw: M05173–M05199 (FreshToast CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10); M05430–M05434 (2024-07-08) Cleaned: 000 (Vampires; Vampirism vs Dracosis Thematic Opposition) Docs: 005 §Vampires, §Common Enemies, §Enemy Type Areas, §Unresolved Questions Ledger: vampire/thematic-opposition rows (R28) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Vampirism","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/vampirism.html","text":"Vampirism DEBATED One of the two supernatural conditions that “mess with the natural order” — the conceptual opposite of [[dracosis]] , centred on immortality. Summary — Vampirism is a supernatural affliction developed alongside [[dracosis|Dracosis]] as one of “two [external factors] messing with the natural order of things” (M05199, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10). Where Dracosis is about draconic recklessness, Vampirism is “the opposite of dracosis” and “about immortality” (M05173, M05177, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-10). It is most fully expressed through [[raphael|Raphael]], who embodies both conditions. Nature An external factor that interferes with the natural order — paired with Dracosis as a conceptual dyad (M05187 “Vampirism vs Dracosis”; M05199, 2024-02-10). Thematically tied to immortality and its impulses (M05177). Design flavour: vampires imagined as “naturally conservative… every old vampire should just be a weird boomer who cringes at current-day cultural norms” (M05185, FreshToast, 2024-02-10). Carriers [[raphael|Raphael]] embodies both Vampirism and Dracosis — “he would literally embody them both and it would drive him mad,” but he rises above them (M05192–M05193, 2024-02-10). His Vampirism’s impulses and Dracosis’s recklessness are implied not to impede his already-broken psyche (M05201). Early, undeveloped floating of the idea: “Lyra or Eris is a vampire?” (M01398, FreshToast, 2023-02-10) — never resolved onto those characters. Open questions DEBATED The precise mechanics of Vampirism vs Dracosis and how far each condition drives its host were still being workshopped in the 2024-02-10 session (M05187–M05229). Design history 2023-02-10 — first floated loosely: is Lyra or Eris a vampire? (M01398, FreshToast) — not taken up. 2024-02-10 — developed as a named supernatural condition, defined as the opposite of Dracosis and centred on immortality; attached to [[raphael|Raphael]] as one of two curses he embodies (M05173–M05229, Crunchy/Fresh). Current framing. Provenance Raw: M01398 (2023-02-10); M05173, M05177, M05185, M05187, M05192–M05193, M05199, M05201, M05223, M05229 (2024-02-10; CrunchyConniptions FreshToast) Docs: 002 §Raphael Related: [[dracosis]], [[raphael]]"},{"title":"Chaos Emeralds (Ajax's Seven Relics)","group":"Items","url":"items/chaos-emeralds.html","text":"Chaos Emeralds (Ajax’s Seven Relics) CANON Seven super-rare relics scattered across the world — shards of something Ajax once owned. Collecting them all doesn’t grant power; it makes you powerful, and lets you inherit Ajax’s legacy. Summary — There are seven unique relics scattered in the game, super special and super rare, referred to as the “chaos emeralds” (M01280, FreshToast, 2023-02-09; M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11; ledger row R08). They are pieces of an object owned by [[ajax|Ajax]] that was shattered, related to the spirit he made a pact with (M02420, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). Brought together they do not grant superpowers — instead they give “some kind of revelation” and are the way the player learns about Ajax and his bloodline (M02431–M02432, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). Once all seven are acquired the bearer will “inherit Ajax’s legacy” (M02907, FreshToast, 2023-03-06). Each unique relic has a different interaction with each unique character (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). The name is a Sonic reference and is treated as a working label — hence placeholder_name: true . Description Seven, super-rare — a handful of unique relics scattered in the game, super special/super rare — “7 relics? Like the Chaos emeralds we discussed!” (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). Shards of Ajax’s pact-object — pieces of an object owned by Ajax that was shattered, related to the spirit he made a pact with (M02420, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). Per-character interaction — each unique relic interacts differently with each unique character (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). The first relic is the [[sunderer|Sunderer]] — “First of Ajax’s seven thingies,” the rusty glimmering spear Locke acquires in the tutorial trial (006 §Specific Weapons; proto-implemented as the prologue relic reward). Rules / Effects — what collecting them does Brought together, the relics don’t grant superpowers — they give “some kind of revelation” (M02431, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). They are the way the player learns about Ajax and his bloodline (M02432, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). “Inherit Ajax’s legacy” — the seven relics are informal trials; once all are acquired the bearer inherits Ajax’s legacy (M02907, FreshToast, 2023-03-06). People misinterpret this as acquiring Ajax’s power ; in truth the act of acquiring makes one powerful , and the legacy is a final task to challenge the descendant (006 §Ajax’s Relics). Not garbage — must have purpose — the group explicitly resolved the relics should be actually valuable and sentimental to Ajax, tied to “his whole deal,” not treated as loot filler (M02426–M02429, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-19). May lead to his tomb — floated that the relics lead the player to [[ajaxs-tomb|Ajax’s Tomb]] (M02718, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). The “deliberate dud” DEBATED FreshToast recalled the seven chaos emeralds “were meant to be somewhat of a dud in the end if I recall” (M01425, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). This is the deliberate-dud intent : the emeralds’ payoff is intentionally anticlimactic as a reward — the point is the journey and the revelation about Ajax, not a power spike. This aligns with the story-side motivation chain where Locke gathers them to prove herself / keep them from [[lysander|Lysander]] (see the story doc’s Chaos Emerald Motivations); the emeralds are a plot engine, not an endgame superweapon. Recorded here as a cross-reference to the story-side treatment; the exact nature of the “dud” payoff remains unsettled. Story hooks Motivation chain (story-owned; summarized): Locke meets Lysander → wants to get stronger to support him → overhears Lysander and Eris planning to gather the emeralds to defeat the dragon → decides to gather them herself to prove worthy → finally keeps them away from Lysander. Her motive shifts from hero-pleasing to people-saving — she does not turn selfish/evil (M02419–M02425, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19; ledger row R13). Prologue tie-in — Lysander enters the prologue dungeon looking for a chaos emerald but doesn’t find it (he “misread the clue”); the initial dungeon gains a secret endgame section where the rumored emerald is later found (M02445–M02448, FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2023-02-19). Family history — a relic can inform Locke of family history and key info “like where a chaos emerald is” (M02809, FreshToast, 2023-02-26). Open questions DEBATED The exact nature of the “deliberate dud” payoff when all seven are gathered (M01425, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). placeholder_name — “chaos emeralds” is a Sonic-derived working label; a final in-world name for the relic set was not settled in these notes. Design history 2023-02-09 — first appearance: [[lyra|Lyra]] the Necromancer “has one of the seven chaos emeralds” (M01280, FreshToast) — the seven-relic set exists as a hook. 2023-02-11 — framed as a handful of super-rare unique relics, “7 relics? Like the Chaos emeralds,” each with a different per-character interaction (M01422, spirit_noodle). Same day, FreshToast notes they “were meant to be somewhat of a dud in the end” (M01425). EVOLVED toward a plot device rather than a reward. 2023-02-19 — the design crystallizes: the group insists the relics be valuable and sentimental to Ajax , not garbage (M02426–M02429); defined as shards of Ajax’s pact-object (M02420) that grant a revelation, not superpowers (M02431) and teach the player about Ajax’s bloodline (M02432). Motivation chain and prologue secret-section established the same day (M02419–M02448). supersedes the loose “collect the gems” framing. 2023-02-24 — floated that the relics lead to Ajax’s tomb (M02718, FreshToast). 2023-03-06 — the “inherit Ajax’s legacy” resolution: seven relics are informal trials; the acquiring makes one powerful; the legacy is a final task to challenge the descendant (M02907, FreshToast). current framing. Provenance Raw: M01280 (2023-02-09); M01422, M01425 (2023-02-11); M02417–M02448 (2023-02-19); M02718 (2023-02-24); M02809 (2023-02-26); M02907 (2023-03-06) Cleaned: 000 Part 9 (Chaos Emerald Motivations); Part 5/6 (relics dump) Docs: 006 §Chaos Emeralds, §Ajax’s Relics; 002 §Chaos Emerald Motivations; 004 §7 Relics System Ledger: rows R08, R13, R15 (chaos emerald / relics) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Conflux","group":"Items","url":"items/conflux.html","text":"Conflux CANON [[percival|Percival]]'s made arm — a crafted/prosthetic arm built for the main-game mage, with [[pandora|Pandora]] as the inventor who helps him make it. Summary — Conflux is “his arm” — a made arm belonging to [[percival|Percival]] (Percy), the renamed [[isaac|Isaac]] and main-game party mage. The one canonical mechanical fact about it is its origin: [[pandora|Pandora]] “is also gonna be the person who helps Percival make Conflux (his arm)” (M04343, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Beyond being a made/crafted arm and a piece of Pandora’s inventor résumé, the notes give Conflux little further detail. Description Conflux is Percival’s arm — described consistently in the notes as something made , i.e. a crafted or prosthetic arm rather than his natural limb (M04343, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). It sits within [[pandora|Pandora]]'s wheelhouse of “ergonomic, supportive stuff like prosthetics and machina” (M04346, FreshToast, 2023-10-17) — the same inventor sensibility that produced her cannon [[rainfall|Rainfall]] and automaton [[dianthus|Dianthus]]. Origin / Related characters Maker: [[pandora|Pandora]] helps [[percival|Percival]] make Conflux (M04343, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). This is her explicit “role in Skyward Cinders.” Earlier earmark: the “person who helped Percy make Conflux” existed as a role slot before Pandora filled it. On 2023-08-12, FreshToast earmarked the celebrated “Sha’worr-ma is a contradiction personified…” description for reuse on that person — “Crunchy I like this part and am gonna steal it for the person that helped Percy make Conflux” (M03535, FreshToast, 2023-08-12). See [[sha-worr-ma|Sha’worr-ma]]. The two threads were never explicitly reconciled — Pandora was later named the maker, but whether the Sha’worr-ma description folds into her or a separate helper is left open. Open questions DEBATED Is the Conflux-maker (and its “contradiction personified” description) [[pandora|Pandora]], [[sha-worr-ma|Sha’worr-ma]], or a distinct helper? Pandora was named the maker (M04343) after the Sha’worr-ma earmark (M03535); the reuse target and Pandora were never explicitly reconciled (002 §Sha’worr-ma the Second PROPOSED ). PROPOSED What Conflux actually does — its mechanics, magical function, and appearance — is not stated in the notes. Design history 2023-08-12 — first mention of “Conflux (Percy’s arm)”: FreshToast earmarks the Sha’worr-ma “contradiction personified” paragraph for “the person that helped Percy make Conflux” — “Steal away. I am the flinger, you are the catcher” (M03535, M03538–M03540, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). The arm exists as a fixture; its maker is an open slot. 2023-10-17 — the Conflux-maker is identified: [[pandora|Pandora]] “is also gonna be the person who helps Percival make Conflux (his arm)” (M04343, FreshToast). Fills the slot left open in August. Provenance Raw: M03535, M03538–M03540 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-08-12); M04343 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 lines ~5741–5746 (Sha’worr-ma / Conflux reuse); Pandora block ~5299, ~6839 (“Helps Percival make Conflux (his arm)”) Docs: 002 §Percival (Percy); 002 §Helps Percival make Conflux Ledger: rows R19 (M03526+ Conflux earmark), R23 (M04343 Pandora/Conflux) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Dianthus","group":"Items","url":"items/dianthus.html","text":"Dianthus CANON [[pandora|Pandora]]'s automaton — one of her signature creations, which “eventually attains sentience” and grows close enough to her that “they maybe kiss a lil UwU.” Summary — Dianthus is an automaton built by [[pandora|Pandora]], the inventor of [[the-4-boyes|the 4 Boyes]]. It appears in the roster write-up as one of the “things” she makes — “magic bombs, her cannon [[rainfall|‘Rainfall’]], or the automaton ‘Dianthus’” (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Its distinguishing beat: Dianthus “eventually attains sentience” and its bond with Pandora turns tender — “and they maybe kiss a lil UwU” (M04227). Because of that arc it is a character-adjacent creation, but in the notes it originates and is catalogued as one of Pandora’s inventions. Description An automaton made by [[pandora|Pandora]], named — like all her creations — after a smell she likes (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Part of Pandora’s “ergonomic, supportive stuff like prosthetics and machina” inventor sensibility (M04346, FreshToast, 2023-10-17); a companion-scale creation rather than a weapon like her cannon [[rainfall|Rainfall]]. Sentience relationship Dianthus eventually attains sentience , and its relationship with [[pandora|Pandora]] becomes romantic-adjacent — “and they maybe kiss a lil UwU” (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). This is the note’s phrasing; the “maybe” marks it as a floated idea rather than a locked beat. Because Pandora already has Dianthus as a companion-creation, a separate “magical pet” pitch was routed to another party member instead: FreshToast noted “We have Dianthus already but I think it maybe could be cool if the demi-human got something that’s supposed to be dangerous but is surprisingly loyal and docile” (M04319, FreshToast, 2023-10-17) — see [[titan]] and [[demi-humans]]. Open questions PROPOSED The sentience/romance beat is hedged (“maybe kiss a lil UwU”); whether Dianthus’s arc resolves into a genuine relationship is unsettled (M04227). PROPOSED Dianthus’s form, abilities and combat/utility role are not specified in the notes beyond “automaton.” Design history 2023-10-17 — introduced in the 4 Boyes roster write-up as one of Pandora’s creations — “the automaton ‘Dianthus’” — with the note that it “eventually attains sentience and they maybe kiss a lil UwU” (M04227, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — treated as an established fixture when a new “magical pet” idea comes up: “We have Dianthus already…” so the dangerous-but-docile creature is offered to the demi-human instead (M04319, FreshToast). Confirms Dianthus is already Pandora’s companion-creation. Provenance Raw: M04227 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17, the 4 Boyes roster write-up); M04319 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 lines ~5299–5300 (Pandora creations; “Dianthus eventually attains sentience and they maybe kiss a lil UwU”) and ~6824–6826 (Dianthus automaton) Docs: 002 §Pandora Ledger: Pandora rows (M04227 roster; M04319 magical-pet routing) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Dragon's Blood","group":"Items","url":"items/dragons-blood.html","text":"Dragon’s Blood DEBATED The blood of the slain dragon — offered to Locke as a story choice after the kill, and the source of Ajax’s oldest lie. Summary — Upon killing [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]], [[locke]] is given the opportunity to drink the dragon’s blood (M02661, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). It is framed as a narrative choice , a moment of levity after the fight, that informs Locke’s opinion on [[lysander|Lysander]]'s pursuit of power and her understanding of [[ajax|Ajax]]. Whether it should carry a mechanical effect is unresolved — hence status: DEBATED . Description Dragon’s blood ties directly into Ajax’s mythology: Ajax’s “cover” is that he got his strength from drinking dragon’s blood after wounding one — though this is actually the first instance of Ajax using his power to drain life from the dragon during battle (M02651, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Offering the blood to Locke echoes that legend and lets the player reflect on it. Rules / Effects The choice — after killing Vaenngrym, Locke either: drinks the blood, everybody is grossed out, and nothing happens; or finds solace in her own strength and refuses to partake (M02662, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Consequence — the [[daemon-of-want|Daemon of Want]]'s dialogue changes based on this choice (M02663, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Mechanical effect — DEBATED “Perhaps a mechanical change in the fight as well?” (M02664, FreshToast, 2023-02-21). Open questions DEBATED Whether drinking the blood has any mechanical bearing. The 006 Design Note argues against a purely cosmetic choice: “No mechanical change is extremely unsatisfying when compared to the contrary scenario. No need for this. It’s just confusing and serves nothing” (006 §Design Note) — i.e. either give it real consequences or cut the fake choice. Unsettled. Design history 2023-02-21 — introduced alongside Ajax’s cover story: Ajax pretends his strength came from drinking dragon’s blood, masking his life-drain power (M02651, FreshToast). The same evening it becomes a post-Vaenngrym player choice with no mechanical bearing, purely informing Locke’s view of Lysander and Ajax (M02661–M02662); the Daemon of Want reacts to the choice (M02663); a mechanical effect is floated but left open (M02664). Doc-era note — a later design note pushes back on the “no mechanical change” framing as unsatisfying and pointless, flagging the choice for reconciliation (006 §Design Note). Provenance Raw: M02651 (FreshToast, 2023-02-21); M02661–M02664 (FreshToast, 2023-02-21) Cleaned: 000 Part (Ajax / Vaenngrym encounter) Docs: 006 §Dragon’s Blood, §Design Note Ledger: Ajax / Vaenngrym rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Equipment System (Tier · Quality · Rune)","group":"Items","url":"items/equipment-system.html","text":"Equipment System (Tier · Quality · Rune) CANON The three-axis model for all weapons and armour: Tier (material = base power), Quality (rarity = bonus bandwidth), and Runes (materia-like slots with trade-offs). Summary — Equipment is defined by three orthogonal axes rather than a single “power level.” Tier is the material a piece is made of and sets its base statistics (Iron Steel … Welkinite). Quality — renamed Bronze / Silver / Gold instead of common/rare — does not change base stats but is the item’s bandwidth for extra properties, special effects, and rune slots. Runes are optional materia-like inserts that each carry an upside and a downside, turning gear into an optimization puzzle. The guiding intent is that later gear should expand the player’s repertoire and function differently, not just show bigger numbers (M01433–M01439, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Description The system began as a hard “no numbers go up” stance and evolved, within the same discussion, into the Tier + Quality + Rune model (see Design history). The design philosophy the group settled on: Weapons and armour found in the world are unique and function differently rather than having “higher bigger numbers” (006 §Weapon and Armour Philosophy). Later weapons should expand the player’s repertoire of skills , giving more choice and more interesting decisions (M01448, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11). The precious-metals / alchemy theming (“making lead into gold, but metaphorically ”) is deliberate, tying gear to the game’s growth-through-work theme (M01440, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). The three axes Tier = weapon level — the material ladder; sets base power (M02096, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). See [[weapons]] for the full five-tier ladder, act gating, and drop rules. Quality = weapon rarity — Bronze, Silver, Gold (M01441, FreshToast, 2023-02-11; M02094, 2023-02-16): Quality won’t affect basic statistics but tends to come with additional effects. Example: a plain steel sword is Bronze ; a steel sword with the “light” property (usable by high-finesse characters, with a special weapon skill attached) is a higher quality (M01434, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Quality is the weapon’s bandwidth for bonuses — extra properties, special effects, and (higher quality) more rune slots (M01439, M01437, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Rarity lets developers make loot with neat features drop less frequently and be more desirable — “fun to get new cool rare items” — so long as it isn’t just another name for the illusion of progression a levelling system provides (M01448, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11). Mythic is a 4th quality tier for unique fixed-stat weapons — see [[mythic-weapons]]. Runes — materia-like slots on weapons and armour, each with an upside and a downside. See [[runes]]. Weapon archetypes this enables Because power is expressed as difference rather than magnitude , weapons can occupy niches (M01448, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11): lower overall damage but higher crit; lower accuracy but higher overall damage; terrible single-target damage but great for AoE; cursed weapons — high damage/accuracy/crit but drain the user’s life or another negative effect; blessed weapons — lower overall stats but heal for X; poison weapons. Anti-number-bloat guardrails Enemies do not scale ; later enemies are not given bigger numbers unnecessarily — fights get more complex/tactical instead (M02099, FreshToast, 2023-02-16; M01448, CrunchyConniptions). If a levelling system is ever layered on, it must use linear over exponential scaling to limit number bloat and keep the tactical weight on equipment choice (M02100, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Note the group’s stated order of operations: “We should make our game without leveling, then add leveling later on top of it” (006 §Equipment Progression). Character stats are otherwise static — modification comes from equipment only (M00846, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02). Open questions DEBATED Whether a levelling layer is added at all; if so, effects scale off level while health/base damage rise only a small amount (M02098, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). DEBATED Whether Mythic weapons are a single item or a whole category (006 §Unresolved Questions) — see [[mythic-weapons]]. Various Tier 2/3 material names still unnamed (006 §Unresolved Questions) — see [[weapons]]. Design history 2023-02-11 (Part 6 opening) — original stance: “NO RARITY, TIER OR LEVEL UP SYSTEM WHATSOEVER with respect to characters, weapons, armour or anything else” ; weapons/armour would be unique and function differently rather than have bigger numbers (000 Part 6; the “no rarity” bullet). EVOLVED 2023-02-11 (same session) — supersedes the above. Developed by FreshToast into the Tier (material) + Quality (rarity) + Rune model: Tier = base strength, Quality = bonus bandwidth, Runes = tweaks (M01433–M01439, FreshToast). Quality renamed to Bronze/Silver/Gold (M01441). The “no illusion-of-progress numbers” intent survives — Quality/Runes add properties, not stat bloat — but the literal “no tier/rarity” stance no longer holds (006 §NO Rarity note). 2023-02-16 (Part 8) — finalized: five-tier material ladder, act-based tier gating, drop rules, and the crisp mapping “Tier = weapon level; Quality = weapon rarity” (M02093–M02097, FreshToast). Codified as CANON in 008 §Equipment Progression. Provenance Raw: M01433–M01443, M01448, M01453, M01456 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11); M02090–M02100 (FreshToast, 2023-02-16); M00846 (2023-02-02) Cleaned: 000 Part 6 §Equipment System; Part 8 §Tier/Quality Docs: 006 §Equipment System Philosophy / §NO Rarity note / §Tier System / §Quality System; 008 §Equipment Progression CANON Ledger: rows R08 (M01432–M01443), R12 (M02093–M02097), R05 (static stats) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Jet & Shard System","group":"Items","url":"items/jet-and-shards.html","text":"The Jet Shard System CANON A single reusable item — the Jet — whose effect is reconfigured by slotting Shards. Ships as the party’s primary, customizable heal. Summary — The Jet is a reusable item (the game’s Estus-flask analogue) that starts slotted with the Renewal healing shard but can be re-tuned by swapping Shards into it. Shards come in six families — Anodyne (healing), Shape (physical defense), Dream (magical defense), Balance (trade-offs), Acuity (speed/movement), and Fracture (modifiers) — and two shards of the same [type] are mutually incompatible. This makes the single primary heal a customizable build slot rather than a fixed potion. FreshToast’s own verdict: “This is my first go at it. There’s room for more if you think of anything” ; CrunchyConniptions: “This is gold, I like 👍” (000 §System Notes). How it works Shards are slotted into the reusable Jet to determine its effects (M03685, FreshToast, 2023-09-26). Shards that share a [type] are incompatible with one another — you pick one per family (M03686, FreshToast, 2023-09-26). The Jet comes with the Renewal shard pre-slotted (M03684, FreshToast). The Jet is the primary heal in the game’s healing hierarchy — “precious, versatile and customizable” — sitting above consumables and Locke’s heal skills but below the river flask (006 §Healing System). See the Healing System (systems batch) and [[river-flask|River Flask]] (items-b) for the full hierarchy. Shard roster CANON Full roster, all from a single message (M03684, FreshToast, 2023-09-26): Anodyne — Healing Renewal — instant heal. (Jet ships with this slotted.) Tenacity — heal over time; total health recovered exceeds Renewal. Sanctuary — temporary barrier; functions like temporary bonus health. Shape — Physical Defense Rend — boosts physical defense; brief slash resistance. Puncture — boosts physical defense; brief pierce resistance. Crush — boosts physical defense; brief impact resistance. Dream — Magical Defense Ember — boosts magical defense; brief burn resistance. Frost — boosts magical defense; brief freeze resistance. Spark — boosts magical defense; brief shock resistance. Balance — Trade-offs Ferocity — boosts attack power but reduces defenses until next turn. Serenity — boosts defenses but reduces attack power until next turn. Confidence — guarantee critical hit but reduce accuracy until next turn. Providence — guarantee hit but nullify critical chance until next turn. Acuity — Speed / Movement Zeal — boosts initiative. Rush — move freely until next turn. Fracture — Modifiers Emphasis — reduces charges by 1; other effects are strengthened. Divergence — reduces charges by 1; other effects are weakened but may now affect the entire party. Open questions DEBATED Name collision on “Zeal.” The Acuity shard Zeal shares its name with the favoured (but un-finalized) proposed rename of the AP / Advantage Point resource (Part 11). The AP→“Zeal” rename was never locked and clashes with this shard (Part 14); until resolved the shard keeps “Zeal” and the resource stays “AP” (006 §Acuity Shards note). AP itself is owned by the systems batch. Implementation note (proto) The Jet is realized in the proto as the healing economy’s primary item, though the notes’ shard customization layer is design-intent-facing while the proto pins concrete heal numbers (jets start at 2, cap at 5; each heals 60% of base max HP). Those implemented healing values and the degrading-HP/rest economy are documented under the Healing System (systems batch) and 006 §Implemented Jet/Flask Numbers CANON · IMPLEMENTED ; the [[river-flask|River Flask]] (items-b) is the separate, stronger, hoardable heal. Related [[river-flask|River Flask]] — the separate river heal that sits above the Jet in the hierarchy (items-b). [[equipment-system]] — the Jet/Shard slotting mirrors the runes-on-gear “trade-off build” philosophy. Healing System, degrading-HP ceiling, shields — systems batch. Provenance Raw: M03684, M03685, M03686 (FreshToast, 2023-09-26) Cleaned: 000 Part 15 §Shards (Jet System) Docs: 006 §Jet System (Anodyne / Shape / Dream / Balance / Acuity / Fracture Shards) / §System Notes / §Healing System Ledger: row R20 (M03684–M03686) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Lantern","group":"Items","url":"items/lantern.html","text":"Lantern CANON The light source the player carries into the game’s pitch-dark dungeons — a double-edged tool that reveals the way but also reveals you . Summary — The lantern is given to the player to see in dark places like dungeons, and is carried by [[locke]] in the prologue dungeon (006 §Lantern). Its defining feature is a trade-off: it lets you see, but also lets monsters see you, making it harder to sneak. Some monsters are attracted to the lantern; some may fear it (M02379–M02380, FreshToast, 2023-02-17). Description Dungeons are conceived as completely dark, with vision restricted to how far your light reaches; different dungeons can have different levels of darkness (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). The lantern is the player’s answer to that darkness. In the earliest phrasing it was a “torch” (M01422); by the dedicated design pass it is a lantern given to the player for dark places (M02377, FreshToast, 2023-02-17). It appears in the prologue script: “You and Lysander are in a dark cave and a lantern is carried by Locke” (M02442, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19). Rules / Effects Vision vs. visibility trade-off — the lantern lets you see but also lets monsters see you, making it harder to sneak (M02378, FreshToast, 2023-02-17). Monster reactions — some monsters are attracted to the lantern (M02379); some monsters may FEAR the lantern (M02380, FreshToast, 2023-02-17). Related systems Rest points / camps reset the dungeon Dark-Souls-style; while in a dungeon the main camp occupies the start (ledger row R13, based on M02377–M02381). Sneaking and roaming-creature interaction were pitched as things “different tools you can use” or level design should facilitate, rather than a first-strike bonus (M01429, FreshToast, 2023-02-11) — the lantern is one such tool. [[machina]] — a hovering automaton later proposed as Locke’s portable light, a more characterful alternative to a plain lantern (M03517–M03518, FreshToast, 2023-07-17). Design history 2023-02-11 — dungeons are dark with vision restricted to torch range; different dungeons can have different darkness levels (M01422, spirit_noodle). Early framing calls it a “torch.” 2023-02-17 — the dedicated pass names it a lantern : given to the player for dark places; the see/be-seen trade-off; monsters attracted to it or fearing it (M02377–M02380, FreshToast). supersedes the informal “torch.” 2023-02-19 — appears in the prologue script, carried by Locke in a dark cave with Lysander (M02442, CrunchyConniptions). 2023-07-17 — [[machina]] proposed as Locke’s portable light — a dragonfly-like automaton — an in-world flavor alternative to carrying a lantern (M03517–M03518, FreshToast). The lantern remains the baseline system; the machina is the characterful light source. Both are recorded as CANON in 006. Provenance Raw: M02377–M02381 (FreshToast, 2023-02-17); M01422 (spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11); M02442 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 (Lantern / Exploration) Docs: 006 §Lantern Ledger: row R13 ( item , “Lantern system…”), R13 ( character , prologue script) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Machina","group":"Items","url":"items/machina.html","text":"Machina CANON A tiny hovering automaton — Locke’s dragonfly-like portable light, its wings woven from freeform shimmer. Summary — A machina is a type of automaton. This particular little machina has a tiny core that programs it to hover close to its owner, and its wings are freeform shimmer it uses to flit around like a dragonfly (M03518, FreshToast, 2023-07-17). It serves as [[locke]]'s portable light — a characterful alternative to a plain [[lantern]] (M03517, FreshToast, 2023-07-17). Description Automaton type — “machina” is the name for a class of automaton in the setting (M03518, FreshToast, 2023-07-17). The core — this particular machina is little and carries a tiny core that programs it to hover close to its owner (M03518). Shimmer wings — its wings are freeform shimmer, which it uses to flit around like a dragonfly (M03518). This links it visually to the [[shimmer-sails]] concept, which uses the same “freeform shimmer” material. Rules / Effects Serves as Locke’s portable light (M03517, FreshToast, 2023-07-17) — filling the same role as the [[lantern]] but as a living-seeming companion object rather than a held tool. Related systems [[lantern]] — the baseline dark-dungeon light system; the machina is the in-world, character-flavored version of it for Locke (cross-linked in 006 §Machina). [[shimmer-sails]] — shares the “freeform shimmer” wing material (M03516–M03518, FreshToast, 2023-07-17). Later, the automaton/machina motif ties into character design: [[pandora|Pandora]] is written as being especially into ergonomic, supportive craft “like prosthetics and machina” (M04346, FreshToast, 2023-10-17; ledger row R23). Design history 2023-07-17 — FreshToast pitches the visual reference for Locke’s portable light: “this is the closest to what I’d like Locke’s portable light to be” (M03517), then defines it — a little machina with a tiny core that hovers close to its owner, freeform-shimmer dragonfly wings (M03518). No later revision found; the concept is recorded as CANON in 006. Provenance Raw: M03517–M03518 (FreshToast, 2023-07-17); related M04344/M04356 (2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 (lines ~5530–5534, ~5862–5864) — Machina (Automata) / Locke’s portable light Docs: 006 §Machina; 001 §Machina (Automata) Ledger: row R19 ( world , “Machina: a type of automaton…”) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Mythic Weapons","group":"Items","url":"items/mythic-weapons.html","text":"Mythic Weapons DEBATED A 4th quality tier: unique, fixed-stat weapons that sit outside the Tier/Quality systems — “overclocked” for a while, then intentionally outclassed. Summary — Mythic is a fourth [[equipment-system|Quality]] tier reserved for unique weapons with fixed statistics that exist outside the normal Tier and Quality systems. This lets the designers overclock a weapon a little and place it anywhere in the game (not just the endgame): a Mythic can appear as an Act 1 reward and then be outclassed by ordinary Act 2 finds, so the player gets a cool signature weapon for a stretch without breaking the power curve. The name “Mythic” was chosen over “legendary” — “a bit of a mouthful” (M01443, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Rules / Effects Fixed statistics, unique — Mythic weapons have set stats and exist outside the Tier and Quality ladders, letting developers overclock them but scatter them throughout the game rather than only at the end (M01442, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). A 4th rarity — an “overclocked rarity but similar tier”: decked out with rune slots and properties or a special effect, and maybe higher-than-normal stats (M01485–M01486, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Time-limited relevance — strong only for when you acquire it, so it can be outclassed later. Example: a unique Mythic appears as an Act 1 reward but is later outclassed by Act 2 finds (M01456, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Locke is noted as suited to the idea / it being a Mythic geared toward her (006 §Characteristics). Named Mythic — Ajax’s Broken Sword Hilt A Mythic weapon that appears to be just a broken sword hilt but is actually a magical catalyst that functions like a sword through wind or magical force (M02720, FreshToast, 2023-02-24). It fits the game’s alchemy/wind-magic flavour and ties into the Ajax mythology; not to be confused with the separate spear relic [[sunderer|Sunderer]]. Related [[equipment-system]] — Mythic is the 4th tier of the Quality axis. [[weapons]] · [[runes]] [[sunderer|Sunderer]] · [[warpath|Warpath]] — other unique/relic weapons. Open questions DEBATED Whether Mythic weapons are a single weapon or a whole category — the one-suited-to-Locke version vs. a full category was debated and never resolved (006 §Unresolved Questions; ledger R09). DEBATED Whether Mythics have higher-than-normal stats or only extra rune slots/properties (“maybe higher than normal stats”) (M01486, FreshToast). Design history 2023-02-11 — proposed: unique fixed-stat weapons outside Tier/Quality, dubbed Mythic (over “legendary”); an “overclocked rarity but similar tier,” strong on acquisition but outclassable later; single-vs-category left open (M01442–M01443, M01453, M01456, M01485–M01486, FreshToast). The single-vs-category question was never resolved in chat — hence status DEBATED . 2023-02-24 — first concrete Mythic named: Ajax’s Broken Sword Hilt , a disguised magical catalyst (M02720, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M01442, M01443, M01453, M01456, M01485, M01486 (FreshToast, 2023-02-11); M02095 (2023-02-16); M02720 (FreshToast, 2023-02-24) Cleaned: 000 Part 6 §Mythic Weapons; Part 9 (broken sword hilt) Docs: 006 §Mythic Weapons (4th Quality Tier) / §Mythic Weapon - Ajax’s Broken Sword Hilt; 008 §Quality Ledger: rows R09 (M01485–M01491), R12 (M02094–M02095), R15 (M02720) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Rainfall","group":"Items","url":"items/rainfall.html","text":"Rainfall CANON [[pandora|Pandora]]'s cannon — a magical outlet that lets her use her immense-but-uncontrollable capacity safely, “kinda like a prosthetic for a disability.” Summary — Rainfall is a cannon built by [[pandora|Pandora]], the inventor of [[the-4-boyes|the 4 Boyes]]. It is one of the “things” she makes — “magic bombs, her cannon ‘Rainfall’, or the automaton [[dianthus|‘Dianthus’]]” (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Its narrative purpose is tied to Pandora’s core quirk: she has immense Flow capacity but channels it inefficiently, tiring fast and casting slow. Rainfall gives her “a highly controlled magical outlet… a safe way to use her capacity” (M04350–M04351, FreshToast, 2023-10-17) — FreshToast’s own gloss: “kinda like a prosthetic for a disability” (M04352). Description A cannon — one of [[pandora|Pandora]]'s signature creations, alongside magic bombs and her automaton [[dianthus|Dianthus]] (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Named, like all Pandora’s creations, after a smell she likes — she “names the stuff she makes after smells she likes” (M04227). Rules / Effects Controlled magical outlet — Rainfall gives Pandora “a highly controlled magical outlet… a safe way to use her capacity” (M04350–M04351, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). It is her answer to a genuine weakness rather than raw firepower. Function framing — FreshToast recasts it as “kinda like a prosthetic for a disability” (M04352, FreshToast, 2023-10-17): it compensates for her lack of control, letting her deploy big magic without the fatigue and slow-cast penalties of channeling the Flow directly. Related Answers [[pandora|Pandora]]'s quirk: immense Flow capacity but inefficient channeling — she “gets tired fast and casts slow,” a danger to herself and everyone around her; Rainfall is “sorta that” solution (M04227, M04347–M04350, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Sits beside her other inventions — the automaton [[dianthus|Dianthus]] and, in Skyward Cinders, [[percival|Percival]]'s made arm [[conflux|Conflux]] — as part of her “ergonomic, supportive” inventor sensibility (“prosthetics and machina”, M04346). Design history 2023-10-17 — introduced in the 4 Boyes roster write-up as one of Pandora’s creations: “magic bombs, her cannon ‘Rainfall’, or the automaton ‘Dianthus’,” named after smells she likes (M04227, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — purpose clarified while settling Pandora’s joining motive: with her lack of control and ease of fatigue, “Rainfall is sorta that. It gives her a highly controlled magical outlet… a safe way to use her capacity… (kinda like a prosthetic for a disability, I’m now realising)” (M04350–M04352, FreshToast). Refines the outlet into a named coping mechanic for her quirk. Provenance Raw: M04227 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17, the 4 Boyes roster write-up); M04350–M04352 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 lines ~5299 (Pandora creations) and ~6835–6837 (Rainfall cannon) Docs: 002 §Pandora (incl. §Pandora: Joining Motive / Backstory) Ledger: Pandora rows (joining motive; M04350 Rainfall outlet) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"River Flask","group":"Items","url":"items/river-flask.html","text":"River Flask CANON · IMPLEMENTED The strongest, most precious heal — a flask that drinks the river’s power and carries it into the deep dungeons, kept separate from your everyday healing. Summary — The river flask is a flask that can channel the river’s power, giving a precious effect that lets the party go longer without resting (M04743, FreshToast, 2023-10-30). It is deliberately kept as a separate flask from the ordinary healing [[jet-and-shards|jet]] (M04803, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-30), works like the Estus/Embers relationship, and can be hoarded and used freely for important battles rather than being a per-rest limited resource (006 §River Healing Mechanic). In the notes it is ranked the strongest single heal — and one of the very limited ways to heal past the degrading-HP limit between rests (006 §Healing System). Description Any fresh water source will refill the flask, but the charge drawn from the river is especially good : the flask “gets the properties from the river” and retains them for a while (M03959–M03963, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). The river charge decays over time — “it needs recharging like every few months or something” — so over the course of a run the player will want to head back to the river, at a cadence that depends on their skill (M03949, FreshToast; M03964–M03966, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-13). No hard timer is imposed; a timer “would be annoying, like if you’re trying to explore” (M03968–M03970, 2023-10-13). The mountain-provenance mystery The river’s healing power is written as a lil mystery : it “seems to stem from some mountain feature and is similarly reflected in Fiore’s river” (M03950–M03951, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). The flask is tied to that mystery, and may gain a buff when the mystery is solved (M04793–M04795, FreshToast, 2023-10-30; 006 §River Healing Mechanic). DEBATED exactly when in the story the mystery is solved and the buff received (006 §Unresolved Questions). Rules / Effects Precious, party-affecting heal — the design goal is a heal that lets you go farther without resting; floated as a possible full-party heal that ignores the normal heal limit (M04743–M04745, FreshToast, 2023-10-30). Refill — any fresh water source refills it; the river charge is superior and retained for a while (M03959–M03963, 2023-10-13). Hoardable, not per-rest limited — “not a limited resource, can use freely whenever they intend”; players are meant to hoard it for important battles (006 §River Healing Mechanic). Separate from the jet — kept as its own flask so the two heals “work slightly different — more immersive” (M04803, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-30; 006 §River Healing Mechanic). Implemented values (proto) [IMPLEMENTED] The vertical slice ships the flask with concrete numbers (006 §Implemented Jet/Flask Numbers; proto src/core/campaign/run.ts ): Starts at 1 charge ( flask: 1 ). Heals 35% of base max HP to the entire party ( FLASK_HEAL_RATIO = 0.35 , useFlask ). Its strength in code is being party-wide and hoardable , not per-target size (a single [[jet-and-shards|jet]] heals more per target at 60%). Open questions DEBATED “Heals past the limit.” The notes’ intent is that the river heal is one of very limited ways to heal past the degrading-HP limit between rests (006 §Healing System). The implemented flask does not yet do this — useFlask clamps every character to their current ceiling exactly like the jet (proto run.ts ). As coded, only resting lifts the ceiling. Flagged for reconciliation (006 §River Healing Mechanic). DEBATED When the river mystery is solved and the buff is received (006 §Unresolved Questions). DEBATED “Strongest heal” framing — notes rank the river heal strongest, but per-target the proto jet heals more; unreconciled (006 §Implemented Jet/Flask Numbers). Design history The mechanic was hammered out live across two sessions; latest version is the “separate flask, hoardable, tied to the river mystery” model above. 2023-10-13 — FreshToast’s compromise: in the first game people get a healing item from the river; it needs recharging “like every few months or something”; its power stems from a mountain feature reflected in Fiore’s river — a lil mystery (M03945–M03951, FreshToast). Any fresh water refills it, but the river charge is best and is retained a while (M03959–M03963). 2023-10-30 — FreshToast’s proposal: “you get some kinda flask that can channel the river’s power” providing a precious effect that lets you go longer without resting; weaker players make more regular river visits; could be a full-party heal that ignores the heal limit (M04743–M04745, FreshToast). This is the current core concept. 2023-10-30 — Crunchy’s competing proposal (superseded): the river would just give a flat 2 extra flasks (M04761, CrunchyConniptions). FreshToast pushed back: “I don’t really like the idea of bloating the flask number to achieve that purpose but the idea is sound enough” (M04765). superseded by the combined solution. 2023-10-30 — Combined solution: have the normal healing item improve over time, and have a separate river item heal a fixed amount from the start — keep it separate, “all upside,” tied to the mystery of the river (M04767, FreshToast; M04803, CrunchyConniptions). Confirmed as: river healing is a separate flask from main healing, “works like Estus Embers” (006 §Critical Design Decisions). This is the current model. 2026-06-16 — Proto reconciliation: implemented at 1 starting charge, 35% party-wide heal, currently ceiling-clamped (does not yet pierce the limit) (006 §Implemented Jet/Flask Numbers). Provenance Raw: M03945–M03974 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13); M04738–M04803 (2023-10-30) Cleaned: 000 Part (Game Mechanics — River Healing / Combined Solution) Docs: 006 §River Healing Mechanic, §Healing System, §Implemented Jet/Flask Numbers; 003 §River flask; 007; 008 §Healing Health; 009 (two-resource impl) Ledger: rows R25 (river flask mechanic), R26 (river buff tied to mystery), R21 (river-distance loop) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Rune System","group":"Items","url":"items/runes.html","text":"Rune System CANON Materia-like slots on weapons and armour; every rune carries an upside and a downside, so building is optimization, not best-in-slot. Summary — Runes are optional inserts that slot into weapon and armour rune slots (like materia). The defining rule is that all runes have both an upside and a downside , so choosing runes is a matter of what to sacrifice rather than simply slotting the strongest option. Because a base weapon with no rune is still fine (if sub-optimal), runes stay optional and add build depth that grows as the player finds more of them (M01435–M01437, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Rules / Effects Slots — weapons and armour can carry rune slots (M01435, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). This should all apply to armour as well (M01438, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Salvage — if a weapon drops with a rune already slotted but the weapon itself is bad, the player can dismantle that weapon for its rune (M01435, FreshToast). Two-sided by design — every rune has an upside and a downside, making it “a matter of optimisation and not just slotting the best runes” — a choice of what to sacrifice (M01435–M01436, FreshToast). Optional — since a downside-free base weapon still works, runes are optional; they let the player tweak a weapon to their preferences (M01436, M01439, FreshToast). In FreshToast’s words: “It also makes runes kinda optional since a base weapon with no downside will still be fine, if sub-optimal” (M01436, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Slot count scales — higher [[equipment-system|Quality]] and Tier weapons might have more rune slots (M01437, FreshToast). Described as a material-like system with upsides and downsides that adds a fun layer of complexity as you find more and build your own kit (006 §Rune System). Rune examples Illustrative runes, each showing the upside/downside pattern (M01435, FreshToast, 2023-02-11): Chance to poison on hit, but reduces base damage . Increases base damage, but reduces crit chance . Increases base damage, but makes the wielder take more damage in return . Related [[equipment-system]] — Runes are the third axis of the Tier/Quality/Rune model. [[weapons]] · [[armor-system]] — both carry rune slots. Provenance Raw: M01435, M01436, M01437, M01438, M01439 (FreshToast, 2023-02-11) Cleaned: 000 Part 6 §Rune System Docs: 006 §Rune System (Core Mechanics + Rune Examples); 008 §Equipment Progression (Tier+Quality+Rune) Ledger: row R08 (M01432–M01443) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Shimmer Sails","group":"Items","url":"items/shimmer-sails.html","text":"Shimmer Sails CANON Energy-fin sails made of freeform shimmer — glowing orange like a bubble. Summary — Shimmer sails are an energy-fin sail concept. FreshToast anchored them to a visual reference and described the intended look: “If the orange energy was like a bubble it would fit how I imagine shimmer sails to be” (M03516, FreshToast, 2023-07-17). Like the [[machina]]'s wings, the sails are made of freeform shimmer. Description Energy-fin sail — the core concept is a sail formed from energy rather than cloth (M03516, FreshToast, 2023-07-17; 006 §Shimmer Sails). Orange, bubble-like energy — the intended appearance is orange energy “like a bubble” (M03516). Freeform shimmer — the sails are made of freeform shimmer, the same material as the machina’s dragonfly wings (006 §Shimmer Sails; cf. M03518 for the shared “freeform shimmer”). Related systems [[machina]] — shares the “freeform shimmer” material; both were pitched in the same 2023-07-17 visual-reference pass (M03516–M03518, FreshToast). Design history 2023-07-17 — FreshToast introduces the energy-fin sail as the reference for shimmer sails, specifying orange bubble-like energy (M03516). Recorded as CANON in 006 with no later revision found. Provenance Raw: M03516 (FreshToast, 2023-07-17); related M03517–M03518 (freeform shimmer) Cleaned: 000 (lines ~5535–5538, ~5862) — Shimmer Sails Docs: 006 §Shimmer Sails; 001 §Shimmer Sails Ledger: row R19 ( world , “Visual reference: an energy-fin sail…”) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Sunderer","group":"Items","url":"items/sunderer.html","text":"The Sunderer CANON · IMPLEMENTED A rusty spear with a GLIMMER — the first of Ajax’s seven relics, and the explicit symbol of [[locke|Locke]]'s claim to Ajax’s legacy. Summary — The Sunderer is a rusty spear, marked by a distinctive glimmer, that Locke stumbles into and claims during the prologue/tutorial dungeon. It is the first of Ajax’s seven relics and, thematically, the load-bearing prop of Locke’s arc: where the sword [[lysander|Lysander]] gave her shatters against a golem, the spear she finds and takes for herself endures — making the Sunderer a symbol of her independence and her status as heir to Ajax’s role. It is realized in the proto vertical slice as a permanent relic -kind treasure reward. Description A rusty spear with a GLIMMER — the glimmer marks it as more than scrap (006 §Specific Weapons). The first of “Ajax’s seven thingies” (relics), acquired in the trial Locke passes in the tutorial (M03670, FreshToast, 2023-09-10). See Ajax’s Relics / [[ajaxs-tomb|Ajax’s Tomb]] (locations batch) — the trial’s end is the secret entrance to the tomb, which Lysander and Eris mistake for a dead end. Locke stumbles into and acquires the Sunderer during her separation from Lysander and Eris in the tutorial, then arms herself with it and reunites with them for the next battle (006 §Specific Weapons). Story role symbolism Explicit symbol of Locke’s status as heir to the role of Ajax (M05558, FreshToast, 2024-08-24). Independence from Lysander — the sword Lysander gave Locke SHATTERS against the golem’s stone body (“the power he gave her breaks”); the Sunderer is power she sought for herself and developed , which is why the spear represents her independence (006 §Specific Weapons; M05564, FreshToast, 2024-08-24). Locke chooses to do her own thing, though the notes concede the spear doesn’t strictly enable that — “a little murky, if convenient” (M05564, FreshToast). Lysander notices it — a “somewhat important” beat where Lysander comments on the spear or tries and fails to pick it up (mjolnir/excalibur style), dismissing it as worthless/a waste of time while it turns out to be Locke’s best find (M05526–M05544, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2024-08-24). Meant to read as a footnote that becomes the real prize. Implementation (proto) [IMPLEMENTED] The Sunderer ships in the proto prologue dungeon as a relic -kind treasure reward at the “Glimmer in the Rubble” node — “Ajax’s spear — the first relic. You are armed in earnest now.” It is found after the borrowed sword shatters on the slime’s stone-cored body, so the two-weapon thesis is coded into the dungeon graph itself. Locke’s in-combat spear kit (Pierce / Sunder / Phalanx / Emberlance) is the Pierce family; Sunder applies Defense Down on hit, matching the spear’s guard-breaking flavour (proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts node n2a ; run.ts applyReward case 'relic' ; src/core/combat/content.ts HERO_SKILLS — per 006 §Specific Weapons). Companion prop — Lysander’s backup sword. The simple sword Lysander gives Locke in the tutorial is symbolic of “power he gives her”; it shatters against the golem’s stone body (“the power he gave her breaks”), setting up the Sunderer’s meaning (006 §Lysander’s Backup Sword). Not a standalone item — it exists to be broken. Related [[locke|Locke]] — wielder; [[warpath|Warpath]] — the parallel relic weapon (Flynt/Steele’s). Ajax’s Relics / Chaos Emeralds and [[ajaxs-tomb|Ajax’s Tomb]] — the seven-relics framework (locations/items-b batches). [[weapons]] — spear type and skills. Design history 2023-09-10 — introduced: Sunderer is the first of Ajax’s seven relics, acquired in Locke’s tutorial trial; the trial’s end is the secret entrance to Ajax’s tomb (M03670, FreshToast). 2024-08-24 — deepened into a symbol: heir-to-Ajax status and independence-from-Lysander meaning locked in; the “Lysander notices / can’t lift it / calls it a waste” beat added (M05526–M05564, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). Proto — implemented as the n2a relic reward, gated behind the sword-shatter beat (006 §Specific Weapons [IMPLEMENTED]). Provenance Raw: M03670 (FreshToast, 2023-09-10); M05522, M05526–M05544, M05558–M05564 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions/spirit_noodle, 2024-08-24) Cleaned: 000 Part 15 §Ajax’s Tomb + Sunderer; §Lysander Noticing Sunderer Docs: 006 §Specific Weapons (Sunderer, CANON · IMPLEMENTED ) / §Lysander’s Backup Sword; 004 §Lysander Noticing Sunderer; 003 §Ajax’s Tomb / Hidden Connection; 002 (heir symbolism) Ledger: rows R20 (M03670), R30 (M05522–M05564), R18 (Flynt Steele cross-ref) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Transcend (Cyrus's Staff)","group":"Items","url":"items/transcend.html","text":"Transcend CANON The peachwood staff wielded by [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]], made from a branch he played with as a child. Summary — Transcend is the signature weapon of [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus Sunworth]]: a peachwood staff he carries and wields, made from a branch he played with as a child (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). The sentimental origin — a childhood branch — ties the weapon to his character. Description A staff of peachwood (M04227). Fashioned from a branch Cyrus played with as a child , giving it personal, sentimental significance (M04227). Wielder [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus Sunworth]] — protagonist of the Caelian Odyssey / Luminarium iteration. Provenance Raw: M04227 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17) Docs: 002 §Cyrus Sunworth Related: [[cyrus-sunworth]]"},{"title":"Warpath","group":"Items","url":"items/warpath.html","text":"Warpath CANON Flynt’s massive greatsword — a Dragonslayer-sized blade that Steele inherits, broken, and carries as a relic that can briefly reforge itself in energy. Summary — Warpath is [[flynt|Flynt]]‘s enormous sword, explicitly modelled on Guts’ Dragonslayer from Berserk . After Flynt dies partway through the game, his adoptive sibling [[steele|Steele]] picks it up — but from then on Warpath is broken , and becomes a relic that can only temporarily retake its complete form, “like energy or something.” The broken-blade-that-flickers-back-to-whole is a visual and emotional motif tied to Steele’s grief and decline. Description Flynt’s weapon — a massive sword “like Guts’ Dragonslayer” (Berserk reference) (M04661, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). Passes to Steele — Steele picks up Warpath after Flynt dies (M04661, FreshToast). Broken thereafter — from that point Warpath is broken and becomes a relic (006 §Specific Weapons). Temporary reforging — the relic can temporarily retake its complete form , but as energy rather than solid steel — “like energy or something” (M04661; M04670, FreshToast, 2023-10-24). Story role Warpath tracks the Flynt→Steele tragedy: a whole weapon while its owner lives, then a broken relic carried by the grieving survivor, briefly flaring back to wholeness. It parallels [[sunderer|the Sunderer]] and Ajax’s other relics as a “broken weapon that is more than it appears” motif. Flynt and Steele are orphaned adoptive siblings working personally for Lysander; Flynt dies tragically midway through, Steele at the end (002 §Flynt/§Steele). Related [[flynt|Flynt]] (original wielder) · [[steele|Steele]] (inheritor) — characters batch. [[sunderer|Sunderer]] · [[mythic-weapons]] — other unique/relic weapons. [[weapons]] — sword type. Provenance Raw: M04661, M04670 (FreshToast, 2023-10-24) Cleaned: 000 Part (Flynt Steele / Warpath); §Steele Docs: 006 §Specific Weapons (Warpath); 002 §Steele CANON / §Flynt; 005 (Berserk reference) Ledger: rows R25 (M04661), R18 (Flynt Steele profiles) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Weapons (Tiers, Types & Skills)","group":"Items","url":"items/weapons.html","text":"Weapons (Tiers, Types Skills) CANON The material tier ladder, the categorized weapon roster, and the built-in skills each weapon type carries. Summary — Weapons sit on a five-tier material ladder (Iron/Ash up to Titanstone/Welkinite) where the tier sets base statistics, gated by act so power creep is paced. The roster is grouped into Melee, Ranged, and Magic families, and each weapon type carries built-in skills (Slash, Pierce, Snipe, Wild Swing, etc.), several of which use the game’s positional and overkill mechanics. Tier is only one of three axes — see [[equipment-system]] for how Quality and [[runes]] layer on top. Tier ladder (material) CANON Five tiers, tiered by material like Skyrim — “an orichalcum sword will outperform a steel sword”; the material only sets base statistics (M01433, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Some tiers hold two similar-but-different variants for variety. The finalized ladder (M02093, FreshToast, 2023-02-16): Tier 1 — Iron / Ash — weakest in all regards. Tier 2 — Steel / Yew — well-rounded, direct upgrade to Iron/Ash. [H material] variant — lower base damage than Steel/Yew but larger range. [H material] variant — less damage than Steel/Yew but improved critical-hit chance. Tier 3 — Ebonsteel / [J material] (second name TBD). Tier 4 — Orichalcum / Bruniglas — Bruniglas is dragon glass : an obsidian-like material formed from stone melted by a dragon’s breath and re-cooled (alt name floated: Hyrrglas) (M02090–M02091, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Tier 5 — Titanstone / Welkinite. Earlier informal name: Windsteel — “slightly lower damage than Orichalcum but higher crit chance” (M01433, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). In the finalized Part 8 ladder the crit-variant role is filled by the unnamed Tier 2 [H material] variant; “Windsteel” is the earlier, informal label. See Design history. Tier distribution by act CANON Tiers are gated by story progression (M02093, FreshToast, 2023-02-16): Act First half Second half Act 1 Tiers 1 2 Tier 2 only Act 2 Tiers 2 3 Tiers 3 4 Act 3 Tiers 4 5 Tier 5 only Drop rules CANON When the player has access to two tiers, the lower tier takes priority and drops more often ; the higher available tier appears infrequently. Gear is not available for purchase , and gear will never drop at Gold quality — Gold must be earned/found, not bought (M02093, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Weapon roster CANON Full categorized roster (M02161, FreshToast, 2023-02-16): Melee — Axe · Hammer · Spear · Sword Ranged — Shortbow · Longbow · Crossbow Magic — Staff · Orb Notes on association and behavior: Spear — Thane’s weapon; also Locke’s, once she takes up the [[sunderer|Sunderer]] (006 §Weapon Types). Bow — associated with Sable (006 §Weapon Types). Staff — associated with Isaac / Percival; sits in a more back position (006 §Weapon Types). Orb — sits in a more front position; “THE ORB IS USED TO BONK AND TELEPORT WITH MAGIC WIND” (M02162, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Weapon skills CANON Weapons carry built-in skills tied to their type, several using positional / overkill mechanics (M02157–M02160, FreshToast, 2023-02-16): Sword Slash — single-target front attack; deals half of any overkill damage to the next enemy behind. Pierce — single-target attack, front rank; low damage but halves the enemy’s damage resistance. Axe Slash — single-target melee; deals half overkill damage to the next enemy behind. Wild Swing — Cleave attack that hits the two front enemies. Staff Magic attack against any target. Modular AoE skill that damages both front or both back. Longbow Ranged attack against any target. Snipe — ranged attack, back rank only; high damage. Keyword — Cleave: causes a skill to hit all targets rather than one (M02154, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Positioning and rank-targeting these skills rely on (the 2×3 grid, front/back ranks) are owned by the systems batch; the skills above are the equipment-facing surface of that system. Visual presentation Use icons instead of full art for weapons and armour (006 §Visual Presentation). Related [[equipment-system]] — Tier/Quality/Rune model these weapons plug into. [[runes]] · [[mythic-weapons]] · [[armor-system]] Named weapons: [[sunderer|Sunderer]] (Locke’s spear) · [[warpath|Warpath]] (Flynt’s greatsword) · Lysander’s backup sword (see [[sunderer|Sunderer]]). Open questions DEBATED Material names for the Tier 2 [H] variants and the Tier 3 [J material] (006 §Unresolved Questions). Design history 2023-02-11 — first tier concept: material-based tiers à la Skyrim (Steel, Orichalcum, informal Windsteel = lower damage/higher crit) (M01433, FreshToast). EVOLVED 2023-02-16 (Part 8) — supersedes the above. Finalized as the five-tier ladder (Iron/Ash → Welkinite), with the crit variant demoted to an unnamed Tier 2 [H] slot and Windsteel dropped; act distribution and drop rules added (M02093, FreshToast). Weapon roster and per-type skills defined the same day (M02154–M02162). Provenance Raw: M01433 (2023-02-11); M02090–M02093, M02154–M02162 (FreshToast, 2023-02-16) Cleaned: 000 Part 6 §Tier System; Part 8 §Tiers / §Weapon Types / §Weapon Skills Docs: 006 §Tier System / §Tier Distribution by Act / §Drop Mechanics / §Weapon Types (Part 8) / §Weapon Skills (Part 8) / §Visual Presentation; 008 §Tier Ledger: rows R12 (M02093, M02090–M02091, M02096–M02097), R08 (M01433) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Affinity System","group":"Magic","url":"magic/affinities.html","text":"Affinity System CANON Every person is born attuned to one of three elemental affinities — fire, cold, or electricity — which shapes the magic they can channel. Summary — Magic affinities are the working magic system for the main game (007 §Affinity System). Each character is attuned to one of three affinities — Fiery (Pyrokinetic) , Cold (Cryokinetic) , or Electric (Electrokinetic) — and characters begin the game already “awakened,” with their affinity and the barest knowledge of magic (M04315–M04316, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Which affinity a person receives appears to be largely random ; the academic study of affinities is called Thaumaturgy , and its practitioners have found no reliable predictor for who gets what (M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). The three affinities Affinity Kinesis Personality association (theory, dismissed) Fiery Pyrokinetic passionate loyal Cold Cryokinetic thoughtful relaxed Electric Electrokinetic creative curious The kinetic terms Pyrokinetic / Cryokinetic / Electrokinetic were coined alongside the affinity list (M03053, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). The personality pairings are a theory held by some, not established fact — see Origin below. Per-character affinities (main game) Known affinities as listed in the notes (M03051–M03052, FreshToast, 2023-03-15): Affinity Characters Fiery [[locke]], [[thane]], [[sable]], [[eris]], [[ajax]] Cold [[damian]], [[astrid]], [[soren]], [[cultboy]] Electric [[percival]], [[ruby Note — [[ruby|Ruby]]'s listed Electric affinity is complicated by her identity as a magical item/catalyst who “cannot channel magic herself” (007 §Character Traits); the implemented proto instead ships her as a spell-casting Mender. Recorded as-listed here; the conflict lives on her character page. Origin of affinity Affinity is largely random (M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). Several explanations have been proposed and dismissed: Temperament — the passionate/thoughtful/creative pairings above; “often dismissed as vague and unjustified among academics in Thaumaturgy.” Circumstances of one’s birth — time, place, etc. Heritage. “Yet, no consistent link has been found which leads researchers to think it is largely random” (M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). Thaumaturgy (academic study) Thaumaturgy is the scholarly discipline that studies affinities (M03051, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). Its academics are the ones who dismiss the temperament theory and weigh the birth-circumstance and heritage theories, ultimately leaning toward affinity being largely random. See the broader magic-culture context in [[sorcery]]. Testing an affinity — the Aeromantia plant CANON Affinity is tested with an Aeromantia plant : a dandelion-like plant that reacts to your affinity when blown on (M03055–M03056, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). It was conceived as the setting’s equivalent of the Hunter × Hunter water-divination test — but framed around wind rather than water (M03054, FreshToast, 2023-03-15). Baseline (main game) CANON Characters start with their affinity and the barest knowledge of magic ; each has already “awakened” (M04314–M04316, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Skill-design philosophy The affinities carry an overarching theme of giving, taking and sharing (M03070, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-18): Fire skills — an offensive or supportive set of skills; burning; AoEs (area-of-effect). Lightning/Electric skills — about chaining attacks ; “lightning is the link between two things.” Overarching theme — giving, taking and sharing. [[locke|Locke]]'s signature flame skill that grants AP to an ally is cited as an example of this “giving” theme (M03070, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-18; see [[locke]]). Open questions DEBATED Alternative framework — Genesis / Stasis / Nexus. A parallel triad tied to the giving/taking/sharing theme was proposed: Genesis (Pushing / Giving), Stasis (Pulling / Taking), Nexus (Sharing / Equalising) (M03732, spirit_noodle, 2023-10-06). The notes never clearly pick one as final. Pyro/Cryo/Electro is presented as the working system; Genesis/Stasis/Nexus is recorded as an alternative conception (007 §Genesis/Stasis/Nexus). This mirrors the same dual-framing flagged in 001 . DEBATED Ruby’s affinity vs. her catalyst identity — whether Ruby “has” Electric affinity or merely channels for others is unresolved between the affinity list, her lore identity, and the proto (007 §Character Traits; see [[ruby]]). Design history 2023-03-15 — original concept: three known affinities ( Fiery / Cold / Electric ) with a per-character roster, the Pyro/Cryo/Electro kinetic naming, the temperament/birth/heritage theories and the “largely random” conclusion, the Thaumaturgy discipline, and the Aeromantia-plant test — all posted together (M03051–M03056, FreshToast). This is essentially the current system. 2023-03-18 — skill-design philosophy layered on: giving/taking/sharing , fire = offense/support/AoE, lightning = chaining/links, and Locke’s AP-granting flame skill as the “giving” exemplar (M03070, spirit_noodle). 2023-10-06 — the alternative Genesis / Stasis / Nexus triad proposed by spirit_noodle as a conceptual parallel to Fiery/Cold/Electric; never resolved against the working system (M03732) — supersedes nothing; recorded as an open alternative. 2023-10-17 — baseline confirmed: characters begin already awakened , holding their affinity and the barest knowledge of magic (M04314–M04316, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M03051–M03056 (FreshToast, 2023-03-15); M03070 (spirit_noodle, 2023-03-18); M03732 (spirit_noodle, 2023-10-06); M04314–M04316 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 Part 11 §Affinities; 000 Part 14 §Three Affinities System Docs: 007 §Affinity System; 001 §Character Affinities; 008 §Affinities Ledger: rows R17, R20 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Character Skills (Per-Character Rosters)","group":"Magic","url":"magic/character-skills.html","text":"Character Skills (Per-Character Rosters) DEBATED The exploration and combat skills sketched per party member — mostly proposed in the 2023-02-11 party-management dump, with per-character exploration skills flagged as “heavily debated” and never finalized. Summary — Each party member was given draft exploration skills (for dungeon-crawling: discovery, risk mitigation, movement) and, in some cases, combat traits. Most stem from spirit_noodle’s 2023-02-11 party-management dump (M01422), with follow-on additions by CrunchyConniptions the same day (M01448–M01449). Whether exploration skills survive is DEBATED — advocated passionately as Genshin-style party management, but flagged for scope creep (007 §Exploration Skills). Combat skills here are the notes’ design intent; the proto ships a different combat roster (see 007 §Implemented Skill Roster and [[weapon-skills]]). Named weapon skills (Slash, Pierce, Wild Swing, Snipe, Modular AoE, Leaping Strike, Balanced Slash) live on [[weapon-skills]]; the modular-skill mechanic on [[skill-system]]; [[judgment-cut]] is an unassigned skill. Locke Reserve borrow (combat): [[locke|Locke]] can borrow a skill from a character in reserve, once per battle ; each character has a designated reserve skill she can use (007 §Locke). Ties to her leadership/versatility role (M01880–M01881, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). Flame AP-grant skill: CANON a flame-based skill that gives AP to another character (000 Part 11) — an expression of the affinity theme “giving, taking and sharing.” See [[ap-economy]]. DEBATED Weakness-finding: “Maybe Locke’s thing is identifying weaknesses” (000 Part 23) — she finds enemy weak points (e.g. golem joints) and does extra damage depending on position and ability used. Liked, not finalized. Warps around the party with support-effect skills; has movement/modular abilities but does not need to slot them (M01880–M01881, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). Thane Exploration skill: provides a +5 defense buff (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). Combat — Pierce Attack: ignores a portion of enemy damage resistance (007 §Thane; cf. the Sword Pierce on [[weapon-skills]]). Combat — Taunt: makes enemies more prone to attack [[thane|Thane]] (007 §Thane). Spear modular skills: Leaping Strike and Balanced Slash — see [[weapon-skills]] (M02171–M02172, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Isaac Exploration — see illusory walls: [[isaac|Isaac]] has an exploration skill that lets him see / spot illusory walls (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). A later reframing gives him “sees secrets better but the torch reach is shortened heavily” (M01448, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11). Combat role: magic specialist (Staff / Resolve), struggles to move in combat (more specialized) (007 §Isaac). Grew up a child magic prodigy who discovered the ability to manipulate reality with his mind (007 §Isaac). Leonard (Attribution corrected — the two magic-vision skills come from different authors on different messages.) Exploration — light up a larger area: [[leon|Leonard]] should be able to light up a larger area with his magic spells (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). Exploration — see in the dark: doesn’t need a torch — he can see in the dark with magic , useful against light-sensitive enemies that would otherwise spot your torch first (M01449, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11). Combat — decoy: can create a decoy rather than turn invisible; the decoy is occasionally used for comedic effect in tandem with his social anxiety (007 §Leonard). Has the best mobility at high AP cost ; more modular skills (007 §Leonard; M01874, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). Clara Exploration — long-range initiate: [[clara|Clara]] has a skill that initiates attacks from a longer distance (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11); reframed as “can attack from a distance, but then you are completely unable to detect traps” (M01448, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11). Combat — delayed high-crit shot: a ranged attack with a 1-turn delay, high damage, that almost always crits ; also has a retreat shot (007 §Clara). Initiative: her initiative increases in the back position (007 §Clara). Sable Exploration — traps lures: [[sable|Sable]] can place traps or lure enemies , and can spot other traps herself (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). Combat: most mobile party member and the most AP-efficient ; more modular skills; some directly scale with [[escalation]] (007 §Sable; M01874, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). Ruby Exploration — decoy copy: [[ruby|Ruby]] can send a copy of herself out so enemies attack the copy instead of the party (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). Exploration — see rare metals PROPOSED : “Maybe Ruby has an exploration skill that allows her to see rare metals” (M01449, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11) — fits the alchemy theme, not confirmed. Combat identity is contested: notes give her Strength (a magic catalyst that cannot channel magic herself); the proto implements her as a spell-casting Mender (007 §Ruby; DEBATED ). Blood Magic Character (unnamed) Someone gets an ability akin to blood magic : they can sacrifice a bit of their blood/HP to refill the jar (the healing Jet / resource). Very risky, a very big health drain (M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). But the character can also retrieve blood — “it’s tricky,” designed to incentivize actually using him rather than treating him as a consistent sacrificial pawn (M01422). DEBATED Alternative: FreshToast preferred the character sacrifice health to heal another directly rather than interact with the Jet/estus mechanic (M01430, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Character never assigned — placeholder . Open questions DEBATED Do exploration skills survive at all? Passionately advocated (“It’s such a fun mechanic… I’m quite adamant about it” — M01448, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11) but flagged for scope creep / overcomplication / turning into a platformer ; alternative is character-agnostic puzzle dungeon design (007 §Exploration Skills). “Whether to keep exploration skills” is still open (007 §Unresolved Questions). DEBATED Which character gets [[judgment-cut]] — unassigned. DEBATED Ruby’s Strength-catalyst identity vs the proto’s spell-casting Mender (007 §Ruby). Design history 2023-02-11 — party-management dump proposes the exploration skills per character: Thane +5 defense, Isaac illusory walls, Clara long-range initiate, Sable traps/lures, Leonard light-up-larger-area, Ruby decoy copy; plus the blood-magic HP-sacrifice character (M01422, spirit_noodle). 2023-02-11 (same day) — CrunchyConniptions expands and re-frames: Isaac sees secrets but shorter torch, Clara attacks from distance but can’t detect traps (M01448); adds Leonard sees in the dark with magic (no torch) and the light-sensitive-enemy use case, plus proposed Ruby sees rare metals (M01449). FreshToast pushes back that exploration skills may not be needed and prefers direct HP-sacrifice healing (M01425, M01430). 2023-02-13 — mobility/modular profiles firmed up per character (Thane/Isaac specialized, Sable/Leonard mobile, Locke warps) (M01874, M01880–M01881, FreshToast). 2023-02-14 — the unassigned [[judgment-cut]] melee skill added to the pool (M02053, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M01422 (spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11); M01448–M01449 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11); M01425, M01430 (FreshToast, 2023-02-11); M01874, M01880–M01881 (FreshToast, 2023-02-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 5 §Party Management / Exploration; 000 Part 7 §Character Skills Docs: 007 §Character Skills, §Exploration Skills (Heavily Debated), §Blood Magic Character; 008 §Exploration Skills / sneaking taxonomy; 002 §(Thane exploration buff) Ledger: rows 471, 473, 474, 476, 488, 490 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Judgment Cut","group":"Magic","url":"magic/judgment-cut.html","text":"Judgment Cut PROPOSED A melee attack that can strike any target for solid damage at the cost of AP — an unassigned skill the team wanted in the game but never pinned to a character. Summary — Judgment Cut is a melee skill that “targets anyone” and deals solid damage while spending [[ap-economy|AP]] (M02053–M02054, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). It began as a one-word reference to the Devil May Cry / Vergil move (M01292, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-09) and was later fleshed out into a concrete skill concept. Which character receives it was explicitly left open — “Some character, I don’t care who, gets a judgment cut attack” (M02053). Description Melee attack that can target anyone on the field, regardless of rank (M02054, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Deals solid damage but uses [[ap-economy|AP]] (M02054). Nature of “targets anyone” makes it a rank-agnostic reach skill — unusual for a melee ability, which normally reaches only the front (see [[weapon-skills]]). Rules / Effects AP-gated: consumes AP like other powerful actions; not a free basic attack (M02054, 2023-02-14; see [[ap-economy]]). Damage: described only as “solid” — no numbers were assigned in the notes (M02054). Open questions DEBATED Which character gets it? Left deliberately undecided — “I don’t care who” (M02053, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Still listed under Unresolved Questions in 007 (“Which character gets judgment cut attack”) (007 §Unresolved Questions). The proto ships no skill named Judgment Cut; the implemented finishers are per-character (Locke’s Emberlance, Thane’s Earthsplitter, etc. — see [[weapon-skills]] and 007 §Implemented Skill Roster). Judgment Cut remains an unimplemented concept. Design history 2023-02-09 — first appears as a bare reference: “Judgement cut” (M01292, spirit_noodle) — a nod to the DMC/Vergil move, no mechanics attached yet. 2023-02-14 — fleshed out into a skill concept: a melee attack targeting anyone, solid damage, costs AP, character-agnostic (M02053–M02054, FreshToast) — supersedes the bare reference; adds all current mechanics . Provenance Raw: M01292 (spirit_noodle, 2023-02-09); M02053–M02054 (FreshToast, 2023-02-14) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 §Judgment Cut Attack Docs: 007 §Judgment Cut Attack; also referenced 009 §Special Abilities Ledger: rows 393, 395 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Skill System (Slots, Modular Skills, Costs)","group":"Magic","url":"magic/skill-system.html","text":"Skill System (Slots, Modular Skills, Costs) CANON How characters acquire, slot, and pay for skills: a level-gated unlock cadence with no skill trees, position-dependent “modular” skills, and AP costs that scale with power. The proto ships a fixed 4-skills-per-hero list. Summary — Skills are gained on a level-gated cadence (no skill trees — SCRAPPED anti-feature, “F**k skill trees”, 000 Part 3) with a small number of skill slots per character. Many skills are modular : one skill with two behaviors that switch on whether the caster is in the front or back rank. Every skill costs [[ap-economy|AP]], with strength scaling roughly exponentially against AP cost. The vertical slice ships a concrete version: 4 skills per hero (two basics, one utility, one 4-AP finisher) as TypeScript constants (007 §Implemented Skill Roster). For AP generation/spending rules see [[ap-economy]] (systems). Named skills live on [[weapon-skills]], [[character-skills]], and [[judgment-cut]]. Position mechanics are on the [[position-system]] page (systems). Skill acquisition slots (design intent) Progression is a refined, level-gated unlock cadence with NO skill trees and NO enemy scaling (007 §Level Structure Options). 20-level structure CANON, leading : start at Level 1 with 2 skills and base attributes; Levels 2–20 alternate attribute/skill/trait gains. Odd levels give character skills, even levels give passive traits. End state: 4 character-skill slots , ~ 8 skills total (start with 2), ~ 5 passive traits (007 §Level Structure Options). 10-level structure DEBATED, alternative : start with 3 starter skills ; Levels 2–10 alternate trait and skill each level (007 §Level Structure Options). The earlier “no leveling at all” stance (000 Part 6) EVOLVED into this refined cadence; the no-grind intent (tiny scaling, no treadmill) survives. Modular skills Definition: a skill with 2 uses that change depending on whether the character is in the front or the back rank (M01870, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). “Pretty common — most characters have them” (M01870). Nearly every weapon gets a modular skill with a secondary effect that has movement attached (M01873, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). Reverse modular: abilities that can target anybody but have different effects on either rank (007 §Modular Skill System). Worked examples (Thane’s Spear): Leaping Strike “(Front → Back)” and Balanced Slash “(Front → Back)” with crit scaling on target resistance (M02171–M02172, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16) — see [[weapon-skills]]. The Staff’s Modular AoE (both front or both back) is another (M02159, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Purpose: tactical depth, position-based gameplay, more strategic options (007 §Modular Skill System). Skill costs (AP) Every skill costs [[ap-economy|AP]]; skill strength should increase exponentially per AP cost (000 Part 3). “Maybe nearly all character skills use AP” (000 Part 3). If most fights end around turn 4, 4+ AP skills are finishers (000 Part 3). See [[ap-economy]] for the generate-vs-refill economy debate. Implemented roster (proto vertical slice) CANON The proto ships a fixed skill list as TypeScript constants ; each hero carries 4 skills — two basics, one utility, one finisher at 4 AP (007 §Implemented Skill Roster; proto src/core/combat/content.ts ). Effect kinds in the proto: attack (single or scope: 'all' ), heal , status , cleanse , move . Finishers cost 4 AP and are gated to round 4+ or [[escalation]] 20 (e.g. Locke’s Emberlance, Thane’s Earthsplitter, Isaac’s Inferno, Ruby’s Benediction — 007 §Implemented Skill Roster). Implemented skills carry rank constraints only ( fromRanks / targetRanks ) — there are no per-rank effect deltas yet , so the designed modular Front/Back effect -switching above remains design intent (007 §Implemented Skill Roster; GAME_DESIGN.md §5.8/§17). Open questions DEBATED 10-level vs 20-level structure and exact HP scaling left open (007 §Level Structure Options / §Unresolved Questions). DEBATED Modular effect-switching not implemented — the proto has rank constraints but no per-rank effect deltas (007 §Implemented Skill Roster). CANON, dropped No skill trees — rejected outright (000 Part 3). Design history 2023-02-13 — modular skill system defined: a 2-use skill that changes front/back; most characters have them; nearly every weapon gets a modular skill with movement attached (M01870, M01873, FreshToast). 2023-02-16 — Thane’s Spear skills (Leaping Strike, Balanced Slash) and the Staff’s Modular AoE give concrete modular examples (M02159, M02171–M02172). (later doc-era) — level-structure cadence formalized (20-level leading, 10-level alternative), skill trees scrapped; the “no leveling” stance evolved into the level-gated cadence (007 §Level Structure Options). 2026-06-16 (proto reconciliation) — vertical slice ships fixed 4-skills-per-hero constants with rank constraints only; modular effect-switching recorded as unimplemented intent (007 §Implemented Skill Roster). Provenance Raw: M01870, M01873 (FreshToast, 2023-02-13); M02159 (FreshToast, 2023-02-16); M02171–M02172 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16) Cleaned: 000 Part 7 §Modular Skill System; 000 Part 8 §Level Structure Docs: 007 §Modular Skill System, §Level Structure Options, §Implemented Skill Roster Ledger: rows 491, 492 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Sorcery (Luminarium System)","group":"Magic","url":"magic/sorcery.html","text":"Sorcery (Luminarium System) PROPOSED The academy magic of Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium — sorcerers channel [[the-flow|Flow]] through a set of ~20 Glyphs , combining them through training into complex, often personal magic. Summary — Sorcery is the magic system of the separate iteration Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium (001 §Luminarium), distinct from the main game’s [[affinities|Affinity System]]. A sorcerer channels [[the-flow|Flow]] through Glyphs — a determined set of around 20 primitive magical symbols — and combines them into new, complex magic (M03791–M03792, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; M04229, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). What combinations a sorcerer can achieve is “generally unique to a sorcerer or faction” and “the result of significant training,” so combined magic “can be roughly anything” (M04229, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Each of the Caelian Odyssey cast — the 4 Boyes and Cyrus — is designed to represent “a general facet of Sorcery and magic” (M04226–M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). The Glyph system PROPOSED Magic is built from Glyphs (001 §Glyphs System; M04229, FreshToast, 2023-10-17): The team settled on around 20 glyphs (“It’s shaping up to be around 20”), though “exact definitions not yet clear” — what each glyph covers had not been fully pinned down (M03791–M03792, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Combining glyphs into new, complex magic is “generally unique to a sorcerer or faction” and “the result of significant training,” meaning combined magic “can be roughly anything” (M04229, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Named/known glyphs: The River Glyph — originally the Water Glyph , renamed (“Look I’ve even renamed the water glyph to the River glyph,” M03880, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). Alias: Water Glyph. A sealing/binding glyph — “There is a glyph which does that,” raised when discussing a magic-sealing ability for [[titan|Titan]] (M04406, FreshToast, 2023-10-18). Named glyph fusions from the cast: Cyrus later picks up Earth to produce glass sorcery and Motion to produce explosive sorcery , both fusions with his Flame affinity (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17; 002 §Cyrus). Flow and the facets of Sorcery Sorcerers channel [[the-flow|Flow]] , whose two facets are Capacity (capable of big magic) and Channeling efficiency (how tired you get and cast speed) (001 §Sorcery; 007 §Sorcery Flow facet). Each of the 4 Boyes embodies a general facet of Sorcery (M04226–M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17): [[alistair]] — specialisation : solves all problems with ice carefully shaped into weapons and tools (‘Frost’ affinity). [[colette]] — the power of names and foreign uses/interpretations of sorcery. [[pandora]] — the practical uses of Sorcery (enchantment, alchemy) and magical anomalies ; the [[the-flow|Flow]] Capacity-vs-efficiency exemplar. [[titan]] — ‘Body’ affinity, used to heal and punch; fights unarmed, supplemented with magic. [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]] — a well-rounded sorcerer “by Caelian standards,” the reference point for balanced Sorcery. Baseline and awakening CANON Sorcerers start with their affinity and the barest knowledge of magic ; each has already “awakened” (M04314–M04316, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). This is the same awakening baseline used in the [[affinities|Affinity System]]. Loadout rule — sorceries fixed once slotted DEBATED For the Luminarium iteration, sorceries are fixed once slotted : “While one can change weapons based on enemy weaknesses, sorceries remain fixed once slotted” — a deliberate constraint to keep character-building meaningful and to reject rapid Dark-Souls-style gear-swapping (006 §Loadout rule; 008 §Loadout rule; note 7294). Related systems [[the-flow|The Flow]] — the magical current Sorcery channels. [[affinities|Affinity System]] — the main-game elemental system; Sorcery is the Luminarium-iteration counterpart. [[affinities#thaumaturgy-academic-study|Thaumaturgy]] (covered under [[affinities]]) — the academic study of affinities in the main-game framing. Open questions PROPOSED Exact glyph count (“around 20”) and the meaning of each glyph are undefined in the notes (M03791–M03792, M04229). DEBATED The “sorceries fixed once slotted” loadout rule is noted but not finalized (006 §Loadout rule; note 7294). Design history 2023-10-13 — glyph system taking shape: “around 20” glyphs, definitions not yet clear (M03791–M03792, FreshToast); the Water Glyph renamed to the River Glyph (M03880, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — the combining rule set: glyph fusions are “unique to a sorcerer or faction… the result of significant training… can be roughly anything” (M04229, FreshToast); the 4 Boyes + Cyrus framed as facets of Sorcery, and the awakening baseline stated (M04226–M04227, M04314–M04316, FreshToast). 2023-10-18 — a sealing/binding glyph confirmed to exist while discussing Titan’s magic-sealing ability (M04406, FreshToast). Provenance Raw: M03791–M03792, M03880 (FreshToast, 2023-10-13); M04226–M04227, M04229, M04314–M04316 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17); M04406 (FreshToast, 2023-10-18) Cleaned: 000 Part 15 §Glyphs / Sorcery (lines 6392–6395, 6703–6705) Docs: 001 §Sorcery (Luminarium System); 001 §Glyphs System; 007 §Academy magic (Luminarium); 006/008 §Loadout rule Ledger: rows R21, R23, R24 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"The Flow","group":"Magic","url":"magic/the-flow.html","text":"The Flow PROPOSED The magical current a sorcerer channels — the raw substance of Sorcery in the Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium setting, and the source of that world’s river-and-current aesthetic. Summary — Flow is the magical energy a sorcerer draws on and channels to cast (001 §Sorcery). A sorcerer’s aptitude with Flow has two facets: Capacity (how much magic you can produce — “capable of big magic”) and Channeling efficiency (how quickly you tire and how fast you cast) (001 §Sorcery; 007 §Sorcery Flow facet). Flow is scoped to the separate iteration Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium rather than main-game canon (001 §Luminarium). It also names the general aesthetic of the setting — the river-and-current motif the team leaned into (M03779, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). The two facets PROPOSED A sorcerer’s relationship to Flow breaks into two measures (001 §Sorcery; 007 §Sorcery Flow facet): Capacity — how much magic one can channel; high capacity means one is “capable of big magic.” Channeling efficiency — how tired you get and how fast you cast. Low efficiency means you fatigue quickly and cast slowly. These are independent: a sorcerer can be enormous in Capacity yet poor in efficiency. [[pandora]] is the canonical example — she “has immense Flow capacity (capable of big magic) but struggles to channel Flow efficiently (gets tired fast and casts slow),” and compensates with cunning and her inventions (M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Her uncontrolled capacity is precisely what makes her a danger to herself and others and drives her to the Luminarium academy (M04347–M04351, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Aesthetic and setting role “Flow” doubles as the name for the general aesthetic of the world. When the team discussed making rivers — especially the big one — culturally important across Khorath (river-related holidays and sayings), FreshToast noted “This works pretty well with Flow and the general aesthetic of the setting” (M03779, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). The current/river motif of Flow interlocks with the river culture documented in [[world-map-and-travel|the world’s locations]] and the [[river-flask|River Flask]] healing item. Related systems [[sorcery]] — the wider magic-culture of the Luminarium (Glyphs, facets, Caelian standards); Flow is the energy those systems channel. Each of the 4 Boyes represents “a general facet of Sorcery and magic” (M04226–M04227, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). [[affinities|Affinity System]] — the main-game elemental magic system; distinct from Luminarium Sorcery/Flow, though the two iterations share the same broader world. [[pandora]] — the character built to embody the Capacity-vs-efficiency split. Open questions PROPOSED The Flow’s exact mechanics, and whether “Capacity / Channeling efficiency” become in-game stats, are unspecified in the notes — only the two-facet concept and the Pandora example are given (001 §Sorcery; M04227). Design history 2023-10-13 — “Flow” first named, as the aesthetic/throughline of the setting, in the context of river culture: “This works pretty well with Flow and the general aesthetic of the setting” (M03779, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — Flow given mechanical shape via [[pandora]] : the Capacity / Channeling efficiency split, with Pandora as the high-capacity / low-efficiency exemplar (M04227, FreshToast). Later formalized in 001 as the two facets of Sorcery. Provenance Raw: M03779 (FreshToast, 2023-10-13); M04226–M04227, M04347–M04351 (FreshToast, 2023-10-17) Cleaned: 000 Part 15 §River culture (lines 6296–6297); 000 §Sorcery / Pandora (lines 6703–6705, 6817–6820) Docs: 001 §Sorcery (Luminarium System); 007 §Sorcery Flow facet; 002 §Pandora Ledger: rows R21, R23 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Weapon Skills (Designed Roster)","group":"Magic","url":"magic/weapon-skills.html","text":"Weapon Skills (Designed Roster) EVOLVED The per-weapon skill families the team designed on 2023-02-16 — Sword, Axe, Staff, Longbow, and Spear each get a basic attack plus a signature skill. The runnable proto ships a different, synthesized skill list, so these designs are documented intent rather than implemented content. Summary — On 2023-02-16 the team laid out weapon-typed skills: Melee gets an axe, the Generalist a sword, the Mage a staff, and Ranged a bow (M02156, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Each weapon has a basic attack and a signature skill built around rank (front/back) and positional reach. Most of these named designs (Slash’s overkill carry, Pierce’s resistance-halving, Wild Swing, Snipe, Modular AoE, Leaping Strike, Balanced Slash) are not implemented as written — the proto uses a separately synthesized roster (007 §Implemented Skill Roster; DEBATED design intent vs implemented). They remain valid future content. Sword skills (Generalist) Slash — single-target front attack; deals half overkill damage to the next enemy behind the target (M02157, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Pierce — single-target, front rank , low damage but halves the enemy’s damage resistance (M02157, 2023-02-16). Note: the proto’s “Pierce” is a flat power-7 jab with no resistance-halving — the designed effect is unimplemented (007 §Implemented Skill Roster). Axe skills (Melee) Slash — single-target melee attack; deals half overkill damage to the next enemy behind (M02158, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Wild Swing — a cleave attack that hits the two front enemies (M02158, 2023-02-16). “Cleave” is the keyword for a skill that hits all/multiple targets rather than one (007 §Keywords). Staff skills (Mage) Magic Attack — magic attack against any target (M02159, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Modular AoE — does damage to both front or both back depending on the caster’s rank (M02159, 2023-02-16) — an example of the [[skill-system|modular skill system]]. Longbow skills (Ranged) Ranged Attack — ranged attack against any target (M02160, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Snipe — ranged attack, back rank only , high damage (M02160, 2023-02-16). Spear skills (Thane) Both are [[skill-system|modular skills]] framed “(Front – Back)”: Leaping Strike — (Front → Back) single-target attack (M02171, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Balanced Slash — (Front → Back) single-target attack with increased crit chance based on the target’s resistance (50% of Resistance) (M02172, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16). Thane’s spear kit is the canonical worked example of modular position-based skills (007 §Modular Skill System). See [[character-skills]] for Thane’s fuller kit (Pierce Attack, Taunt). Keywords Cleave — makes a skill hit all/multiple targets rather than one (007 §Keywords). Open questions DEBATED Design intent vs implemented: none of these named families ship in the proto as written; the proto’s Pierce/Sunder/Phalanx/Emberlance (Locke), Cleave/Bulwark/Rending Blow/Earthsplitter (Thane), etc. are synthesized names for the vertical slice (007 §Implemented Skill Roster). Implemented skills carry rank constraints only ( fromRanks / targetRanks ) with no per-rank effect deltas , so the designed Front/Back effect-switching remains intent (007 §Implemented Skill Roster). Design history 2023-02-16 — full weapon-skill roster laid out in one sitting: weapon-to-class mapping (M02156), Sword (M02157), Axe (M02158), Staff (M02159), Longbow (M02160) by FreshToast; Thane’s Spear skills Leaping Strike and Balanced Slash by CrunchyConniptions (M02171–M02172) — all same day. 2026-06-16 (doc-era reconciliation) — checked against the runnable proto; the designed families were found not implemented as written , superseded in-engine by a synthesized roster, and re-tagged DEBATED design intent vs implemented (007 §Implemented Skill Roster). The designed families are retained as future content. Provenance Raw: M02156–M02160 (FreshToast, 2023-02-16); M02171–M02172 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-16) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 §Weapon Skills Docs: 007 §Weapon Skills (Sword/Axe/Staff/Longbow/Spear); 006 §(weapon skill archetypes) Ledger: rows 396, 397, 398, 399, 400 in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Accessibility & Combat-Signal Taxonomy","group":"Systems","url":"systems/accessibility-and-combat-signals.html","text":"Accessibility Combat-Signal Taxonomy PROPOSED The coarse offensive/defensive grouping that combat signals fold into, plus an optional auto-fading floating-text aid for readability and accessibility. Summary — Enemy/ability signals group into a coarse taxonomy — offensive (attack / debuff), defensive (defend / buff), or both (M01405, FreshToast, 2023-02-10; 008 §7). Alongside this, an accessibility aid was proposed: optional floating text explaining things like status effects that is small and fades on its own after a short time without requiring interaction , toggleable in settings (M01406, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). This coarse taxonomy complements the detailed [[enemy-intent|Enemy Intent]] icon set — it is the grouping the signals fold into, not a replacement for the icons. Combat-signal taxonomy Enemy/ability signals group into three coarse buckets (M01405, FreshToast, 2023-02-10; 008 §7): Offensive — attack / debuff. Defensive — defend / buff. Or both. This is the coarser layer above the fine-grained [[enemy-intent|Enemy Intent Indicator]] taxonomy (the Core Indicators and the 23-icon set). The two are complementary and should not be duplicated: the icon set says exactly what an enemy is about to do; this taxonomy is the offensive/defensive category it lands in. Interacting with telegraphed intent There are abilities that do extra damage to enemies that are going to attack — i.e. abilities that interact with an enemy’s telegraphed intent (M01403, FreshToast, 2023-02-10; 008 §7). No purely self-defensive abilities — except something like [[thane|Thane]]'s taunt (M01404, FreshToast, 2023-02-10; 008 §7). Accessibility — optional floating status text An accessibility aid, distinct from the combat telegraphs (M01406, FreshToast, 2023-02-10; 008 §7): Optional reminders that can be turned off in settings. Small floating text explaining things like status effects . Fades on its own after a short time without requiring interaction — no click/dismiss needed. Purpose: accessibility / readability. (Compare the proto’s implemented status bank (008 §6 Status Effects System) — the floating text would explain those effects to the player; the auto-fade text itself is not coded, it is design intent.) Related systems [[enemy-intent]] — the fine-grained intent icons this taxonomy groups. The player-side [[rank-targeting-ui|rank-targeting / readability UI]] is a parallel readability system (008 §7 PROPOSED Rank-Targeting). General accessibility caution in the notes: “just be wary to not lose too much accessibility” (M03065, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-17). Open questions PROPOSED The floating-text aid and the offensive/defensive taxonomy are design intents, not decided/implemented systems; exact wording, timing of the fade, and how the taxonomy maps onto the final icon set are unspecified (008 §7). Design history 2023-02-10 — In one thread FreshToast proposes: vague enemy signals grouped as offensive (attack/debuff) / defensive (defend/buff) / both ; abilities that do extra damage to enemies about to attack; no purely self-defensive abilities except Thane’s taunt; and an optional, settings-toggleable floating status text that fades on its own for accessibility (M01402-M01406, FreshToast, 2023-02-10; 008 §7). Provenance Raw: M01402, M01403, M01404, M01405, M01406 (FreshToast, 2023-02-10) Cleaned: 000 §Combat Signals / Accessibility (floating status text, lines ~2432-2437) Docs: 008 §7 Combat Signal Taxonomy Accessibility Status Text Ledger: Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (mechanics rows)"},{"title":"Anti-Meta Systems","group":"Systems","url":"systems/anti-meta-systems.html","text":"Anti-Meta Systems EVOLVED Systems designed to discourage everyone from converging on the single “best” build — because “everyone using the same builds is boring.” Summary — Two anti-meta mechanics were discussed: a points system that makes the most popular characters/weapons/items expensive to field, and an invasion system where using the most powerful, popular hero makes you more likely to be invaded and have that power stolen (008 §14). Both belong to the early co-op-online vision ; the invasion mechanic in particular is inseparable from the superseded [[two-path-system|Hero-vs-Undying]] framing, so this is preserved as a record rather than an active first-game system (008 §10 EVOLVED ). Points system (anti-meta) A system that disincentivizes meta builds/teams by cost (008 §14; M00128, M00172, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25): It is expensive to use the most popular character, weapons, items, etc. If a player wants a specific build the point system “won’t allow,” they can still use it, but it loses effectiveness — less damage, longer cooldowns, and so on. Guiding line: “Incentivize to embrace the randomness. Everyone using the same builds is boring” (008 §14). This points idea is shared with the [[spirit-collection-system|spirit-collection]] character-acquisition system, where points improve the probability of getting a certain character and can be retrieved once used, or spent on equipment/items/skills instead if the player “embraces the randomness” (008 §11). The design tension it expresses — “give people the ability to quell the randomness at a cost; people want to be in control” — is the same across both. Invasion system (anti-meta) A multiplayer anti-meta lever tied to the [[two-path-system|Hero vs Undying]] concept (M00243, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26; 008 §14; §10): “By using the most powerful and popular hero, you are more likely to be invaded and your power stolen.” Purpose: “Disincentivizing people from just using the best heroes.” Because invasion depends on other players invading yours to “borrow” a hero’s power, it only functions in the co-op-online model. See [[two-path-system]] for the full Hero/Undying framing this sits inside. Open questions EVOLVED Whether any anti-meta system survives into the single-player first game at all — both mechanics were built for online co-op (008 §10, §14; Parts 9-24). See [[overview]]. Design history 2021-05-25 — Anti-meta points system proposed: expensive to use the most popular characters/weapons/items; a disallowed build can still be used but loses large effectiveness (less damage, longer cooldowns) (M00128, M00172, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25; 008 §14). 2021-06-26 — Invasion anti-meta emerges from the Hero/Undying two-path concept: using the best/most popular hero makes you a target for invasion and power theft (M00243, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26; 008 §10, §14). Parts 9-24 — supersedes as active design. The first game is reframed single-player; the two-path/invasion framing (and thus the invasion anti-meta) is tagged an early/superseded concept, preserved as a record (008 §10 EVOLVED ). Provenance Raw: M00128, M00172 (CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25); M00243 (CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26) Cleaned: 000 Part 1 §Anti-Meta System Docs: 008 §14 Anti-Meta Systems; 008 §10 Two-Path System (Anti-Meta Through Invasion) Ledger: row R01 (“Anti-meta points system…”), Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Action Points (AP) Economy","group":"Systems","url":"systems/ap-economy.html","text":"Action Points (AP) Economy CANON The per-turn action budget that gates how much a character can do in a round — the currency of every skill. Summary — In the shipped proto, AP is a flat per-round budget that REFILLS each round — not a pool that starts at zero and is earned over the fight. Each hero has maxAp = 3 ; enemies have 2 (the boss has 4). Skills cost 1–3 AP; finishers cost 4+ AP and are gated to round 4+ or [[escalation]] ≥ 20 (008 §2 Action Points System; proto src/core/combat/content.ts , state.ts ). Unspent AP does not carry over. The original notes-era design was the opposite — a per-character pool starting at zero that certain actions generate and powerful actions spend (M00858, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02); the proto rejected that generate-and-spend model in favour of the flat refill. Rules — implemented (proto) CANON · implemented (008 §2 Action Points System; proto src/core/combat/state.ts ) Budget: heroes maxAp = 3 ; enemies 2; boss 4. At the start of every round, living characters’ AP is refilled to maxAp ( startNextRound sets ap: c.maxAp ). Costs: skills cost 1–3 AP ; finishers cost 4+ AP and are hard-gated to round 4+ OR escalation ≥ 20 ( canPayAndCast ). No carry-over: unspent AP is reset by the per-round refill. A character’s turn ends automatically once their AP reaches 0 ( reduceUseSkill calls endActorTurn when ap === 0 ). Escalation surge (once): when [[escalation]] first reaches 20 , every living hero gains a one-time +2 AP , and their maxAp is permanently raised +2 for the rest of the fight ( grantApBonus , AP_BONUS_AT_ESCALATION = 20 , AP_BONUS_AMOUNT = 2 ). No AP economy verbs: there are no AP-generating skills, no held/auto-trigger skills, and no AP steal/remove effects in the proto (008 §2). Related systems The whole system exists to serve the “end fast” philosophy : skill strength was meant to scale with AP cost, and “if most fights end around turn 4, AP 4+ skills should be finishers” (M01868, M01869, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). See [[escalation]]. Nearly all character skills consume AP; each weapon subtype has only 3–4 “neutral” skills (M01364, M01365, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). [[locke|Locke]] has a flame-based skill that grants AP to an ally (008 §Affinity Magic; M03070, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-18) — the “giving/taking/sharing” theme. Open questions DEBATED Generate-and-spend vs flat refill. The notes describe AP as a per-character pool that starts at zero and is gained over the fight (“Assume gain AP every turn”; AP steal rare and enemy-only; escalation break points feed AP). The proto rejects this in favour of the flat 3-per-round refill with one escalation-20 surge, reconciled in the GameBible: “3 AP per turn… Always full refund. Every turn. No carry over” (008 §2, DEBATED design intent vs implemented ). Both are recorded: the pool is the notes design intent, the refill is what the game implements. DEBATED Renaming AP to “Zeal” was the leading proposal (over Spirit / Grit / Tenacity), to tie it to a stamina/breath ludonarrative theme, but was not finalized (Part 11). It collides with the existing “Zeal” Acuity Jet shard (boosts initiative, Part 14), a clash that must be resolved before adoption. The proto keeps the plain term “AP” (008 §2). Design history 2023-02-02 — original concept (“Advantage Point System”): starts at zero per battle , accrues over the battle, unique per character (unlike Voice of Cards); some actions generate AP, stronger actions spend AP (M00858, CrunchyConniptions). (Ledger R05.) 2023-02-10 — “maybe nearly all character skills use AP” (M01365, FreshToast). 2023-02-13 — AP scaling settled toward finishers: skill strength should increase (near-exponentially) per AP cost assuming AP-per-turn gain; 4+ AP skills = finishers because most fights end around turn 4 (M01868, M01869, FreshToast) — this survives into the implemented finisher gate . 2023-02-13 — AP steal/remove effects are very rare and only available to enemies (M01872, FreshToast) — notes intent; not coded in the proto . 2023-02-20 — AP listed alongside escalation/shields as a core system; briefly debated whether escalation was redundant with AP (“If anything I’d drop escalation… since it has the same goal as AP”; “AP’s pretty core rn”) (M02572, M02610, M02611, M02613, M02627, FreshToast). Later (proto era) — the generate-and-spend pool was replaced by a flat 3-per-round refill (heroes) with a single escalation-20 +2 AP surge ; unspent AP does not carry over (008 §2, DEBATED design intent vs implemented ). (This is the current presentation above; supersedes the zero-start pool.) Provenance Raw: M00858 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02); M01364, M01365 (FreshToast, 2023-02-10); M01868, M01869, M01872 (FreshToast, 2023-02-13); M02572, M02610–M02613, M02627 (FreshToast, 2023-02-20) Cleaned: 000 Game Mechanics §Advantage Point (AP) System (lines 1586–1591) Docs: 008 §2 Action Points System (AP); 008 §4 Resource Systems; 007 §Advantage Point (AP) System Ledger: rows R05 (M00858), R08 (M01364/M01365), R10 (M01868/M01869), R11 (M01872) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Armor System","group":"Systems","url":"systems/armor-system.html","text":"Armor System DEBATED Divinity-style Physical/Magical armour that provides percentage resistances and depletes alongside health — discussed but never settled or implemented. Summary — Armour is modelled after Divinity: Original Sin ’s two-track armour, but repurposed to grant percentage resistances rather than being a separate health pool. Armour takes damage simultaneously with health , and as it depletes the resistances it provides shrink. The two tracks are Physical and Magical (with “Physical and Mental” floated as an alternative framing). All of the [[equipment-system|Tier / Quality / Rune]] rules apply to armour as they do to weapons. Status: this Divinity-style armour layer was discussed on 2023-03-05 but never settled or implemented — the same DEBATED idea appears (with the identical bleed-resist worked example) on [[damage-types|Damage Types Mitigation]], which is the canonical combat-mitigation page. The shipped proto instead mitigates damage with flat Physical/Magical stats ( def / res ), not a depleting percentage-resistance armour pool. This page records the concept and its equipment-model ties; see [[damage-types]] for the implemented model. Rules / Effects DEBATED Two tracks — Physical and Magical armour, drawn from Divinity, but here they provide a bonus to percentage resistances and take damage simultaneously to health (M02897, FreshToast, 2023-03-05; 006 §Physical/Magical Armor). Alternative framing floated: Physical and Mental instead of Physical and Magical. Depletes with health — armour takes damage simultaneously to health, not as a separate shield pool ahead of it (M02897, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). All equipment systems apply to armour — Tier (material base stats), Quality (Bronze/Silver/Gold bandwidth), and Rune slots all apply to armour as well as weapons (006 §All systems apply to armour as well; M01435 “weapons and armour to have Rune slots” and M01438 “this should all apply to armour as well”, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). Resistance mechanics example Worked example from the notes showing how armour and a base resistance combine, and how the resist erodes as armour is chipped away (M02889–M02895, FreshToast, 2023-03-05; 006 §Resistance Mechanics Examples): 10% base bleed resist 20 physical armour → 30% total bleed resist 10 physical damage dealt → 10 physical armour left → 20% bleed resistance (with 20% bleed resist as the fixed floor) This worked example is transcribed from the notes’ shorthand; the exact formula relating armour points to resistance % is illustrative, not fully specified. Related [[equipment-system]] — the Tier/Quality/Rune model armour shares with weapons. [[runes]] — armour carries rune slots. Physical vs. Magical armour interacts with the combat resistance and shield mechanics owned by the systems and magic batches. Open questions DEBATED Whether the two tracks are named “Physical / Magical” or “Physical / Mental” (006 §Physical/Magical Armor). Provenance Raw: M01435 (rune slots on weapons armour), M01438 (FreshToast, 2023-02-11, “this should all apply to armour as well”); M02897 (Divinity Physical/Magical armour → percentage resistances, depletes with health) and M02889–M02895 (bleed-resist worked example) (FreshToast, 2023-03-05) Cleaned: 000 Part 6 §Armor System Docs: 006 §Armor System (Physical/Magical Armor · Resistance Mechanics Examples · All systems apply to armour as well) Ledger: row R08 (M01432–M01443, full loot/equipment system); row R16 (M02889–M02897, bleed/armour worked example, 2023-03-05) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Damage Types & Mitigation","group":"Systems","url":"systems/damage-types.html","text":"Damage Types Mitigation CANON Two damage channels — physical and magical — each countered by its own mitigation stat, plus the player-side toolkit for reducing incoming harm. Summary — Combat damage is physical or magical . Physical damage is mitigated by Damage Resistance (a derived stat under Toughness) and physical armour; magical damage by Damage Resistance and magical armour (008 §5 Damage Types). In the proto these resolve as flat per-character numbers: def mitigates physical, res mitigates magical, each point ≈ 0.5% reduction (008 §5 Damage Formula; proto src/core/combat/resolution.ts ). Player-side mitigation splits into four tools — Healing, Redirection, Evasion, and Disruption (008 §5; M05438, FreshToast, 2024-08-20). A DEBATED Divinity-inspired armor/resistance layer was discussed but not settled. Damage types the implemented formula Damage channels (008 §5 Damage Types): Physical Damage — mitigated by Damage Resistance (a derived stat under Toughness) and physical armor. Magical Damage — mitigated by Damage Resistance and magical armor. CANON · implemented Formula (no notes-era formula exists; these are the proto’s computed values — resolution.ts ): Accuracy: roll d100 vs clamp(attacker.acc + status accDelta + enemyEdge×2, 5, 99) . Blind = −40 accuracy. A miss deals 0. Crit: roll d100 vs clamp(attacker.crit + enemyEdge×2, 0, 95) ; a crit multiplies damage by ×1.6 and adds +1 [[escalation]] . Magnitude: power × (1 + offense/100) × outMult , then × (1 − mitigation/200) (each mitigation point ≈ 0.5% reduction), then × inMult , then ×1.25 if the defender is [[shields-and-wounded|Wounded]] , then ×1.6 if crit , then variance ±12% . offense = the attacker’s str (phys) or mag (mag); mitigation = the defender’s def (phys) or res (mag). Minimum 1 damage on any hit. Protection buff halves the final damage of the next hit, then is consumed. Healing: power × (1 + caster.mag/200) × variance(90–112%) , minimum 1. Player-side mitigation tools CANON Four tools (008 §5 Damage Mitigation Tools; M05438, M05456–M05466, FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2024-08-20): Healing — scarce and capped; “like white health in Darkest Dungeon” but actually a degrading-max-HP ceiling (see below). Redirection — a tougher character takes damage aimed at another (e.g. Thane’s taunt). Evasion — reducing the likelihood of a character being hit (avoid rate in FE3H, dodge in any RPG). Disruption — impairing the enemy’s ability to attack successfully or optimally: forcing a different action OR reducing accuracy. Evasion vs Disruption (008 §5): Evasion = the character becoming harder to hit; Disruption = forcing the enemy to take a different action OR reducing their accuracy (“equivalent of avoid rate in FE3H”). Healing Health (degrading max-HP) CANON Four healing sources, ascending: consumables (weakest, possibly removed), healing skills, the healing Jet , and the river heal (strongest, single-use) (008 §5 Healing Health, Part 18). See [[machina|the Jet]] and [[river-flask|River Flask]] (items batch). CANON · implemented Degrading Maximum HP: the healable HP ceiling starts full and ratchets down as you take damage, until you rest — healing is a stop-gap, not full recovery (008 §5; proto src/core/campaign/run.ts degradeCeilings , CEILING_DEGRADE_PER_FIGHT = 3 , floor 60% of base). See [[shields-and-wounded|Wounded]] for the under-half-HP amplifier. Related systems [[shields-and-wounded]] — the Wounded flag adds ×1.25 to incoming damage; shields negate a lethal hit. [[escalation]] — feeds the enemy accuracy/crit enemyEdge . The 8×9 status bank modifies outMult / inMult and accuracy (Strength/Defense Up/Down, Blind, etc.) (008 §6). Open questions DEBATED Armor / Resistance layer (Divinity-inspired), Part 11. A Divinity-style physical/magical armor layer was discussed: armor gives a bonus to percentage resistances and takes damage simultaneously with health. Worked example: 10% base bleed resist + 20 phys armor → 30% total; after 10 physical damage → 10 phys armor left, 20% bleed resist (008 §5; M02889–M02898, FreshToast, 2023-03-05). Variant naming floated as Physical/Mental rather than Physical/Magical. Not settled, not implemented. See [[armor-system]] for the dedicated page on this debated layer and its equipment-model ties. Design history 2023-03-05 — original armor/resistance discussion: Divinity-style physical/magical armor giving a bonus to percentage resistances and taking damage alongside health; the bleed-resist worked example; possible Physical/Mental naming (M02889–M02898, FreshToast). (Ledger R16.) — remains DEBATED , not adopted. 2023-10-30 — healing reframed: the “white health like Darkest Dungeon” framing was superseded by the degrading-max-HP ceiling; healing bound as a stop-gap (M04814–M04829, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). (Ledger R26.) 2024-08-20 — the four player-side mitigation tools (Healing, Redirection, Evasion, Disruption) and the Evasion-vs-Disruption clarification were laid out (M05438, M05456–M05466, FreshToast/spirit_noodle). (Ledger R29.) Proto era — the two-channel physical/magical model was implemented as flat def / res mitigation in the damage formula (008 §5 Damage Formula, CANON · implemented ). (Current presentation above.) Provenance Raw: M02889–M02898 (FreshToast, 2023-03-05); M04814–M04829 (2023-10-30); M05438, M05456–M05466 (FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2024-08-20) Cleaned: 000 Damage Mitigation Tools (lines 8906–8914); 000 armor/bleed worked example (lines 5112–5120) Docs: 008 §5 Damage Mitigation Systems; 008 §5 Armor/Resistance Layer; 008 §5 Damage Formula Ledger: rows R16 (M02889…), R26 (M04814…), R29 (M05438…) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Dungeon-Run Loop","group":"Systems","url":"systems/dungeon-run-loop.html","text":"Dungeon-Run Loop PROPOSED The out-of-combat world loop: you enter a dungeon, push deeper through a chain of nodes and fights spending finite run resources, camp to reset your worn-down health, and either clear the boss or wipe — with harder self-selected runs paying out better rewards. Summary — The dungeon run is the game’s repeatable core loop, framed by the team as “re-creating the chalice dungeons” of Bloodborne — repeatable dungeons you go through (the “act of dungeoning”) to grind materials for your weapon and better rewards (M04547, M04549, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-22). Players are meant to have control over a dungeon’s challenge level via a risk mechanic — make it harder, get better loot (M04566, M04569, 2023-10-22). Within a run you traverse a [[navigation-compass|compass]]-navigated node graph, fight through encounters (some of which [[#ambush|ambush]] you), spend limited run resources ([[shimmer-sails|Jets]], the [[river-flask|River Flask]]), watch your maximum HP ratchet down until you [[#camp–rest|camp and rest]], and — on defeat — get sent back to re-provision. Most of this is design intent; the vertical-slice proto ships a compressed, playable version. The chalice-dungeon framing PROPOSED The loop was crystallized on 2023-10-22 as a Bloodborne chalice-dungeon analogue — “similar, not the same” : Repeatable dungeons you go through to grind materials to put into your weapon — though “grind is the wrong word,” it is the act of “dungeoning” (M04547–M04556, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-22). You are incentivized to do harder versions of the dungeons for better rewards — “you make the dungeon harder, your reward is better” (M04551, M04564, M04565, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-22). The player has control over the dungeon’s challenge level , replicable “through some kind of risk mechanic ” (M04566, FreshToast; M04569, FreshToast, 2023-10-22). Serves the same purpose as chalice dungeons without being identical. DEBATED Distinguished from Borderlands’ Mayhem mode, which is “just pumping numbers up which you eventually scale to match… not much of a difficulty slider” (008 §13; 000 lines 7349-7352). This ties directly to the [[#riskreward-luck-bonus|risk/reward luck bonus]] below, which is the leading candidate for the “risk mechanic.” The run loop (structure) The proto ships a single playable dungeon, “The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb” (the prologue), which demonstrates the intended shape of a run (008 §13; proto: src/core/campaign/content.ts ): start → fight → a branch (treasure path vs. ambush-elite path) → riverside camp → fight → boss It is a CANON · implemented compass-navigated node graph — never a spatial map (see [[navigation-compass]]). Each node offers next-node choices and may attach a fight, a reward, an ambush, or a camp. Run resources Resources are consumed over a run and are the currency of the risk decision — push deeper and you spend down your safety margin. CANON · implemented values (008 §4; proto: src/core/campaign/run.ts ): [[shimmer-sails|Healing Jets]]: start a run with 2 , cap 5 ; each jet heals one character for 60% of their base max HP . [[river-flask|River Flask]]: start with 1 ; heals the whole party for 35% of base max HP each use. Relics: collected, named, non-duplicating (boss/treasure rewards — see [[treasure-and-rewards]]). (Glint/gold and the wider provisioning economy from Part 17 are design intent — not modeled in the proto run state.) Degrading maximum HP CANON · implemented The health system is what makes a run a war of attrition . Maximum healable HP (the ceiling ) starts full and ratchets down as you take damage, until you rest — healing is a stop-gap, not full recovery, so characters get worn down across a run (008 §5, Part 18). Proto implementation ( src/core/campaign/run.ts ): Each won fight degrades every party member’s ceiling by 3 HP , down to a floor of 60% of base max ( CEILING_DEGRADE_PER_FIGHT = 3 , CEILING_FLOOR_RATIO = 0.6 ). Current HP is clamped to the ceiling. A knocked-out hero who survives a won fight is revived at 25% of ceiling so one bad fight doesn’t silently end a run ( KO_RECOVER_RATIO ). This drives the whole run-loop tension: the further you push without resting, the lower your ceilings — which is exactly what the risk/reward bonus rewards. Camp / rest Resting is the pressure-release valve. CANON · implemented rest at a camp restores the ceiling to full and heals to it (008 §5; proto: run.ts — rest ). In the notes’ fuller (design-intent) framing, rest points also re-provision the party and bank any gold acquired (banked gold is never lost, protecting the economy) (008 §8, Part 17). The vertical-slice dungeon places a riverside camp mid-run between the two fight beats (008 §13). Resting resets your accumulated risk — under the luck-bonus proposal, resting would zero out the bonus you’ve been building by pushing on. Ambush CANON · implemented Some encounters ambush the party: an enemy makes a sound and rushes in, and if caught the party’s positioning scatters at the start of battle (008 §2, Part 11). Proto: an ambush node flag triggers a Fisher–Yates shuffle of the heroes’ starting ranks before the fight is built (proto: src/core/campaign/run.ts — scatterFormation ; ambush on DungeonNode in src/core/campaign/types.ts ). Darkness bands are modeled as a darkness?: number field intended to weight ambush rate — currently flavor in the proto ( campaign/types.ts ). Ambushes are used to “spook players in dark areas.” Counter: attack the ambusher just as they reach you — a DEBATED QTE element ( “a bit meh” ), used as a small, telegraphed minigame, sparingly (008 §2). Players can use a consumable item to pre-emptively know whether they’ll be ambushed. Locations are designed for it: open fields with charging bulls, foggy bogs with ninja frogs (008 §2). PROPOSED Crossroads variation: an ambush might let [[locke|Locke]] (or an enemy) take an extra turn (008 §2). The vertical slice’s branch offers a treasure path vs. an ambush-elite path — the reward-vs-risk choice in miniature (008 §13). Defeat: death-run provisioning DEBATED design intent vs implemented The notes’ full death-run (Part 17, 2023-10-22): on defeat you are sent to the nearest rest point and must provision again (M04502, FreshToast, 2023-10-22). “Like other corpse-run mechanics, it is grounded in another system: provisioning” (M04503, FreshToast, 2023-10-22). Provisions and glint (gold) earned while exploring are left behind but can be reclaimed by defeating the enemy that beat you (slightly stronger the second time); defeated again before reclaiming, they’re lost. Compared to Lords of the Fallen (008 §8). DEBATED Loadout rule: weapons can be swapped to exploit enemy weaknesses, but sorceries/skills are fixed once slotted — a deliberate constraint to keep character-building meaningful; rapid Dark Souls-style gear-swapping was rejected as game-breaking (008 §8). What the proto ships instead: a simpler defeat model — a party wipe sends the run to a lost state; there is no reclaim-from-the-enemy, no glint banking, and no provisioning economy in run state (proto: src/core/campaign/run.ts — absorbCombat defeat branch; RunState has no glint field). The softer analogue is the knocked-out-but-recovered rule above. The hard rule “never lose found items” is canon and honoured — items/relics are never stripped (008 §8, §1 Core Game Loop). Risk/Reward luck bonus DEBATED The leading candidate for the chalice-dungeon “risk mechanic.” A proposed luck bonus that grows the longer a player goes in a dungeon without resting (better loot/rewards/multipliers), encouraging players to push their luck — modeled loosely on Lords of the Fallen ’s multiplier and Darkest Dungeon ’s light system (008 §8, Part 17). Origin: “I think it’d be cool if the player was rewarded with some kinda luck bonus the longer they spent in a dungeon without resting” (M04506, FreshToast, 2023-10-22). “I kinda want players to push their luck as much as possible” (M04516, FreshToast, 2"},{"title":"Enemy Intent Indicator System","group":"Systems","url":"systems/enemy-intent.html","text":"Enemy Intent Indicator System CANON Slay-the-Spire-style telegraphs that show what each enemy is about to do, so the player reacts with foreknowledge. Summary — At the start of each round the game telegraphs every living enemy’s planned action; the player then reacts. The finalized design taxonomy is 23 indicators built from a handful of base types (Attack, Protect, Support, Disrupt, Gambit, plus Prepare and Unknown) and their dual combinations (M05439–M05441, FreshToast, 2024-08-20). The proto ships a distillation — 6 readable intent categories — rather than the full 23-icon set (008 §7; proto src/core/combat/intents.ts ). The idea began as a heavily-debated “Foresight” mechanic on 2023-02-09 (M01308, CrunchyConniptions) and the exhaustive 23-indicator writeup was authored 2024-08-20. Rules — implemented (proto) CANON · implemented (008 §7; proto src/core/combat/intents.ts , state.ts ) Every living enemy’s planned action is telegraphed at the start of each round (re-rolled each round) and the player reacts ( declareEnemyIntents ). Ships 6 readable intent categories : Attack ⚔️, Dual ⚡ (a hit + a status), Disrupt 🌀, Support 🩹, Defend 🛡️, Gambit 🎲 ( INTENT_CATEGORIES , classifyIntent ). Classification rules: finisher + attack → Gambit ; attack + (negative status on foe OR a shove) → Dual ; plain attack → Attack ; status/shove only → Disrupt ; heal → Support ; shield/self-buff → Defend . Enemy targeting is currently auto-chosen (lowest-HP, front-most) rather than fully telegraphed per-icon ( chooseTarget ). The 23-indicator design taxonomy (design target) CANON design (008 §7; M05439–M05441, FreshToast, 2024-08-20): Core indicators: Attack (shows physical vs magical) · Protect (mitigate incoming) · Support (heal / buff / cleanse) · Disrupt (afflict debuffs) · Unknown (uncategorizable / movement only) · Prepare (turn taken without action) · Gambit (the most deadly action of powerful enemies; almost always a dual indicator). Dual indicators combine two of Attack / Protect / Support / Disrupt / Gambit; the icon takes the primary indicator’s appearance with added visuals for the secondary. Precedence: Gambit Attack Protect (Defend) Disrupt/Support (which varies by case). All 23 (M05440): Physical Attack, Magical Attack, Phys/Magical Attack ×(Protect, Support, Disrupt), Protect, Protect/Support, Protect/Disrupt, Support, Support/Disrupt, Disrupt, Disrupt/Support, Gambit, Gambit/(Phys Attack, Mag Attack, Protect, Support, Disrupt), Prepare, Unknown = 23 total (M05441). CANON The settled comparison is ~10 more indicators than Slay the Spire (23 vs ~13) — StS doesn’t specify attack type or have a Gambit; StS changes its attack symbol by damage amount, “but we shouldn’t do that” (008 §7). (The in-session “only 5 more — no wait, 10 more” was an arithmetic self-correction, Part 23, not a design disagreement.) Design notes interaction Unknown is deliberate: “Unknown indicator is fun. To keep a degree of unpredictability” — used sparingly, like when StS goes “???” (M05442, M05443, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2024-08-20). Conditional interruption: some actions are interruptible with a certain damage type or enough damage — “if a dragon’s about to breathe fire, it’s got flames flickering from the sides of its jaws”; deal enough damage to downgrade a scary Gambit (008 §7). Player influence: players can interact with intents — “moving enemies around… forcing them into states where they must/want to change their action” (008 §7 Q A). There are abilities that do extra damage to enemies that are going to attack (M01403, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). Gambit telegraphing: animations, special visual/sound effects, or a bark signalling “BIG THING COMING THIS TURN OR NEXT” (008 §7 Q A). Related systems Distinct from [[rank-targeting-ui]] — that is the player-side targeting feedback (a skill’s own from-rank/target-rank), whereas intent indicators are the enemy-side telegraph. The two are complementary and should not be duplicated (008 §7). Intents interact with [[position-system|positioning]] (shoving enemies to force action changes) and the status bank (Disrupt/Support map to debuffs/buffs). Open questions DEBATED Foresight vs tension. FreshToast initially preferred the tension of not knowing and worried telegraphing makes combat reactive rather than proactive, and easier; resolution: enemies signal future actions and the player is rewarded for reacting correctly — “let it simmer” (M01308–M01343, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-09). The proto’s 6-category distillation vs the 23-icon set: the 23 are the design target; GAME_DESIGN.md §5.4 lists them as content still to be authored (008 §7). Design history 2023-02-09 — original Foresight concept: a Slay-the-Spire-style mechanic where enemies signal their next move and the player attacks/defends/parries; heavily debated (FreshToast wary of it making combat reactive/easier), resolved as “enemies signal future actions and reward the player for reacting correctly,” left to simmer (M01308–M01343, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast). (Ledger R08.) 2023-02-10 — early adjuncts: signals group into offensive/defensive/both; abilities that do extra damage to attacking enemies; “no purely self-defensive abilities except something like Thane’s taunt”; optional fade-on-their-own floating status-text reminders for accessibility (M01402–M01406, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). 2024-08-20 — the full 23-indicator taxonomy was authored: 7 base types, dual-indicator precedence rules, all 23 combinations, the ~10-more-than-StS comparison, and the “Unknown is fun” note (M05439–M05444, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). (This is the current design taxonomy above.) Proto era — implemented as a 6-category distillation (Attack/Dual/Disrupt/Support/Defend/Gambit) re-rolled each round; the 23-icon set remains the target (008 §7, CANON · implemented ). Provenance Raw: M01308–M01343 (CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-02-09); M01402–M01406 (FreshToast, 2023-02-10); M05439–M05444 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2024-08-20) Cleaned: 000 Part 5 §Foresight; 000 §Enemy Intent Indicators (M05439 block) Docs: 008 §7 Enemy Intent Indicator System Ledger: rows R08 (M01308…), and the M05439-block mechanics rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Escalation Mechanic","group":"Systems","url":"systems/escalation.html","text":"Escalation Mechanic CANON A per-fight counter that makes battles deadlier the longer they run, pushing them toward a rapid close. Summary — Escalation starts at 0 and rises +1 per new round, +1 per critical hit (either side), +1 per character defeat (008 §2 Escalation Mechanic; proto src/core/combat/state.ts ). As it climbs, enemy attackers gain an accuracy/crit edge; when it first reaches 20 , every living hero gets a one-time +2 AP surge (see [[ap-economy]]). It is one of the twin “end fast” pressures alongside the [[shields-and-wounded|Wounded]] mechanic. First conceived 2023-02-17 as a mechanic that “causes fights to become deadlier as they go on” (M02383, FreshToast, 2023-02-17). Rules — implemented (proto) CANON · implemented (008 §2 Escalation Mechanic; proto src/core/combat/state.ts , resolution.ts ) Counter: starts at 0 ; +1 per new round, +1 per critical hit (either side), +1 per character defeat ( startNextRound , the crit/death branches in applyEffect ). Effect on lethality (enemy-only): only enemy attackers get an escalation edge — enemyEdge = floor(escalation / 4) , adding enemyEdge × 2 to both accuracy and critical chance (≈ +2% acc and +2% crit per 4 points, enemy-side only) ( resolveAttack ). The player side gets no penalty from escalation. Single break point at 20: the first time escalation reaches 20, every living hero gains a one-time +2 AP surge (and maxAp +2 for the fight) ( grantApBonus ). There is no escalation-10 break point and no recurring “extra AP each turn” in code. Scaling skills (designed, not wired): some characters ([[locke|Locke]], [[sable|Sable]], most powerful enemies) are designed to have skills that increase or scale with escalation; no such skills are wired into the proto yet (008 §2). Related systems AP: the escalation-20 surge is the proto’s only escalation→AP interaction. See [[ap-economy]]. Remnants (design intent, not coded): consuming OR letting a Remnant fade was meant to raise escalation, and character defeats both raise escalation AND leave a Remnant (M02507, M02618, FreshToast, 2023-02-19/20). The proto ships no Remnants — on defeat a character simply dies and the line compacts forward (008 §2 Remnant System; see [[position-system]]). Twin “end fast” pressure with the [[shields-and-wounded|Wounded]] mechanic (008 §2 Combat Duration). Open questions DEBATED Escalation curve. The notes specify a graded curve — +2% acc/point up to 10; then +1% acc crit/point up to 20 — with two break points (10 → +1 AP each; 20 → ongoing extra AP each turn) (M02620–M02624, FreshToast, 2023-02-20). The proto compresses this to a single floor(escalation/4) enemy-only acc+crit edge plus a one-time +2-AP surge at 20. The graded curve and the escalation-10 break point are unimplemented design intent (008 §2, DEBATED design intent vs implemented ). DEBATED Renaming “Escalation” was raised (“a bit of a mouthful and could be renamed”) but no replacement was chosen (Part 11). The proto keeps “Escalation” (008 §2). Design history 2023-02-17 — original concept: “Escalation is a mechanic which causes fights to become deadlier as they go on, bringing them to a more rapid close.” Begins at 0; increases per round, per certain skills, and by 1 on crits or a character dying; increases accuracy/crit/chance-effects by 2% per level ; some characters have escalation-scaling skills (M02383, FreshToast). 2023-02-19/20 — tied to the Remnant (“Shadow”) system: character defeats raise escalation AND leave a shadow; consuming or letting a shadow fade also raises escalation (M02507, M02618, FreshToast) — the Remnant coupling is unimplemented . 2023-02-20 — the graded two-break-point curve was written out: for each point up to 10, +2% accuracy; for each point up to 20, +1% accuracy and crit; first break point → every character instantly +1 AP ; second break point → all characters get an extra AP each turn (M02619–M02624, FreshToast). Later (proto era) — the graded curve was replaced by a single enemy-only floor(escalation/4) acc+crit edge, and the two break points collapsed to a single +2 AP surge at 20 ; +1 per round / +1 per crit / +1 per defeat survive (008 §2, DEBATED design intent vs implemented ). (This is the current presentation above; supersedes the graded curve.) Provenance Raw: M02383 (FreshToast, 2023-02-17); M02502, M02507, M02572 (2023-02-19/20); M02618–M02624 (FreshToast, 2023-02-20) Cleaned: 000 Part 8/9 §Escalation Mechanic Docs: 008 §2 Escalation Mechanic (Part 8 / Part 9); 008 §2 Combat Duration / “End Fast” Ledger: provenance rows under mechanics (M02383 and the M02618–M02624 escalation thread) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Navigation Compass (Compass-over-Maps)","group":"Systems","url":"systems/navigation-compass.html","text":"Navigation Compass (Compass-over-Maps) CANON How the player finds their way through the world and dungeons: a compass-first navigation model that deliberately avoids maps and minimaps. Summary — Soaring Cinders navigates by compass, not map . The proto ships CANON · implemented compass-style node navigation with no minimap : each dungeon node presents next-node choices labelled with compass-style prompts — “Descend,” “Press into the dark,” “Take the faster, darker way” — rather than a spatial map (008 §3; proto src/core/campaign/types.ts DungeonNode.compass , src/web/screens.ts ; GAME_DESIGN.md §14 Compass maps. No minimaps. ). This grew out of a 2021 design rule that “maps are taboo” — a Skyrim-inspired preference for a compass that “guides you to the fun” over a map you stare at (M00092, M00108, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13). The rule was later softened: the real taboo is a world too hard to traverse without a map, not maps themselves (M00115–M00116, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13). Description (current model) Compass over maps: navigation emphasizes a compass; any actual map is “very simple and not that necessary” (M00108, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13; 008 §3). No minimap in the proto: the vertical slice ships node navigation with no minimap ; each DungeonNode carries compass prompt labels for its onward choices (008 §3, §13; proto types.ts , screens.ts ). Node graph, never a spatial map: the playable dungeon “The Descent into Ajax’s Tomb” is a compass-navigated node graph — start → fight → branch → riverside camp → fight → boss — never a spatial map (008 §13; proto src/core/campaign/content.ts ). See [[dungeon-run-loop]] for the full run structure. Rules / Design intent Minimal Maps is one of the summarized core design pillars — “compass-based navigation preferred” (008 §Summary of Core Design Pillars; see [[overview]]). Maps are taboo (debated): the standing design rule, quoted verbatim from the notes — “Maps are boring. Maps are tedious. Maps are effective and productive. Maps are not fun. Maps are for boring nerds” (M00092, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13; 008 §3, §19). Purpose of a map, if used: only to “guide people to the fun, not because MAPS are fun” — they guide you to the fun (M00095–M00096, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13). The real taboo: a world so hard to traverse yourself that you’re forced onto the minimap — flagged against Final Fantasy and Witcher as what to avoid (M00115–M00117, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13; 008 §3). Related systems The river doubles as an in-world wayfinding aid — “the river also acts as a visual compass” (M04021, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). See [[river-flask]] and [[dungeon-run-loop]]. The compass node graph is the traversal layer of the [[dungeon-run-loop|dungeon run]]; nodes may attach a fight, reward, [[dungeon-run-loop#ambush|ambush]], or camp. Open questions DEBATED “Maps are taboo” is explicitly tagged debated: FreshToast disagreed about maps outright but accepted “pleasant alternatives,” liking Skyrim/Ghost-style maps that show where found things are (M00093, M00105–M00106, FreshToast, 2021-05-13). The compromise landed on compass-first with a de-emphasized simple map. DEBATED Elevation cues — FreshToast wished the compass had “a better way to indicate elevation” (M00111, FreshToast, 2021-05-13); unresolved. Design history 2021-05-13 — the “maps are taboo” design rule is stated: maps are effective but not fun, “for boring nerds” (M00092, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13). FreshToast pushes back, preferring Skyrim/Ghost-of-Tsushima maps that hint at cool stuff (M00093, M00105–M00106, FreshToast). 2021-05-13 — the position lands on compass-over-maps : “The use of the compass, I love! Emphasising the map even less… the actual map is very simple and not that necessary” (M00108, CrunchyConniptions); “The compass is a neat minimap, definitely better in some ways” (M00109, FreshToast). 2021-05-13 — Crunchy re-scopes the taboo: his real dislike is minimaps , and the real taboo is a world too hard to traverse without one (M00110, M00115–M00116, CrunchyConniptions) — refines the blanket anti-map stance . 2023-10-13 — the world’s river is designated a “visual compass,” reinforcing in-world wayfinding over UI maps (M04021, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). 2026-06-16 — implemented-code pass: the proto ships compass-style node navigation with no minimap , with per-node compass prompts, honouring the compass-over-maps pillar (008 §3, §13; proto campaign/types.ts , web/screens.ts ). (Current presentation above.) Provenance Raw: M00092–M00118 (CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2021-05-13); M04021 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 1 §Design Rules (Maps are taboo); 000 Part 3 §Navigation Docs: 008 §3 Navigation Systems; 008 §13 Vertical-Slice Dungeon; 008 §19 Design Rules; 008 §Summary of Core Design Pillars Ledger: the M00092–M00118 maps/compass thread in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"New Game+ System","group":"Systems","url":"systems/new-game-plus.html","text":"New Game+ System PROPOSED A Fire-Emblem-Three-Houses-inspired second-playthrough layer where a special resource lets you buy skills early and gamble on random gear rolls. Summary — In New Game+, players earn and spend a special resource to buy skills early and acquire random rolls on gear at varying rarities , inspired by Fire Emblem: Three Houses (M05684, FreshToast, 2024-09-04; 008 §15). Discovering a piece of gear unlocks it in a gacha menu for repeat random rolls; skills get cheaper the closer you are to their natural unlock; and obsolete gear can be broken down into the resource . The whole design assumes a classic levels-and-rarities gear model in the late game and is not implemented in the proto (008 §preamble lists NG+ among systems “not yet coded”). Core concept Inspired by Fire Emblem: Three Houses (M05684, 2024-09-04; 008 §15): Earn and spend a special resource in NG+. Buy skills early. Acquire random rolls on gear at varying rarities. Gear system (NG+) Once you discover a piece of gear at any rarity, unlock it for the gacha menu , where you can purchase a random roll of it at available rarities (008 §15). Rarities for purchase unlock as you progress through the game. Gear found in the previous playthrough is available for purchase. Lowest rarity is especially cheap — to encourage experimentation, or early acquisition of usually-unavailable build options. Unique pieces of gear are available but at a higher price and with fixed statistics . Gear purchased scales to level , and all gear can have its level (but not rarity) increased via the new resource. Skills purchase (NG+) Skills are expensive , but the closer you get to a skill’s natural unlock, the easier it is to acquire (008 §15). Decision-unlock skills remain unavailable for purchase — but you can acquire the item used to get alternative-path skills , meaning all 14 path skills can be unlocked in NG+ . Resource acquisition (NG+) Earned naturally alongside other rewards , but most efficiently acquired from quest completion and filling out the bestiary (008 §15). You can now break down gear into this resource in addition to what is naturally found — so obsolete gear serves an additional purpose and can always be converted into something useful. Assumptions The whole NG+ design leans on how gear scales into the late game (M05685, M05686, FreshToast, 2024-09-04; 008 §15): “This kinda assumes a lot about how gear scales into late game.” “But I assume it all has classic levels rarities” — i.e. levels inflate the numbers a bit and higher rarities give more/better bonuses. This ties NG+ to the still-unimplemented Tier + Quality + Rune equipment model (see [[progression-and-difficulty]] and 008 §9 Equipment Progression), which is likewise design-target-only. Open questions PROPOSED Whether gear actually uses “classic levels rarities” late-game — the entire NG+ economy depends on an assumption FreshToast flagged as unconfirmed (M05685-M05686, 2024-09-04; 008 §15 Assumptions). Design history 2024-09-04 — New Game+ proposed in full: a Three-Houses-inspired special resource to buy skills early and roll random gear; the gacha-unlock, cheap-lowest-rarity, unique-fixed-stat, level-scaling, skill-cost-near-unlock, all-14-path-skills, and gear-breakdown rules (M05684, FreshToast, 2024-09-04; 008 §15). 2024-09-04 — Follow-up caveat: the design “assumes a lot about how gear scales into late game,” presuming classic levels rarities (M05685, M05686, FreshToast, 2024-09-04; 008 §15 Assumptions). 2026-06-16 — Implemented-code pass confirms NG+ is not yet coded — it remains notes-era design intent (008 §preamble). Provenance Raw: M05684, M05685, M05686 (FreshToast, 2024-09-04) Cleaned: 000 §New Game+ (lines ~7479-7496) Docs: 008 §15 New Game+ System Ledger: Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (mechanics rows)"},{"title":"Game Type & Structure (Overview)","group":"Systems","url":"systems/overview.html","text":"Game Type Structure (Overview) EVOLVED The top-level shape of Soaring Cinders: what kind of game it is, how a session flows, and how the vision moved from a co-op online dungeon-crawler to a single-player JRPG. Summary — Soaring Cinders is a turn-based JRPG with Dark Souls / soulslike adventure influences , character-based like Genshin Impact, focused on party building optimization, battle, and exploration. The current articulation frames the first game as single-player , with co-op/multiplayer treated as a future or sequel aspiration rather than a launch feature (008 §1; Parts 9-24). Several Section-1 framings — a 100-floor dungeon, online cross-platform co-op — are recorded here as early-vision concepts that were later superseded (008 §1 EVOLVED ). Description (current vision) Genre: JRPG with “coop dark souls adventure elements”; combat feel sits between Dark Souls and NieR: Automata (008 §1, §2). Perspective: Third person; whether it is traditional top-down 2D or isometric 3D was still under discussion (008 §1). Character system: Character-based, “similar to Genshin Impact” — a roster the player builds a party from (008 §1). Structure: A [[dungeon-run-loop|dungeon-crawling]] adventure. The playable proto ships a compass-navigated node graph (start → fight → branch → camp → fight → boss), never a spatial map (008 §13 CANON · implemented ). See the Design history below for the earlier 100-floor framing. Focus areas: Party building optimization, battle, and exploration (008 §1). Core game loop Bonfire and estus system from Dark Souls — rest points restore you; healing is scarce (008 §1). See [[shields-and-wounded]] for the current health/healing model. You never lose items or equipment you find — this is a load-bearing rule that survives across every iteration (008 §1; §9 “Never lose items or equipment you find”; §Summary of Core Design Pillars “Persistent Progress”). Foster camaraderie by incentivizing playing with the same people again and again, so the player gets “the feeling of ‘we’ve been through so much together’” (008 §1). (This was written for the co-op vision; the sentiment carries into the single-player party’s cohesion.) Core design pillars The doc’s summary of pillars (008 §Summary of Core Design Pillars). Pillars marked below shifted with the single-player reframing: Skill-Based Rewards — performance determines reward type (see [[treasure-and-rewards]]). Player Choice — adjust difficulty on the go, not at the start (see [[progression-and-difficulty|difficulty]]). No Time Limits — freedom and exploration over timed pressure; “time limits are taboo” (008 §19). Minimal Maps — compass-based navigation preferred; “maps are taboo (debated)” (008 §19). Combat Balance — between Dark Souls weight and NieR fluidity. Strategic Depth — turn-based with [[enemy-intent|intent indicators]] and [[position-system|positioning]]. Persistent Progress — never lose found items/equipment. Exploration Rewards — treasure hunting with meaningful rewards. EVOLVED Coop-Focused (“start solo-able, scale to require teamwork”) and Character Collection (spirit/gacha with anti-meta incentives) belong to the early co-op vision; the first game is single-player (008 §Summary; Parts 9-24). See [[spirit-collection-system]] and [[anti-meta-systems]]. Open questions DEBATED 2D top-down vs isometric 3D perspective was never settled (008 §1). EVOLVED The degree to which co-op ever returns (as a sequel/future goal) is aspirational, not scheduled (008 §1). Design history 2021-03-13 — First framing of “the gaming part”: “Coop dark souls adventure with engaging combat” (M00040, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-03-13). This is the origin of the whole game-type concept. 2021-05-25 — Early core concept solidifies as a 100-floor dungeon , character-based (like Genshin), co-op online ; early floors solo-able, later floors increasingly complex so co-op becomes central (Ghost of Tsushima nightmare-raids reference) (M00128, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25; 008 §1, §13 Difficulty Scaling). 2023 onward (Parts 9-24) — supersedes the above. The first game is reframed as a single-player JRPG ; the 100-floor dungeon, online co-op, and cross-platform items are tagged EVOLVED early concepts, with co-op kept only as a future/sequel aspiration (008 §1 EVOLVED ). 2026-06-16 — Implemented-code pass: the proto ships the single-player structure as a compass-navigated node graph vertical slice; many notes-era systems (Remnants, Jet/Shard customization, the two-path system, NG+, anti-meta, spirit collection) are documented but not yet coded (008 §preamble). Provenance Raw: M00040 (CrunchyConniptions, 2021-03-13); M00128 (CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25) Cleaned: 000 Part 1 §Core Game Concept Docs: 008 §1 Core Game Concept; 008 §Summary of Core Design Pillars Ledger: row R01 (“Core game concept: 100-floor dungeon…”), Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Party Consultation — Outcome Resolution","group":"Systems","url":"systems/party-consultation.html","text":"Party Consultation — Outcome Resolution PROPOSED The dialogue mechanic where the player has [[locke|Locke]] consult exactly one party member at a prompt — and the rules for how that choice pays off. Summary — At certain prompts the player has Locke consult another party member , and the choice resolves against a hidden “correct answer” (M01893, FreshToast, 2023-02-14; 008 §20). Outcomes are usually neutral ; a smart or lucky pick yields a good outcome; it rarely backfires humorously , always in good fun. Crucially, there is a correct answer, and one or more characters knows it — so consulting the right party member is rewarded. This page records only the outcome-resolution rules ; the roster and flavour of the mechanic live in the narrative/dialogue docs. Rules / effects (outcome resolution) From the 2023-02-14 exchange (008 §20 PROPOSED ): Usually neutral — “most of the time it’s a neutral outcome” (M01894, FreshToast, 2023-02-14). Smart or lucky → good outcome — “pick something smart or lucky and you get a good outcome” (M01894). Rarely backfires, humorously — “rarely it backfires humorously when somebody messes up,” and “it’s always in good fun” (M01895, FreshToast; M01896, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). There is a correct answer — “but there is a correct answer” (M01897, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). One or more characters knows it — “one or more of the characters knows it” (M01898, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14). Consulting the character who knows is the path to the good outcome. The team judged it “neat and easy to do honestly” (M01899, FreshToast, 2023-02-14) — i.e. low-cost, high-flavour. Related systems The consult prompt is a dialogue mechanic ; the [[shopkeeper-system|recurring Shopkeeper]] and the “dialogue never repeats” thread sit in the same conversational-systems space (008 §20). Because the player picks exactly one party member, the mechanic leans on party composition — see [[overview]] and the party roster in [[locke|Locke]]'s circle. Open questions PROPOSED The mechanic is a design intent, not a decided/implemented system; exact prompt frequency, how “correct answer” knowledge is surfaced to the player, and the roster of consultable characters are not specified in 008 (008 §20). Design history 2023-02-14 — Consultation outcome model defined in a single exchange: neutral by default, good on a smart/lucky pick, rare humorous backfire, and a hidden correct answer that one or more characters knows; deemed “neat and easy to do” (M01893-M01899, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14; 008 §20). Provenance Raw: M01893-M01899 (FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14) Cleaned: 000 §Party Consultation (lines ~3184-3189) Docs: 008 §20 Miscellaneous Mechanics — Party-Consultation — Outcome Resolution Ledger: Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv (mechanics rows)"},{"title":"Combat Position System","group":"Systems","url":"systems/position-system.html","text":"Combat Position System CANON Where each combatant stands governs what they can hit, what can hit them, and how skills behave — the tactical spine of turn-based combat. Summary — The finalized design is a 2×3 grid (2 rows × 3 columns = 6 spaces): the front row are Vanguards (orange), the back row are Sentinels (yellow), built with 4 active characters in mind (008 §8 Combat Position System v2.0; M05108, FreshToast; M05149, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-12-20). Movement is simple and perfectly deterministic — never random, generally one step. This grid explicitly replaced the earlier Darkest-Dungeon-style row line, which the team judged “an inferior imitation” (M05124, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-12-20). The playable proto ships a simplified vertical-slice version — a single-axis rank line (heroes ranks 1–4, enemies ranks 1–3, rank 1 = front) — not the grid; the grid remains the documented design target (008 §8, DEBATED design intent vs implemented ). Description — the 2×3 grid (design target, notes-design) Grid: 2 rows × 3 columns = 6 spaces, built for 4 active characters (it could occasionally flex — events with only 2, or jumping in a 5th NPC) (008 §8; M05108, FreshToast, 2023-12-20). Rows / roles: Front row = Vanguard , which must be orange ; back row = Sentinels , which must be yellow (000 lines 8135–8136; M05149, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-12-20). The names Vanguard/Sentinel were liked on sight (M05147, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-12-20). Why 6: Largely arbitrary but deliberate — as few spaces as possible to avoid becoming a “bad XCOM”; 2 deep (not 1) for easy lateral movement; 6 is visually pleasing over 5s/7s/11s (008 §8; answering M05124’s “why 6?”). Movement: Perfectly deterministic — never random, generally one step, no contradiction-chains (“if that would result in weird movement or some contradiction, then no moving happens”). You always know where a shoved character goes. Inspired lightly by XCOM and D D (M05104, FreshToast, 2023-12-20); comparisons to Into the Breach, Etrian Odyssey, Fire Emblem, Yu-Gi-Oh (008 §8). Targeting: Most actions can hit someone in front or in back (some hit either). Area actions hit all 3 spaces in a set, or 2 in a lane (top/middle/bottom straight line — e.g. a big thrust). Many actions also care about where the actor stands (008 §8). Tactics, not just targeting: “The point of the position system is not just a matter of targeting, enemies change tactics and become more/less effective based on position as well” (M02538, FreshToast, 2023-02-20). CANON AoE vs multi-position enemy bonus An AoE that hits an enemy which occupies multiple positions deals bonus damage — “as if hit twice but not double damage, maybe an extra 20%.” This is load-bearing for the [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]] climax, where he fills all 4 positions (000 lines 3141–3142; M01842, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). Rules — the shipped rank line (proto-implemented) CANON · implemented The proto does not ship the 2×3 grid. It implements a single-axis rank line : rank 1 = front (closest to the opposing side), higher numbers = further back; heroes have up to 4 ranks, enemies up to 3 (008 §2 Battle Mechanics; proto src/core/combat/types.ts Position = 1|2|3|4 ). The GameBible and GAME_DESIGN.md §5.3 frame this same shipped system as the Front / Mid / Back band model. Rank governs which ranks an actor can act from ( fromRanks ) and which ranks a skill can target ( targetRanks ) (008 §2). Shoves are deterministic single steps via the move skill effect (008 §8). On a defeat, survivors compact forward to close the gap (008 §2; proto compactPositions ). This forward-compaction replaces the scrapped corpse system and the [[escalation|Remnant]] trace, neither of which is coded. Ambush scatters the party’s starting ranks: an ambush node flag triggers a Fisher–Yates shuffle of hero ranks before the fight builds (008 §2 Encounter Initiative; M?—see 008). Related systems Turn order is driven by Speed/Initiative , not position — the implemented rank line has no position-based turn order and no movement cost (008 §8 Formation Mechanics note). See [[ap-economy]] for the action budget spent to move and act. [[rank-targeting-ui]] — the PROPOSED UI that telegraphs a skill’s own from-rank/target-rank. Formations, pairing, and position-based team skills are DEBATED design layered on the grid (008 §8 Party/Team Skills); not implemented. [[locke|Locke]]'s leadership is designed to be expressed through formation mechanics — “she is glue that brings them together” (008 §18). Open questions DEBATED The 2×3 grid vs the shipped single-axis rank line: the grid is the documented design target; the rank line is the vertical-slice implementation (008 §8, DEBATED design intent vs implemented ). SCRAPPED for first game A 3×2 variant (adding a centre 3rd column) was considered — “just complicated… didn’t fix some of my gripes with DD but I liked how it looked” (M05141, FreshToast, 2023-12-20) — left open only as possible post-first-game experimentation. SCRAPPED Behind-area / pincer combat (an area behind both sides, surrounding/assassin play) was rejected as “a step too far in terms of scope”; recorded as a Finalized Design Decision: “No behind-area combat mechanics (too much scope)” (008 §2 Behind-Area/Pincer, Part 23). Design history 2023-02-02 — original concept: a battle map with a positioning line , “Back Back / Front front,” Darkest Dungeon Radiant Historia style (M00859, M00851, CrunchyConniptions; “Back Back / Front front” is M00860, FreshToast). (Ledger R05.) 2023-02-07 — DD-style refinement: a line of 4 positions per side , blue = character / orange = enemy, back rank = back 2, front rank = front 2 (reversed for enemies), simplified pips (M01084, M01093–M01098, FreshToast, 2023-02-07). 2023-02-09 onward — the frontliner/backliner DD-style role system (Part 9) carried the Vanguard-vs-Sentinel role philosophy forward but kept literal DD rows. 2023-12-20 — the Combat Position System v2.0 (2×3 grid) was adopted to explicitly replace the prior DD-style row system: “our previous version was essentially an inferior imitation of Darkest Dungeon… Addressing this was crucial” (M05124, CrunchyConniptions). Front row = Vanguard (orange), back row = Sentinels (yellow), stated as “sentinals must be yellow and vanguards orange” (M05149, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-12-20) — supersedes the DD line . The grid also removed the need for a corpse system and forced 4v4s. (This is the current design presentation above.) Color-swap correction: the raw phrasing lists the back role first — “sentinals must be yellow and vanguards orange” (M05149) — so a reader following word-order could wrongly map front→yellow / back→orange. The canonical mapping per the 000 notes is unambiguous: Front = Vanguard = orange; Back = Sentinels = yellow (000 lines 8135–8136). Do not swap them. Later (proto era) — the playable proto ships a simplified single-axis rank line (heroes 1–4, enemies 1–3) as a vertical-slice stand-in; the 2×3 grid remains the design target (008 §2/§8, DEBATED design intent vs implemented ). Provenance Raw: M00851, M00859, M00861 (CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02); M00860 (FreshToast, 2023-02-02); M01084–M01109 (FreshToast, 2023-02-07); M01842 (2023-02-13); M02538 (2023-02-20); M05104–M05169 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-12-20) Cleaned: 000 Part 20 §Combat Position System; 000 lines 8135–8136, 3141–3142 Docs: 008 §8 Combat Position System v2.0; 008 §2 Battle Mechanics Ledger: rows R05 (M00859/M00860/M00851), R07 (M01084…), R10 (M01842), R14 (M02538) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Progression & Difficulty","group":"Systems","url":"systems/progression-and-difficulty.html","text":"Progression Difficulty CANON How the player grows over a run and how the game’s challenge is tuned — the settled position being that difficulty is adjusted on the go through in-game means , never chosen from a menu at the start. Summary — Progression is deliberately bare-bones and level-gated, with no skill trees and no enemy scaling ; the point of leveling is “the illusion of progress” because “the skill is what matters” (008 §9). Difficulty is built in as an inherent challenge , with in-game (not menu) tools letting the player nudge it up or down: the strong, settled design position is that a player should never pick a difficulty at the start of the game but instead choose challenge later, intuitively, as part of play (M05422-M05426, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-19; 008 §16). Character progression (design intent) Progression uses a level-gated unlock cadence — no skill trees, no enemy scaling — where leveling delivers skills and traits rather than raw power. Leveling is “the illusion of progress”; “the skill is what matters” (008 §9 CANON ). Final structure (20 levels) (008 §9): Level 1: 2 starting skills + base attributes. Levels 2-20: technically +1 attribute every level; odd levels grant character skills, even levels grant passive traits . 4 character-skill slots; end with ~8 skills (start with 2) and ~5 passive traits. Linear (not exponential) scaling — HP and base damage rise only a little, to preserve tactical/equipment depth; enemies do NOT scale . DEBATED Alternative structure (10 levels): 3 starter skills; levels 2-10 alternate trait/skill. The 20-level structure is the leading/final-favored version, with the 10-level variant as the alternative; exact HP numbers were never nailed down (008 §9). SCRAPPED Skill trees are an explicit anti-feature — listed twice in the Part 3 “Fuck-this list” (“Fuck skill trees”) (M00864, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-02; 008 §9). Any earlier XP-based / Skyrim-skill-tree / skill-points-per-level framing revived a source-rejected feature. DEBATED design intent vs implemented: the proto implements no leveling at all — no XP, no level, no per-level unlock, no enemy scaling. Heroes ship with a fixed 4-skill kit and fixed stats (008 §9; proto src/core/combat/content.ts ; GAME_DESIGN.md §14 “No leveling. Progression is equipment + skills only.”). The “no skill trees / no enemy scaling / linear scaling” intent is honoured; the level-gated cadence itself is unimplemented design intent. Equipment progression (the Tier + Quality + Rune model) is a separate design and is not implemented in the proto (008 §9 Equipment Progression DEBATED ). Run-level progression that IS implemented — degrading max-HP, healing jets, relics — lives in [[shields-and-wounded]] and [[treasure-and-rewards]]. Difficulty philosophy (current position) The settled position, stated in full in 2024 (M05422-M05426, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-19; 008 §16): “Difficulty is weird. I think I have decided how to make that work.” (M05422, M05423) Challenge the player and encourage them to overcome the challenge — “so we make the game inherently challenging” (M05424, M05425). But there are ways for the player to adjust that difficulty lower or higher through in-game stuff (M05425). “I completely reject the idea of having the player select a difficulty in the beginning of the game. It makes no sense if you think about it. The player does not know what that choice means. Instead give them that choice later as part of the game in an intuitive manner that nudges them to take on challenges.” (M05425, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-19) TL;DR: “ALLOW PLAYER TO ADJUST DIFFICULTY ON THE GO.” (M05426, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-19) A YouTube video on difficulty in games was shared as “probably the best video I’ve seen that discusses difficulty” (M05427, M05428, 2024-05-19; link not preserved in the doc). Difficulty scaling (early framing) EVOLVED The early scaling picture: early floors so easy people can solo them, growing more complex as you progress until reliance on co-op becomes central (Ghost of Tsushima nightmare-raids reference) (008 §13 Difficulty Scaling). This co-op-centric endpoint is superseded — the first game is single-player (Parts 9-24; see [[overview]]). In-game difficulty levers (design intent) The “adjust on the go” philosophy is meant to be delivered through in-game systems rather than a menu: Dynamic-difficulty challenges: areas/dungeons accessible early but challenging for that progression point — early = a reward for playing smartly, later = a return trip for a boost to tackle a greater challenge (M01819, M01820, FreshToast, 2023-02-12; 008 §Difficulty Scaling reference). PROPOSED Player-controlled dungeon challenge: a chalice-dungeon-style loop where “you make the dungeon harder, your reward is better,” the challenge level set by the player via a risk mechanic (M04547-M04569, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-22; 008 §13 PROPOSED Chalice-Dungeon loop). Related to the DEBATED Risk/Reward Luck Bonus (008 §8). Escalation — the per-fight [[escalation]] counter is part of the “end fast” combat philosophy, making individual fights deadlier the longer they run. Music / progression motif A thematic progression layer: “as you unlock more of the game… gather more of the great souls,” more instruments are unlocked, “eventually unlocking the voice at the end” — “all you need is one good track” (M00245, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-07-01; 008 §9 Music/Progression System). Open questions DEBATED 20-level vs 10-level cadence, and exact HP numbers (008 §9). DEBATED Whether the chalice-dungeon / risk-based difficulty lever is adopted, and how “time”/progress in a dungeon is measured for a luck bonus (008 §8, §13). Design history 2021-05-25 — Difficulty scaling first framed for the co-op vision: early floors solo-able, later floors require co-op (Ghost of Tsushima nightmare raids) (M00128, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25; 008 §13). 2021-07-01 — Music-as-progression motif: unlock instruments/great souls, “eventually unlocking the voice at the end” (M00245, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-07-01). 2023-02-02 — Skill trees explicitly rejected on the “Fuck-this list” (M00864, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-02) — forecloses any XP/skill-tree progression. 2023-02-11 — A no-level “we don’t level up / no leveling as our identity” stance is floated, then left “pin on this / debate ongoing” (Part 6; 008 §9 EVOLVED ). 2023 (Part 8) — supersedes the no-level stance. The team re-adopts a bare-bones level system to deliver skills/traits (the 20-level or 10-level cadence), keeping the no-grind, no-enemy-scaling intent (008 §9 CANON , EVOLVED ). 2024-05-19 — current difficulty position. “Difficulty is weird… allow player to adjust difficulty on the go”; menu-select difficulty explicitly rejected (M05422-M05426, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-19; 008 §16). Provenance Raw: M00128 (CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25); M00245 (CrunchyConniptions, 2021-07-01); M00864 (spirit_noodle, 2023-02-02); M01819-M01820 (FreshToast, 2023-02-12); M05422-M05428 (CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-19) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 §Progression; 000 §Difficulty (lines ~6752-6758) Docs: 008 §9 Progression Systems; 008 §13 Difficulty Scaling; 008 §16 Difficulty System Ledger: rows for “No skill trees”, “Progression-system design direction”, “Introduce challenges/dungeons/areas accessible early”, Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Rank-Targeting / Combat-Readability UI","group":"Systems","url":"systems/rank-targeting-ui.html","text":"Rank-Targeting / Combat-Readability UI PROPOSED Player-side visual feedback for how a skill’s rank/target range works and what the player has selected — distinct from the enemy-side intent telegraphs. Summary — PROPOSED A player-facing readability layer for the [[position-system|rank/position system]]: colored indicators show which rank a skill can be used from (yellow) and which it can target (blue), potential targets are highlighted (with an AoE link), selection is made obvious via glow/halo, and enemy health that would be deleted is previewed Darkest-Dungeon-style (008 §7 Rank-Targeting UI; M01030–M01047, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-06). This is player-side targeting feedback — distinct from and complementary to the [[enemy-intent|Enemy Intent Indicators]]; the two should not be duplicated. All conceived in a single 2023-02-06 readability thread; not implemented. Description PROPOSED (008 §7 Rank-Targeting / Combat-Readability UI; all from the M01030–M01047 thread, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-06) Rank indicators on a skill: a yellow indicator shows which rank a skill can be used from ; a blue indicator shows which rank it can target . Left arrow = left rank (back for party, front for enemy); right is the opposite. Framed as more immediately intuitive than text like “Skill only usable from back rank” (M01030, M01031, M01032, M01035, FreshToast). Iteration / simplification: the blue symbol could be dropped so only the yellow appears (M01034, FreshToast). Target highlighting: instead of a target symbol, highlight the enemies that are potential targets , with a visual link between them for AoE effects . Visual reminders sit underneath enemies; Darkest Dungeon does something similar with its “4 dots” (M01033, M01036, M01037, FreshToast). Selection feedback: when a character is selected it should be more obvious — a drop-shadow glow under the character, a halo , or a low-effort eye-catching arrow; the health bar could change color or glow (from the same thread). Enemy health preview (Darkest-Dungeon style): highlight the portion of an enemy’s health bar that would be deleted by the selected action (“the enemy themselves are highlighted and the portion of their [health] that would be deleted is highlighted” — noted as harder to implement). Otherwise show enemy health as numbers in the lower UI (M01042, M01043, M01044, M01047, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). Related systems Reads directly off the [[position-system|rank/position system]] — the yellow/blue arrows visualize a skill’s fromRanks / targetRanks . Distinct from [[enemy-intent]]: that telegraphs what the enemy is about to do; this shows the player’s own skill range and selection. Complementary, not duplicative (008 §7 note). Color note: the same 000 UI thread uses blue for a character’s position, orange for an enemy’s position on the combat map (000 line 1852) — separate from the yellow/blue skill-range indicators here. Open questions PROPOSED Whether to keep both the yellow (used-from) and blue (target) indicators, or drop the blue so only yellow appears (M01034, FreshToast, 2023-02-06). PROPOSED The DD-style health-deletion preview is the preferred option but noted as “more difficult to implement” (M01042, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-06). Whole system is PROPOSED / notes-design — not implemented in the proto. Design history 2023-02-06 — the entire proposal was worked out in a single combat-readability thread: yellow = used-from-rank / blue = target-rank arrows, the option to drop blue, potential-target highlighting with AoE links, selection glow/halo feedback, and the DD-style enemy-health-deletion preview with numbers in the lower UI (M01030–M01047, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). (Ledger R06.) — no later revision recorded; remains PROPOSED . Provenance Raw: M01030–M01047 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-06) Cleaned: 000 lines 1853–1871 (Battle Positioning / UI Indicators) Docs: 008 §7 Rank-Targeting / Combat-Readability UI (lines 476, 478, 480) Ledger: row R06 (M01030…M01047) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Reserve Skills System (Locke's Borrow)","group":"Systems","url":"systems/reserve-skills-system.html","text":"Reserve Skills System (Locke’s Borrow) DEBATED Locke’s signature party mechanic: she borrows a designated skill from a benched ally, once per battle — leadership and versatility over personal power. Summary — The Reserve Skills System lets [[locke|Locke]] borrow a skill from a character in reserve to use, once per battle ; each character has a designated reserve skill that Locke can call on (M01391, FreshToast, 2023-02-10; 008 §5). It exists to express Locke’s design identity: her strength is leadership and versatility rather than personal power , making her dependent on and defined by her party (M01392, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). The mechanic is designed but not implemented — it is documented in the GameBible and GAME_DESIGN.md §5.8 but sits on the proto’s Still-deferred list, absent from the combat reducer and UI (008 §5, DEBATED design intent vs implemented ). Description — the borrow mechanic (notes-design) What it does: Locke borrows one skill from a character in reserve (a benched party member), usable once per battle (M01391, FreshToast, 2023-02-10; 000 Part 5 §Reserve Skills System). Designated reserve skill: each character has a single designated reserve skill that Locke may use — not their whole kit (M01391; 000 Part 5). Design intent: reflects that “Locke’s strength is in her leadership and versatility, rather than personal strength and thus dependent on her party” (M01392, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). It complements Locke’s support identity — she “warps around her party with a variety of skills and useful support effects” (M01881, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). Rules / status DEBATED design intent vs implemented — not yet wired in. The borrow-one-ally’s-skill mechanic is designed ( GAME_DESIGN.md §5.8 ) but explicitly listed as Still-deferred ; it is not in the combat reducer or UI (008 §5; proto GAME_DESIGN.md §17 Still deferred ). Locke’s implemented kit is instead a fixed spear set — Pierce, Sunder, Phalanx, and the finisher Emberlance (008 §5; proto src/core/combat/content.ts ). The exact implementation of reserve skills was flagged unresolved in the notes themselves (000 Part 5). Related systems Ties into the broader borrowing-power motif: in the [[two-path-system|two-path]] lore, “the original Undying is very good at borrowing powers” — a thread that later informs Locke’s leadership-through-borrowing design (M00244, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-26; 008 §10). Layered on the [[position-system|position system]] and reserve/bench roster — a benched character is a “character in reserve.” See also the DEBATED [[position-system|team skills]] built around party pairings (008 §Team Skills). Locke’s kit and role: see [[locke|Locke]] and [[character-skills|character skills]]. Open questions DEBATED Exact implementation is unresolved — the notes explicitly list “exact implementation of reserve skills” as an open item (000 Part 5); the proto ships Locke’s four spear skills only (008 §5). DEBATED Whether the once-per-battle borrow ever ships, given it’s on the Still-deferred list (008 §5; GAME_DESIGN.md §17 ). Design history 2023-02-10 — original concept: FreshToast proposes “RESERVE SKILLS” — “locke gets to borrow a skill from a character in reserve to use, once per battle. Each character has a designated reserve skill, which Locke can use,” justified by Locke’s leadership/versatility identity over personal strength (M01390–M01392, FreshToast, 2023-02-10). (Origin.) 2023-02-13 — reinforced by Locke’s support-warper framing: she “has some movement and modular abilities but does not need to even slot them” and “warps around her party with a variety of skills and useful support effects” (M01880–M01881, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). 2026-06-16 — implemented-code pass: the borrow mechanic is documented but placed on the Still-deferred list; Locke’s implemented kit is the Pierce / Sunder / Phalanx / Emberlance spear set instead (008 §5; proto content.ts , GAME_DESIGN.md §17 ). (Current status: designed, not implemented.) Provenance Raw: M01390–M01392 (FreshToast, 2023-02-10); M01880–M01881 (FreshToast, 2023-02-13) Cleaned: 000 Part 5 §Reserve Skills System (notes lines 2365–2367; 2087–2088; unresolved-implementation note 2522) Docs: 008 §5 Reserve Skills System / Locke’s Borrow; 007 §Reserve Skills System; 002 §Locke Ledger: the M01390–M01392 Reserve Skills thread in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Shields & Wounded State","group":"Systems","url":"systems/shields-and-wounded.html","text":"Shields Wounded State CANON Two survivability mechanics: a one-shot shield that saves you from a killing blow, and the Wounded flag that makes low-HP combatants more fragile. Summary — Shields are a one-shot “extra life”: every character starts a fight with exactly 1 shield , and a blow that would be lethal instead drops them to 1 HP and shatters the shield; shields reset every encounter (008 §2 Shield System; proto src/core/combat/state.ts ). Wounded = under half HP (strictly 50% of current max ); a Wounded defender takes +25% damage from every hit (008 §5 Wounded Mechanic; proto resolution.ts , WOUNDED_TAKEN = 1.25 ). Both serve the “end fast” battle philosophy — shields let players take risks, Wounded lets either side pick off weakened combatants. The shield began as a 2-shield system (M02484, FreshToast, 2023-02-19) and was revised to 1 because “death on 2nd shield break is unintuitive” (M02495, M02496, FreshToast, 2023-02-19). Shield System — implemented (proto) CANON · implemented One-shot protection (“extra life”) (008 §2; proto state.ts ) Every character starts a fight with exactly 1 shield . When a blow would be lethal and the shield is intact, the character is instead dropped to 1 HP and the shield is consumed (“shield shatters — saved from a killing blow”). Shields reset to intact every encounter ( makeCharacter / createCombat set shield: true ; the lethal-blow check in applyEffect ). It stacks with the Protection buff , a separate once-per-fight effect that halves the next incoming hit before the shield ever triggers ( status.ts protection ). Player-side mechanic; maybe a few bosses or Lysander’s party can break shields early — e.g. a 2nd-phase [[lysander|Lysander]] opening that immediately removes a shield (M02494, M02585, FreshToast, 2023-02-19/20). Purpose: gives leeway for big-damage attacks, makes death less imminent, lets players take risks / pick fights when estus is out (“the thing I like most about a shield is they let you take more risks,” M02553, FreshToast, 2023-02-20). Wounded Mechanic — implemented (proto) CANON · implemented (008 §5 Wounded Mechanic; proto resolution.ts isWounded , WOUNDED_TAKEN = 1.25 ) “Wounded” = strictly 50% of current max HP. A Wounded defender takes +25% damage from every hit. Applies to both sides : the player picks off weakened enemies; enemies threaten weakened party members. Reinforces the “end fast” philosophy (final solution, M04834–M04845, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-30). Related systems Both mechanics push the “end fast” combat philosophy alongside [[escalation]] and finisher gating (008 §2 Combat Duration). See [[ap-economy]]. Wounded is the surviving piece of the [[damage-types|Healing Health overhaul]] — the degrading-max-HP ceiling and Wounded were adopted together (Part 18). Shields replaced the scrapped corpse/[[position-system|Remnant]] system as the frontline-defense answer (M02514, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-20). Open questions DEBATED Thane’s 2 shields not coded. The notes single out [[thane|Thane]] as special with 2 shields (“Thane probably gets a 3rd shield eventually,” M02491, FreshToast, 2023-02-19); the proto gives all characters exactly 1 with no per-character override (008 §2, DEBATED design intent vs implemented ). DEBATED Weight-of-death concern. CrunchyConniptions repeatedly flagged that a “second chance” mechanic reduces the impact of death and slows momentum, suggesting it might work best as a boss-only mechanic (M02512, M02581, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-20). FreshToast’s counter: it won’t make the game much easier “since we just account for it in the numbers” (M02588, FreshToast, 2023-02-20). DEBATED Per-skill wounded interactions and a well-rested bonus are mentioned in the notes but the proto implements only the flat +25%-damage-taken amplifier (008 §5, DEBATED design intent vs implemented ). Design history 2023-02-19 — original 2-shield concept: a character begins each fight with 2 shields and cannot recover them except through extraordinary means; a hit dropping HP to 0 loses a shield; losing both = defeated. Some characters might get passive boosts on shield loss; Thane probably gets a 3rd shield; really scary bosses (2nd-phase Lysander) can break shields early (M02483–M02494, FreshToast). 2023-02-19 — revised to 1 shield : “I’m realising now that death on 2nd shield break is unintuitive” → “Instead have 1 shield which puts you to 1 instead of 0 once” (M02495, M02496, FreshToast) — supersedes the 2-shield version; the proto matches this . 2023-02-20 — shields settled as player-side only (“shadows for everybody, shields for player characters and maybe a few bosses or perhaps just Lysander’s party,” M02511) and confirmed to reset every fight (M02604, FreshToast). CrunchyConniptions proposed doubling down to fully replace the shadow/corpse system, mostly giving frontliners shields (M02514, M02515). 2023-10-30 — the Wounded mechanic (final solution) was decided: under-half-HP characters become Wounded and more vulnerable; same for enemies; a well-rested counterpart floated; reinforces “end fast” (M04834–M04845, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). (Ledger R26.) Later (proto era) — Wounded implemented as a flat +25% damage taken below 50% of current max HP; shield implemented as the 1-shield drop-to-1 version resetting each encounter (008 §2/§5, CANON · implemented ). (Current presentation above.) Provenance Raw: M02483–M02515 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-19/20); M02531, M02553, M02585–M02604 (2023-02-20); M04834–M04845 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-30) Cleaned: 000 Part 9 §Shield System; 000 Game Mechanics §Wounded Mechanic Final Solution (lines 7723–7735) Docs: 008 §2 Shield System (Part 9; confirmed Part 17); 008 §5 Wounded Mechanic (Part 18 / Part 14) Ledger: row R26 (Wounded, M04834…) and the M02483–M02515 shield thread in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Recurring Shopkeeper System","group":"Systems","url":"systems/shopkeeper-system.html","text":"Recurring Shopkeeper System PROPOSED One single, recurring shopkeeper who “appears everywhere because of game reasons,” always has something new to say, and never blocks your access to the menu — a silly (possibly creepy, possibly scamming) lil guy doing his best. Summary — A PROPOSED system built around one recurring shopkeeper who shows up throughout the game and always has fresh dialogue, grown out of a “make a game where dialogue never repeats” discussion. He is quest-linked (has some element of quest progression), unvoiced , and not a barrier to the menu — his dialogue happens on the side, Slay-the-Spire style, so it never blocks the shop. The team’s guiding note is “keep it weird” , with an open DEBATED idea that he could be a creepy, subtly-scamming merchant. This is a design intent, not a decided or implemented system. Core concept PROPOSED One single, recurring shopkeeper who “appears everywhere because of game reasons” and “always has something new to say” — “a silly lil guy doing his best” (M04428, M04429, CrunchyConniptions; M04431, FreshToast, 2023-10-20). Quest-linked: FreshToast had “planned for a shopkeeper who you meet a lot and has some element of quest progression” (M04430, FreshToast, 2023-10-20). Delivery — Slay-the-Spire style, non-blocking Unvoiced and not a barrier to the menu: “I think if we keep it unvoiced and not a barrier to the menu we good” (M04436, FreshToast, 2023-10-20). Dialogue happens on the side , like Slay the Spire : “could work like slay the spire” / “dialogue on the side” (M04438, M04439, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-20). FreshToast: “Yeah I’d want it exactly like that” (M04441, FreshToast, 2023-10-20). The shop menu is always accessible; the character talk is decorative flavor layered beside it. “Keep it weird” The team explicitly embraced the improbability of one merchant being everywhere rather than explaining it away — “keep it weird… its fine… we know its a game… I am always happy to see the merchants” (M04432–M04435, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-20). Open questions DEBATED Creepy / scamming merchant: “will our shopkeeper also be creepy — like he somehow scamming you, he is getting the good deal not you… neh neh neh, that type” (M04442–M04447, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-20). FreshToast: “We could get one of those I like creepy merchants” (M04448, FreshToast, 2023-10-20). Whether he is played straight-silly or creepy-scamming is unsettled. PROPOSED The exact nature of his “element of quest progression” is unspecified (M04430, FreshToast, 2023-10-20). Design history 2023-02-15 — first seed: a “recurring, plucky merchant character” (M02075, FreshToast, 2023-02-15; 000 Part 8 §Recurring Merchant, 002 §Recurring Merchant). At this point it is a character sketch, not yet a system. 2023-10-20 — developed into a system during the shopkeeper-dialogue discussion: one shopkeeper who is everywhere with always-new dialogue (M04428–M04431), quest-linked (M04430), delivered unvoiced and Slay-the-Spire-side-style, non-blocking (M04436–M04441), with a “keep it weird” philosophy (M04432–M04435) and an open creepy/scamming variant (M04442–M04448, CrunchyConniptions FreshToast, 2023-10-20) — current version; supersedes the bare “plucky merchant” sketch by giving it delivery rules and a quest hook . Provenance Raw: M02075 (plucky merchant seed, FreshToast, 2023-02-15); M04428–M04441, M04436, M04438, M04439, M04441 (system + delivery, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-20); M04432–M04435 (“keep it weird”); M04442–M04448 (creepy/scamming variant, 2023-10-20) Cleaned: 000 Part 8 §Recurring Merchant; 000 lines 7241-7253 (shopkeeper system discussion) Docs: 008 §20 Recurring Shopkeeper System Ledger: src_chunk R12 (“Recurring, plucky merchant character”, FreshToast, 2023-02-15) + src_chunk R24 (the full recurring-shopkeeper system: everywhere / always-new dialogue / quest-linked / unvoiced Slay-the-Spire-side / “keep it weird” / creepy-scamming variant, M04428–M04448) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Spirit Collection System","group":"Systems","url":"systems/spirit-collection-system.html","text":"Spirit Collection System EVOLVED An early gacha-style character-collection layer: you collect the cast “like Pokémon, but they are spirits,” pop a capsule, and become the released spirit. Summary — Players collect characters as spirits : pop a capsule, a spirit is released, and the player becomes that spirit (008 §11). Some spirits are heroic, some villainous, some “something else.” Acquisition runs on a points system that raises the probability of getting a specific character, with a retrievable-points twist that lets players either quell or embrace the randomness. Like the [[two-path-system]] and [[anti-meta-systems|invasion anti-meta]], it is part of the early co-op-online vision , flagged “Early Concept” in 003/004, and is not an active first-game system after the single-player reframing (008 §11 EVOLVED ). Core mechanics People collect the characters like Pokémon, but they are spirits (008 §11). Pop a capsule and the spirit is released. The player becomes the spirit — you play as the collected spirit, not alongside it. Some spirits are heroic, some villainous, some “something else.” (Narrative note: the world’s “spirits” were later named [[drykksar|Daemons]]; the collection framing here predates and is separate from that lore.) Character acquisition system A points system that improves the probability of getting a certain character (M00228, M00229, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-26; 008 §11; shared with the [[anti-meta-systems|anti-meta points system]]): A points system improves the probability of getting a certain character. Once used, the point can be retrieved. If a player embraces randomness, they could put points into something more beneficial instead — equipment, items, skills, etc. Guiding lines: “Incentivize to embrace the randomness. Everyone using the same builds is boring” and “Give people the ability to quell the randomness at a cost. People want to be in control” (008 §11). Open questions EVOLVED Whether any spirit/character-collection layer survives into the single-player first game — the mechanic is inseparable from the gacha/co-op vision (008 §11; Parts 9-24). See [[overview]]. Design history 2021-05-26 — Spirit Collection introduced: collect characters like Pokémon but as spirits; pop a capsule, the spirit is released, and the player becomes the spirit; heroic/villainous/“something else” spirits (M00226, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-26; 008 §11). 2021-05-26 — Character-acquisition points system defined: points improve a target character’s probability, are retrievable once used, and can instead be spent on equipment/items/skills if the player embraces randomness; “give people the ability to quell the randomness at a cost” (M00228, M00229, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-26; 008 §11). Parts 9-24 — supersedes as active design. First game reframed single-player; the spirit-collection/character-gacha is tagged “Early Concept” (003/004) and kept as a record (008 §11 EVOLVED ). Provenance Raw: M00226, M00228, M00229 (CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-26) Cleaned: 000 §Spirit Collection System (lines ~910-914) Docs: 008 §11 Spirit Collection System Ledger: row R02 (“Spirit Collection System: players collect characters like Pokemon, but they are spirits…”), Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Treasure & Rewards","group":"Systems","url":"systems/treasure-and-rewards.html","text":"Treasure Rewards PROPOSED How the game pays the player out: exploration-driven treasure hunting where effort equals value, performance-based boss rewards keyed to how well you fought, and a compact set of implemented run-reward kinds. Summary — Rewards span three threads. Treasure hunting is a stated core pillar ( “Treasure hunting is the best!” ) built on the principle that the more effort and resources you sink in, the better the reward — hidden treasures most players never find are made especially valuable to reward exploration. Boss rewards are PROPOSED to be performance-based : players who do poorly get defensive items, those who do well get offensive items, the exceptional get cursed items — plus a boss-retry item to re-attempt for better loot. The playable proto ships a compact, fixed set of reward kinds at dungeon nodes. Finally, the CANON AoE-vs-multi-position bonus rewards hitting an enemy that fills multiple positions. Most higher-level reward logic is design intent; the reward kinds are implemented. Treasure hunting A stated core design pillar — “Treasure hunting is the best! Need game mechanics for this! Many!” (M00090, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-03-20; 008 §12). Core philosophy — effort equals value: “The longer it takes you to find a treasure, the better it is” (M00005, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-02-16), refined to “the more effort and resources you need to put in” (M00006, FreshToast, 2021-02-16). A system that finds treasures on a map that most people don’t find and makes those especially valuable , to incentivize players to seek them out (M00007, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-02-18). Treasure mechanics (008 §12): Hidden treasures require exploration; clues and hints scattered through the world; puzzle-solving elements. Environmental storytelling leads to treasures. Rare treasures from difficult-to-reach locations. This ethos feeds the [[dungeon-run-loop|dungeon-run loop]]'s harder-run-better-reward principle and the [[dungeon-run-loop#riskreward-luck-bonus|risk/reward luck bonus]] (M04565, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-22). Performance-based boss rewards PROPOSED On the first boss fight, a system identifies player skill and rewards accordingly (008 §13): Players who do poorly: rewarded with HP and armor (defensive help). Players who do well: rewarded with damage-dealing items (offensive). Players who are exceptional: can receive cursed items . Rationale: “Balancing the game for both low skill players and high skill players” (008 §13). Boss retry PROPOSED For every boss there is a corresponding item, found through exploration , that lets players retry that boss to get better rewards by performing better (008 §13). Ties the treasure-hunting thread back to boss rewards — explore to find the retry item, then re-clear the boss more skillfully for the higher tier of loot. AoE vs multi-position bonus CANON An AoE that hits an enemy which occupies multiple positions deals bonus damage — “as if hit twice but not double damage, maybe an extra 20%” (M01842, FreshToast, 2023-02-13; 008 §8; 000 lines 3141-3142). This is load-bearing for the [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]] climax , where he fills all four positions, making an AoE hit on him meaningfully more rewarding. It is a combat-side reward rule tied to the positioning system (see the [[position-system|2×3 grid]] / rank line). Implemented reward kinds CANON · implemented The proto grants a fixed set of reward kinds at dungeon nodes (008 §13; proto: src/core/campaign/types.ts RewardKind , run.ts applyReward ): relic — a named, non-duplicating collectible (e.g. “The Sunderer” , [[sunderer|Ajax’s first relic]]). maxhp — +4 base max HP to the whole party. heal — a % of base max HP restored to all. jet — +1 [[shimmer-sails|healing jet]]. flask — +1 [[river-flask|River Flask]]. The proto grants these as fixed per-node rewards — it does not yet measure player skill or offer boss retries (008 §13, DEBATED design intent vs implemented). Loot economy rules Never lose found gear CANON · implemented The oldest recorded loot decision, and still [IMPLEMENTED] : “you never lose items or equipment you find” (M00141, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25; 006 §Loot System Notes). In code this holds — on defeat, the run routes to the lost phase but acquired relics persist on RunState.relics and are never dropped ; only in-dungeon provisions / loose glint are at risk (proto src/core/campaign/run.ts ). It rests on the provisioning/death rules: banked gold and found gear are safe; only what you brought and what you gained loose in the dungeon can be left behind (006 §Death Mechanics). Gear acquisition constraints Gear is not available for purchase , and gear never drops at Gold quality — Gold must be earned/found (see [[weapons|weapon drop rules]]). Loot boxes are considered acceptable only for cosmetics / additional resources, “applied in a reasonable manner” — Apex/Overwatch cited as done-well examples (006 §Loot System Notes). Open questions DEBATED The performance-based boss reward tiers, cursed-item tier, and boss-retry items are design intent — the proto grants fixed per-node rewards and does not measure player skill (008 §13). PROPOSED How “effort/resources put in” is actually measured to scale treasure value (M00006, FreshToast, 2021-02-16). Design history 2021-02-16 — original effort→reward principle: “the longer it takes… the better it is” (M00005, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-02-16), immediately refined to “the more effort and resources you need to put in” (M00006, FreshToast, 2021-02-16). 2021-02-18 — extended into a find-the-hidden-and-make-it-valuable system to incentivize exploration (M00007, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-02-18). 2021-03-20 — reaffirmed as a core pillar: “Treasure hunting is the best! Need game mechanics for this! Many!” (M00090, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-03-20). 2021-05-25 — founding loot rule: “you never lose items or equipment you find” (M00141, CrunchyConniptions) — the oldest loot decision, never overturned. 2023-02-13 — the AoE-vs-multi-position bonus (~+20%) established, load-bearing for the Vaenngrym climax (M01842, FreshToast, 2023-02-13). 2023-10-22 — the treasure/effort ethos folded into the harder-dungeon-better-reward chalice loop: “you make the dungeon harder, your reward is better” (M04564, M04565, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-22). (See [[dungeon-run-loop]].) 2023-10-22 — Alternative Loot System (REJECTED): a checkpoint-gated moral-loot fork was proposed — skip the checkpoint → a cursed weapon that siphons the player’s life for a damage boost; play normally → a blessed weapon that heals (M04530–M04531, CrunchyConniptions). FreshToast rejected it: “I don’t think straight up discouraging interaction is going to get you the thing you want” (M04532, 2023-10-22). Only the checkpoint-gated delivery was rejected; the cursed/blessed weapon properties survive as weapon-design examples (see [[weapons]] / [[equipment-system]]). 2026-06-16 — implemented-code pass: proto ships fixed per-node reward kinds ( relic / maxhp / heal / jet / flask ) and the AoE-multi-position bonus; the performance-based boss tiers and boss-retry remain unimplemented design intent (008 §13; proto: src/core/campaign/* ) — supersedes the performance-tier system as shipped behavior, though it remains the design target . Provenance Raw: M00005, M00006, M00007 (treasure effort principle, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2021-02-16–18); M00090 (“Treasure hunting is the best!”, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-03-20); M00141 (never lose found gear, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25); M01842 (AoE multi-position bonus, FreshToast, 2023-02-13); M04530–M04532 (rejected alternative loot system, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-10-22); M04564, M04565 (harder-run-better-reward, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-22) Cleaned: 000 lines 3141-3142 (AoE bonus); 000 Part 17 (chalice/reward loop) Docs: 008 §12 Treasure Hunting Systems; 008 §13 Boss Reward Systems; 008 §13 Vertical-Slice Dungeon Reward Kinds; 008 §8 AoE vs multi-position bonus Ledger: src_chunks R01 (treasu"},{"title":"Art & Animation Direction","group":"Development","url":"development/art-direction.html","text":"Art Animation Direction CANON The team’s agreed art-and-animation pipeline for the JRPG: simple 3D/sprite models paired with gorgeous 2D portraits, with work split across specialised artists to minimise animation load. Summary — The overarching art goal is to minimise the need for animation and split work across specialised artists (M01351, M01355, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). The pipeline is structured by where each asset is used: simple animated chibi sprites for the overworld and roaming monsters, portrait art drawn from a portion of each character’s key art (the “PMD portrait” idea), simplified stretchy key-art with Darkest-Dungeon-style splash frames in combat, and overworld-sprite cutscenes with occasional full-art visuals. The guiding line is Crunchy’s: “simple game models with gorgeous portraits work well” (M01359, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). This section also folds in the earlier (2021) souls-like-era visual identity work — the [[lambent-corporation|Lambent Corporation]] logo, the Leora emblem, and the first colour palette — which predate the JRPG but established the team’s design instincts. The art-and-animation pipeline (Caelian Odyssey / Skyward Cinders) Agreed 2023-02-09; the overarching goal is to minimise animation and split work among specialised artists (M01351, M01355, FreshToast) (000 Part 5 §Art Animation Direction). Overworld. Out-of-combat sprites should be simple, easily animated chibis; the same chibis are reused for roaming monsters (M01344, M01353, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Portraits / conversation. For conversation, use a portion of the character’s key art as the portrait, with expression variants. FreshToast: “The portrait idea I took from PMD” (Pokemon Mystery Dungeon) (M01345, M01358, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Combat. A simplified version of the key art with stretchy animation; skills are splash frames, “like Darkest Dungeon” (M01346, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). PROPOSED Maybe really flashy skill frames, “Like kill bill/borderlands character intros” (M01349, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Cutscenes. Use the overworld sprites with the occasional full-art visual, “like PMD:EoS” (Explorers of Sky). Pokemon as a franchise “basically does semi-narrated overworld cutscenes” (M01348, M01357, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Art-team division. Split work among specialised artists — a background/full-visual artist, a character artist for the key character art, and a chibi character artist (M01355, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). References. Ni no Kuni does the model/portrait split (M01347, CrunchyConniptions), Fire Emblem Awakening does this (M01354, CrunchyConniptions), and “New pokemon does roaming lil dudes instead of grass these days” (M01356, FreshToast) (all 2023-02-09). Guiding line. “I think simple game models with gorgeous portraits work well” (M01359, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09). Related aesthetic notes from later parts: film-style black bars for cutscenes, icons instead of full art for weapons/armor, 4-directional sprites, and the paladin’s winged bucket helm (000 Part 7 §Aesthetic Details). Lambent Corporation logo (souls-like era) FreshToast concepted a logo for the [[lambent-corporation|Lambent Corporation]] on 2021-08-02, from the early souls-like project (M00559, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). Design intent: Simple enough to be understood “at a distance or a glance,” and not colour dependent so it can be reused in many places — e.g. as a glowing icon in the light of a boss’s assigned colour (M00559, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). Somewhat villainous: angular shapes meant to emphasise “the angular, faceted nature of [[lucite|Lucite]]” (a crystal-like being/thing) (M00559, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). “A little uneven and jagged, to try and lowkey get across that it can’t be controlled,” because “sharp means danger in most people’s minds” (M00560, M00561, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). Versions produced: orange-glow and white (M00562, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). The proposed “official” primary advertising logo is “White with prismatic edge and glow” (M00563, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). Leora emblem / game silhouette (souls-like era) FreshToast produced a silhouette-emblem for the game / for Leora (the souls-like-era protagonist) on 2021-08-26, iterating through many attempts (M00809, FreshToast, 2021-08-26): Multiple attempts shown — labelled Attempt 1, 2, 2.5, 3, and 4; “Maybe simple is best” and eventually “This is the one I think I like the most? I’m gonna leave it for now” (M00816–M00824, FreshToast, 2021-08-26). The design is built around a “rainbow/6 colour concept,” with colouring “on the flame-y tips” to be added later (M00812, M00826, spirit_noodle/FreshToast, 2021-08-26/27). A gold version was attempted the next day; FreshToast felt “a bit lost” trying to keep it within the 6-colour concept, and spirit_noodle suggested the gold version “might require more shading” (M00825, M00826, M00827, 2021-08-27/28). FreshToast was simultaneously “trying to get a janky ass gradient working” for the emblem’s colour (M00811, M00813, FreshToast, 2021-08-26). Color palette (souls-like era) FreshToast produced a “First attempt at the overall colour palette” on 2021-08-06 (M00587, FreshToast, 2021-08-06). Open questions PROPOSED Whether combat gets “really flashy skill frames” like Kill Bill / Borderlands character intros, or the plainer Darkest-Dungeon splash frames only (M01349, FreshToast, 2023-02-09). Design history 2021-08-02 — souls-like-era visual identity begins: [[lambent-corporation|Lambent]] logo concepted — simple, colour-independent, angular/villainous to echo [[lucite|Lucite]]; orange-glow and white versions, “official” white-with-prismatic-edge advertising version (M00559–M00563, FreshToast). 2021-08-06 — first attempt at the overall colour palette (M00587, FreshToast). 2021-08-26 → 2021-08-27 — Leora/game emblem silhouette iterated (Attempts 1–4 plus a gold version), built on a rainbow/6-colour concept with “flame-y tips” (M00809–M00827, FreshToast/spirit_noodle). 2023-02-09 — for the JRPG restart, the full art-and-animation pipeline is agreed: chibi overworld sprites (reused for roaming monsters), PMD-style key-art portraits, simplified stretchy key-art with Darkest-Dungeon splash frames in combat, PMD:EoS-style cutscenes, and a three-way split among specialised artists — all to minimise animation. Guiding line: “simple game models with gorgeous portraits work well” (M01344–M01360, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions). This is the current pipeline. Provenance Raw: M00559–M00563, M00587, M00809–M00827 (FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2021-08-02 → 2021-08-27); M01344–M01360 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-09) Cleaned: 000 Part 2 §Lambent Corporation Logo Design Work, §Character Design - Leora Emblem/Silhouette Work, §Color Palette; 000 Part 5 §Art Animation Direction Docs: 009 §Art Animation Direction; 009 §Lambent Corporation Logo Design Work; 009 §Character Design - Leora Emblem/Silhouette Work; 009 §Color Palette Ledger: art/design rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Caelian Odyssey (Iteration)","group":"Development","url":"development/caelian-odyssey.html","text":"Caelian Odyssey (Iteration) CANON The project’s final iteration (from late 2023): a turn-based JRPG built about the [[the-luminarium|Luminarium]] magic academy, distinct from and succeeding the earlier “Skyward Cinders” framing. Summary — “Caelian Odyssey” is the name the team settled on for the current iteration of the game, written on 2023-10-17 as “Caelian Odyssy - The Luminarium” (M04249, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). The name first surfaced in May 2023 as the thing that should take priority over [[project-magitech|Project Magitech]] (“Caelian Odyssey needs priority” — M03196, FreshToast, 2023-05-20). By October 2023 the JRPG was reframed to be about the [[the-luminarium|Luminarium]] with no rival antagonist faction, its four non-mage party members recast as nonmagical historical/fictional archetypes around the mage [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]] (M04226, M04306–M04307, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). It is the successor iteration to Skyward Cinders , whose more character-conflict-driven framing it explicitly steps back from. Finalized name — “Soaring Cinders”: the group’s current name for this game is Soaring Cinders (it is this wiki’s title). It refers to the same game the chats called Caelian Odyssey , and before that Skyward Cinders — three names for one continuous project across time. “Soaring Cinders” was adopted after the Discord archive ends (2024-09-06), so the name itself does not appear in the source chats; the different titles are not different games. [[project-magitech|Project Magitech]] is the sole exception — a separate, shelved second-game concept, never part of this line. What the iteration is The current turn-based JRPG, titled “Caelian Odyssy - The Luminarium” (M04249, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17). The name was first used two months earlier when the team chose to prioritise it over [[project-magitech|Project Magitech]] (M03196, FreshToast, 2023-05-20). Design pivot: “No antagonist faction to the Luminarium” / “We’re gonna keep it about the Luminarium” (M04306–M04307, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). The [[the-luminarium|Luminarium]] magic academy is the iteration’s hub and centre of plot. Party structure: the four “boyes” are each based on a nonmagical historical or fictional archetype (thief, samurai, knight, explorer/cowboy), while [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]] is “a young, archetypal, fantasy mage and also representative of a well-rounded sorcerer by Caelian standards” (M04226, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). “Caelian” is the setting-adjective (cf. the hub city [[caelia|Caelia]]). Scope: by October 2023 the project spanned two narratives / two games — Caelian Odyssey plus the earlier/main game — with the river mechanic threaded across both plots (“TWO narratives now, in fact” — M04059, FreshToast, 2023-10-13; M04075). Relation to Skyward Cinders Caelian Odyssey supersedes the “Skyward Cinders” working title/framing. Under Caelian Odyssey the party members’ “personal conflicts should be largely internal and (unlike Skyward Cinders) are highly secondary to the main plot (with the exemption of Cyrus)” (M04226, FreshToast, 2023-10-17) — an explicit contrast with the prior iteration. The two names briefly co-exist in October 2023 (Caelian Odyssey title M04249 on 2023-10-17; “Skyward Cinders” referenced M04226 same day and M04811+ on 2023-10-30), so the exact rename boundary is fuzzy — see [[development-timeline]] for the timeline treatment. Iteration lineage (for orientation) Early-concept / co-op souls-like (2021): protagonist [[leora|Leora]]; antagonists [[lambent-corporation|Lambent Corporation]] and [[lucite|Lucite]]. Scrapped. Skyward Cinders (2023-02 → 2023-09): the JRPG restart; character-conflict-forward framing. Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium (2023-10 → 2024): the last in-chat iteration name, about the Luminarium; this page. Soaring Cinders (finalized, post-archive): the game’s current real-world name — the same game as Caelian Odyssey / Skyward Cinders, just its settled title. This wiki is named for it. Project Magitech : a separate, deprioritized second-game concept (not part of this game’s line) — see [[project-magitech]]. Open questions DEBATED Whether “Skyward Cinders” and “Caelian Odyssey” were parallel names for one iteration or a rename-in-progress; both appear in October 2023 (see [[development-timeline]] Open questions). Design history 2023-05-20 — the name “Caelian Odyssey” first appears as the priority over [[project-magitech|Project Magitech]] (M03196, FreshToast). 2023-10-13 — project confirmed as two narratives/games with the river mechanic spanning both (M04059, M04075, FreshToast). 2023-10-17 — iteration named “Caelian Odyssy - The Luminarium” (M04249, CrunchyConniptions); reframed to be about the [[the-luminarium|Luminarium]] with no antagonist faction; party recast as archetypes + mage Cyrus, explicitly contrasted with Skyward Cinders (M04226, M04306–M04307, FreshToast). This is the current iteration framing. 2024-08 → 2024-09 — prologue screenplay drafted under the “CAELIAN ODYSSEY” title; worldbuilding continues to the archive’s end 2024-09-06 (M05471–M05693; see [[development-timeline]]). Provenance Raw: M03196 (name first used, 2023-05-20); M04059, M04075 (two-game scope, 2023-10-13); M04226, M04249, M04306–M04307 (iteration reframing, 2023-10-17) — FreshToast, CrunchyConniptions Cleaned: 000 Part 15–16 (Luminarium iteration); 000 Part 11–12 (naming vs Project Magitech) Docs: 001 §“Magic Academy” vs “The Luminarium” — two different game iterations; 001 §The Luminarium (Caelian Odyssey: The Luminarium iteration); 002 (iteration scope) Ledger: meta rows 711 (iteration named), 734 (two-game scope), and 696/699 (priority over Project Magitech) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Considered & Dropped (Name Brainstorms and Alternates)","group":"Development","url":"development/considered-and-dropped.html","text":"Considered Dropped (Name Brainstorms and Alternates) SCRAPPED A traceability record of name brainstorms and alternate ideas that were floated in the Discord chat but were not kept or never finalized. Summary — This page transcribes the 009 “Considered Dropped” provenance appendix. It preserves material that survives only in the raw Discord archive: discarded name brainstorms, alternate spellings, and abandoned framings. None of the following is canon — every entry is a discarded or alternate idea unless it was adopted elsewhere (in which case it lives on that entity’s own page). Pure joke entries from the same brainstorms are omitted. Each entry cites its raw message id(s) (009 §Appendix — Considered Dropped). Discarded full-name brainstorm — the “Astrum” line (2021-08-01) Artarius (M00494); Percival Lumenson (M00495); Percival Brightsworth (M00496); Percival Brightlord (M00497); Astrum (M00498); Percival Astrum (M00499); Alexander Julius Astrum (M00500); Artarius Julius Astrum (M00501); Cornelius (M00502); Maximum (M00503, raw spelling); Maximus (M00504); Lucius (M00505); Artarius Lucius Astrum (M00506). (raw M00494–M00506, FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2021-08-01) Discarded name — “Royce” Royce (M00455, FreshToast); Royce Larkin (M00457, spirit_noodle). (raw M00455, M00457, 2021-08-01) Character-name brainstorm not kept (2021-08-01) The serious-leaning entries amid a largely joke list: Darius (M00295); Dorian (M00296); Felix (M00313); Keith (M00316); Robin (M00318); Rowan (M00319); Mycelio (M00330); Cicero (M00331/M00332). (raw M00281–M00334, FreshToast/spirit_noodle/CrunchyConniptions, 2021-08-01) Surname/name brainstorm not kept, incl. “Gaius Monroe” (2023-02-09) Alvaro (M01235); Cattenach (M01236); Larnach (M01237); Lathurna (M01238); Artair (M01240); Kilian (M01241); Archie (M01242); Archibald (M01243); Laurier (M01244); Monroe (M01245); “Gauis Monroe” (M01246, raw spelling; i.e. [[gaius-wornwall|Gaius]] Monroe); Barlowe (M01247); Finch (M01248). (raw M01235–M01248, FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2023-02-09) Alt name for Locke — “Pyrrha” Proposed during a “final pass on [[locke|Locke]]” (“there are other cool names… Like Pyrrha”); Locke was kept (“Locke sounds like a protagonist… is good”). (raw M01935–M01941, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-14) Light-capital city-name brainstorm, not adopted (2021-08-01) Light City (M00447); Raidian (M00448); Lightstream (M00449); Stellaris Maximus (M00451); Lumenland (M00467); Prismere (M00484); Brighthaven (M00490); Lighthaven (M00491); Lambentville (M00493); Glinthaven (M00510) — among many others (Glintwell, Glintgate, Glintmoor, Gleamshore, etc.). (raw M00447–M00510, all three members, 2021-08-01) Alt spelling for Drykksar — “Sardrykkr” Listed alongside “Drykksar” as candidate spellings; [[drykksar|Drykksar]] was kept (the condition/parasite causing Dracosis). (raw M02695, FreshToast, 2023-02-22) Thorne “represent two groups” — mixed-lineage rationale On whether [[thorne-tabby-tumblemane|Thorne]] should specifically be a cat-man, the answer was “just mixed” / “He should represent two groups”; non-cat options floated were gerbil and (theoretically) squid. (raw M03405–M03409, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-06-04) Death-pictured-as-a-child alternative An early alternate framing — [[death|Death]] was at first perceived “as a child, partly because of an inherent thought… that thinks they must have poor judgement skills” — but the other approach was preferred (“but i dig this approach too”). (raw M00932–M00933, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-05) Upload-to-superintelligence ending (Project Magitech) A proposed ending for the magic-humans-vs-AI conflict — “maybe you upload your intelligence and become a superintelligence.” Floated for the separate [[project-magitech|Project Magitech]] concept. (raw M03244–M03246, spirit_noodle, 2023-05-20) Provenance Raw: M00281–M00334, M00447–M00510, M00932–M00933, M01235–M01248, M01935–M01941, M02695, M03244–M03246, M03405–M03409 (all three members; 2021-08-01 → 2023-06-04) Cleaned: brainstorm scatter across 000 Parts 3–5, 11–12 (name discussions) Docs: 009 §Appendix — Considered Dropped (provenance) Ledger: name/brainstorm rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Design Philosophy","group":"Development","url":"development/design-philosophy.html","text":"Design Philosophy CANON The design commandments the team returned to across three years of chat: no time limits, distrust of maps, mechanics that interact elegantly, and a homey, cozy game that is “not a hassle to pick up.” Summary — The team’s design philosophy is a loose but stable set of rules and tastes, most first stated in 2021 and reaffirmed through 2024. The hard rules are “time limits are taboo” and (with dissent) “maps are taboo.” Beneath those sit a cluster of preferences: a game should feel homey and cozy; its mechanics should interact elegantly ; challenge matters (a “git gud” leaning); a game is “a series of meaningful choices”; and puzzles should be “few, short and good only.” Most of these are aspirations rather than finalized mechanics. The rules Time limits are taboo CANON The first stated design rule. “Time limits are frustrating and annoying”; “Time limits are not a fun mechanic. use other fun mechanics”; “Time is used for measuring. its very good for measuring. measuring is not fun.” The rule closes with a small manifesto: “Let there be chaos. let there be freedom. let there be comfort” (M00091, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13; 000 §Rule 1; 008 §Rule 1). This is the design reason the [[dungeon-run-loop|dungeon runs]] measure progress by fights/rooms rather than a clock. Maps are taboo DEBATED The second rule, and the one that drew immediate pushback. “Maps are boring. Maps are tedious. Maps are effective and productive. Maps are not fun. Maps are for boring nerds” (M00092, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13). FreshToast disagreed in the same minute — “I disagree about maps but there are pleasant alternatives” (M00093, 2021-05-13) — and the position softened into a nuanced one: maps “can be nessecary to travers giant worlds, but they are inherently not fun” (M00094), so “you would only use to guide people to the fun, not because MAPS are fun” (M00095, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-13). The REAL taboo was later clarified as the case where the world is too hard to traverse yourself so you must lean on a minimap; the dislike is “more about minimaps specifically” (000 §Rule 2). The preferred alternative is a compass over a map, Skyrim-style, with a very simple actual map (000 §Navigation Systems). Because it was never fully resolved, this rule is tagged DEBATED . Preferences and taste Make the game homey / cozy CANON A recurring north star. “Make the game homey” — cozy, easy to pick up, “not a hassle to pick up” (M03691, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-09-26; 000 §Make the game homey; 009 Appendix). The touchstone is Dark Souls 1’s Firelink Shrine: “In dark souls 1, first bonfire gives you more estus flasks. I am now realizing I want that feeling. The homey feeling of firelink shrine” (M04097, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). The feeling is later summarized as “its familiar and homey” (M05292, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-02-13). The team also wants the home village to feel endearing so the player longs to return (009 §Part 19 Critical Reminders). Mechanics should interact elegantly CANON Stated flatly by spirit_noodle: “Mechanics should interact elegantly” (M02896, spirit_noodle, 2023-03-05; 000 §line 5138; 009 Appendix). The related deterministic-design principle — “Movement is both simple and perfectly deterministic (never random)” — comes out of the [[position-system]] discussion: strategy should be easy to follow, always knowing where a character will go, “No guessing” (009 §Part 20). Chaos is welcome, but only “as result of unintended or exciting new interaction. Instead of confusion” (009 §Part 20). And no single mechanic should take over all strategy (009 §Part 22 Critical Reminders). A game is a series of meaningful choices CANON Stated as “A game is a series of meaningful choices” (M00246, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-07-03) — Crunchy’s rendering of the well-known Sid-Meier maxim — and endorsed by FreshToast, who noted the more famous version uses “interesting” but he preferred Crunchy’s word: “I’d replace ‘interesting’ with ‘meaningful’ but otherwise yes this is important” (M00247, FreshToast, 2021-07-03). Related early quotes from the same era: “Awesomeness is a byproduct, not the goal” (M00232, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-06-13, crediting “Lettuce”); “At essence of all games are like puzzles to solve to proceed”; “Video games should be full of ellipsis” (009 §Design Philosophy Quotes). Challenge matters (“git gud”) CANON On whether the developer should scaffold low-skill players, the team leaned toward Miyazaki: “I don’t think it’s the responsibility of the developer to foster skill in the player. Otherwise, you’re playing the game for them”; “maybe Miyazaki is right. just bully your players to ‘git gud’” (M00219, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). The escape hatch: “If challenge matters then yes, if not then let them overlevel like in a jrpg or whatever” (M00223, FreshToast, 2021-05-25) — answered simply, “Challenge matters!” (009 §Progression System Debates). Skill-based rewards were floated but judged “incredibly exploitable” and dropped in favor of just making rewards more numerous or higher quality (009 §Progression System Debates). Puzzle philosophy PROPOSED Puzzles should be “few, short and good only”; “fuck riddles.” A good puzzle works like a boss fight — introduce a mechanic, complicate it, then introduce something new or force the player to reinterpret it. The intent is light puzzling to set mood and break pacing: simple, unobtrusive, “make em think just a little” (009 Appendix, source 000 lines 2760-2774). Suggested placement structures: big exploration with a good puzzle at the end; a puzzle in the middle with a climactic fight; or an ongoing puzzle interacted with in brief chunks — roughly one puzzle per dungeon across ~7 dungeons (009 Appendix, source 000 lines 2775-2778). Repeated dialogue breaks immersion PROPOSED The aspiration to a game “where dialogue never repeats,” acknowledged as hard for talking shopkeepers. Dragon’s Dogma’s “They’re masterworks all” is cited as the good case; Bethesda / Starfield repetition as the painful one (“Bethesda doesn’t count. They put their voice talent through the meat grinder”) (009 Appendix, source 000 line 7232ff). Compare the later immersion-through-familiarity principle: references that “you just get it . No explanation. No lore dump” (009 §Part 24 Design Philosophy). Death should be punishing but fair CANON Death “has to be punishing, but not too punishing” — don’t lose everything, but give a chance to right a dumb mistake; don’t force the player to return or punish them for exploring; make them long for home in sentiment and return naturally during the story. The team wants players to “push their luck as much as possible,” with each player deciding how far to go (009 §Part 17 Design Philosophy). The four pillars When the team enumerated what the game is , they named four pillars — Narrative, Battle, Party Management, Exploration — alongside four “key aspects”: Narrative, Gameplay, Art direction, Sound design ( Music) (009 §Four Pillars, Part 8; cf. the party-management notes, M01422, spirit_noodle, 2023-02-11). UX-polish appendix (aspirations) PROPOSED Consolidated low-severity polish intents from across the notes (009 Appendix — Design Principles UX Polish). Each is descriptive design intent, not a finalized mechanic: Title screen must exist, look pretty with cool music, and say something like “Press any button to begin journey” (000 line 1607). Character-selection feedback should be obvious — a drop-shadow glow/halo under the character, an eye-catching arrow, or a colour/glow change on the health bar (000 lines 1861-1866). UI consistency : input, colour-coding, and abbreviations must be consistent AND explained; intuitive limits, rounded numbers, intelligent use of space (000 lines 3170-3172, 951-953). Break-pots destructibles as you explore, “incredibly snappy in and out of combat” (000 lines 3757-3759). The fountain could work like Dark Souls crows — sacrifice an item for a reward (00"},{"title":"Development Timeline","group":"Development","url":"development/development-timeline.html","text":"Development Timeline CANON The chronological arc of the project across the Discord archive, 2021 → 2024, tracing the major design eras from a co-op souls-like to the JRPG Caelian Odyssey. Summary — The team (CrunchyConniptions, FreshToast, spirit_noodle) began chatting in early 2021 about a co-op souls-like, spent 2021 building combat/movement systems and a souls-like visual identity, went largely quiet through late 2021 and 2022, then relaunched in February 2023 as a turn-based JRPG. Through 2023 they built out characters, mechanics, the magic system, and the world; branched off a separate second-game concept ([[project-magitech|Project Magitech]]); and in October 2023 renamed the JRPG around the [[the-luminarium|Luminarium]] as Caelian Odyssey . Work continued into 2024 with prologue screenplay drafts and worldbuilding. The team’s own timeline estimate was roughly “2 years to put things together. Another year for polish and last touches before launch” (000 Part 11 §Development Timeline). Era 1 — Co-op souls-like (2021-02 → 2021-09) The archive opens 2021-02-13 with casual chat (M00001, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-02-13). The early direction was explicitly a “Coop dark souls adventure with engaging combat,” drawing on Dark Souls, Bloodborne, and Sekiro (000 Part 1 §Design Discussions). The aspirational pitch was “Skyrim times 10 with good gameplay and online co-op,” cross-platform, with an epic narrative (M00165–M00167, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). Project names in this era were SHATTER GLASS (the current starting project) and PL (a wall-running project) (000 Part 2 §Project Names Mentioned). This era’s protagonist was Leora, and antagonists were framed around the [[lambent-corporation|Lambent Corporation]] and [[lucite|Lucite]]. Milestones/work in this era: 2021-08-02 — [[lambent-corporation|Lambent]] logo concepted (M00559, FreshToast, 2021-08-02). 2021-08-06 — first overall colour palette (M00587, FreshToast, 2021-08-06). 2021-08 — FreshToast begins a “more legible document containing all of what we’ve talked about,” reorganising and stuffing it with worldbuilding — “It’s like 6% done though lol” (M00586, M00590, FreshToast, 2021-08-06/14). 2021-08-26 → 27 — Leora/game emblem silhouette iterations (M00809–M00827, FreshToast, 2021-08-26/27). Technical decisions: debate over UE4 vs UE5 (UE5 “not production ready”), purchase of cel-shading marketplace assets to reverse-engineer (000 Part 2 §Technical Development Notes). Era 2 — Quiet / hiatus (2021-10 → 2023-01) Activity drops sharply. The 2021-10-25 “Game Idea: Planet of the apes… but cats” exchange (M00832–M00837, CrunchyConniptions/spirit_noodle, 2021-10-25) is followed by sparse link-sharing through late 2021 and only scattered messages in 2022 (“snow games,” “digging games” — M00842, M00843, spirit_noodle, 2022-05-04). The next substantive burst is February 2023. Era 3 — JRPG restart / Skyward Cinders (2023-02 → 2023-09) The project relaunches as a turn-based JRPG on 2023-02-02 with a wave of JRPG and dungeon reference material (M00844, M00845, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-02). The core “gaming deconstruction” pitch is laid out days later: “We fight the hero,” “We fight blacksmith who changes their armor based on your attack,” “We don’t level up” (M01160–M01163, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-08). Key work in this era: 2023-02-09 — the [[art-direction|art-and-animation pipeline]] is agreed (chibi sprites, PMD-style portraits, splash-frame combat) (M01344–M01360, 2023-02-09). 2023-02 — character roster, party composition, and the [[ruby|Ruby]]/homunculus decision solidify (000 Part 3). 2023-02-22 — [[drykksar|Drykksar]] / Dracosis condition named (M02695, FreshToast, 2023-02-22). This era’s working title is Skyward Cinders , later referenced explicitly as the prior iteration whose “personal conflicts” were more central than in the successor (M04226, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). Era 4 — Project Magitech branch (2023-05) A separate, second-game concept — [[project-magitech|Project Magitech]] — is brainstormed at length on 2023-05-20 and 2023-05-31: an all-powerful AI antagonist, a simulation twist, and a “sell it as a normal fantasy game” marketing plan (M03078+, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast/spirit_noodle, 2023-05-20/31). It is explicitly deprioritized in favor of the main JRPG: “this effort should be put into Caelian Odyssey instead” / “Caelian Odyssey needs priority” (M03164, M03196, FreshToast, 2023-05-20). Notably this is the first appearance of the name Caelian Odyssey . Era 5 — Caelian Odyssey / the Luminarium (2023-10 → 2024) In October 2023 the main JRPG is reframed around the [[the-luminarium|Luminarium]]. On 2023-10-17 the title is written as “Caelian Odyssy - The Luminarium” (M04249, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-17), and the design pivots to be about the Luminarium with no rival antagonist faction (M04306, M04307, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). The 4 non-mage party members are recast as nonmagical historical/fictional archetypes, with [[cyrus-sunworth|Cyrus]] as the mage (M04226, FreshToast, 2023-10-17). 2023-10-13 — a significant team argument over creative ownership and cohesion of the design (M04040–M04065; see [[team-attribution|Team Attribution]]). 2023-10-30 — healing/skills system worked out for Skyward Cinders naming still in use (M04811–M04818, FreshToast, 2023-10-30). Era 6 — Prologue drafting worldbuilding (2024-08 → 2024-09) Work continues into 2024 with the tutorial/prologue screenplay (“FADE TO TITLE: ‘CAELIAN ODYSSEY’”) and structural questions like “Is our game linear?” — answered “basically linear but there’s some branching,” compared to Dark Souls 3 (M05471–M05475, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2024-08-22). Setting worldbuilding continues, e.g. golden-age-pirate grog/citrus lore for the Caribbean-like climate (M05673, M05677, FreshToast, 2024-08-28). The archive’s last message is 2024-09-06 (M05693, FreshToast, 2024-09-06). The team’s own launch estimate “When do you think we will have game to sell? 3 years?” — “Idk, a year? 2 years?… I think 2 to 3 years is likely.” “I think 2 years to put things together. Another year for polish and last touches before launch.” “It really depends on how intense production stage will be.” (000 Part 11 §Development Timeline) Open questions The exact boundary between the “Skyward Cinders” name and “Caelian Odyssey” is fuzzy: both names appear in October 2023 (M04249 Caelian Odyssey title on 2023-10-17; M04226 “Skyward Cinders” 2023-10-17; M04811/M04818 “Skyward Cinders” still in use 2023-10-30). DEBATED whether these were parallel names for the same iteration or a rename-in-progress. Design history 2021-02 → 2021-09 — co-op souls-like era: SHATTER GLASS / PL, Leora protagonist, [[lambent-corporation|Lambent]]/[[lucite|Lucite]] antagonists; combat, movement, and visual identity work (M00001, M00559–M00587, M00809–M00827). 2021-10 → 2023-01 — hiatus; sparse activity (M00832–M00843). 2023-02 → 2023-09 — JRPG restart as Skyward Cinders : “gaming deconstruction,” no leveling, [[art-direction|art pipeline]] agreed, roster built (M00844, M01160–M01360). 2023-05 — [[project-magitech|Project Magitech]] second-game concept branched and deprioritized; name “Caelian Odyssey” first used (M03078+, M03164). 2023-10 — main JRPG reframed as Caelian Odyssey around the [[the-luminarium|Luminarium]]; 4 archetypal party members + mage (M04226, M04249, M04306). 2024-08 → 2024-09 — prologue screenplay and setting worldbuilding; archive ends 2024-09-06 (M05471–M05693). This is the latest state. Provenance Raw: M00001 (2021-02-13) → M05693 (2024-09-06), spanning the full archive; era anchors cited inline Cleaned: 000 Parts 1–24; explicit launch-estimate quotes in 000 Part 11 §Development Timeline Docs: 009 §Development Timeline; era material distributed across 009 Parts 1–24 Ledger: timeline/era rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Enemy Design Philosophy","group":"Development","url":"development/enemy-design-philosophy.html","text":"Enemy Design Philosophy CANON The rules the whole bestiary obeys: enemies get harder through tactical complexity, not bigger numbers — you go from fighting bandits to a dragon, never from bandits to bandits with quadruple HP. Summary — The bestiary is governed by a few finalized principles: (1) progression comes from tactical complexity, not HP bloat; (2) enemies vary in sensory gimmicks (blind, invisible, light-sensitive) so different dungeons reward different skills; (3) scary enemies are built through resource starvation, escalation, and phases that express their power, plus tapping relatable fears; and (4) classic creature traits are preserved (hydrae have loads of heads, griffons are bird-lions, dragons breathe fire). “Simple fantasy world upfront, weird stuff out back.” Scaling progression CANON “Later enemies will not have bigger numbers than beginning enemies… They will rather be more complicated and tactical fights” — maybe some bigger numbers for balance, “but with reason” (005 §Scaling Progression; the leveling/quality-system note). Bandits → dragon, not bandits with quadruple HP: progression is meant to move from bandits to a dragon rather than “bandits to bandits with quadruple HP” (005 §Scaling Progression). Backed by the linear-scaling decision: “Linear scaling over exponential scaling to limit number bloat and reduce numerical impact of later levels to emphasi[ze] the tactical depth of combat and equipment choice” (M02100, FreshToast, 2023-02-16). Recycle minibosses to flatter the player: “you can throw what was once a miniboss at the player in a pair or trio to make the player feel cool” (M01498, FreshToast, 2023-02-11). The strategic-progression arc is goblins → dragon → [[lysander|Lysander]] : the game teaches different strategic approaches through gameplay ([[imps|Imps/Goblins]] → [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]] → [[lysander|Lysander]]). “The best strat to beat goblins is raw individual overwhelming power” (005 §Combat Strategy). Proto note: the “make fights end fast” instinct is implemented as the Escalation system (enemies gain an accuracy/crit “edge” the longer a fight runs) — an item owned by the systems batch. See [[escalation]]. Enemy variety DEBATED Discussed sensory gimmicks that let different dungeons incentivize different skills (M01448-region, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-02-11): Some enemies are blind . Some enemies are invisible and can only be seen with magic, but you can hear them . Some enemies are sensitive to light and “will see your torch before you see them” — countered by a party member who sees in the dark without a torch (005 §Enemy Variety). See the [[encounters-and-ambushes|lantern/light interaction]]. “Different dungeons have levels and enemies that incentivize different skills to be used” (005 §Enemy Variety). Making enemies scary Techniques the team catalogued for frightening encounters (M01799–M01801, FreshToast, 2023-02-11): Mechanically scary: resource starvation; extended conflict; events or phases that express the monster’s power (M01799). Thematically scary: make it seem you only stand a chance because it’s weakened; have it kill something else scary; tap into common relatable fears — body horror, fear of heights, megalophobia, thalassophobia (M01800). Herd instinct: “humans are herd animals. If the characters are scared — especially non-player characters like the villains — that’s a signal… for people to also feel apprehensive” (M01801). Making a monster interesting needs “both writing experience… AND game design experience/creativity to make it work mechanically” (005 §Making Enemies Scary). These techniques are applied hardest to [[vaenngrym|Vaenngrym]] (best-a) and its [[fear-effect|fear effect]]. Creature design philosophy CANON Consistent with the vision: “simple fantasy world upfront, weird stuff out back” (005 §Core Concept). Keep classic traits intact: “Hydrae have loads of heads, griffons are bird-lions and dragons breath[e] fire” (M04623, FreshToast, 2023-10-24); “I think I want goblins as is, not changed much. There aren’t enough goblins out there” (M04618, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-24). See [[imps|Imps]] and [[minor-creatures|Hydrae/Griffons]]. Tactical complexity (summary of the throughline) Later enemies focus on tactical complexity rather than stat inflation, incentivizing different party compositions and strategies for different dungeons (005 §Tactical Complexity). This is the design intent that the proto’s [[ossuary-sentinel|Ossuary Sentinel]] boss enacts — beaten by reading intent, not out-damaging 132 HP. Design history 2023-02-11 — core scaling ideas: recycle minibosses in pairs/trios (M01498); the “make it scary” toolkit — resource starvation, phases, relatable fears, herd instinct (M01799–M01801); enemy-variety sensory gimmicks (blind/invisible/light-sensitive) (M01448-region). 2023-02-16 — linear-over-exponential scaling locked to emphasize tactical depth (M02100, FreshToast). 2023-10-24 — “keep classic traits intact” fixed as a creature-design principle (M04618, M04623). 2024-09-01 — the ethos continues to guide new creature discussion (e.g. slimes following natural forms, M05683). These principles are recorded as finalized decisions (005 §Design Decisions Finalized). Provenance Raw: M01448-region, M01498, M01799–M01801 (2023-02-11); M02100 (FreshToast, 2023-02-16); M04618, M04623 (2023-10-24); M05683 (2024-09-01) Cleaned: 000 (Enemy Design Philosophy; Making Enemies Scary; Creature Design) Docs: 005 §Enemy Design Philosophy, §Scaling Progression, §Enemy Variety, §Making Enemies Scary, §Creature Design Philosophy, §Core Concept, §Tactical Complexity, §Design Decisions Finalized Ledger: scaling/creature-design rows in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"Monetization & Business","group":"Development","url":"development/monetization-and-business.html","text":"Monetization Business CANON The team’s stance on money: loot boxes are fine if reasonable and cosmetic, the game will likely ship under a UAE commercial license, and a realistic ship window is roughly two to three years of development. Summary — The monetization posture is moderate: loot boxes and gacha-style elements are acceptable “applied in a reasonable manner” — cosmetics or extra resources — with Apex Legends and Overwatch cited as done-right examples (M00170–M00173, FreshToast, 2021-05-25). On the business side, the team looked into selling the game through a UAE commercial license (needed above AED 50,000 / ~£11,200 revenue, and untaxed there), noting the UK would tax it regardless (M03032–M03038, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-14). The working development timeline is “2 years to put things together” plus “another year for polish and last touches before launch” (M03044–M03046, 2023-03-14). Monetization stance CANON Loot boxes are not objected to in principle: “I don’t actually have much issue with loot boxes applied in a reasonable manner such as for earning cosmetic items or additional resources” (M00170, FreshToast, 2021-05-25). Cited exemplars of doing it well: “I think Apex Legends does it well” (M00171, FreshToast, 2021-05-25) and “Overwatch’s loot boxes were fine too, honestly” (M00173, FreshToast, 2021-05-25). Fortnite was raised with uncertainty (M00175, 2021-05-25) — “is it predatory? I hear epicgames are good natured” (M00177, CrunchyConniptions) — answered “it’s had its ups and downs” (M00179, FreshToast, 2021-05-25). Crunchy took the feedback on the gacha elements “into account” and resolved to use those titles as references (M00169, M00174, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). This sits alongside a separate, mechanically-oriented anti-meta idea discussed the same day — “a system by which players are disincentivised to use meta builds/teams… ie points system where it is expensive to use the most popular characher, weapons, items” (M00172, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25) — which is a balance concern rather than monetization, but was captured in the same exchange. An early pricing thought-experiment framed the ambition: FreshToast wondered whether anyone would buy a hypothetical “$500 game up for sale on steam,” and Crunchy answered “what if it was skyrim times 10 with good gameplay and online coop,” “cross platform,” “epic narrative” (M00163–M00167, 2021-05-25). This is an aspirational pitch, not a real price point. Legal business CANON The team investigated UAE commercialization. “In the UAE, you need a commercial license for… any revenue above AED 50,000” (M03032–M03033, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-14) — noted as “11,200 British pounds” (M03034, 2023-03-14). Crucially, “you don’t get taxed on it” in the UAE, whereas “in the uk you will get taxed on it regardless” (M03035–M03036, 2023-03-14). Conclusion: “so, we will likely sell the game through a commercial license” (M03037, 2023-03-14), with the team confident they were prepared — “Me and noods have looked into this heavily and are now more than prepared to do so” (M03038, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-14). A Reddit r/gamedev thread on legal needs and a game-dev legal guide were shared as resources (009 §Part 11 Resources; 009 §Part 12). Development timeline CANON The ship-window estimate below is the business framing; for the full project chronology see [[development-timeline]]. Asked “When do you think we will have a game to sell? 3 years?” (M03040–M03041, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-14), the estimate landed at: “Idk, a year? 2 years? I was being generous with a year probably. I think 2 to 3 years is likely” (M03042–M03043, FreshToast, 2023-03-14), refined to “I think 2 years to put things together” (M03044, CrunchyConniptions) plus “another year for polish and last touches before launch” (M03046, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-03-14) — “It really depends on how intense the production stage will be” (M03045, FreshToast, 2023-03-14). Related engine/timeline context: UE5 was judged “not production ready,” so unless building for two-plus years the team might start in UE4 and convert later — “Two years is the least that I was expecting” (009 §Part 2 Technical Development Notes). Development delegation philosophy A later business-model stance (2024-05-18) frames the studio as delegation-first: “This is the future of digital work” (M05409, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-18) — piecemeal the project and delegate anything that can be done by anyone (M05410, 2024-05-18): “really at the core of it, we only need 1 thing[]: Game design” (M05414, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-18), with the code, audio, voice, animation and art delegated to freelancers (M05415, 2024-05-18). The Japanese industry (freelance 3D modelers and anime animators) is cited as the model (M05417, M05418, 2024-05-18), and “digital nomad” work is pitched as a team perk (M05419, 2024-05-18; 009 §Part 22 Freelance/Delegation Philosophy). Earlier, an Upwork freelancer ( Jahongir Erkinov ) had been shared as a concrete lead (M03076, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-05-16; 009 §Part 12). Open questions DEBATED How exactly to balance the anti-meta (“expensive to use meta builds”) system (009 §Part 1 Unresolved Questions). Whether skill-based rewards should exist at all — floated, then judged exploitable (009 §Progression System Debates; see [[design-philosophy]]). Design history 2021-05-25 — the monetization stance is set: loot boxes acceptable if reasonable/cosmetic, Apex Overwatch as good examples, Fortnite left ambiguous; the “$500 game / Skyrim ×10” aspirational pitch is floated the same day (M00163–M00175, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25). 2023-03-14 — the business plan is worked out in one session: UAE commercial license above AED 50,000 (untaxed there, taxed in the UK), likely sell under that license, and a “2 years + 1 year polish” timeline (M03032–M03046, CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-03-14) — the most detailed monetization/business plan . 2024-05-18 — the delegation-first studio model is articulated: piecemeal the project, core members do game design while code/audio/voice/animation/art are freelanced, Japan cited as the model, digital-nomad work as a perk (M05409–M05419, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-18) — the most recent business-model statement . Provenance Raw: M00163–M00175 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2021-05-25); M03032–M03046 (CrunchyConniptions/FreshToast, 2023-03-14); M03076 (Upwork lead, 2023-05-16); M05409–M05419 (delegation model, CrunchyConniptions, 2024-05-18) Cleaned: 000 §Other Notes (Monetization / Legal); (no dedicated 000 monetization section — anchored to raw) Docs: 009 §Monetization Discussion (Part 1); 009 §Legal and Business (Part 11); 009 §Development Timeline (Part 11); 009 §Freelance/Delegation Philosophy (Part 22) Ledger: loot-box/monetization-context rows (M00169–M00175) in Documentation/Verification/discord_provenance_ledger.csv"},{"title":"References and Inspirations","group":"Development","url":"development/references-and-inspirations.html","text":"References and Inspirations CANON The consolidated pile of games, anime, films, mythology, music and educational content the team named as touchstones across all 24 parts of the design chat — with Dark Souls and Bloodborne as the acknowledged core. Summary — Across three years of discussion the team cited dozens of works as inspiration. The stable core is Soulsborne — “Dark souls and bloodborne will always be the core to me (until some[one] makes a better game)” (M00064, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-03-13) — layered with JRPG, tactics, and character-collection games (XCOM, Fire Emblem, Genshin/Pokémon), plus anime, film, mythology and a running soundtrack playlist. This page consolidates those references; individual mechanics they inspired live on the [[overview|systems]] and [[design-philosophy]] pages. The core: Dark Souls Bloodborne The stated foundation. “Coop dark souls adventure with engaging combat” was the earliest clear gaming direction (M00040, CrunchyConniptions, 2021-03-13), and “Dark souls and bloodborne will always be the core to me” fixed the north star (M00064, 2021-03-13). Specific praise: Bloodborne’s “quality over quantity,” its thematic traps and weapon design (009 §Part 1); memorable FromSoft bosses — Sif, Artorias, Maria, Friede (009 §Part 1); Sekiro’s refusal to scale to skill (“The challenge forces you to improve on your own,” 009 §Part 1). Dark Souls also supplies the homey Firelink-Shrine bonfire feeling (see [[design-philosophy]]) and the “crows” sacrifice-an-item mechanic (009 Appendix). Caveat noted early: “Dark Souls has a problem with lack of build diversity in built in progression” (M00049, FreshToast, 2021-03-13). Games (by role) Souls-likes action Sekiro — best FromSoft bosses; grapple points in the environment; no skill-scaling (009 §Parts 1, 2). Ashen, Absolver — souls-like references; Absolver’s “goldlinking” steal mechanic (009 §Part 2). Lords of the Fallen — luck-multiplier system, “nerve-wracking” (009 §Part 17). NieR: Automata — combat feel (009 §Part 1); progression “toned down” reference (009 §Part 1). Ghostrunner, Titanfall 2 — grappling / movement-combat mixture; Titanfall 2’s grapple “loved” (009 §Part 2). DMC, Bayonetta, Transformers Devastation, Metal Gear Rising: Revengeance — hack-and-slash / scoring / slicing feel (009 §Part 2). Katana Zero, Hotline Miami, SuperHOT — one-hit-kill / puzzle-combat approaches (009 §Part 2). JRPG, tactics party XCOM D D — position-system inspiration for the [[position-system]] (009 §Part 20). Fire Emblem (“Fremblem”) / Three Houses / Radiant Dawn — grid-based inspiration; Three Houses for NG+ and battalion-communication issues (009 §Parts 20, 21, 24). Etrian Odyssey — similar to the 2×3 grid; Yu-Gi-Oh as a card-game analogue (009 §Part 20). Slay the Spire Into the Breach — deterministic, readable communication of outcomes (009 §Part 20). Pokémon — “counts as JRPG”; nuzlockes and sentimental attachment; evolution-through-hardship analogy; exploration skills (Klee/Amber-style) (009 §Parts 11, 13). Persona / SMT — fusion system; Persona 5 specifically for UI, portraits, animation, the interrogation-scene in-medias-res opening, and music (009 §Parts 13, 19). Darkest Dungeon — cited as what not to do for positioning (“inferior imitation avoided”) and for frustrating unpreventable deaths, but also Death’s Door and its light system (009 §Parts 17, 20). Golden Sun, Zelda / Zelda-likes, Xenoblade — dungeon-design research (Golden Sun “renowned for its dungeons”) (009 §Part 21). Genshin Impact — character/gacha model, artifact system, exploration skills, progression (009 §Parts 1, 13, 17). Valheim — progression system (009 §Part 1). Skyrim — exploration, compass-over-map navigation, skill trees, first-person horror (009 §Parts 1, 2). Divinity: Original Sin 2 — narrator, source-points/armor system, writing; also cited with problems (abuse cases, OP warfare) (009 §Parts 1, 2, 11, 13). Other named games Death Note (Light vs L narrative structure) (009 §Part 1); Ghost of Tsushima (nightmare-raid co-op) (009 §Part 1); Infamous: Second Son (neon powers) (009 §Part 1). Fallout (Nuka Cola, radio, political messaging, treasure troves), Disco Elysium (readable adverts/books; explicitly an inspiration source), Helldivers 2 (parody propaganda) — all feeding the anachronism/immersion philosophy for Caelian Odyssey (009 §Part 24). Hades / Cult of the Lamb — genre options for the [[project-magitech|Project Magitech]] concept (009 §Part 12). Vampire the Masquerade — cited as what not to do (too-complex vampire org) (009 §Part 22). Monetization exemplars — Apex Legends and Overwatch loot boxes “done well”; see [[monetization-and-business]]. Anime, manga film Hunter x Hunter — narrators; the Phantom Troupe as a party-vs-party antagonist template (009 §Parts 2, 5). Frieren — Himmel’s dungeon exploration reflecting JRPG habits; peaceful nostalgic tone (009 §Part 24). Ranking of Kings, Bocchi the Rock — underdog storytelling “done right,” measured/personal cruelty (009 §Part 19). My Hero Academia (All Might/Deku mentorship), Red Dead Redemption 2 (Dutch/Arthur mentor dynamic and gang decline) — mentor/protégé references (009 §Parts 17, 22). Spider-Verse / Spider-Man — “great power, great responsibility”; a good version of the death/legacy theme (009 §Part 17). Berserk (Guts’ Dragonslayer → Warpath weapon), Fate/Zero (what makes a king), Kaguya-sama , Sword Art Online — assorted character/scene references (009 §Parts 17, 19). Game of Thrones — measured cruelty, reactions to cruelty, Jon Snow as underdog, and a debate on mature vs “too horny” sex handling (009 §Part 19). Death Note, Albedo (Overlord), Gojo (JJK), Shadow the Hedgehog — character-personality / ability / “magical skates” references (009 §Part 2). Mythology, real world MTG Celtic and Norse mythology — via Tolkien, as a source for creature names (goblins as house spirits, kobolds, hobs, bugbears) (009 §Part 17). The Bible — Satan/fallen-angel and Raphael/angelic-purity framing for the character Raphael (009 §Part 20). Real-world biomes — salt flats, thermal pools, coastal biomes, redwood forest, sinkhole forests, Garden of the Gods (from a TikTok level-design reference) (009 §Part 11). Real-world flavor for Caelian Odyssey — golden-age pirate history, Caribbean climate/culture, the 18/1900s of “wacky invention,” 90s carbonated drinks and glass-bottle sodas (009 §Part 24). Magic: The Gathering — an old MTG set referenced for feathered-dragon design in the [[vaenngrym]] feather debate (009 §Part 11). Educational technical content YouTube channels : Mark Brown’s Game Maker’s Toolkit (GMTK), Design Doc , Extra Credits (009 §Part 2). GDC talks on characters, camera, AI, and enemy design; a ~1h40 gacha-RPG review using Genshin as a case study; a water-level design video (009 §Part 2). Courses communities : multiple Udemy courses for Unreal Engine and C++; r/unrealengine; r/gamedev legal resources (009 §Parts 2, 11). Assets : Unreal marketplace (Frank Pack animations; a cel-shading asset “to rip apart and replicate”), Mixamo, and a 40GB Sonniss sound-effects pack (009 §Parts 2, 11). Academic sources quoted on game design — functional vs aesthetic information; challenge and reward from “learning / using / using-in-extraordinary-situations” the mechanics (009 §Design Quotes from Academic Sources). Soundtrack playlists A running set of Spotify (and one YouTube) playlists shared as “soundtrack inspiration.” The first: “This is what I’ve got so far for what might be good soundtrack inspiration” (M00580, FreshToast, 2021-08-03), with earlier YouTube playlists (M00272, 2021-07-24) and many later ones through 2024 (e.g. M02022, 2023-02-14; M02899, 2023-03-05; M05389, 2024-03-30). FreshToast liked keeping each playlist “around 20 minutes” (M01363, 2023-02-09). Also referenced: sea-shanty YouTube links and the Borderlands 1 opening song (009 §Part 1). Open questions References are descriptive touchstones, not commitments; several games are cited both as models"},{"title":"Team Attribution","group":"Development","url":"development/team-attribution.html","text":"Team Attribution CANON The record of who created what, as stated by FreshToast during the 2023-10-13 team argument: Caelian Odyssey is a confluence of the team’s ideas collated by FreshToast, with CrunchyConniptions having concepted 6 of the 7 Odyssey main characters and chosen the core classic-fantasy aesthetic. Summary — On 2023-10-13, during a heated exchange over creative ownership and design cohesion, FreshToast laid out an explicit attribution of who contributed what to Caelian Odyssey (raw M04050–M04057, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). The key facts: of the 7 Odyssey main characters, CrunchyConniptions concepted 6 (M04051); the core classic-fantasy aesthetic was CrunchyConniptions’s choice (M04053); and the overall project is “a confluence of our ideas, collated by [FreshToast],” who states his sole creation “would not even resemble what we have discussed so far” (M04057). This attribution is drawn directly from the raw Discord export and is absent from the compiled 000 notes and the 00X docs by design — it comes from an interpersonal dispute rather than a design section, so it is preserved here as the canonical authorship record. Note on tone. These lines are excerpted from an argument. They are quoted faithfully as the record; the frustration in them is part of the source (see [Style Guide §Honesty rules]). Authorship (raw M04050–M04057, FreshToast, 2023-10-13) 6 of 7 Odyssey mains are Crunchy’s concepts. “You concept 6 of our 7 main characters in Odyssey” (M04051, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). The core classic-fantasy aesthetic is Crunchy’s choice. “The core classic fantasy aesthetic was your choice” (M04053, FreshToast, 2023-10-13). FreshToast is the collator/producer of the setting and narrative. He describes spending “an evening redefining half of my vault and notes because you wanted to change the magic system” (M04050), and asks “why on earth am I going to produce a setting and narrative for us?” — noting “TWO narratives now, in fact” (M04058, M04059, 2023-10-13). Major decisions were jointly confirmed. “I have confirmed every major decision along the way with you MANY [times]…” — noting Crunchy’s “casual disinterest,” “which is fine but you CHOSE not to contribute to it” (M04054, 2023-10-13). Some elements were added on Crunchy’s request. “You asked me JUST NOW to name this river you’re so in love with for you ” (M04055); “[[ruby|Ruby]] arbitrarily has a hammer now because you asked” (M04056, 2023-10-13). The project is a collaboration collated by FreshToast. “It is a confluence of our ideas, collated by me. My sole creation would not even resemble what we have discussed so far” (M04057, 2023-10-13). Context These statements come from a 2023-10-13 argument that began over whether the river ([[caelia|Caelia’s Breath]]) needed a dedicated mechanic. The dispute broadened into questions of design cohesion and whose ideas the game embodies (M04040–M04065, FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13). The exchange later cools with “I think there is a lot of miscommunication happening here” (M04060, CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13) and FreshToast affirming “I will try to make what you want work and I like the idea too but it must be cohesive” (M04064, 2023-10-13). The three-person team throughout is CrunchyConniptions , FreshToast , and spirit_noodle (consistent “Team Members in Discussion” across 000 Parts 1–24). Open questions The identity of the 1 of 7 Odyssey mains not concepted by CrunchyConniptions is not stated in these messages. PROPOSED — undetermined from raw M04050–M04057 alone. Design history 2023-10-13 — during the river-mechanic argument, FreshToast states the authorship split: Crunchy concepted 6 of 7 Odyssey mains, chose the core classic-fantasy aesthetic; the project is a confluence of the team’s ideas collated by FreshToast (M04050–M04057, FreshToast). This is the sole and current attribution statement. Provenance Raw: M04050–M04057 (FreshToast, 2023-10-13); surrounding argument M04040–M04065 (FreshToast/CrunchyConniptions, 2023-10-13) Cleaned: not present in 000 by design — this authorship statement comes from an interpersonal dispute, not a design section Docs: not present in 00X by design Ledger: not separately rowed; sourced directly from raw per batch instructions"},{"title":"World (index)","group":"World","url":"world/index.html","text":"Cosmology, history, themes, races, factions, culture."},{"title":"Characters (index)","group":"Characters","url":"characters/index.html","text":"Party, antagonists, the Luminarium cast, NPCs, entities."},{"title":"Locations (index)","group":"Locations","url":"locations/index.html","text":"Regions, settlements, dungeons, biomes."},{"title":"Story (index)","group":"Story","url":"story/index.html","text":"Arcs, the two-path system, beats, quests, endings."},{"title":"Bestiary (index)","group":"Bestiary","url":"bestiary/index.html","text":"Bosses, enemy types, conditions, scaling."},{"title":"Items (index)","group":"Items","url":"items/index.html","text":"Weapons, armor, tools, consumables, relics."},{"title":"Magic (index)","group":"Magic","url":"magic/index.html","text":"The Flow, affinities, spells & skills."},{"title":"Systems (index)","group":"Systems","url":"systems/index.html","text":"Combat, dungeons, economy, progression, UX."},{"title":"Development (index)","group":"Development","url":"development/index.html","text":"Philosophy, inspirations, art, dropped ideas, timeline, iterations."}];